1. Product vision and non-negotiable principles
Field Shift helps a farmer understand current farm conditions, identify evidence-supported issues, evaluate feasible multi-season crop rotations, explore alternative environmental scenarios, track actual crop cycles, and inspect why the system reached its conclusions. The main value is not displaying lots of numbers: it is connecting location, crop, soil, water, climate, history, management practice, rotation science and uncertainty into decisions a farmer can understand.

The product has four connected jobs:
1. Observe: show the current state of a farm and relevant field-level differences.
2. Plan: recommend suitable crop sequences, durations and trade-offs for each field.
3. Investigate: re-fetch available data, recalculate supported indicators and explain current conditions through Deep Analysis.
4. Learn from records: preserve plans, actual crop-cycle events, historical analyses and what-if branches so the farmer can understand change over time.
Product rules
- A farm may contain multiple fields. Each field can have a different soil profile, crop history, rotation, water setup, crop-cycle state and alerts.
- Farm-wide weather, climate and terrain context may be displayed outside a selected field view when appropriate, but never merge field-specific soil or crop values as if every field were identical.
- Recommended, planned and actually planted crops are different states. A recommendation must never masquerade as an active crop.
- Use deterministic, testable code for scores and scientific calculations. AI is an explanation/investigation layer, not the source of truth for crop status, formulas, or numerical outputs.
- Historical records are snapshots. A new analysis or updated model must not silently rewrite an old result.
- Every meaningful number must carry its unit, time period, source type and relevant limitations. Use exact timestamps and location/source context when available.
- Distinguish observation, farmer report, estimate, forecast, model output, hypothesis and recommendation.
- The best rotation is the best feasible rotation under the farmer’s priorities, hard constraints and explicitly accepted risk preferences—not merely the largest raw score.
- If evidence is insufficient, say so. “Unknown”, “not measured”, “not available”, “stale”, “estimated”, and “not yet confirmed” are legitimate interface states.
- Never label a crop disease, nutrient deficiency, exact root-zone moisture, yield, carbon stock or field-level satellite result as confirmed unless the supporting method and data truly establish it.
- The UI should be visual and refined, but visual sophistication must never hide data limitations or turn fake values into authoritative-looking figures.
- Keep the overall site dark-only, restrained, clean and professional. Do not offer bright/amber/high-contrast themes or glowing-AI visual variants.
2. Final decision register — apply these decisions over older variants
1. One shared Farm View: Home, Maps and My Farms all open the same canonical private farm view for a selected farm, with selected-field drilldown where relevant. Do not build three subtly different farm-detail experiences.
2. Maps is a main navigation destination. Clicking the Home map preview navigates to the Maps page; do not expand the preview into a separate competing map mode.
3. Multiple fields per farm: one farm can contain multiple independently analysed fields. Map geometry represents the overall farm footprint. Individual fields do not require separate map polygons in the initial version; they are managed as distinct records and selected in the Farm View.
4. Farm drawing: the owner draws a polygon by adding points and connecting segments until the polygon closes. Editing, undo, redo, cancel, validation, area calculation, save, rename and delete must be real working interactions.
5. Analysis route: the main-nav Analysis page contains the What-If Simulator only. What-If flow is farm → field → saved rotation/analysis baseline → scenario factors. Rotation comparison is on Rotations.
6. Remove market/economic simulator and financial figures. Do not show fake prices, fake profit, costs, market intelligence or commercial claims. User-entered cost data is outside the current requested scope unless it already exists solely as a neutral record and is required for an existing calculation; do not make it a featured system.
7. Timeline: represent crop periods as explicit dated windows, e.g. “Oct 2026 – Mar 2027 · Wheat”. Use “Year 1/Year 2” only as an outer grouping for multi-year horizons, never as a replacement for seasonal date windows. Crop-history questions ask crop plus approximate planting and harvest month/year; do not force fixed Winter/Spring/Summer/Autumn slots globally.
8. Regions and crops: design for UAE demo farms and a few valid global presets. A crop whose validated parameters are missing for a location/horizon must be clearly marked unsupported/unavailable, not assigned a guessed score.
9. Local-only for now: no login, no account profiles, no cloud persistence, no Firebase Auth/Firestore/Storage, and no claimed cross-device sync. Use browser-local structured persistence.
10. Provider/key decision: provider choice is unrestricted. Clearly list selected providers and any required keys for the owner to configure. Prefer stable, appropriate APIs; try to use verified available integrations. Never expose secret keys in browser bundles, logs or prompts. Do not stop at naming APIs—implement the connector and status/error handling as far as the environment allows.
11. Gemini: use Gemini for grounded explanation and investigative tasks through a configured server/proxy path where a server exists. If no key or network-backed AI is available, fall back to a deterministic evidence-based explanation and clearly identify the fallback. Do not make the core score depend on LLM output.
12. Map imagery: use a suitable satellite/imagery source with a valid usage/attribution plan. Design the map source behind an adapter so the imagery provider can be changed without rebuilding polygon-drawing logic.
13. Vegetation health: only display satellite vegetation indicators/imagery when a real supported image/data pipeline is implemented. Otherwise show “Unavailable” with a useful reason; do not show fabricated NDVI numbers.
14. Notifications: in-app only for this version. No email, mobile push, or claims that an alert ran while the app was closed.
15. Language: English only for this version; keep layout code maintainable for a later RTL/localisation pass, but do not spend current scope on Arabic translation.
16. Calendar remains a separate main-nav page. Link crop-cycle milestones into it rather than duplicating it inside Farm Analysis.
17. Crop photography: use appropriately licensed/reusable imagery and attribution when required; do not reuse the same crop photo for different crop types. If a crop photo cannot be verified or loaded, use a crop-specific designed illustration/icon fallback and label it correctly.
18. Confidence badges: distinguish two concepts visually, not just by changing the text: (a) Recommendation Confidence — stability/strength of the model recommendation; (b) Data Coverage — completeness, recency, spatial fit and provenance of the underlying evidence. Use distinct icon + label + badge shape or pattern and a legend. Colour alone must not convey the distinction.
19. Farm lifecycle/shared data: implement the Farm State and Crop Lifecycle System and the Shared Farm Data System. These are accepted scope. Other separately proposed additions (a separate Decision Centre, standalone action centre, a new standalone data-confidence dashboard, standalone recommendation/outcome tracking product) are not to be added as new top-level modules. Existing alerts, provenance, risk mitigation, logs and history inside established features remain in scope.
20. Global Farming Network: its long-term concept is documented, including public farms, city-level aggregates, farmer connections, messaging, communities, comparisons and regional intelligence. However, cross-user publishing/messaging/aggregation cannot work honestly in local-only/no-login mode. Build the local-only-compatible shell and local/private map experiences; mark cross-user capabilities as “Requires shared backend — not available in this local build” or keep them as disabled future interfaces. Do not fabricate other farmers, public profiles or live community activity. Preserve the architecture and detailed requirements for a future backend.
21. Deep Analysis automation: a browser-only local build cannot guarantee execution while the browser/app is closed. Implement a truthful best-effort local scheduler that runs after startup/while the app remains open and reports this constraint. Do not label it as a reliable cloud background job. Future unattended execution requires a backend/runtime worker.
