- Every crop type must have a distinct correct photo, or a distinct crop-specific illustration when imagery is unavailable. Do not use one wheat photo for wheat, beans, lettuce and maize.
- Store crop display name, image source URL/asset, credit/license, alt text and fallback. Do not hotlink a random search-result thumbnail without checking stable availability and reuse conditions.
- For text over photos, use layered dark gradients rather than a flat transparent black rectangle only. Example: bottom-to-top gradient rgba(5,12,7,.86) at bottom through rgba(5,12,7,.22) at the image midpoint to transparent near the top. Add a subtle border/inset shade if needed. Never sacrifice readable foreground text to show the image.
- Use object-fit: cover, carefully chosen object positions, focal-point presets per crop, and loading=lazy for below-the-fold photos. Explicit image dimensions/aspect ratios prevent layout shifts.
- Build useful crop image fallbacks: distinctive crop silhouettes/illustrations and crop label. An image error must not collapse the card.
- Avoid unlicensed brand marks or screenshots from reference products. Build original components inspired by interaction principles.
5.7 Iconography and illustrated data
- Use one consistent icon set (Lucide React is already installed) with 1.75–2 px stroke and a consistent 16/18/20/24 px size scale.
- Icons support labels; they do not replace text for important controls.
- Create a distinct icon system for weather, water, soil chemistry, soil structure, vegetation, crop stage, planting, harvest, alert, data source, calculator and confidence. Do not randomly mix emoji into professional surfaces.
- Use custom SVG illustrations/mini diagrams for soil layers, root-zone water, crop succession, rotation loops and data flow where they materially help comprehension.
- 3D visuals are allowed only when they communicate scale, terrain or a real agronomic concept. Prefer a small, optimised terrain/field visualization or a high-quality isometric explainer over a decorative spinning globe. Maps remain a proper interactive geospatial component, not a fake 3D globe used as a substitute.
5.8 Motion language
Use restrained, purposeful animation and reduced-motion fallbacks.
- Route/page content: 160–240 ms fade/translate of 4–8 px; never make users wait for an elaborate transition.
- Popover/drawer: 180–260 ms with opacity and small directional slide; close with matching motion.
- Farm selection on Maps: camera eases to the existing saved location, 450–800 ms depending on distance/zoom, then selected boundary settles with a brief outline emphasis. Do not pulse indefinitely.
- Data loading: skeleton shimmer or a progress indicator with labelled phase; never fake numeric counters rolling to a predetermined result.
- Charts: small one-time draw-in on first appearance, and animate only the series that actually changed. Keep exact value access possible immediately.
- Timeline progress: animate state transition but do not imply elapsed real time from an arbitrary animation.
- Hover: 120–180 ms subtle border/tone changes; lift no more than 1–2 px and not on every surface.
- Map drawing: show vertex handles, live segment, valid/invalid state, snapping assistance where safe and an explicit “Close boundary” action as alternative to clicking the initial point.
- Follow prefers-reduced-motion; avoid flicker, lightning/rain effects behind analytical content, animated backgrounds, continuous blur effects and full-screen video backgrounds.
5.9 Premium interaction details
Use these deliberately where appropriate:
- Progressive disclosure: simple summary first, technical details one click away.
- Sticky page-level context on long pages.
- Command-style search for farms/fields/crops/analysis logs, but only for records available locally.
- Hover/focus preview of a farm row and keyboard-selectable actions.
- Split panel: map on one side, list/details on the other.
- Side-by-side comparison with synchronized category rows and clear differences.
- Inline source tags that open evidence drawers.
- Undo for local polygon editing before save, and clear confirmation for destructive persisted actions.
- Keyboard shortcuts only when shown in a help/shortcut menu and non-conflicting with browser defaults.
- Skeletons shaped to the eventual content; contextual empty-state illustrations; honest retry controls.
PART IV — PAGE-BY-PAGE PRODUCT SPECIFICATION
6. Home — the daily farm tracker
Purpose
Home answers: “Which farm needs attention, what is its general condition, and what should I look at first?” It is not a raw-data warehouse and must not rerun a full crop rotation generation every time Home opens.
Desktop composition (1440 × 900 reference)
1. App shell/header/horizontal top navigation as described above.
2. Page heading: “Good [time of day]” is optional and must not be the main feature. Prefer a concise “Farm overview” title and last refresh label.
3. Farm selector beneath the title, around 44–48 px high, spanning only the necessary width. Show farm name, city/region and farm status dot; opening it lists saved farms with status and field count.
4. Main hero/status region across approximately 7–8 grid columns, with a separate weather card across 4–5 columns or beneath it at medium breakpoints. Hero background can use a farm/crop photograph with a forest overlay; image contrast must not compete with the status.
5. Secondary row: 3–4 key indicator summaries only, selected from actual available indicators (e.g. water, soil, active crop/cycle, unresolved alert). Do not fill all slots with unavailable metrics.
6. Map preview and “Open Maps” action. A click anywhere on the preview opens Maps; it does not begin drawing on Home.
7. Recent relevant items only: recent completed Deep Analysis, next crop milestone, or important unresolved farm alert. Do not add an artificial “recent activity” feed when there is nothing to report.
Status card contents
- Selected farm name and location label (never overstate GPS precision).
- Status label: Good / Needs Attention / Alert / Insufficient data.
- Last analysed/updated timestamp and whether the current report is stale.
- A short reason for the status, such as “One field has a supported low-water warning” or “No active concern found in the available data.”
- Active crop and lifecycle status only if confirmed; otherwise show “No active crop confirmed” and the planned next crop where available.
- One relevant CTA, such as “Open Farm View,” “Review alert,” or “Run Deep Analysis.” Do not show multiple competing primary actions.
Weather card
Display only supported weather concepts:
- Current conditions and forecast are separate modes/labels.
- Temperature, precipitation/rain chance, wind, humidity or high/low range according to available provider fields.
- Location and retrieved/valid time; units selectable only if a consistent conversion layer exists.
- Use a weather illustration or restrained iconography; avoid rain animations behind the values.
- Weather at farm/coordinate context may be reused at farm level, not treated as separate exact observation for each field unless provider resolution supports it.
- A forecast provider outage should preserve a previous valid forecast with its original timestamp and visible stale label, or show unavailable.
Farm health status logic
Use a consistent domain status function shared by Home, Maps and Farm View. Proposed qualitative statuses:
- Good: no active supported high-risk alert and no supported indicator meets a concern rule.
- Needs Attention: at least one supported concern needs monitoring, but no current qualifying Alert condition.
- Alert: at least one validated high-priority rule is triggered, or multiple evidence sources support a high-priority concern.
- Insufficient data: evidence is too incomplete/stale to assign a responsible state. This is not equivalent to Good.
Status must be derived from explicit documented rules, not an arbitrary average of all metrics. A serious critical issue must not be hidden by averaging many good indicators. However, avoid declaring an alert from a weak or coarse proxy alone. A farm’s map outline colour uses the worst supported field status; unknown data must not automatically be painted green. If all fields are unknown, use the neutral insufficient-data colour.
Home interactions
- Switch farm: update status, weather context, map preview, alerts and crop lifecycle together; cancel or discard stale in-flight requests for the previous farm.
- Click current farm: open canonical Farm View.
- Click map: navigate to Maps and preserve selected farm.
- Click alert: mark it read only when opening/viewing, navigate to the relevant farm/field and retain the alert ID/context.
- Click “Deep Analysis”: start or open the selected farm’s Deep Analysis workflow; do not confuse with a daily status refresh.
- Home must not silently update crop status, create plans, or overwrite a rotation.
Home states
- First run/no farm: visual welcome, brief description and one CTA “Create farm / Start New Analysis”; optionally “Load demo example” explicitly labeled. No fake farms pre-populated as real.
- No analysis yet: explain that farm details exist but no rotation has been generated, link to New Analysis.
- Farm exists without selected field: show farm aggregate and prompt to pick a field for field-level values.
- Partial sources: keep supported weather/status and list what is unavailable.
- Nothing needs attention: show calm neutral message, not a green success panel overflowing with fake data.
7. Maps — interactive geospatial workspace
Purpose
Maps is the authoritative private place for locating saved farms, drawing/editing a farm outline, switching between a farmer’s farms, and opening Farm View. It must feel like an excellent geospatial workbench, not a generic page with a map screenshot.
Layout
- Full main content canvas below the 64–72 px application header, with minimal outer gutters.
- Top-left floating search control: approximately 360–440 px desktop width, 48–52 px height, with search icon, type-to-search, clear affordance and result list. Results cover city/place lookup and the owner’s saved farm names. Use a real geocoding provider.
- Left-side vertical map tool rail: 52–60 px wide on desktop; contain select/pan, draw boundary, edit boundary, undo, redo, cancel, layer control and help icons as appropriate. Disable actions that do not apply in the current mode.
- Top-right My Farms floating card (user specifically requested farms at the top right): approximately 270–320 px wide, max-height around 45–55 vh, scrollable list with farm thumbnail/status/name/field count. Selection eases camera to saved farm, highlights its outline and updates a visible selection state. Avoid covering the map attribution or core controls.
- Bottom-left map info/scale/attribution area; coordinate/status information can appear only where useful. Map attribution must remain visible.
- A small clear legend indicating the private farm outline/status colours and active drawing/editing state.
- Main canvas uses real satellite imagery where an imagery source is configured and legally/technically available. Add a fallback streets/terrain layer if imagery fails; report the fallback.
- Do not add fake roads, farm imagery or a fake “NASA layer”. Layer labels must match actual sources.
Farm boundary drawing workflow
A. Enter drawing mode by selecting “Draw farm boundary.” Show a clear instruction strip: “Click the map to add boundary points. Close the shape to finish.”
B. Each click adds a node. Draw line segments between consecutive nodes in a distinct accent outline. Show an actively moving segment from last node to pointer if supported and accessible.
C. Close the polygon by clicking the first node, or use a visible “Close boundary” button. At least three distinct vertices are required. A self-intersecting polygon, invalid geometry, repeated adjacent point, implausibly small area or other invalid shape must be flagged with a plain-language repair message.
D. Provide Undo, Redo, Cancel drawing, Clear draft, Edit vertices and Save boundary. Undo/redo is for the unsaved drawing session only until the edit is committed. Keyboard alternatives must be offered where practical.
E. On closing a valid polygon, calculate area geodesically from the polygon coordinates. Use a geospatial library such as Turf.js area; do not approximate degrees squared as hectares. Convert square metres to hectares/acres only for display and store canonical units.
F. Show a confirmation panel with calculated area, boundary preview, location summary, and a field to pick an existing farm or create a farm name. If a matching farm name exists, do not silently overwrite. Ask whether they intend to link/edit that existing farm’s boundary or cancel/create a distinct name. If replacement/edit is confirmed, preserve geometry version/history as appropriate.
G. Save only after confirmation. Persist polygon coordinates, area, source (owner drawn), geometry version and timestamp. The drawn geometry is private and may identify the exact farm location.
H. On editing an existing boundary, show the current outline plus draft edits. Cancel restores saved boundary. Save confirms the new geometry and recomputes area; ask whether related field-area entries should be reviewed if the new farm area becomes inconsistent with the sum of field areas.
I. Delete map drawing is distinct from deleting a farm. Explain the consequence. Removing geometry leaves the farm, fields, rotations, analysis runs, crop-cycle events and activity history intact. Delete farm is a separate explicit destructive flow.
Layer and map interactions
- Private My Farms layer: the owner can see saved farm boundaries at their true saved geometry. Colour each boundary by the farm’s aggregate status derived from its worst supported field.
- Farm focus: clicking inside a saved private boundary selects the farm. Avoid accidental selection of nearby overlapping boundaries; use hit-testing and a clear selected outline.
- Zoom to selected farm: use camera bounds with padding for the right-hand farms panel. Do not snap to a global default after a farm is clicked.
- Search place: pan/zoom to the geocoded place; do not overwrite farm location just because the user searched for a location.
- Satellite layer: only show if tiles load; support fallback layers and network-error state. Layer switch should not reset drawing or selected farm.
- Drawing mode: disable accidental farm selection while adding points. Show a visible mode badge and Esc/cancel behaviour.
- No farm yet: show a map with clear draw/search controls and one first-run tip; never create a fictitious boundary automatically.
- No imagery: retain map interaction and boundaries over an available fallback basemap; explain why the imagery layer is unavailable.
- Preserve map state on navigation to Farm View and back, including camera, layer, selected farm and any saved non-active filter. Do not leave half-completed drawings on a route change without confirming cancel/save.
Multi-field rule
The farm polygon represents the overall farm footprint. The first version does not require individual field polygons on the map. Fields are records within the farm and are separately listed in Farm View. The farmer may enter area and soil/input details per field. Total entered field area should be compared with the polygon-derived farm area, with a tolerance and informative warning; never silently change typed field area to force equality. If the user later chooses to enable field polygons, design that as an optional extension, not a replacement for the farm geometry.
Map tech and robustness
Prefer a maintained map renderer with a drawing library that supports polygon editing, map interactions, and geospatial calculations. MapLibre GL JS + Terra Draw + Turf.js is a strong candidate; Terra Draw supports multiple map renderers and editable polygons, while Turf’s area calculates geodesic polygon area. Google Maps’ old Drawing Library should not be chosen for a new build because its functionality became unavailable in May 2026. See official references listed in the final research section.
8. My Farms — farm list and management entry
My Farms should be a readable farm portfolio, not a duplicate of the map page. The goal is to select, organise, rename, inspect or delete a farm; deep farm science stays in Farm View.
Layout
- Title row: “My Farms” and a single primary action “Add farm”/“Draw farm on map”.
- Summary strip: total saved farms, total tracked fields, count of farms needing attention—only computed from local records and clearly contextualised.
- Responsive list/table of farms with image or map thumbnail, name, city/region, field count, current status, last Deep Analysis timestamp, latest plan status and last update.
- Search and filter by status/name; sort by name, status or most recently updated. Do not add filters whose source values do not exist.
- Each row has primary selection/open action and a three-dot menu for rename, edit map boundary, export, or delete. Delete has confirmation and a clear explanation of linked data.
- On mobile, switch to vertical farm cards with the same hierarchy and actions.
Management behaviour
- “Add farm” provides two routes: (a) begin New Analysis and create a named farm from inputs, or (b) open Maps and draw the farm footprint. These routes converge on the same stable farm record; creating a farm in one route must not create a duplicate when the other route later links it.
- A farm created through New Analysis may exist without map geometry. A drawn outline may link to an existing analysis farm or create a new farm awaiting field-level details and analysis.
- If duplicate names are allowed, display disambiguating location/ID rather than assuming name equals identity. The user asked for a matching name to link to an already analysed farm; confirm that linking/editing is intended before action.
- Renaming the farm updates visible labels across Home, Maps, My Farms, Farm View, Rotations context, Calendar event titles and logs, while immutable IDs and saved historical content remain intact. Historic reports may show the farm name as it was at run time plus a link to the current farm; do not rewrite their data snapshot.
- When deleting a farm, explain how fields, geometry, analyses, Deep Analysis logs, crop cycles, local schedules and calendar events are affected. Support export first. Do not cascade-delete without explicit user confirmation.
9. Canonical Farm View — shared farm detail and field drilldown
Purpose and navigation
One shared Farm View is reached from Home, Maps, My Farms, alerts and any farm/field link. It is the canonical place to review farm status and navigate into fields. This avoids multiple pages contradicting one another.
Farm-level overview layout
- Header: farm name, city/region, private/local label, farm status, last relevant data update and menu actions (rename, map boundary, settings, export/delete).
- Optional minimized map pinned to bottom-left if opened from Maps; compact dimensions around 260 × 170 px desktop, collapsible, with a clear map/expand icon and X. This is a navigation affordance, not a duplicate dashboard.
- Hero status summary with a photo/map thumbnail and short interpretation, plus the status reason and a “Deep Analysis” button.
- Farm-level context section: weather/forecast (if actual forecast service connected), longer climate context, broad terrain/elevation where supported. State whether values are shared to the farm or specific to a field.
- Fields overview: list/card per field, each with field name, area/unit, current crop-cycle status, selected rotation, health status and one or two evidence-supported key flags. Do not mash all soil metrics from all fields into one “farm soil” number.
- Latest rotation overview and recent analysis snapshots: compact context only, linked to Rotations/Deep Analysis Logs for more detail.
- Activity/crop-cycle milestones only when there is real recorded activity. Avoid a decorative activity feed when no events exist.
Select a field
Clicking a field opens its detailed view within the same farm context, e.g. /farms/:farmId/fields/:fieldId. Keep farm-level weather/climate/terrain context available in a compact header/sidebar, but the selected field becomes the source of truth for soil, crop, irrigation, rotation, crop lifecycle, status, alerts and field-specific data.
Field-level layout
1. Field header: field name, area, soil profile status, crop-cycle status and status badge; edit-field action.
2. Compact summary strip: active/planned crop, last analysis, water status if supported, soil summary if supported, vegetation observation only if real, relevant current alert count.
3. Rotation/Crop Cycle module: sequence timeline with dated windows, planned vs actual states, and lifecycle actions.
4. Tabs or anchored sections: Overview; Water; Soil; Crop & Vegetation; Risks & Observations; Activity History; Analyses/Deep Analysis. Use only sections that fit the field data and current implementation. Avoid 15 top-level tabs; group advanced data progressively.
5. Each section has Simple and Advanced display mode. Simple mode translates into farmer-friendly meaning, key evidence and next consideration. Advanced mode expands charts, date ranges, source records, formulas, parameters and detailed warnings.
6. Deep Analysis remains a separate explicit workflow. It can present its final report in the Field View, and its detailed execution records are accessible through the Deep Analysis Logs page.
7. Keep common farm-wide context visible with a clear “Farm conditions” label. If a selected field’s exact weather differs from the farm-level proxy, label it accordingly rather than imply field-level precision.
Field settings
- Edit field name and area/unit.
- Soil properties and sample/testing date/source.
- Irrigation setup, crop/management inputs and practices.
- Crop history with actual or approximate dates and source.
- Update preferred crop/rotation priorities only through clear controls.
- Field deletion is distinct from farm deletion and must explain what happens to linked field-specific records. The app can preserve a tombstone/archive in local history when necessary to keep snapshots interpretable.
Re-analysis and stale records
Changing location, soil, irrigation, crop history, priority or field configuration must not edit the original inputs of an old analysis run. Mark affected recommendations “May be outdated”/“Inputs changed since this run” and offer a new analysis. For routine data refresh, retrieve new sources and recalculate only the selected workflow; do not unexpectedly run the full crop-rotation search every time a field view is opened.
PART V — CORE AGRONOMIC WORKFLOWS
10. New Analysis — the guided recommendation workflow
Purpose
New Analysis generates and explains suitable multi-season crop-rotation candidates for a selected farm/field. It is the main workflow for creating new recommendations. It must not be confused with Farm View’s current-condition display, Deep Analysis or the What-If simulator.
Entry points
- Main “New Analysis” action on Home and My Farms.
- Farm View → field → “Create/Update Rotation Recommendation”.
- A missing-rotation empty state.
- Optional re-analysis from a saved plan, but re-analysis must explicitly capture whether values are unchanged, refreshed from new sources, or edited by the farmer.
Wizard UX
Build a polished multi-step wizard with a progress indicator and explicit back/next controls. Ask the minimum essential questions first, then optional questions that materially improve output. One question or coherent group per step; no giant form dump. Save the current draft locally so accidental navigation does not erase answers. Show a review step before running calculations. The farmer can edit any answer from the review screen.

Suggested step order:
1. Choose context: existing farm, existing field, or new farm/field. If an existing field is selected, prefill known values and show where each came from. Never silently overwrite saved fields.
2. Location: geocoder/search, optional map click, coordinates, display name, country/region and selected coordinate. Confirm before fetching location-specific information. If drawing an outline later, link that geometry to the same farm record instead of creating a duplicate.
3. Field and soil: area/unit, known soil texture, pH if measured/known, texture fractions if available, drainage, salinity/EC if known, organic matter/carbon if known, slope/terrain where appropriate. Each entered value includes “Measured/soil test”, “Farmer estimate”, “Mapped estimate” or “Unknown”. Do not force all answers if data is genuinely optional.
4. Water/irrigation: rain-fed/irrigated/mixed, irrigation type where relevant, water availability/constraints, irrigation system/application efficiency if known, routine/practice description only if useful. Do not infer volume from a statement like “water for 20 minutes” without emitter/flow, area, number of emitters and other needed variables.
5. Crop history: crop plus approximate planting and harvest month/year or actual dates. Allow fallow/no previous crop/unknown. Avoid fixed four-season assumptions. Validate overlap and order, but accept approximate records with a visible source/precision label.
6. Planning horizon: start date/window, number of growing cycles or years to consider, climate/region scope. Use crop-specific duration rather than assuming all crops are annual.
7. Priorities: water saving, soil health, climate resilience, crop suitability, yield/productivity only where supported, crop diversity/rotation break as appropriate. Explain each slider in plain language; keep hard feasibility constraints separate from personal preferences.
8. Optional accuracy details: fertilizer/amendments and management practices, pest/disease history, soil laboratory results, heat/frost sensitivity, preferred/avoided crops, field constraints. Mark optional and explain why each answer helps.
9. Review: concise structured summary of all inputs, selected data sources and unknowns; user may edit or choose “Continue with these limitations.”
10. Initial analysis/calculation: retrieve available data, validate, run deterministic model and generate candidate rotations.
11. Adaptive risk-tolerance step: only when candidate rotations contain a material trade-off that changes the recommendation. Ask targeted questions grounded in the results before final display.
12. Final results: top recommendation and alternates, score breakdowns, risks, crop timelines, confidence, source/data coverage, comparison and actions to save as a plan.
Data fetching sequence
- Geocode location to validated coordinates and a human-readable region.
- Fetch only available sources that are relevant to selected inputs and planning horizon.
- Normalize units, timestamps/time zones, grid resolution and missing-value conventions.
- Calculate climate features only from sufficiently complete data: growing-season temperature distribution, degree days, rainfall amount/distribution, dry spells, extreme heat/frost exposure, seasonality where applicable.
- Retrieve soil context if available but retain source/resolution/uncertainty. Do not treat mapped estimates as a soil lab test.
- Build a calculation input snapshot. The snapshot must contain actual values used, units, data source references, retrieval times, any interpolations, fallbacks and fields omitted because unavailable.
- Run the deterministic crop suitability and rotation candidate engine.
- Run agronomic feasibility checks before ranking.
- Build a structured report payload, then use AI only to explain supported results in farmer-friendly language.
- Save the run before presenting final actions, so results can be reopened and traced.
Adaptive risk-tolerance questions
This is an important product differentiator. It should appear after the first valid candidate calculation but before the final ranked recommendation is presented, and only when a real candidate trade-off deserves the farmer’s decision.

Each question includes:
- The choice Field Shift is considering and why it might help the stated priority.
- The material disadvantage/risk introduced by that choice.
- Evidence supporting the risk and evidence limitations.
- Severity / uncertainty and potential agronomic consequence if supported.
- Mitigation options and whether they require information or resources not yet known.
- Clear options: “Accept this trade-off”, “Prefer a balanced alternative”, “Reject this added risk”, or “Explain further”. Do not frame acceptance as smarter or more ambitious.
Example: “Rotation A is estimated to better match your productivity priority, but under the available evidence it has greater pest-family continuity risk. Would you accept that trade-off if you also commit to the listed monitoring/management measures, or should we prioritise a lower-risk balanced option?” This is illustrative language only; actual question facts must come from candidate-specific model outputs and evidence.

If the user accepts, include that risk preference in the final decision record and rank only feasible candidates consistent with that preference. Explicit acceptance cannot override a hard agronomic/safety constraint. If they reject it, filter or re-rank candidates and return the best feasible balanced option rather than merely removing the best result and leaving no answer. If several risks matter, avoid overwhelming the farmer; ask the few high-impact questions, combine only when the trade-offs are truly related, and show a summary of accepted/rejected trade-offs before finalising.
Priority weights
Use a documented normalized weighting scheme with bounded influence; avoid letting one slider silently dominate. Suggested mapping from a preference rating r_i in [0,4] is a positive transformed preference (r_i + 0.5)^1.6, followed by normalization so sum(w_i) = 1, with each weight bounded to a documented maximum and minimum. If clipping weights, renormalize all non-fixed weights and explain the effective weights in Advanced mode. Make sure all-zero preferences remain mathematically valid. The old code appears to clamp weights in an approximate [0.05, 0.40] range; audit the current implementation and either retain it with verified tests or replace with this clearly documented rule. Do not show slider percentages that do not match the weights actually used.
Results ordering
Rank candidates in descending order, best valid recommendation first. The score must have a consistent documented interpretation and must never override feasibility. Do not reorder candidates after displaying them merely because AI text suggests something else.
Required results
- One primary recommendation with a plain-language rationale tied to the farmer’s own field data and priorities.
- Alternative rotations, each with sequence, crop duration and date windows, full cycle length, overall score and score components, crop-specific suitability, benefits, limitations, current risks where supported, possible mitigations and data/confidence caveats.
- Explanation of why a high individual crop score does not automatically make the best sequence: crop family continuity, timing, water/soil carryover, duration overlap, infeasible transitions, resource bottlenecks and sequence-wide benefits can change ranking.
- A comparison entry point to Rotations page (not a second competing comparison interface embedded everywhere).
- “Save as Plan” action with selected farm/field and planned date review.
- “Try in What-If” link for supported scenarios on the Analysis page.
- A visible “what could change this recommendation?” section explaining key uncertainties.
Save and update rules
- Save each analysis as an immutable run containing input values, source snapshots/references, retrieval times, formulas/model version, candidate scores, feasibility decisions, adaptive trade-off answers and final rank.
- Choosing a rotation creates or updates a plan only after explicit confirmation. It does not set crop status to Active.
- Reanalysis creates a new run. The older run remains accessible and its values unchanged.
- If input data changes, show “Inputs changed since this run” or “New data available” and let the user decide whether to run again.
11. Rotation engine, Rotations page and detailed rotation view
Purpose
Rotations is where the farmer understands why one complete crop sequence is better for their field and priorities than other feasible sequences. It is not a marketplace, financial simulator or raw list of crop cards.
Rotation page composition
- Context bar with farm, field and the source analysis run.
- Top recommendation with crop sequence across a horizontal/vertical timeline; each crop tile includes distinct image, dates/duration, suitability and lifecycle label if the plan is already being tracked.
- Summary strip with score, recommendation confidence, data coverage, top reasons and major trade-off.
- Candidate list sorted highest to lowest. Show a compact summary by default; expand for detail. Keep top options visibly differentiated, but do not use a decorative medal/badge to imply scientific certainty.
- Filters limited to actual supported dimensions: score rank, risk preference, priority alignment, feasibility and saved plans.
- Clearly labelled comparison action (compare 2–3 candidates, subject to view width) with synchronized rows rather than two long text blocks.
Rotation card
Each candidate card should show:
1. Rotation name/short strategy title grounded in score components (e.g. “Water-conserving balanced rotation”, only if calculation supports it).
2. Sequence and duration/date windows for each crop.
3. Overall score with meaning and score scale.
4. Recommendation Confidence badge (distinct icon, such as shield/check/ring) and Data Coverage badge (distinct icon, such as database/layer stack), with textual tooltip/legend.
5. Strongest benefits and top limitations in concise content.
6. Most material risks and matched mitigations.
7. Short “Why this order?” explanation that cites the actual sequence interactions.
8. An expand/open control to detailed rotation report.
Detailed rotation view
A detailed view may use tabs/anchors with no more than 7–8 core groups:
- Overview / decision summary.
- Season-by-season timeline.
- Crop suitability and factors.
- Rotation score breakdown.
- Water/resource balance where supported.
- Soil/long-term dynamics projections where supported.
- Risk register and mitigations.
- Evidence/methodology and data provenance.
Include:
- Why this rotation won under the farmer’s weights.
- Why major alternatives ranked lower, referencing real component score differences and hard constraints.
- Per-season crop identity, planting/harvest window, duration and assumptions.
- Each crop’s suitability factors and limiting factors.
- Which improvements are modelled versus measured.
- Important unsupported claims the system refused to make.
- A timeline of suggested activities only where the existing product has evidence-based actions and date assumptions. Do not duplicate the Calendar application.
- A technical calculation expansion with formula, inputs, normalized units, intermediate values, output, model version, source and limitation.
- A “Save as Plan” action that invokes the shared crop-cycle flow.
Comparison interface
Allow selection of two candidates. Present rows for total score, priority alignment, water demand/deficit, soil trajectory, climate resilience, feasibility, risk, crop diversity, duration and confidence only where each metric is calculated for both candidates and comparable. Explain when an item is missing for one candidate. Use a difference column (“+8 score points”, “lower estimated water demand”, “not comparable”) only when mathematically grounded. Avoid double-bars or radar charts whose values are not normalized to comparable scales. A small multiples chart or paired horizontal bars can be used for consistent score components.
Custom Rotation Builder (advanced, inside Rotations)
Let a technically interested farmer create a custom candidate sequence using the same crop knowledge base, feasibility rules, timeline model and scoring engine as New Analysis. This must not be a separate calculator with divergent rules.
- Start from a generated candidate or an empty sequence for the selected field and planning horizon.
- Add a supported crop to a valid time slot, remove an unplanted future crop, reorder compatible sequence items, and choose allowed duration for perennial/multi-season crops when the knowledge base supports it.
- Show crop thumbnail/name, date window, duration, key suitability limits and sequence position. Provide accessible drag handles plus non-drag “Move earlier/later” controls.
- After an edit, validate geometry of the timeline: planting/harvest windows, overlap, growing period, perennial carryover, return interval, maximum consecutive family constraints, soil/rotation constraints and supported water/resource budget. Do not permit a silent invalid sequence.
- Show state labels: Feasible; Conditionally feasible (identify missing farmer decision/data); Not feasible (name blocking constraints). A high score never overrides infeasibility.
- Provide “Compare with original” and “Recalculate candidate”. Calculate score/risk/components using the exact shared engine. The custom draft has its own ID and input snapshot, linked to its baseline; do not rewrite the generated rotation.
- “Save as Plan” uses the Crop Cycle workflow and requires farm/field/date confirmation. Until saved, it remains a candidate draft, not a plan or actual crop cycle.
- If an edit is invalid, preserve the user's prior valid candidate and give a specific reason plus an allowed correction. Do not auto-replace the farmer’s crop without explicit consent.
Whole-rotation resource budget and stress test
For each rotation and each dated crop window, where data supports, show a seasonal resource budget: modelled crop water demand, effective rainfall, planned/recorded irrigation contribution, estimated shortfall and high-demand periods. Resource-budget results are modeled and must show assumptions. Do not add market revenue/cost numbers.

A rotation stress-test panel can reuse the Analysis/What-If engine to test defined stress scenarios: hot/dry historical year, lower water availability, delayed planting, missing irrigation, or crop-specific heat/frost period. Each scenario shows the exact changed assumptions and outputs. Do not label these as statistical probability unless the scenario library/model supports it. The stress-test summary should highlight downside and stability across scenarios, not only average score. If no valid historical/parametric scenarios are available, omit that claim and provide manual supported What-If controls instead.
Cycle length and review-by date
The engine must choose both per-crop duration and total rotation cycle length, not assume every crop occupies one uniform season. For annual crops, use one growth cycle by default if validated; for perennials/multi-year crops, test allowed durations from the crop knowledge base. Evaluate candidate total cycle lengths appropriate to the planning horizon (for example, 2–6 seasonal slots where supported, or longer where perennial duration requires) and favour coherent sequences rather than repeating one crop just to fill the timeline. If the engine models repeated cycle stability/drift, present that explicitly as a projection.

The plan can include a “Review this plan around…” date only when a rationale exists, such as when a new season’s data is expected, the next planting decision is due, or a slow soil trajectory needs several cycles. Label it a recommended review date, not an agronomic guarantee. The review interval must be explainable; do not present a made-up countdown.
Rotations as a portfolio of fields
If the farm has several fields, Rotations can show per-field saved plans. A possible advanced view called “Farm Rotation Overview” may show each field’s chosen sequence and reveal resource conflicts (e.g. multiple planned planting peaks) only when the required dates/resources are available. Do not automatically impose one rotation on every field. Any cross-field planning must be an integrated optional view of existing field plans, not a separate fake optimisation engine.
12. Analysis — What-If Simulator only
Navigation scope
The Analysis item in main navigation opens the What-If Simulator; do not keep the simulator as its own separate main-nav item. Other ways to reach it may include a “Try scenario” action from a saved rotation or Field View, but navigation still resolves to Analysis. Rotation comparison stays on Rotations.
User flow
1. Pick farm.
2. Pick field.
3. Select a saved New Analysis run / selected rotation as the baseline. If no valid saved run exists, offer New Analysis and do not fabricate a baseline.
4. Display a baseline summary with immutable input snapshot, source times, calculation version, priorities, risk preferences and sequence.
5. Choose one or multiple supported scenario variables. Use sliders, numeric inputs, dropdowns or toggles appropriate to the units and variable. Show actual baseline and modified values simultaneously.
6. Validate input bounds and dependencies before recalculation.
7. Re-run the same shared deterministic engines using changed inputs, not a duplicate approximation of the engine.
8. Compare baseline vs scenario and optionally generate alternative rotations under the scenario conditions.
9. Save the scenario or branch a new scenario. Hypothetical outputs stay separate from actual farm records.
Scenario controls
Potential supported variables, only when implemented by the engine:
- Soil texture, pH, salinity/EC, organic matter or water-holding parameters within sensible model ranges.
- Rainfall/precipitation pattern, temperature, heat exposure, drought duration and relevant weather series.
- Irrigation availability, application efficiency and water limits.
- Soil moisture/storage starting state where the model supports it.
- Location change (must refetch relevant climate/terrain/soil data if the location changes; don’t simply change a label).
- Crop or management assumptions, crop start date or crop duration where valid.
Every control must identify whether it is a hypothetical override or an actual farmer-recorded edit. Do not offer nutrient/salinity/soil inputs that do not affect the real calculation code. Do not allow impossible or unsupported values to quietly pass.
Scenario calculation pipeline
- Clone the immutable baseline input snapshot.
- Apply user overrides and preserve an exact “changed parameters” list.
- Validate each input and any downstream dependency.
- Recompute climate features, crop suitability, water model, soil projection, feasible rotations and score components using the same services as New Analysis.
- Reapply hard feasibility rules and the saved risk preference unless the user explicitly changes the risk preferences for the scenario.
- Check that score interpretation remains comparable; note if method/version/inputs changed so comparison is not apples-to-oranges.
- Report which changes caused output differences and separate direct mathematical effects from qualitative AI interpretation.
- If alternatives are newly better, present them with their changed rank and reasons. If no better alternative exists, say so; do not fabricate a superior rotation.
Required What-If output
- Baseline vs scenario values, side-by-side.
- Score change and component deltas.
- Per-crop suitability/risk change.
- Estimated water/resource balance changes where supported.
- Soil projection changes where supported, clearly marked as modelled.
- Changed feasibility status/hard constraints.
- Newly top-ranked rotation, if one changes.
- Uncertainty/coverage changes and limitations.
- “What changed and why?” explanation referencing actual changed variables and deterministic calculations.
- Scenario name, saved time, baseline run ID, engine/model version and changed parameters.
Scenario isolation rules
- Scenario records are hypothetical. They do not update a field’s actual crop, field soil profile, farm map, current status, actual calendar, actual planting dates or actual Deep Analysis history.
- “Adopt as plan” is a separate explicit workflow: create a new proposed plan or New Analysis run, then require confirmation. It must not directly convert simulated climate into real farm data.
- Reset returns all controls to the immutable baseline and removes unsaved overrides. Saved scenarios are not erased by reset.
- Side-by-side scenario comparison requires a shared baseline or a visible explanation of differing baseline inputs.
13. Calendar — separate crop-cycle schedule
Purpose
Calendar presents planned and actual crop milestones over time. It is not an independent source of crop state; it reads the shared Crop Cycle/activity records.
UI
- Month/week/list views; default view chosen for readability. Use a visual timeline for long crop rotations and a calendar grid for dates.
- Filters by farm, field, crop cycle, planned/actual event type, status and category only when relevant.
- Crop events use crop-specific image/icon markers; different event types use distinct icon + label, not colour alone.
- Date range controls include navigation arrows and a “Today” action. Local timezone handling is explicit.
- Add/Edit event controls can create supported farm activities; they must link to a farm/field/cycle and save locally.
- Planned events are visually different from completed/actual events. An expected harvest date is not displayed as a confirmed harvest.
Event types
Planting/transplanting, crop-stage milestone (if supported), irrigation, soil sampling/test, fertiliser/amendment, inspection, pest/disease observation, harvest, crop-cycle change and other user-defined farm note. Do not invent automated crop-stage dates unless crop metadata and planting date support them.
Date propagation
- Editing a planned planting date can shift dependent future planned dates according to crop durations and an explicit recalculation rule. Show a preview before saving if multiple calendar items change.
- Completed events remain historical facts and are never silently moved when a plan changes.
- A change to an active crop is recorded as an activity and may update future planned crop items; it must not falsify the past timeline.
- Calendar updates and Crop Cycle updates must be committed through the same service/transaction where possible to prevent orphan events.
14. Farm State and Crop Lifecycle System (approved)
Core distinction
The system tracks what is recommended, what the farmer plans, and what has actually happened. It should be the visible life-cycle capability built on the shared data model.
States
- recommended: exists in a specific analysis run, no commitment implied.
- planned: farmer explicitly saved the sequence for a farm/field.
- active: actual planting/cycle start has been confirmed.
- paused_or_changed: farmer reports pause, abandonment or divergence from plan.
- completed: farmer confirms crop/cycle completion/harvest where appropriate.
- not_confirmed: legacy/incomplete status; never infer Active from a date passing.
Individual crop sequence items may be upcoming, planned, active, harvested/completed, changed, or unknown even when the overall rotation plan is still active. Support multiple fields within a farm, each having separate cycle state.
Farm View Crop Cycle module
Place the full Crop Cycle module within Farm View near rotation details. Home gets only a compact summary. The module shows:
- Farm and selected field identity.
- Selected rotation plan and source analysis run.
- Sequence timeline with crop-specific imagery or illustration, exact/approximate start/end windows, planned vs actual markers and per-crop status.
- Current crop only if planting is confirmed.
- Next planned milestone and its source/date basis.
- Actions appropriate to current state: Save as Plan, Edit Planned Dates, Confirm Planting, Record Activity, Confirm Harvest/Completion, Record Crop Change, Resume/Complete when supported.
Record a planting
The flow asks crop/field, actual planting date (or date range if uncertain), optionally crop variety and stage, and optional notes. If date is approximate, record date precision (month-only, week, exact day). Save a planting activity event and update the crop item status in one validated operation. Then update Farm View, Home summary, Calendar and Deep Analysis context.
Record harvest or crop change
When harvest is recorded, capture actual date (or approximate date), optional amount/unit/quality where available (do not require yield for basic completion), and notes. Update the relevant crop item’s actual state and future timeline only after confirmation. When farmer reports a different crop from the recommendation, record what was planted; never hide the deviation or overwrite the original recommendation.
Crop timeline/date rules
- Display windows such as “Oct 2026 – Mar 2027 · Wheat”, not fixed season labels everywhere.
- Distinguish planned dates from actual recorded dates using a line/marker style and text label as well as colour.
- Only show “days since planting” or “days remaining” where actual/valid dates and a supported duration estimate exist. Label remaining duration as estimated if not based on an exact crop-cycle model.
- Perennial or multi-year crops must have crop-specific duration stages. Do not force one annual card for every crop.
- If the farmer moves the planned start, recalculate only future planned dates according to documented duration assumptions; keep actual past events immutable.
Data source of truth
- Farmer-confirmed activity is the authoritative source for actual planting/harvest dates unless a future trusted integration has explicit permission and provenance.
- Satellite data can indicate vegetation/change, but cannot alone prove crop identity or planting date. It may offer “possible crop/stage” suggestions only if a validated model exists, and the farmer must confirm.
- AI may extract a structured draft from a natural-language input, but must show the extracted fields and ask the farmer to confirm. Do not treat an AI parsing result as an actual event until saved by the farmer.
PART VI — DEEP ANALYSIS, HISTORY AND AUTOMATION
15. Deep Analysis — full present-condition investigation
Purpose
Deep Analysis is a dedicated, on-demand investigation of the selected farm and its fields. It re-fetches currently available relevant data, validates the inputs, reruns supported current-condition calculations, considers saved field information and confirmed management practices, and produces a comprehensive report of strengths, weaknesses, possible issues, uncertainty and evidence-supported recommendations.

It is not the crop-rotation candidate generator, not the What-If sandbox, and not the trace page alone. Its result is a readable farm health investigation; the full record of what the system actually did is available separately in Deep Analysis Logs.
Entry and scope
- Main button within Farm View: Deep Analysis or Run Deep Analysis.
- On the entry panel, select whether to analyse the selected field, all fields in the current farm, or a particular subset. Whole-farm analysis aggregates only field results computed individually and shows differences/conflicts; it must not pretend all fields have identical soil/crop/water inputs.
- Distinguish a light “Refresh current conditions” from full Deep Analysis if the app offers both. Only Deep Analysis reruns its declared comprehensive pipeline.
- Let the farmer choose Simple Report or Advanced Report. Both are the same run/data; Advanced adds detail. It must not create a different score by mode.
Deep Analysis inputs
Use saved records where valid and ask only high-value missing questions. Inputs can include:
- Farm and field location, area and boundary (if saved).
- Soil profile and known lab-test results, including source/date.
- Active/planned crop and rotation status, confirmed planting dates and crop stage if known.
- Available weather and historical climate.
- Rainfall, irrigation/water source and water availability.
- Crop management records, activity history and pest/disease observations.
- Farmer practices such as irrigation routine, watering frequency, amount/duration, method, soil amendments or other management where relevant.
- Known constraints and farmer priorities where relevant to interpretation.
- Existing analysis and previous Deep Analysis snapshots to assess change.
Natural-language farm-practice capture
Allow the farmer to describe their real practice in plain language, e.g. “I irrigate with drip for about 35 minutes every second morning unless rain is expected.” AI or deterministic parsing may create a structured draft containing frequency, method, duration, conditions and unknowns. Show the extracted interpretation in a review UI: what was understood, which units are assumed, which values are unknown, and exactly what will be saved. Ask for confirmation before using it in calculations. A duration cannot safely be converted to litres or millimetres without flow rate, emitter count/spacing, irrigated area and operating assumptions. If those are missing, retain the duration as a recorded practice and don't fabricate irrigation volume.

Persist practices as records with source (farmer-entered, AI-parsed-confirmed, sensor/imported), confirmation time, value/unit, precision and effective date. Ask whether the routine is stable for future scheduled analyses. If it changes by season/weather, let the farmer select “varies; ask/record per run” instead of saving a false permanent routine.
Data refresh and calculation workflow
1. Create a unique run ID, timestamp, scope and explicit status queued, running, completed, partial, failed or cancelled.
2. Load selected farm/field/crop-cycle state and previous comparable runs.
3. Fetch the latest available source records relevant to the selected field; never mark a response current merely because it was fetched just now if the underlying source observation is old.
4. Record source URL/product, retrieval timestamp, observation time/window, units, coordinate or grid resolution, license/attribution, quality flags, fallbacks and error state.
5. Validate ranges, units, dates, spatial fit, stale measurements, conflicting values and missing inputs.
6. Run supported deterministic calculations: current weather/context summary, water balance where sufficient inputs exist, soil indicators that are genuinely available, vegetation-change detection where supported, crop-stage/season interpretation when justified, hazard rules, crop/rotation context, historical comparisons and data limitations.
7. Identify evidence-backed findings and rank them by consequence, confidence and relevance to the selected crop/stage—not by AI dramatic phrasing.
8. Generate a readable report from a structured findings payload. AI can help explain the findings but may only use the collected facts and attached evidence. If AI is unavailable, produce a deterministic summary of findings and caveats.
9. Save report snapshot, calculation outputs, source references, trace events, version metadata and any recommendations before marking the run complete.
10. Update Farm View/Home/Logs using the new saved run without overwriting old history. Show the exact last completed run time and whether any important source was partial/stale/unavailable.
Final report structure
A. Executive summary — overall current condition status and why; number of fields analysed; the top 1–3 findings; what is unknown.

B. Field-by-field status — each field gets its own status, confirmed crop/cycle state, available key indicators and field-specific findings. Whole-farm status is an aggregate based on documented rules and worst supported critical field status, not a blind average.

C. Strengths — only observations supported by sources/records/calculations. Each item links to the evidence that supports it.

D. Issues and watch points — current data and severity, why it matters to the selected crop, actual source/date and uncertainty, plus a plain-language explanation. Risks are not diagnoses.

E. Recommendations — possible inspections, data checks, mitigations or follow-up actions tied to each issue, with urgency and rationale. The system should not invent a precise treatment, rate or irrigation amount when inputs are insufficient.

F. What changed since the last comparable run? — compare supported indicators against a prior snapshot and explain differences, data gaps and comparability limits. If no material change is supported, explicitly say so.

G. Farmer-practice context — how recorded irrigation/management influences the analysis, what assumptions were used, and what should be corrected if the AI-parsed practice is wrong.

H. Data limitations — source failures, stale data, coarse spatial resolution, missing soil lab measurements, model assumptions, unsupported values and affected conclusions.

I. Technical details (Advanced) — source table, calculation breakdown, formulas, inputs/units, intermediary values, model version, trace timeline and links to relevant log steps.
Overall health and component indicators
Do not collapse agricultural condition into an unexplained universal score. A summary status may be shown, with component indicators where supported: water availability/balance, crop suitability/current crop stress context, soil chemistry/physical/biological context, vegetation activity, exposure to weather hazards, management-data completeness and trend. Some are observed, some estimated, and some modelled. Make that difference explicit.

If a numerical overall score is retained from the existing engine, audit its meaning and weighting and label it as an index, not a direct measurement. Keep the component scores visible in Advanced mode and state whether data coverage affects score computation. Critical concerns cannot be washed out by several good component scores.
16. Deep Analysis Logs — trace, history and audit views
Navigation and purpose
Deep Analysis Logs is a dedicated secondary main-nav page/area that lists saved run records and is the authoritative trace/history view. The Deep Analysis workflow/report remains in Farm View; do not create a second copy of its analysis engine inside the log page.
Log list layout
- Page title “Deep Analysis Logs”.
- Filters by farm, field, date range, run type (routine/full/investigative), status and issues detected where available.
- Each row shows date/time, farm/field scope, completion status (completed/partial/failed/cancelled), overall status or “No material change”, key summary, duration if measured and source coverage.
- Sort newest-first by default. Support searching locally saved log content but never search other users’ records in local-only mode.
- Clicking a row opens a modal/detail overlay with X in the top-right; the overlay contains the immutable snapshot, summary, source coverage, findings, calculations and a full expandable execution trace.
- If reached from a Farm View, preserve a back link to that farm/field. Closing the modal returns to the previous context, not blindly to Home.
Execution trace: show actual system work only
Each step represents an actual work event generated by code. Do not display invented “thinking steps” or fabricated calls. The system can record events such as:
- Run created and scope resolved.
- Farm/field records loaded.
- Farmer practice records loaded/parsed/confirmed.
- Each external source request started/completed/failed, including source ID/product, timing, HTTP/status class, retry and observation period without exposing secrets.
- Data normalization and quality checks.
- Each deterministic calculation started/completed with calculation ID/version, actual input snapshot IDs, formula name, units and numeric output.
- Rule/threshold evaluations and generated findings with rule ID/version and evidence references.
- AI explanation/investigation request started/completed/failed, with prompt version and structured input references but not hidden chain-of-thought.
- Report assembly and persistence result.
Each trace item has: timestamp, elapsed duration where measured, step name/type, outcome state, short explanation, related source/data/calculation IDs, warnings/errors and an expand/collapse control. Use neutral progress/complete/warning/error icons. Show nested steps for complex stages. A collapsed trace should remain scannable; full payloads should be opt-in and redacted.

Important: This is an execution log, not access to a model’s private chain-of-thought. Show actual tool/API calls, formulas, source evidence, steps and outputs, not internal hidden reasoning tokens or invented stream-of-consciousness text.
Historical comparability
- Every historical run retains its output and inputs as a snapshot, with app/calculation version and data source timestamps.
- Compare any two runs, but check whether the same field, metric definition, units, crop stage, source resolution, season window and calculation version are comparable.
- If the formula/version changed, show a caution and either compare raw common inputs only or mark the comparison non-comparable.
- Charts show gaps as gaps, not zero. Include unit, selected period, source and each series’ freshness. Distinguish values measured at different spatial scales.
- Display “No material change detected” when supported; don't invent new findings on every automated run.
AI investigation of changes
A user can ask for a deeper investigation of a selected difference, e.g. “Why did the water balance change between these two runs?” The investigation may:
1. Retrieve the two run snapshots and their data/source/calculation records.
2. Build a time-ordered evidence set.
3. Identify changes in observations, user inputs, source products, calculations and missing values.
4. Generate plausible hypotheses ranked by evidence strength, and identify alternatives.
5. Retrieve external scientific source material only if browsing/research service is configured; each claim must link to the retrieved reference. If research retrieval fails, say that research was not completed.
6. Distinguish direct calculation changes from external environmental change, correlation from causation, and observed fact from hypothesis.
7. Save investigation result and links to runs/sources as a distinct investigation record, without modifying original runs.
Automation settings and realistic local limitation
Expose a “Schedule Deep Analysis” control with options such as every day, every 3 days, weekly, or disabled, plus the farm/field scope and a local notification preference. Ask the farmer to review/confirm stable management practices before enabling a recurring analysis. Show last run, next eligible run, last result and scheduler status.

Local-only truth rule: when the app is fully closed, a normal browser app cannot reliably execute a scheduled job. Implement local best-effort due-run checks when the app opens and while it remains open (e.g. a lightweight interval/visibility-aware scheduler), and clearly label: “Runs when Field Shift is open; missed due runs are checked on next launch.” Do not promise a true daily background job while the browser is closed. Avoid busy loops and duplicated runs. Use idempotency keys/run schedules so reopen/retry cannot create duplicate results for one scheduled occurrence. A future service worker may assist certain notification/task patterns but must not be advertised as a substitute for dependable data/API compute while closed.
Scheduling edge cases
- Prevent overlapping runs for the same farm/field schedule. Queue or skip an overlapping interval using a written rule.
- A scheduled run stores scope/config snapshot; if farm settings change, the next run uses latest saved inputs and records the change.
- If an API fails, log failure/partial coverage and do not erase the last good result.
- If no internet is available, either skip the online-data portions or run only valid local calculations, marking partial. Do not create fake fresh values.
- Disable/pause schedule, edit interval, run now and view history must all work.
- Display next due time with local timezone. Browser sleep/closed-app gaps are handled honestly.
17. Farm activity and practice records
This is a practical record capability integrated into the shared farm/field system, not a separate operations platform.

Supported event types include planting/transplanting, irrigation/watering, soil sampling/test, fertiliser/amendment, tillage/traffic where relevant, inspection, pest/disease/weed observation, crop-stage note, harvest, crop change and farmer note/photo. Each activity has date/time or date precision, farm/field/cycle link, source, data/units, notes and optional attachment reference.

Use a compact activity drawer from Farm View/Crop Cycle and a history list in Advanced mode. User entries are explicitly labelled farmer-reported; uploaded photos/observations are not independently verified unless there is a verification process. Correcting an entry must preserve an edit trail or at minimum a clear updated timestamp and prior-value record when relevant to an analysis snapshot.
Watering entry and model-use rules
- Frequency alone is not irrigation volume.
- Duration alone is not irrigation depth.
- With flow rate (litres/hour or emitter rate), emitter count, irrigated area, irrigation efficiency and duration, an estimated volume/depth may be calculated and the formula shown.
- Where those inputs are absent, store the practice description and use qualitative context only; do not substitute a default volume.
- Recorded rain must be merged with model inputs without double-counting forecast rain or using a forecast as observed precipitation.
- A new observation should affect future analysis runs; historical runs remain snapshots.
PART VII — SCIENCE, INDICATORS AND CALCULATION CONTRACTS
18. Farm intelligence indicator catalogue
The purpose of this catalogue is to plan what Field Shift can track, not to require displaying every metric on every page. A metric must appear only when its calculation, units, source and limitations are supported. Use progressive disclosure: brief meaning first, technical detail second.
18.1 Weather and climate
Potential supported indicators, depending on provider and source time resolution:
- Current observed/modelled conditions, labelled with source and valid time.
- Forecast temperature/range, precipitation probability/amount, wind and humidity, clearly labelled forecast and with forecast horizon.
- Historical daily/monthly temperature and precipitation.
- Temperature/rainfall anomaly against a defined baseline and same period of year.
- Heat-stress day count, frost days, longest dry spell, rainfall distribution and growing-season climate window where crop-specific thresholds exist.
- Reference evapotranspiration ET₀ when the required meteorological variables are present.
- Growing degree days (GDD) only with crop-specific base and upper temperature rules.
- Climate trends only when record length and method justify a trend claim.
Never treat a forecast as a measurement; a modelled historical reanalysis as a direct farm sensor; or a climate normal as current weather. Label aggregation period and local time zone.
18.2 Water and irrigation
Keep these concepts distinct:
- Precipitation: amount falling in the stated period.
- Effective rainfall: portion assumed to contribute to root-zone water after runoff/interception/deep drainage assumptions.
- Crop water demand / crop evapotranspiration ETc.
- Reference evapotranspiration ET0.
- Irrigation applied/recorded, irrigation supply and application efficiency.
- Soil-water storage and available water capacity.
- Water deficit / balance and potential stress.
- Soil moisture measured or modelled at a stated depth/scale.
- Irrigation water quality (EC/salinity, pH, sodium-related measures, chloride/boron where tested).
- Water use and use efficiency only when recorded or calculated from sufficient actual inputs/outcomes.
A rainfall total alone is not root-zone water, and a satellite/regional moisture estimate is not necessarily field-specific root-zone moisture. Avoid recommending exact watering schedules/volumes without the crop, growth stage, soil, irrigation method, current conditions and capacity inputs needed by that calculation.
18.3 Soil chemistry
Track individually where data is available:
- pH.
- Electrical conductivity (EC)/salinity and, where supported, sodicity-related indicators.
- Soil organic carbon or organic matter (do not treat them as exactly interchangeable without conversion assumptions).
- Nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium with test method, unit and extraction context where known.
- Secondary/micronutrients such as Ca, Mg, S, Fe, Zn and others only if measured/supported and crop-relevant.
- Cation exchange capacity (CEC) and other properties when the source supports them.
Use a laboratory-test result as different evidence from mapped soil predictions. Every displayed nutrient value includes the test/sample date, method/unit if known, depth and status. Do not infer exact N/P/K from satellite vegetation indices.
18.4 Soil physical properties
Where measured or available through a suitable source:
- Sand/silt/clay fractions and texture class.
- Bulk density, compaction, infiltration, drainage, water-holding capacity and effective rooting depth.
- Structure/aggregate stability, slope/terrain, erosion exposure and ground cover.
- Soil depth and restrictive layers where supported.
Many physical properties require field measurement, soil tests or appropriate mapping. If unavailable, show “Not measured” and optionally recommend collecting a sample/field observation; do not create values to fill a chart.
18.5 Soil biology/ecology
Possible user-recorded or tested indicators: microbial biomass/respiration, earthworm/soil-fauna observations, biodiversity and cover diversity. These require real observations or specialist measurement. Avoid generic scores from remotely sensed greenness.
18.6 Crop/vegetation/land surface
- Crop identity and growth stage: farmer-confirmed first; model-estimated only with a validated method and clearly marked uncertainty.
- Vegetation index such as NDVI only when genuine reflectance/satellite data, quality masks, field intersection and valid timestamps exist.
- Vegetation time-series and deviations from same-season history where comparability is valid.
- Crop growth and heat stress by crop/growth stage where validated thresholds exist.
- Terrain/elevation/slope from appropriate DEM sources; an erosion-risk proxy only if the method/input is explained.
- Ground cover/land degradation trends where source coverage and method support them.
Vegetation index formulas such as NDVI = (NIR - Red) / (NIR + Red) are valid only for suitable reflectance inputs. A vegetation decline is a change signal, not a diagnosis. Clouds, phenology, canopy density, soil background, irrigation and other factors can change the signal.
18.7 Pests/disease/weeds and biodiversity
Use farmer observations, identified symptoms, crop-specific validated risk models and reputable extension/source evidence. Distinguish:
- Farmer reported / photograph shared.
- Candidate diagnosis or AI suggestion.
- Weather-based risk alert.
- Confirmed diagnosis by qualified source/test.
Do not present an AI image label as a confirmed pathogen or infestation. Recommendations for high-consequence pesticide/treatment use require appropriate, locally relevant evidence and should avoid unsupported dosage/timing.
18.8 Operations, harvest and yield
Record planting, irrigation, amendments, inspections, pest/disease/weed observations, harvest date, yield, quality/losses and notes when the farmer wants to log them. Store values with unit, area basis, source and date. A farmer-entered harvest figure is a reported outcome, not independently verified. Yield per area is calculated only when actual harvest amount and actual area are available and the units align.
18.9 Indicator availability states
Each indicator should use one of:
- Available — observed/measured
- Available — modelled/reanalysis
- Available — mapped estimate
- Available — farmer-reported
- Calculated from listed inputs
- Stale
- Partial
- Unavailable — source/credentials not configured
- Unavailable — no coverage/data
- Not measured
- Not applicable
Do not render unavailable numeric values as zero. Do not invent “sample data” on a real farm view. Explicitly labelled demo data is permitted only in demo mode.
19. Decision engine and formula catalogue
All core calculation functions should be deterministic/pure wherever practicable: same normalised inputs and model version produce the same outputs. Seed any probabilistic simulations using a hash of the input snapshot and model version so saved run results are reproducible. Store intermediate values needed for explanation and audit. AI text does not change the scores.
19.1 Data normalisation and validation
- Normalize units once at the ingestion boundary. Examples: Celsius internally for temperature, mm for water depth, square metres/hectares for area, dates in ISO 8601 with explicit timezone semantics.
- Preserve original provider units and raw/source reference for traceability.
- Validate plausible ranges for each variable using domain-specific constraints, not a single global range.
- Handle provider fill values (e.g. missing codes) as missing, not as a numerical observation.
- Interpolate short data gaps only when scientifically appropriate and explicitly mark values as interpolated. Do not interpolate long periods, discontinuities or categorical crop status by default.
- Detect outliers with a robust method when appropriate; do not automatically drop an extreme event without checking whether it is real.
- Compare cross-source observations only when units, periods, definitions, timestamps and spatial resolution are meaningfully comparable.
- Keep exact missing/fallback reasons in the source record.
19.2 Crop knowledge base
Each crop entry must be versioned and include, where available:
- Stable crop ID, display name, botanical/family group and image/attribution.
- Crop type, annual/perennial behaviour, cycle duration/range, growth stage structure and planting/harvest windows/constraints.
- Temperature/base/upper thresholds, GDD needs and heat/frost sensitivity.
- Water-demand/crop coefficient information by growth stage when sourced.
- Soil texture, pH, drainage and salinity tolerance ranges with source/country applicability.
- Nutrient demand/carryover assumptions where valid.
- Rotation family, disease/pest sequence considerations, compatible/preferred predecessor/successor relationships when evidence supports them.
- Regional applicability, source/reference, units, confidence and missing parameters.
A generic placeholder parameter is not equivalent to a crop-specific validated parameter. If a crop does not have enough credible parameters for its region, return “Not rated — key crop parameters unavailable” and exclude it from the feasible ranking where the missing data is essential.
19.3 Crop suitability score
Suitability is crop-and-field-and-window specific. Do not use the current location’s general score for every crop/date.

A candidate factor pipeline may include temperature/GDD fit, rainfall/water fit, soil texture/pH/drainage/salinity fit, slope/terrain where relevant, growing-period fit and supported hazard exposure. Each factor returns a normalized suitability score with source/method and missingness metadata. Do not apply a factor that lacks inputs or valid crop thresholds as if it were known.

A weighted geometric mean can penalize a severe weak factor more strongly than an arithmetic mean. If scores are normalized to [0,1], a general form is:
S_crop = 100 × exp(Σ_j v_j × ln(max(ε, s_j))), where v_j are valid normalized factor weights, s_j are factor scores in (0,1], and ε prevents numerical ln(0) only for computation. If a critical factor is truly failed rather than extremely poor, do not hide it behind ε; apply a hard-feasibility status.

A limiting-factor guard may cap a score if a crop-specific critical threshold is violated, but thresholds must be documented, sourced and tested. Missing values are not given an ideal score. Report which factors were included/excluded and how this affects recommendation confidence.
19.4 GDD and seasonal climate features
Use crop-specific base and upper temperature thresholds. One common daily approximation is GDD_day = max(0, (min(Tmax, Tupper) + max(Tmin, Tbase))/2 - Tbase) with crop/source-specific details. The exact convention must be explicit and consistent. Sum only across a defined growing window and valid daily inputs. Do not make precise phenology claims if planting date, crop parameters or weather history are missing.

Calculate rainfall totals, intensity/dry-spell indicators, heat-stress days and frost risk using documented, crop-specific thresholds and suitable dates. When comparing different seasons, keep the exact comparison window visible. Trend methods such as Mann–Kendall/Theil–Sen may be used when record length/quality justifies trend analysis; avoid extrapolating short series into a confident climate prediction.
19.5 Water balance (FAO-style, only when inputs support)
- Reference evapotranspiration: use FAO-56 Penman–Monteith when required temperature, humidity, wind and radiation variables are available at suitable time resolution. If not, use a documented fallback such as a supported provider’s ET0 product or do not compute ET0; label method/fallback.
- Crop evapotranspiration: ETc = Kc × ET0 where Kc is crop- and growth-stage-specific and sourced. Do not reuse a generic Kc for every crop/stage without qualification.
- Effective rainfall must use an explicit method and period; do not treat all rainfall as effective water.
- A simple root-zone bucket update is conceptually S_t = clamp(S_{t-1} + P_eff,t + I_eff,t - ETc_t - D_t - R_t, 0, TAW), where S is available root-zone storage, P_eff effective precipitation, I_eff effective irrigation, D drainage/deep percolation and R runoff according to the selected model. Inputs and terms must have compatible units/time steps.
- Available root-zone water and stress thresholds require soil properties, rooting depth and depletion assumptions. Use TAW/RAW-style concepts only when parameterized appropriately. Do not imply a coarse satellite moisture product supplies the exact root-zone storage.
- Report deficit, stress days or irrigation amount only where the model and inputs support those outputs. An estimated model result needs confidence/uncertainty and source details.
19.6 Multi-season soil dynamics
If the project includes projected soil nitrogen, organic carbon/organic matter, ground cover, erosion exposure or rotation effects, implement an explicit state-transition model with versioned equations and assumptions, not a score that changes arbitrarily by crop name. Keep long-term projections visibly separate from current measured soil properties. Explain per-season effects and uncertainty. A rotation modelled to improve soil must not be presented as a measured improvement.
19.7 Rotation generation and feasibility
- Generate candidate sequences through constraint-aware search, not arbitrary permutations alone.
- Apply hard constraints first: unsupported crop/region parameters where essential, invalid planting/harvest windows, duration conflicts, crop-family repetition constraints when justified, crop succession restrictions, soil/water hard constraints and farmer-excluded crops.
- Perennial crops and multi-season durations must be represented correctly. A crop that persists for several seasons occupies that part of the timeline; the sequence cannot replace it prematurely.
- Evaluate a full sequence for each crop’s suitability plus cross-season effects: crop-family/disease breaks, nitrogen/resource carryover where modelled, water demand over time, seasonal timing, rotation diversity and feasible transitions.
- Deduplicate equivalent sequences and prevent unbounded exponential search. Use an explainable candidate-generation limit and retain evidence of whether candidates were omitted due to limits.
- Maintain a candidate feasibility record: feasible, conditional or rejected, with reasons. Only feasible candidates can be called best recommendations. Conditional candidates require the appropriate farmer confirmation or missing input.
19.8 Rotation score
For each candidate define component scores on consistent scales, such as water efficiency, soil trajectory, climate resilience, crop suitability and productivity only where supported. Preference weights shape the final objective; they do not change underlying component values.

A general weighted objective is rawScore = Σ_i w_i × componentScore_i, with all components normalized consistently and all weights normalized to 1. Apply hard constraints and critical risk caps separately so a high weighted average cannot wash out a serious agronomic issue. If the implementation uses penalties for risk or infeasibility, each penalty needs a documented meaning and must not double-count a component already included in a score.

For the selected priority model, five sliders may be used: Water Saving, Soil Health, Climate Resilience, Crop Suitability, Productivity. Preset chips can include Balanced, Save Water, Build Soil, Climate Resilience and Productivity; a user changing a slider switches to Custom. Use five-stop sliders labelled Not a concern, Somewhat, Important, Very important, Top priority. Display effective weights with a live donut/ring only if the preview actually recalculates based on cached/valid data; debounce and mark preview provisional.

Weights candidate rule: slider r_i ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}; importance a_i=(r_i+0.5)^1.6; normalize w_i=a_i/Σa_j, then iteratively clamp to a documented lower bound 0.05 and upper bound 0.40 and re-normalize unconstrained weights. If the current set of priorities makes the clamp mathematically infeasible, handle it and show a tested fallback. Show any clamp note. These are preferences only.

Hard guardrails discussed in the existing brief included not calling a rotation “leading” when modelled soil trajectory falls below 35/100, median-scenario water stress is severe, or average crop suitability is below 40/100. Treat these as candidate thresholds to audit and validate—not universal scientific laws. Retain only if the source/crop context and validation justify them; otherwise replace with transparent crop-/region-specific feasibility rules and record the rationale. Never quietly remove a threshold without noting it in the implementation decision log.
19.9 Climate resilience / uncertainty simulation
Where enough historical climate data and crop response functions exist, test candidate rotations against a deterministic set of historical years or reproducible perturbation scenarios. Report result distributions, median/range and the scenario definitions. Do not label a simulation as a probability of success unless the model is validated and has a statistically meaningful calibration set. A Monte Carlo simulation should use a seeded PRNG and retain seed/model version so results can be reproduced.
19.10 Confidence model — two independent concepts
Never collapse recommendation confidence and data coverage into a single ambiguous badge.

A. Recommendation Confidence reflects how stable and well-supported the recommendation is under plausible input uncertainty and model limitations. Potential components: agreement of key factors, stability of ranking under plausible variations, completeness of crop-specific model parameters and separation between the top candidates. Use a transparent defined calculation or categorical logic; do not invent a “87% chance of success” unless calibrated validation data supports that probability.

B. Data Coverage reflects evidence availability and fitness: field/location coverage, key-variable completeness, freshness, spatial resolution relative to field area, source reliability and agreement across comparable sources. It describes the evidence, not whether the recommendation is good.

UI distinction: Recommendation Confidence uses a shield/check/ring icon and a compact outline-style badge; Data Coverage uses a layered-database icon and a segmented/patterned badge. Each has a distinct tooltip/legend, title and text label. Colour is a redundant secondary signal, not the only difference. Examples: “Recommendation confidence: Moderate — the top two options are close”; “Data coverage: Limited — no lab soil test and moisture is regional.”
19.11 Explainability/audit payload
For every crop factor, candidate and final result preserve enough structure to show:
- input variable, normalized unit, source ID/observation date and provenance;
- factor formula/parameter source/version and intermediate values;
- score output and whether data was missing/estimated/interpolated;
- applied hard constraints/penalties and reason;
- score component contribution and normalized priority weight;
- comparison context and uncertainty.
The AI explanation uses this payload. It must not invent mechanisms or claim that a factor affected ranking if it did not enter the calculation.
PART VIII — CONNECTED INNOVATION FEATURES
20. AI Earth Observer / Field Signal (new integrated concept, not a disconnected app)
Purpose
Add a proactive monitoring capability that detects meaningful changes in available environmental/vegetation observations and then routes the farmer to the correct existing Farm View or Deep Analysis investigation. It should feel like Field Shift noticed a potentially important change, not like another generic alert feed.
Placement
- A small “Field Signals” section appears on the selected Farm View only when a valid signal exists or a recent scan status needs to be shown.
- The signal appears in the relevant field’s Crop & Vegetation/Overview section, with its history available in Deep Analysis Logs/history.
- A map layer/marker may highlight a farm with a signal, but do not imply a pixel/zone location when the source resolution is too coarse for that.
- Do not create a new top-level main-nav page in this version. This is an integrated monitoring ability feeding into existing pages.
What a signal means
A Field Signal is a reproducible rule/model output from observed or modelled data, such as vegetation-index decline relative to a comparable past observation, an unusual rainfall/temperature period, or a high-risk forecast crossing a validated crop-specific threshold. It is not automatically a diagnosis.
Detection pipeline
1. Select the relevant farm/field and discover which real products cover the location and time period.
2. Retrieve observations via a configured connector and store provenance, observation time, spatial resolution and quality flags.
3. Reject cloud-contaminated/invalid imagery/observations where product quality flags indicate invalid coverage.
4. Establish a baseline using comparable periods and/or a time-of-year baseline if sufficient history exists.
5. Calculate change: e.g. delta = latestComparableValue - baselineValue, with normalized percentage/standardized change only if mathematically meaningful and baseline is nonzero/valid.
6. Apply thresholds only after they are documented, tested and suitable for the metric, crop stage and source product. Avoid a single universal NDVI threshold for every crop and season.
7. Cross-check potential explanations against temperature, rainfall, water-balance estimates, crop-stage timing and farmer activity history when available.
8. Produce a signal object: status, observed change, rule ID/version, source values, uncertainty, possible explanations, alternative hypotheses and suggested inspection.
9. Dedupe repeated signals for the same metric/time/window. Escalate only if material change occurs, new evidence arrives or the signal changes meaningfully.
10. Save the signal and make it available to Deep Analysis. Do not auto-modify crop status or automatically prescribe a treatment.
Signal presentation
Use a compact row with an appropriate icon, short title, date, evidence label, severity and status. Example: “Vegetation activity changed more than its recent baseline” with a subtext such as “Based on two comparable valid observations; cause not established.” Clicking opens an evidence drawer showing the chart, baseline, values, dates, source/resolution, cloud/quality information and possible next checks. The “Investigate with Deep Analysis” action starts an investigation with the signal ID included in the context.
Limits and fallbacks
- If no authentic vegetation data is available, hide vegetation-change signals and show “Satellite monitoring unavailable for this source/configuration” in source settings; do not manufacture an NDVI result.
- If source refresh has no new valid imagery, say no new observation is available, not “no change detected.”
- If only a regional/coarse dataset is available, show a regional signal, not a precise within-farm alert.
- Weather risk can use a forecast only if a true forecast endpoint is configured. Historical data alone cannot be used to claim a future hazard.
- Use app-open local checks for a local-first release. Closed-browser continuous monitoring requires a future backend and cannot be promised now.
Why it improves Field Shift
It creates an integrated “observe → investigate → explain → track” loop from the same farm records and source metadata. It adds proactive value without creating another separate dashboard or duplicating Deep Analysis. Implement it only after the shared data, reliable connectors and logging are established.
21. Global Farming Network — documented end-state, honest local-mode behaviour
Product concept
The Global Farming Network is a connected experience linking a public directory of farms, an interactive global/city map, farmer communication, communities, regional comparisons, environmental history and real-world evidence. It has three purposes: Discover farms and regional patterns, Connect farmers, and Understand local-vs-regional conditions and crop-rotation outcomes.
Critical implementation constraint
Publicly shared farms, user accounts, friend relationships, messaging, community posts, cross-user aggregates and regional evidence require a shared authenticated backend. The current build decision is local-only/no login. Therefore:
- Preserve the full feature design and data contracts in this prompt.
- In the current local-only build, do not pretend that public data from other users exists. Show a well-designed “Global network requires shared backend” state with a concise explanation and a preview of the kinds of information the future network can provide.
- If demo/example content is required for a presentation, make it a clearly labelled, locally bundled “Illustrative demo data” toggle. Demo profiles and statistics must be obviously illustrative and cannot be mixed with real local farm results or presented as verified/active users.
- Do not implement fake login, simulated real messaging or fake friends that appear live. Build a future-ready feature boundary so the backend can implement it later without changing the private farm model.
Future-ready screen layout
- World map (pan/zoom) with cities that have published farms highlighted/coloured by supported aggregate conditions.
- Search field supporting city/country/farm name. Disambiguate duplicate place names with region/country. Private/unpublished farms must not appear in public search.
- Map layer controls for public farm condition and supported regional environmental layers.
- My Farms side panel, clearly separated from public farm data.
- Selected city panel with published farm list, city aggregate summary, sample size, data window, last update and coverage.
- Selected public farm profile, using a safe filtered view of the private Farm View layout.
- Legend, attribution, source/freshness and an insufficient-data state.
Public location privacy — absolute rule
Public maps must never show exact farm coordinates, private boundaries, precise pins, access routes, directions or high-zoom imagery that reveals the plot. Only city-level location/area is allowed. The private owner can see their exact saved farm boundary in their own Maps. Never load a full private farm object into the public view and simply hide coordinate widgets; create an allowlisted public projection on the backend in the future. In local-only mode, do not expose a public projection at all without explicit local preview scope.
Publishing workflow for future backend
- Farmer chooses a farm to publish.
- Preview public profile and fields to be shared.
- Choose shareable groups: profile/description, crop/rotation summary, soil properties, water/irrigation context, aggregate indicators, history/outcomes, selected analysis report, community posts.
- Every group has a clear description and on/off control. Default is private.
- Explicitly acknowledge city-level public location only; exact coordinates and boundary are never a public option.
- Show last publication/update time and unpublish control.
- Unpublishing removes the farm from public search/profile and future aggregate inputs according to a defined refresh/removal process.
City-level condition colouring
- City colour is calculated only from eligible explicitly published farms associated with that city.
- Do not infer “no farming” from no published farms; say “No farms published here” where appropriate.
- Use only comparable, adequately fresh metrics. Use sample size thresholds, data coverage and uncertainty; suppress or neutralize small sample groups when identifying a farm would be possible.
- The aggregate must state which indicators are included, the calculation version, time window, number of contributing farms and last update.
- Do not assume drier is always worse or wetter is always better. Condition must be crop-/region/context-aware and indicators retain their separate meanings.
- If data is insufficient, use a neutral hatch/grey state and text “Insufficient data”, not green or red.
- Rebuild the aggregate when eligible published snapshots change; a failed refresh leaves the last valid aggregate with a stale timestamp.
Regional comparison — Compare My Farm with Regional Averages
The future feature compares the current user’s chosen field/farm to eligible peer data with careful normalization. Suitable comparands may include climate anomaly, soil properties (only same method/units/depth), water-use/irrigation records (normalized by area/crop where possible), crop rotations, crop outcomes, vegetation trends and reported practices. Do not compare different crops or growing stages as if identical.

Comparison outputs should show: user's value, region's median/percentile/range when enough samples exist, eligible sample size, comparison time window, crop/soil/management comparability warning, source/measurement type and limitations. Keep personally identifiable farm values private. Explain that the region summary is context, not proof the farmer should copy another farm’s practices.
Public farm profile
Use a shared visual family with the private Farm View, but a strict allowlist. Suggested sections: profile header (farm name, city/country, description, publication/update time), owner-approved crop/rotation summary, selected soil/water indicators, relevant environmental context, approved trend charts, shared outcomes/case studies, public community activity and contact actions when account/permission support exists. Never leak private notes, private logs, exact coordinates, geometry, unshared yield or private analysis.
Social features in future backend mode
- Add/remove friends or connection requests.
- Direct messages with permission controls, delivery/error states and abuse protections.
- Block/unblock and mute/unmute must apply across profile, search, messages, map entry points and community interactions.
- Communities/servers with descriptions, membership rules, join/leave, posts/replies, search, membership roles, moderator controls, content reporting and removal.
- Posts and user claims are not scientific validation. Label self-reported, documented or independently verified evidence clearly. Community content must not automatically alter the deterministic crop engine.
- Build rate limiting, anti-spam, reporting and moderation into the future architecture. Do not include these “live” as broken mock buttons in local mode.
Regional history, weather and hazards in future backend mode
- Regional environmental history stores period, dataset, units, resolution, source version and retrieval time.
- Forecast products must be true forecasts; historical data adds context but cannot itself predict future weather.
- Regional early warnings may include supported heat, drought, rainfall/flood exposure, frost and validated pest-weather conditions. Each warning identifies which farms/crop stages may be exposed only when permissioned data supports that matching.
- Keep alerts deduplicated and explain what was detected, why it matters and what remains uncertain.
- Use privacy-safe aggregation, small-sample suppression and secure deletion/unpublish handling.
Future network data entities
In a later shared backend: user account/profile, private farm, farm publication config, public farm projection, farm metric snapshot with provenance, city/region IDs, region aggregate, external dataset record, connection/friend state, block/mute state, conversation/message, community/membership/post/reply/report, crop outcome/case-study record, alert rule/subscription/notification and audit/job record.
22. Additional connected product enhancements
These concepts must integrate into existing pages rather than produce new top-level tabs by default.
22.1 Rotation Decision Replay
Within a detailed rotation’s “Why this was chosen” section, let the farmer step through the decision: key inputs → hard feasibility rules → crop-level factor scores → sequence interactions → component scores → personal weights → final ranking → risk-tolerance answer. Each step links to its formula/source evidence. It must replay the actual saved decision record, not rerun with today's data and pretend it is the original. This makes an award-quality scientific explanation visual and verifiable.
22.2 “What changed since last run?” comparison
Already connected to Deep Analysis Logs and Farm View, not a new product. When a current run differs from a previous one, separate causes into: changed farm inputs, new source observations, data source/version changes, calculation/model changes and actual indicator movement. If they cannot be separated, say which explanation remains uncertain.
22.3 Field Signals → Deep Analysis bridge
The Earth Observer signal opens the exact source/metric/time window in Deep Analysis. The trace shows which sources and calculations were used. The saved report appears in Farm View/history. This cross-link is the key innovation: detection, investigation and explanation share one run ID/evidence graph, not three isolated features.
22.4 Cross-field resource conflict overview
Inside Farm View’s farm-level overview, optionally show whether planned planting/harvest windows or modeled irrigation demand overlap across fields when those values exist. It is a compact decision aid, not a separate resource optimisation tool. Never claim the farm lacks capacity unless the farmer has entered capacity constraints.
22.5 Insight quality, not alert quantity
An empty finding list is acceptable. Prioritize signal novelty, evidence strength and farmer relevance. Suppress repetitive non-changing alerts, and explain when the system has not seen new valid data rather than implying a successful “no change” check.
PART IX — SHARED DATA MODEL, SOURCES AND TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE
23. Shared Farm Data System — one source of truth (approved)
Principle
The Shared Farm Data System is the underlying architecture; Crop Cycle is the visible feature built on top. It connects Home, Maps, My Farms, Farm View, New Analysis, Rotations, What-If, Calendar, Deep Analysis, Logs, Data & Trust and the future Global Farming Network. It is not another UI dashboard.
Identity and record model
Use stable IDs (UUID or collision-safe generated IDs) for every domain entity. Display name and coordinates are mutable attributes, never the primary identity. Use schema versioning and migrations.

Farm
- farmId, name, optional description, city/region/country labels.
- Approximate center for geocoding/context and private geometry reference.
- boundaryGeometryId, calculated polygon area/unit and area-calculation method/version.
- createdAt, updatedAt, archived/deleted state, tags/notes where supported.
- Farm-level weather/climate/terrain location context and field IDs.
- Local publication/network flags should remain disabled/private in local-only mode; do not represent publish state as globally live.
Field
- fieldId, farmId, name, area/value/unit, soil profile and source/provenance.
- Irrigation/water setup and crop-management inputs.
- Crop history items with date precision/source.
- Selected/current crop cycle, saved plan IDs and analysis-run IDs.
- Field-level condition indicators/status/alerts.
- No separate field geometry is required in initial scope; model it as an optional future geometry reference.
Farm geometry
- geometryId, farmId, GeoJSON polygon coordinates in WGS84 longitude/latitude, version, calculated area in square metres and display conversions, created/updated times, source=user_drawn, visibility=private, validity status.
- Keep previous geometry version or a bounded edit history for recovery/analysis integrity.
- Public projections must not contain this private geometry.
Crop plan / crop cycle
- cycleId, farmId, fieldId, source analysis run ID, selected rotation and immutable sequence snapshot, lifecycle state, planned/actual dates, created/updated timestamps, record version.
- CropSequenceItem: item ID, sequence index, crop ID, expected duration/range, planned start/end, actual start/end, lifecycle status, stage and stage-source if known.
- Actual planting/harvest events reference the exact field/cycle/item. Correcting one date must not rewrite another field’s dates.
Analysis run
- analysisRunId, farmId, optional fieldId, run kind, started/completed times, run status, saved inputs, priority weights, hard constraints, farmer risk answers, source refs/snapshots, source observation/retrieval times, normalized values, model/calculation version, candidate list, results, final selection, warning list and provenance references.
- Historical run content is immutable after finalization. Any annotation or correction is an additional record.
Data/source record
- sourceRecordId, provider, product/endpoint, source version if known, request parameters with secrets/redacted fields removed, request/retrieval timestamp, observation period, units, source spatial/temporal resolution, raw/cache reference, quality flags, licence/attribution, status/error class and fallback relationships.
- Cache key includes dataset/product + native spatial cell or appropriately rounded location + variable set + time window + version. Do not expose raw private coordinates in analytics/log exports.
Metric observation / calculated metric
- Metric name, value, unit, valid time/period, data type (observed/measured, reanalysis/modelled, mapped estimate, user reported, calculated, forecast), source IDs, calculation ID/version, uncertainty/quality flags, field/farm scope and comparability notes.
- Missing is represented as missing with a reason; no zero as substitute.
Activity record
- activityId, farm/field/cycle/sequence links, activity type, date/time or date precision, normalized values/units, user notes, source/confirmation state, created/updated times and correction linkage.
Deep Analysis run and trace
- deepAnalysisRunId, farm/field scope, source record IDs, inputs, calculation outputs, report snapshot, findings, recommendations, status, duration, createdAt, app/model version, schedule trigger if any.
- Trace event ID, parent event ID, sequence number, event type/name, timestamps, duration, status, actual inputs/outputs refs, warnings/errors, source/calculation IDs and redacted detail payload.
- Save the detail needed to reproduce/explain the run without storing secrets or unnecessary personal data.
Scenario
- scenarioId, baseline analysisRunId/cycleId, changed parameters, source values, output snapshot, engine version, name/notes, createdAt and hypothetical=true. This record is not merged into actual crop/field data.
Calendar event
- eventId, farm/field/cycle/item/activity link where relevant, event type, planned/actual dates, status, source and notes. Completed events remain immutable historical facts.
Local app preferences
- Selected farm/field, display units, UI choices and schedule settings may be stored separately from scientific records. No user-account identity required in local-only mode.
State update rules
- Renaming farm/field updates labels everywhere but retains IDs.
- Editing farm geometry recalculates polygon area and stores new geometry version; it does not automatically change each field’s manually entered area.
- If total field areas differ from the farm polygon area beyond a documented tolerance, show a review warning. Do not silently force equality.
- Removing geometry from Maps does not delete farm/fields/history.
- Saving a recommendation as a plan creates a crop cycle; it does not mark planting active.
- Confirming planting creates an actual activity and changes the relevant crop state atomically.
- Editing future plans updates appropriate future planned Calendar events. Completed events remain unchanged.
- Re-running New Analysis creates another immutable run. It does not silently replace an active cycle.
- Deep Analysis reads current records and saves a new snapshot; it never silently edits actual crop state.
- What-If operates on a cloned immutable baseline; all changes remain hypothetical.
- Deleting a farm/field is a separate destructive workflow that explains effects on geometry, records, logs, schedules and calendar history; support export before deletion.
24. Local persistence, offline support and data portability
Persistence choice
Use IndexedDB for structured records and histories because the app needs many related entities, indexed lookup, versioned schema upgrades and sizeable local logs. Keep localStorage for small display preferences only. Do not use cloud database/services in this release.
Local repository service
Create a data access/repository layer such as:
- FarmRepository
- FieldRepository
- GeometryRepository
- CropPlanRepository
- ActivityRepository
- AnalysisRunRepository
- SourceCacheRepository
- DeepAnalysisRepository
- ScenarioRepository
- CalendarRepository
- SettingsRepository
Components must not read/write IndexedDB/localStorage directly. Every write uses validated repository functions and returns success/failure. Data updates should use transactions where multiple records must remain consistent.
IndexedDB migration
- Include schemaVersion and migration code.
- Detect legacy farm data from the current localStorage store, validate it, and migrate into IndexedDB without destroying the original until the migration succeeds.
- Show a migration report if some records could not be imported and provide export/recovery options.
- Preserve old analysis runs as legacy snapshots when their data supports it; clearly label missing/legacy fields rather than filling them with invented defaults.
- Existing pre-seeded sample farms must not be identified as verified. First-run state should be empty except for an explicit “Load demo farm” option. Demo records have source=DEMO, are visually labelled and can be removed easily.
Export/import and deletion
- Provide a Settings option to export all local Field Shift data as a versioned JSON archive (and optionally CSV for selected tabular records). Include source/license metadata and schema version; exclude credentials/API secrets.
- Provide import with dry-run validation, duplicate resolution, schema migration, and a preview of records to add/update before commit.
- Provide “Export farm” and “Delete farm” actions.
- Provide “Delete all local Field Shift data” with high-friction confirmation and a final verification step.
- Explain that browser storage can be cleared by browser settings and is not equivalent to an authenticated cloud backup. Do not promise recovery if user clears browser data without exporting.
- Provide storage usage/retention tools for large trace logs and cached imagery. If pruning history, let the user control retention; do not delete the only source of a historical analysis silently.
Offline rules
- Local records, saved runs and calculation reports should open when offline.
- Online API sections show cached data with its actual observation/retrieval time or an explicit unavailable state.
- No network call is required to open the app shell or browse saved data.
- Avoid using a service worker to cache private API payloads without explaining scope; keep sensitive user data in local storage and ensure any caching policy has a clear deletion path.
25. API and data-source integration plan
Integration rules
- Implement every provider behind a small connector interface so it can be replaced without changing UI or calculation logic.
- On app start, do not fire all API requests for all saved farms. Fetch only what the current page/selected run requires, plus configured local scheduled jobs.
- Use AbortController/timeouts, bounded retries with backoff, rate-limit handling, request deduplication, and TTL caching according to source update cadence.
- Never place secret keys in public frontend code. If a server endpoint is used, keep keys in local server environment variables/secrets and return only required normalized values. A client-side “API key required” field must explain that a browser-provided key could be exposed.
- The user will configure keys. Create .env.example/settings instructions listing the exact env var, provider, needed scopes, where to get a key, and which features remain unavailable until configured. Never invent a usable key or claim to fetch a key yourself.
- For each provider, document license/terms and display required attribution. Do not implement a source if its usage terms conflict with intended use without raising that issue.
- Verify current API status, endpoint parameters, authentication and terms at implementation time. APIs can change; do not hardcode endpoint assumptions from stale snippets.
- Where one source has a failure, continue only calculations that remain valid; mark the run partial when the missing source materially affects output.
Source candidates and responsibilities
The following is a recommended provider registry, not a guarantee that every provider is configured or currently accessible. Test the actual endpoint. Keep the provider adapters replaceable.

Weather and climate
- Open-Meteo Forecast API — operational forecast/current-model context where appropriate; keyless non-commercial tier and attribution terms must be respected. It provides forecast and historical products, but each result must be labelled by model, valid period and observation/forecast type. Official docs: https://open-meteo.com/ and https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api.
- NASA POWER API — daily/hourly/monthly/climatology solar and meteorological time series. Use for historical climate features and supporting crop calculations; show grid-resolution limitations and timestamp convention. Official docs: https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/services/api/ and https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/services/api/temporal/daily/.
- National/local meteorological provider can be added where a stable authorized API exists, especially for official UAE alerts/forecasts. Do not invent an endpoint.
Soil and terrain
- ISRIC SoilGrids — candidate global mapped soil-property priors, not farm lab tests. At the time this prompt was prepared, ISRIC’s official site reported its REST API was temporarily paused and the REST service is beta; check live status before building a hard dependency. Use bulk/static supported access or a validated alternative where lawful/available. Official status/docs: https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids and https://rest.isric.org/soilgrids/v2.0/docs.
- NASADEM or an appropriate DEM service — elevation/terrain context and slope derived with a documented method. Check actual resolution, access method and licence.
- USDA Soil Data Access / Web Soil Survey — potential U.S.-specific soil data source only; do not pretend it has universal global coverage.
- A user-entered soil lab measurement is often more field-relevant than a global map estimate; support manual sample entries with test date/depth/unit and provenance.
Satellite and Earth observation
- Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem APIs — catalogue/search and processing access for Sentinel-2 and other products. Sentinel-2 has multispectral bands with 10 m spatial resolution for selected visible/NIR bands and approximately 5-day constellation revisit under nominal conditions; actual usable observations depend on cloud cover, latitude, scene availability and processing. Do not describe revisit as guaranteed clear imagery. Official docs: https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/analyse/apis and https://documentation.dataspace.copernicus.eu/APIs/SentinelHub/Overview.html.
- NASA Earthdata / SMAP — soil moisture products; secure credentials are often required for data access. Use only when access is configured. Clearly label product resolution/depth; do not equate coarse surface moisture with field-specific root-zone moisture.
- NASA MODIS / LP DAAC — vegetation indices/ET products where supported, with temporal/spatial resolution and quality flags. Do not claim a live field polygon summary unless the pixels are actually queried/intersected and valid.
- NASA GPM IMERG — satellite precipitation estimates for rainfall history/cross-checks where valid; distinguish satellite estimate from local rain gauge measurement.
- NASA GIBS — possible tiled imagery layers for contextual Earth visualization, not a substitute for a crop-calculation data product. Verify product dates, projection and attribution.
- GRACE / GRACE-FO — optional broad groundwater/storage context only where appropriate; resolution is too coarse for individual-field conclusions.
Map renderer, drawing and geospatial calculations
- Preferred stack candidate: MapLibre GL JS for interactive map rendering; Terra Draw for polygon draw/select/edit and consistent geometry interaction; Turf.js for polygon area and geometry utilities. Keep renderer/drawing behind adapters where feasible.
- Basemap/satellite tiles: choose a provider with working coverage, a clear license/attribution rule, and any needed key configured via a safe route. Candidates may include Esri imagery services, MapTiler or another supported provider, but verify terms/keys and scale constraints before choosing.
- Geocoding: use a real place search provider such as Open-Meteo Geocoding, Nominatim within its current usage policy, Geoapify, MapTiler or another provider. Search for places must not mutate saved farm geometry.
- Turf.js polygon area reference: https://turfjs.org/docs/api/area.
- MapLibre documentation: https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/.
- Terra Draw documentation/repository: https://github.com/JamesLMilner/terra-draw.
- Do not choose Google’s Maps Drawing Library for a new build; Google’s official deprecation page states that it is unavailable as of May 2026. See https://developers.google.com/maps/deprecations and https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/drawinglayer.
AI
- Gemini API — use for grounded plain-language explanations, natural-language practice parsing and scoped evidence investigation. Use configurable model ID from environment. Current official model list: https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models.
- If no API key is configured, show a clear setup state and use deterministic explanations rather than fake AI text. Keep the rest of the app usable.
- AI outputs are JSON/schema-validated when used to create structured records. Natural-language irrigation input is only a draft until the user confirms.
Crop data and references
- Use reputable crop parameter sources such as FAO-56 for evapotranspiration/crop coefficients, FAO/ECOCROP-style tolerances where applicable, and suitable agricultural extension/peer-reviewed sources for crop limits and rotation relationships.
- Version each crop parameter and keep citation/source URL with geography, crop, units and retrieval date. Do not copy isolated values without understanding their context.
- Do not make the system dependent on web-search results at every calculation. Curate and version parameters in a local source-backed knowledge base; live research can help an AI investigation but is not itself a deterministic model input unless separately validated.
Images
- Wikimedia Commons can supply reusable crop images, but each media file has its own licence and attribution requirements. Verify the selected file and store artist, page URL, licence name/link and modification note. Official reuse guidance: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Commons:Reusing_content_outside_Wikimedia/licenses/en.
- Prefer downloading/optimising approved assets into the app or using stable URLs with permission; avoid hotlink dependence. Distinct crop types require distinct images.
Source fallback order
Fallback is per variable, not a blanket rule. Examples:
1. Suitable direct observation/lab/sensor value with provenance.
2. Suitable satellite/model/reanalysis product with actual spatial/temporal context.
3. Regional or historical climatology used only for contextual estimates.
4. Farmer-entered known value (if it describes the variable and is dated) can be more relevant than a generic regional prior.
5. Missing/unavailable state if no defensible fallback exists.
Do not silently replace an unavailable source with a different variable and keep the same label. Every fallback changes provenance and may change confidence. Synthetic/demo values can only appear in explicitly labelled demo data, not real farm outputs.
Cache and rate limiting
- Cache historical time-series data long-term by source cell/coordinate resolution + variable + period + provider version; refresh only when source update cadence or user request justifies it.
- Cache forecast data for shorter, provider-appropriate intervals; never recast cached forecasts as observations.
- Avoid calling the same provider twice for identical parameters in the same run. Reuse cache across nearby farms only when their positions genuinely map to the same provider grid resolution.
- Back off on 429/5xx, set per-source timeout, and expose last successful fetch. Avoid repeated retries that exhaust API limits.
- A source failure must not erase last valid data. A last valid record should retain its original observation and retrieval timestamps.
26. Data & Trust Center
The Data & Trust Center explains how Field Shift’s outputs are produced. It is not a substitute for page-specific evidence and does not become a separate giant metrics dashboard.
Main content
- Provider status list: connected/configured, working, rate-limited, needs key, unavailable/temporarily down, not used in current run.
- Dataset/product used, units, observation period, retrieved time, native spatial and temporal resolution, source/attribution link and data type.
- Per-variable missing/fallback logic and quality caveats.
- Calculation catalogue: formula/method, required inputs, units, crop/region assumptions, model version, limits and where used.
- Crop knowledge base sources/version status.
- Distinct explanation for Recommendation Confidence and Data Coverage.
- Demo mode banner/state when applicable; demo outputs cannot be confused with real observations.
Evidence drawer on every page
A source badge or “Why this value?” control opens a consistent drawer with the metric definition, value/unit, observation window, source/product, last fetched time, type (observed/estimated/forecast/calculated/reported), resolution/precision, formula if derived, and limitations. Avoid sending the farmer away from the current task to understand a number.
No secrets in trust UI
Never display actual API credentials, access tokens, authentication headers, private prompt payloads, or full private coordinates in logs. It is acceptable to show whether an API key is configured, not its value.
PART X — REMAINING CONNECTED PRODUCT AREAS
27. Alerts and in-app notifications
Alerts must represent actual supported conditions or meaningful run events. They cannot be generated just to fill a panel.
Alert record
Each alert needs stable ID, farm/field scope, source rule/run IDs, created time, last updated time, severity, status, concise title, evidence summary, why it matters, recommended consideration (not always an imperative), expiry/resolution rule, read/dismiss state and deduplication key.
Alert lifecycle
- New → read → resolved/expired or dismissed, with semantics defined and visible.
- Dismissing an alert removes it from the active inbox but must not erase the underlying analysis/source record.
- A repeated signal of the same issue should update an existing active alert rather than create a duplicate every run; create a new alert only when a new time window or material escalation justifies it.
- A resolved alert should remain in relevant history but not appear as active.
- Failed data fetches are operational warnings, not farm-health alerts unless the lack of data itself affects the assessment. Display data failure separately from agronomic risk.
UI
- Bell icon in the top bar with unread count; count must match actual unread records.
- A right-side notification drawer with filters (all/unread/active) and short rows that show farm + field + reason.
- Selecting an alert navigates to the exact farm/field/evidence section, marks it read consistently and returns correctly when closed.
- “Mark all read” only marks current eligible records. Dismiss buttons require a correct event and state update.
- In local-only scheduled analysis, notifications are only in-app while Field Shift is open. Do not promise push or email.
28. AI Agronomist — grounded assistant, not a fake expert overlay
Purpose
The AI Agronomist helps the user understand a selected farm’s records, explain a rotation, ask questions about an analysis, interpret terms, and investigate differences between saved Deep Analysis runs. It must be contextual and evidence-aware.
Placement and context
- A consistent entry in the global shell opens a right drawer or compact chat panel; don’t launch a full-screen chat by default.
- Context should show the selected farm/field and the scope that will be sent.
- The farmer can switch context or start a general question; general questions must not silently receive a fabricated farm profile.
- The assistant can link to exact Farm View sections, rotation cards, trace items and Data & Trust records.
Data grounding
- Only send the minimum records required for the question.
- Include source snapshot IDs, formula outputs, units, model version and uncertainty where relevant.
- Use structured, validated outputs for parsed farm practices or proposed records. Confirm before saving.
- Explain known evidence, not hidden model thoughts. Do not claim that APIs were fetched or research was performed unless the corresponding trace event confirms it.
- Do not let an LLM recalculate the score in free text. The deterministic engine is authoritative for numeric results.
- If no key/network is configured, display “AI explanation unavailable; showing the model-based summary” and show the deterministic explanation. Do not simulate typing and return a canned statement labeled AI.
- Use safe error handling, response timeouts, message history limits and clear retry/cancel behaviour.
Chat behaviour
- User can ask follow-up questions; context and references remain attached until changed.
- The assistant can show a citation/evidence list from internal run records and, when an actual retrieval/search service is enabled, external sources with URLs/publication names.
- Separate fact, estimate, hypothesis and recommendation in answer formatting.
- If the question requests a decision that the data cannot support, say what is missing and suggest the most useful additional measurement rather than guessing.
- The chat drawer must not obscure key field context permanently; it should be resizable or closeable, remember its width locally and remain keyboard accessible.
29. Settings and local configuration
Settings must be useful, compact, local and truthful. No account/login/profile screens in this version.
Settings groups
1. General: default farm/field, units, date format, interface reduced-motion preference, local display preferences. English-only UI now.
2. Data sources: list connectors and their status, provider documentation link, required environment variable, which features depend on it, last successful request, whether the current run used it, and attribution/license notes. Do not show secrets. Keys should be configured in local server environment/settings appropriate to each provider.
3. Analysis behaviour: default report mode, analysis timeout options, local caching/refresh choices, Deep Analysis schedule intervals, selected farms/fields for schedules and local-run disclaimer.
4. Local data: database status, used storage where supported, export all, import archive, export selected farm, delete one farm, delete all data. Destructive options must be visually separated.
5. About and sources: app version, calculation version, documentation, current known limitations and acknowledgements.
API key instructions
Because this is local-only, store secrets outside the browser bundle. For local development, use environment variables in a local .env/secret mechanism that is excluded from version control. If a source can only be called securely through a server proxy, document that a locally running Express endpoint is required. If the website is later deployed as static-only, do not ship secret keys inside VITE_* values or client JavaScript; a serverless/backend runtime would be required for secrets. The owner is responsible for supplying provider credentials.
Data clearing and privacy
Explain plainly: “Farm records are stored in this browser on this device. There is no account/cloud sync in this version. Export a backup before clearing browser data or moving to another device.” Do not imply local storage is encrypted/authenticated user storage. Keep local farm data isolated from any future public/social module.
30. Page Guide / explain-how-it-works overlay
The previous project includes a Page Guide/modal. Rebuild it as a polished contextual guide, not a generic pop-up of walls of text.
- Entry point: help/Guide icon in the header or settings. The guide can optionally launch contextual walkthroughs from major pages.
- The central concept is a small guided replica of the actual current UI with highlighted controls and animated pointer/cursor actions. The product should not be a disconnected video that shows old layout.
- If implementing a replica is too heavy, use a DOM spotlight/coachmark tour bound to actual UI controls with an animated pointer and short explanatory panels. It must use real controls/routes so the screen reflects changes after each action; do not maintain a separate pretend copy with incorrect state.
- The tour should support next/back/skip/exit, pause on navigation, focus management and reduced motion. Explain farm drawing, saving a plan, marking planting active, rotation comparison, What-If, Deep Analysis report vs trace, and source-confidence badges.
- Keep text short, visually anchored to the item being explained, with a small diagram/animation only where it truly helps.
31. AI Earth Observer source/quality status UI
The Earth Observer may share the Data & Trust Center’s source records. Add a small “Last observation scan” state within Farm View, not a separate data dashboard:
- last completed check time;
- whether a new valid observation was found;
- current valid baseline window;
- any signals created or “no new valid observation available”;
- source and resolution summary.
Do not say “nothing changed” when the source did not update. If a new satellite observation is unavailable due to cloud, acquisition timing or coverage, show that directly. An observer scan is a data/metric pipeline; Deep Analysis is the comprehensive report/investigation. Their responsibilities remain separate but they share data/provenance and can link to each other.
PART XI — FRONTEND/ENGINEERING CONTRACT
32. Frontend route and component architecture
Choose a real typed router (React Router or TanStack Router are both reasonable) rather than maintaining all navigation as opaque tab state. The exact library can be selected after the build environment is verified. Routes should be shareable in local app context and should restore to a safe state on refresh.

Suggested route structure:
- / → Home.
- /maps → Maps.
- /farms → My Farms list.
- /farms/:farmId → canonical Farm View.
- /farms/:farmId/fields/:fieldId → selected field section within canonical Farm View.
- /new-analysis → guided New Analysis wizard.
- /analysis-runs/:analysisRunId or equivalent local route → results/report for a saved analysis run.
- /rotations → saved/candidate rotation overview for selected context.
- /rotations/:rotationId → detailed rotation / Decision Replay.
- /analysis → What-If Simulator only.
- /calendar → Calendar.
- /deep-analysis-logs → saved log list.
- /deep-analysis-logs/:runId → specific log/detail modal or URL-driven detail view.
- /data-trust → Data & Trust Center.
- /settings → local settings and data management.
- /network → Global Farming Network future-ready state/local demo preview only until backend is available.
Use an app-shell layout with persistent navigation and route-level content. URL parameters are stable IDs, not farm names or raw sensitive coordinates. Use a route loader or centralized context to validate that referenced farm/field/run exists; invalid IDs should produce a recoverable not-found state with a link to My Farms.
Component families
Build and reuse a compact, fully styled component library:
- AppShell, Sidebar, TopBar, Breadcrumbs, FarmContextBar, PageHeader.
- FarmStatusCard, WeatherSummary, IndicatorTile, FieldListItem, CropCycleTimeline, CropStageRow.
- SourceBadge, EvidenceDrawer, ProvenanceLine, RecommendationConfidenceBadge, DataCoverageBadge, StatusBadge.
- FarmBoundaryMap, MapSearch, MapToolRail, MyFarmsMapPanel, BoundaryEditor, PolygonVertexHandle, MapMiniature.
- RotationCard, RotationComparisonTable, RotationTimeline, ScoreBreakdown, RiskCard, TradeoffQuestion, DecisionReplayStep.
- AnalysisProgress, TraceEventRow, RunHistoryRow, RunStatusLabel, ScenarioDiff, MetricChart, SourceStatusRow.
- Drawer, Modal, ConfirmDialog, Toast, InlineNotice, EmptyState, ErrorPanel, Skeleton, Tooltip.
Every component must have well-defined props and state behaviour; avoid creating a separate style for each page. Prefer design tokens. Do not build a large untyped App.tsx with every state and handler; domain logic should live in services/stores/hooks.
33. UI state, event and data-flow contracts
Domain services (suggested responsibilities)
- farmService: create, rename, select, archive/delete, farm-level preferences.
- fieldService: field CRUD, soil/water/practice changes, area validation.
- geometryService: create/validate/edit polygon, area calculation and geometry versions.
- cropCatalogService: source-backed crop metadata and applicability.
- dataConnectorService: orchestrate per-source requests, rate limits, provenance and cache.
- analysisService: deterministic pipeline for New Analysis, shared by initial run and What-If.
- rotationService: generate/score feasible candidate sequences and structured evidence.
- cropCycleService: plan/actual status transitions, planting/harvest/activity event creation and date propagation.
- weatherService, soilService, waterBalanceService, vegetationService, hazardService: each implements declared inputs/outputs and failures.
- deepAnalysisService: multi-source retrieval, calculations, findings, recommendations and report snapshot.
- traceService: records actual execution events to local persistence.
- schedulerService: local due-run checks with browser-closed limitation.
- scenarioService: clone immutable analysis baseline and apply scenario overrides without mutation.
- calendarService: event list, date propagation rules and local persistence.
- explanationService: deterministic explanation builder and optional Gemini grounded explanation.
- exportImportService: archive serialization, validation, migration and backup.
Event/state flow
- One selected farm/field context object is shared across current page and header controls.
- A write operation validates input, persists to IndexedDB, then updates UI state from the persisted result. Do not optimistically display success if storage rejects.
- On context switch, abort or invalidate in-flight requests tied to the previous farm and never merge their response into the new selection.
- Use explicit domain events for important changes, e.g. FarmRenamed, BoundaryUpdated, CropPlanCreated, PlantingConfirmed, CropHarvested, AnalysisRunSaved, DeepAnalysisRunCompleted, ScenarioSaved. Events should reflect actual successful persistence.
- Domain events may update dependent local read models/cards, but immutable historical snapshots remain untouched.
- Provide a single consistent loading/error state per asynchronous task; avoid nested duplicate spinners for one request.
34. Visual detail specification by key screen
34.1 Home visual hierarchy
- Header area is calm and compact; no oversized landing-page hero on every login/visit.
- Left 7/12 columns: featured farm status card with photographic or aerial background, deep forest overlay, farm name/status/summary, one primary button, thin status accent edge.
- Right 5/12 columns: weather card with simple weather icon/illustration and high/low/current values; a compact observation timestamp beneath.
- Next row: maximum four indicator tiles, each with icon, value, unit, small trend only where time-series is supported, and source badge; tiles must not have fake sparklines.
- Next row: large-ish map preview (approximately 16:7 aspect ratio) with actual saved boundaries if any; “Open Maps” badge/button in a corner; do not expose exact coordinates publicly (Home is private).
- Lower row: one column for real recent Deep Analysis/report, one for relevant alerts/upcoming crop milestone; collapse/omit empty columns gracefully.
34.2 Maps visual hierarchy
- Map canvas fills available width and height beneath the header.
- Search floats top-left with max width 420 px, proper z-index and its own result dropdown.
- Map tool rail floats left around vertical center/upper third with compact 44–48 px button cells.
- My Farms floating panel sits top-right with 280–320 px width. Each farm item around 64–76 px tall with a tiny photo/thumb or status marker, name, location/field count and status. Scroll if list exceeds max-height. Selection shows a left accent and subtle background, not a bright glowing border.
- Geometry edit controls appear contextual near the bottom or above the map attribution; they must not cover the polygon being edited.
- Polygon style: 2–3 px stroke at normal zoom, semitransparent fill at roughly 10–18% opacity, selected stroke 3–4 px and fill 18–25%, vertex handles 8–10 px with visible focus ring. Ensure handles are usable by touch on mobile (larger invisible hit area).
- On the map a farm’s worst-field state sets its outline colour. Always show a legend with icons/labels for Good, Needs Attention, Alert and Insufficient Data.
- Use animated camera movement and selected outline emphasis as defined earlier; respect reduced motion.
34.3 Farm View visual hierarchy
- Header includes breadcrumb and farm/field selection.
- Top summary row: status + one action (Deep Analysis) + compact update info.
- If farm-level view: weather/climate strip first; field list/portfolio next; shared rotation/analysis summaries after it.
- If field-level view: selected field header, compact condition strip, Crop Cycle/rotation timeline immediately visible, then grouped indicators/sections.
- Use a left-to-right narrative: “Current condition → Crop plan/cycle → Evidence/indicators → Risks and interpretation → History”. Do not place technical tables above the human-readable summary.
- A persistent small “Simple / Advanced” control belongs near the section/tab header, with active-state contrast, keyboard access and explanatory label.
- Advanced mode reveals extra data without changing the underlying calculations or hiding important alerts from Simple mode.
34.4 New Analysis wizard
- Form width around 820–960 px maximum on desktop, centered within main content with a calm side rail or step indicator.
- Progress indicator includes completed/current/upcoming steps and step names, not a percentage that implies calculation has happened before it has.
- Each input has a clear label, helper text, units and source/provenance if prefilled. Unknown/skip is explicit where valid.
- Review screen uses grouped summary panels and a clear Edit link for each group.
- Running screen has a main stage title, real progress track, completed/current/pending steps, source chips that light only when actually used, source failure indicators, cancellation and a truthful partial-completion message. Never show fixed fake “30+ years” or claim SMAP/MODIS/NASADEM/GPM was queried unless traces confirm those APIs were accessed. Progress maps to actual job stages; if precise percent cannot be measured, use indeterminate progress and stage names instead of fabricated percent.
34.5 Rotations page
- Top summary includes source farm/field/run and current priority profile.
- First candidate is expanded as the primary recommendation; next 2 candidates appear as compact expandable rows/cards. Avoid showing 10 huge cards at once. A “View all candidates” control reveals the rest.
- Rotation timeline uses a visual crop sequence with distinct crop imagery and date windows. Crop cards have enough whitespace for name, duration, crop status and top one or two factor labels.
- Compare mode uses a dedicated comparison layout with criteria as rows, candidates as columns and a clear difference/direction column where calculable. Keep table readability at desktop and switch to stacked compare cards on mobile.
- An expandable “Why this won” section contains score decomposition and Decision Replay.
34.6 What-If Analysis page
- Top stage header: farm selector → field selector → baseline selector shown as connected steps/breadcrumbs.
- A permanent baseline strip summarizes rotation/date/input snapshot and includes “view baseline details”.
- Main workspace: left control panel (about 34% width, max around 400 px) and right result comparison panel (remaining width). On narrow screens the controls collapse into an accordion above results.
- Each slider/control shows baseline value, current hypothetical value and unit; an explicit reset action returns to baseline.
- Results use metric change rows/cards with baseline and scenario bars/values, a sequence of crops/timeline, score delta and top altered factors. Use colour plus text arrows/tags; negative change is not always bad (e.g. lower water demand can be positive under a water-saving preference).
- A persistent Hypothetical scenario label remains visible. Save/branch controls appear only after a successful result. Failed calculations preserve inputs and show an error; do not update the baseline.
34.7 Calendar page
- Top header with selected farm/field filter, view switcher and date navigation.
- Desktop: calendar/timeline uses most of width; side panel shows selected day/events when space allows. Compact month list in mobile.
- Event chips contain short title, crop/activity icon and status. Event details open a drawer with planned/actual date, field/cycle, source and edit controls.
- Provide list/table view for accessibility and users who do not prefer visual calendars.
34.8 Deep Analysis report
- Top report header: selected farm/field scope, timestamp, run status and “View trace” link.
- Summary block: status, brief verdict, top findings and data limitations. Use balanced layout with no fake giant “98 score” if that number lacks defined basis.
- Findings arranged by urgency/relevance with a concise title, “what the data shows”, “why it matters”, evidence tag, uncertainty and possible next check/action.
- A “What changed” comparative chart appears only if a comparable prior run exists.
- Expandable Advanced accordion exposes calculations and sources; technical detail is visible on demand, not a 200-row dump by default.
34.9 Deep Analysis Logs page and modal
- Logs list is dense but readable, with compact rows and column alignment. Group by date when useful.
- Modal/detail max width around 900–1100 px and max height 85 vh with a distinct header and X in the top-right; inner content scrolls, background remains stable. On mobile it becomes full-screen.
- Trace row has left event icon, step title, time/duration, outcome tag and chevron. Expanded state uses nested detail panel for input/source/calculation/output. Keep payload text line-wrapped; raw JSON is a code-style disclosure, not the default visual.
- User can compare two runs without losing selected filters. Export log/report only from persisted records and redact tokens/secrets.
34.10 Data & Trust Center and Settings
- Use an operations/control-panel layout with sections and search/filter instead of dozens of giant cards.
- Source list rows show provider icon, name, status, last fetch, product type, data scope and “View details”.
- Formulas appear in expandable technical panels with normal-language first and formula/intermediate values second.
- Settings sections use a left subnav on desktop and grouped accordions on mobile. Destructive local-data controls use a clearly separate lower section, no red everywhere.
35. Responsive breakpoints and accessibility
Suggested CSS breakpoints to adapt at layout transitions: mobile under 640 px; tablet 640–1023 px; compact desktop 1024–1279 px; wide desktop 1280 px and above. Use content-driven breakpoints as needed and test actual widths.
- Desktop uses horizontal top navigation. At tablet widths, compress it and move lower-priority routes into a “More” menu; do not create a persistent global sidebar. On mobile use a compact navigation drawer or bottom navigation with the most important destinations and a “More” list.
- Farm Context Bar stacks on mobile. Charts become horizontally scrollable with a text/table alternative; never shrink axes/labels below legibility.
- Maps tool rail and My Farms panel become bottom sheets/drawers in mobile. Do not cover drawing vertices with the panel; allow panel hide/minimize. Drawing actions need large touch targets.
- Modal becomes full-screen on mobile; close action remains visible and back gesture/history works predictably.
- Keep keyboard focus visible, semantic headings/landmarks, labelled inputs, descriptive button names, accessible chart summary/table and no focus traps beyond modal lifecycle.
- Use minimum 44 × 44 px touch target for key map/tools/actions where practical.
- Status must include a text label and icon/pattern. Do not rely on red/green contrast alone.
- Charts and map drawing must have keyboard/list alternatives for key information.
- Implement reduced motion, adequate text contrast, readable line length and zoom/responsive support.
- Test screen widths 360, 390, 768, 1024, 1280, 1440 and 1920 px. Verify no horizontal overflow, clipped cards, unreachable controls or header/sidebar collision.
PART XII — IMPLEMENTATION AND QUALITY GATES
36. Specific corrections required from the audited current implementation
The existing code must be tested and its outputs compared with the following known risks observed in the supplied dump. These are not cosmetic; they can mislead a farmer.
1. Seeded farms: src/store/farmStorage.ts currently has createDefaultSeedFarm1/2() and a “two verified farms” first-run seed path, with sample analysis data and isDemo: false. Replace this with an empty first-run database. Offer a separate Load illustrative demo farm action; all such records must have explicit demo provenance/banner. Never call a seeded record verified.
2. Hardcoded pH: src/engine/scoring.ts currently uses a fallback like answers.soilPh ?? 6.5. Missing pH cannot be treated as a measured neutral/optimal value. Replace with a factor that is missing/excluded, or a clearly sourced prior if the model is intentionally making a mapped estimate; lower Data Coverage and document the effect on ranking.
3. Hardcoded water quota: src/engine/scoring.ts currently falls back to seasonalWaterQuotaMm ?? 350 in water calculations. Do not silently invent farmer irrigation/water availability. If the number is a defined crop/region scenario parameter, store it as an explicit assumption with source and label; otherwise represent availability as unknown and avoid an exact water-supply conclusion.
4. Overconfident default data quality: calculateConfidence currently begins from a fixed dataQuality = 0.92 described as NASA POWER multi-year verified records. A default 0.92 cannot be assigned to every source/run, especially when fallback or regional prior data is used. Compute Data Coverage from actually returned source records and metric-specific coverage; do not give high quality to a failed fetch.
5. Estimated NDVI: src/services/nasaApi.ts currently derives a regional NDVI estimate from annual precipitation thresholds in fallback logic. It is not a satellite NDVI observation. Do not display it as a real vegetation index; label it as an ecological prior only if there is a well-defined supported inference, and preferably do not show a numeric “NDVI” value at all without authentic observation data.
6. Time-window claims: the local preset sample may include only a small number of annual points; never label it a 30-year historical series unless the actual fetched/saved records cover 30 years. Show the real start/end years, gaps and number of valid points.
7. NASA/API fallback: src/services/nasaApi.ts can fall back to nearest preset agro-climatic region. Keep that feature only if the fallback is explicitly labelled Regional estimate with the region and missing fields. Do not display it as data at the exact farm coordinate.
8. Weather fallback: src/services/liveWeather.ts can produce baseline weather values after failure. Keep a graceful fallback, but the UI must say current online weather could not be retrieved and show when/where the fallback was derived. Do not display a preset temperature as a current observation.
9. Crop parameter specificity: the crop catalog stores detailed numerical defaults (temperature thresholds, water coefficients, pH, salinity, nitrogen values and return intervals). Audit each crop parameter against its source and region. Preserve validated parameters; mark unsupported ones explicitly. Do not let plausible-looking hardcoded values pass as verified science.
10. Fixed sample provenance: where types say DEMO, REGIONAL_PRIOR, CALCULATED, FARMER or a named API source, verify each label matches the actual path that produced the data. Never let the display claim an API source when a fallback supplied the value.
11. AI defaults: server.ts currently builds prompts with fallback values such as Salina, Kansas, silt loam, rainfall and default rotation when context is absent. Remove these invisible defaults. Missing context should be represented explicitly. The AI must not conclude that a user’s farm is in a default US location.
12. AI secrets/configuration: server.ts instantiates GoogleGenAI() and fixes the model ID in code. Make configuration explicit through server env vars, safe startup checks and user-facing missing-key state. Confirm the current official model ID at build time; do not assume a model stays available forever.
13. Theme system: src/index.css currently defines several theme variants including cyan-dark, emerald-dark, solar-amber and high-contrast plus glow effects. Remove theme switching and consolidate into the approved dark forest-green token system.
14. Navigation: the current App.tsx uses a central tab union including a standalone simulator tab. Move What-If to the Analysis page route. Preserve Calendar as main navigation. Replace ad hoc state-only navigation with stable route/deep-link handling.
15. Current map renderer: the code currently imports Leaflet and uses tile-pane filters. Do not simply apply CSS invert/hue-rotate across real satellite tiles as the final visual style, because that can distort imagery and its labels. Use appropriate basemap layers/styles and keep imagery natural, with dark overlays only for UI panels above it.
16. Current fake completeness: a component/import or a static dashboard card does not prove a backend flow is implemented. Test every interactive control and its state write, source call, calculation or navigation destination.
17. Persistence boundary: current persistence uses localStorage records. Move structured data gradually to IndexedDB as specified, with safe migration and export. Do not assume the current local data is disposable; preserve/import it.
18. Scheduled analysis: current daily snapshots/cache logic cannot be assumed to be a robust closed-browser scheduler. Implement a truthful app-open local schedule and make limitations visible.
For each correction, add a regression test and include before/after reasoning in the audit log. Do not fix only the displayed label while leaving the invalid numerical assumption in the engine.
37. Required test plan
37.1 Automated unit tests
- Priority sliders → normalized weights; weights sum to 1 within tolerance, respect documented bounds when feasible, show clamp explanation, all equal / all top / all low edge cases.
- Crop suitability factors with complete, incomplete, out-of-range and unsupported data. Missing pH/water parameters do not become ideal values.
- Rotation feasibility: repeated families, perennial duration, invalid date windows, unsupported crops, critical constraints and crop transition constraints.
- Stable reproducibility: identical saved inputs/model version produce same candidate scores/order/seeded simulation.
- Score component breakdown matches the total; AI explanation cannot change score/rank.
- Water balance mass-balance, units, bounds, root-zone storage, no negative impossible storage, effective rainfall/irrigation efficiency and missing-data handling.
- Soil projection tests: modelled outputs are deterministic/versioned and kept separate from measured soil records.
- Confidence/Data Coverage calculation tests: failed/stale/coarse/missing source lowers or changes coverage according to documented rules; no universal hardcoded high quality.
- Status logic: good/needs-attention/alert/insufficient data, critical concern cannot be averaged away, unsupported low-confidence proxy does not automatically create severe alert.
- Polygon validation: at least three distinct vertices, close ring, self-intersection, invalid coordinates, area calculation, unit conversion, anti-meridian/world-wrap cases where applicable, and geometry edit cancel/undo/redo.
- Crop lifecycle transitions: recommended→planned→active→changed/completed, invalid transition handling, date correction, actual dates immutable after plan edits.
- Crop date propagation into Calendar; planned events update, actual completed events do not move.
- IndexedDB migration from current localStorage schema, duplicate IDs, incomplete records, migration rollback and export/import round-trip.
- Cache keys and source timestamps; API failures do not masquerade as new values; retries do not create duplicate analysis/log/alert records.
- Deep Analysis trace event ordering/durations/status and log immutability; no secret appears in payload/export.
- Scheduler idempotency and due-run behaviour after app closure/reopen; overlapping runs do not duplicate.
- What-If clones baseline and cannot mutate farm/crop cycle/calendar/history; reset and branch semantics.
- Global network local-only gating never renders synthetic users/farms as live and public projection type does not contain exact location/boundary fields.
37.2 Integration tests
- Map drawing → save boundary → computed area → link/create farm → My Farms and Home reflect same farm ID.
- Boundary editor change and cancel restore previous coordinates.
- Existing farm/analysis selected during drawing asks for confirmation before link/replacement.
- New Analysis → save as plan → Crop Cycle = Planned, not Active.
- Planned start date passed with no confirmation remains Planned.
- Confirm planting → activity saved → Farm View/Home/Calendar show consistent active crop/date.
- Record crop change → history preserved, future planned sequence revised only after confirmation.
- New Analysis rerun creates new analysis record; old one unchanged; active cycle is unchanged.
- Deep Analysis re-fetches eligible sources and creates a real trace, report and saved log; partial source failure makes partial status.
- Click Deep Analysis Log → detail opens; X closes; browser/back restores previous context.
- Select a saved scenario and rerun; hypothetical values never appear as actual farm conditions.
- Select a farm on Maps; camera moves; click polygon opens canonical Farm View with minimized map; click mini-map returns with map camera/state preserved.
- Farm rename updates all active labels without corrupting old snapshots.
- Remove map drawing leaves the farm and records; delete farm is a separately confirmed operation.
- Alert read/dismiss state persists and duplicate alerts are suppressed.
- Gemini key absent/network failure uses deterministic fallback, marks it honestly, and keeps core app functional.
37.3 Visual and accessibility checks
- At desktop/tablet/mobile breakpoints, no overlapping, clipped text, hidden buttons, horizontal page overflow, unreadable chart labels, modal escape failure or map tools unreachable.
- Keyboard-only: navigation, dialogs, form wizard, comparison, map tool entry/exit where possible, crop-cycle changes and evidence drawers.
- Screen reader labels and status semantics; ensure icons don’t become unlabeled controls.
- Contrast and status comprehension without colour alone; separate confidence badges are immediately visually distinguishable.
- Reduced-motion mode suppresses unnecessary transitions.
- All images load or show a crop-appropriate fallback with correct attribution; each crop type has a distinct image asset.
- Charts have a text/table alternative and correct units/time windows.
- Run full build/typecheck and fix all errors before saying complete.
37.4 User-journey acceptance tests
Journey A — first farm: start with an empty local state, create a field via New Analysis, save analysis, draw a farm boundary on Maps, link it to the same farm, then confirm that My Farms has one farm—not duplicates.

Journey B — multi-field farm: add two fields to one farm with different soil, crop, and irrigation information; verify the farm status uses the worst supported field, weather remains farm-level, field values remain separate, and each field can have a different rotation.

Journey C — rotation selection: complete the wizard, review data coverage, receive candidates, answer a material risk question, view the recomputed balanced option, compare candidates and save one as Plan.

Journey D — actual crop cycle: confirm planting with actual date, record watering and harvest, edit the plan for a future crop, and verify historical events remain correct across Farm View, Home and Calendar.

Journey E — Deep Analysis history: run Deep Analysis with available data, inspect report, inspect expandable trace, close log modal, run again later, compare runs and see limitations if source set/version changes.

Journey F — What-If: select farm/field/baseline, change rainfall or irrigation within supported range, compare results and save a branch. Verify no actual data changed.

Journey G — no key/offline: disable AI key and a data provider; verify the app opens local records, distinguishes cached/stale/unavailable data, and does not fill cards with fake live metrics.

Journey H — local-only Global Network: verify the network page is honest and nonfunctional cross-user controls are not presented as live. If demo mode is toggled, every synthetic item is labelled “Illustrative demo data” and separated from personal farms.
38. Definition of done
The rebuild is not complete when the landing page looks attractive. It is done only when all applicable criteria pass:
- Product requirements are mapped to code and tests, with all conflicts/constraints documented.
- All core routes render, navigate and preserve selected farm/field context correctly.
- The new visual system is consistent; desktop and mobile layouts have been verified; assets are properly credited and distinct by crop.
- All actions work, all buttons have real effects, all forms validate and save, all error/retry/empty/loading states behave correctly.
- The local data model connects farm and field records across every page; IDs are stable; migration/export/import is tested.
- New Analysis/Rotations/What-If use one shared deterministic engine, not three inconsistent scoring implementations.
- Deep Analysis reports are sourced, traceable, saved and comparable; scheduled runs obey local-only limitations.
- No fake measurements, fake “verified” farms, false real-time claims, fake API successes or untraceable AI claims remain.
- Data Coverage and Recommendation Confidence are separately calculated/labelled/visually encoded.
- Exact private farm location never leaks into the public Global Farming Network view or any future public projection.
- Browser local data remains local; no sign-in/cloud storage is active. API requests are documented and no secret key is bundled in client code.
- No TypeScript/build errors; major user journeys and responsive/keyboard states pass.
- The final handoff explains what was completed, what remains unavailable because of API keys or local-only constraints, how to configure providers, how to test, and what limitations remain.
