42. Global Simple Mode and Advanced Mode — complete content contract
42.1 One engine, two presentation layers
Simple and Advanced Mode expose different levels of detail from the same underlying calculations and saved records. One global setting applies across all routes and persists locally. Advanced mode retains the readable summary from Simple Mode and reveals the technical layers beneath it. The underlying score, status, input values and calculated result never change just because the user toggles mode. The system always computes the full supported result payload and selects what to render.

Simple Mode first displays the most important conclusion/status, 2–3 supporting facts, plain language and at most one main action. It offers “See details” rather than hiding all evidence. Advanced Mode adds raw values, subscores, component contributions, full provenance, input snapshots, formulas, uncertainty, data coverage, historical series and source references. Advanced must be curated and grouped—not an unfiltered JSON/API dump.
42.2 Global mode setting and post-analysis prompt
Settings contains a two-option persistent Display mode control:
- Simple — “Clear summaries, key facts and next steps.”
- Advanced — “Full calculations, source details, trends and model assumptions.”
Mode toggle may also be accessible in the header utility menu; it must be the same global value. Theme is not switchable: dark forest theme only.

After every successful New Analysis and successful Deep Analysis completes while the user is in Simple Mode, show a small, dismissible prompt after the result is visible:
- Title: “Want to see the full reasoning?”
- Body: “Advanced Mode reveals detailed calculations, data sources, confidence limits and the reasoning behind the result.”
- Primary: Switch to Advanced; update global mode and preserve the current result/run state.
- Secondary: Not now.
- Top-right X and Escape close the prompt with the same effect as Not now.
- Do not show before save/completion, during an operation, on an error, or repeatedly on component rerender. Use run ID so it appears once per completed analysis run. No prompt in Advanced Mode.
42.3 Two distinct confidence badges
Recommendation Confidence means stability/strength of the chosen conclusion/ranking under documented assumptions and sensitivity. Use an icon such as a compass/shield with segmented ring, a blue-green outline and full label. It is not a probability of successful yield unless calibrated to support that interpretation.

Data Coverage means completeness, freshness, spatial suitability and traceability of inputs. Use a different visual form: stacked layers/database icon in a compact square or layered badge, neutral/stone-teal surface, full label. It must look different from Recommendation Confidence even in monochrome. Each has its own tooltip and accessible description. Color alone cannot distinguish them. If either value lacks a defined method, show a qualitative honest state—not a made-up percentage.
42.4 Complete mode-by-mode content inventory
Global header and alerts
Simple: route navigation, selected farm/field context when relevant, alert bell + real unread count, AI Agronomist, Help, Settings, global mode. Alert drawer shows plain-language item, severity, farm/field, date and one action.
Advanced: alert rule/ID, observed value and unit, baseline, delta, threshold, exact source/product, observation time vs retrieval time, spatial/temporal resolution, calculation version and evidence link.
Home
Simple: selected farm and status with one sentence reason; weather snapshot with correct Current/Forecast/Recent label; confirmed active crop and stage only if planting is confirmed; one-line rotation with active crop highlighted; plain-language water and soil summaries; alerts only when present; next meaningful crop milestone; one useful action; map preview that opens Maps.
Advanced adds: wind/direction, relative humidity, solar radiation, period-bound rainfall, supported moisture by depth, crop risk inputs only if valid, nitrogen figures only if genuinely calculated and preferably outside the glanceable home, trend magnitude and duration, review countdown rule, all sources and missing-data limitations.
My Farms portfolio
Simple: farm name, broad location, area where known, field count, confirmed active crop or “No active crop confirmed”, status pill and last update.
Advanced: portfolio area/counts and per-farm / per-field matrix. Aggregates must only combine compatible units, populations and time windows; otherwise show separate values and explain incompatibility.
Farm View overview
Simple: farm identity and overall status/reason, supported shared weather/climate/terrain summary, field list and each field's status, rotations at a glance and next milestone if known.
Advanced: per-field details, aggregate rules, mapped polygon footprint vs sum of typed field areas, all available farm-wide metadata and field comparison. Do not average field soil and crop conditions without a defined model.
Field View / Farm Analysis
Simple: selected field, area, lifecycle state, confirmed current crop if any, readable soil/water summary, rotation plan with date windows, relevant alerts and one useful next step.
Advanced: soil-test values and mapped estimates with source and depth, pH/texture/organic carbon/CEC/water capacity/bulk density/salinity/drainage/slope only if available; weather/rainfall series; water balance; valid vegetation history; complete crop history; inputs, formulas, data limits.
New Analysis
Simple: essential questions first; location; soil/water setup; historical crops including approximate planting/harvest month and year; horizon; priorities; top feasible rotation with dates, label, distinct confidence/coverage badges, pros/cons, material risks, mitigation, 1–2 alternatives, plain-language rationale and explicit Save as Plan.
Advanced: raw normalized answers; per-question impact only if defined; crop parameter source/version; per-factor suitability; component scores only when actually implemented; hard constraint checks; score breakouts; date assumptions; supported resource budget; ranking sensitivity only if calculated; reasons candidates were rejected; formula/source references. No financial output.
Rotations
Simple: best feasible rotation first, distinct photo/illustration per crop, sequence and dated windows, active/planned state, 3–4 reasons, core risk/limitation, 1–2 alternatives and an obvious compare/save action.
Advanced: aligned comparison table, score/component deltas, sequence effects, crop family and pest-break logic when evidence supports it, water/soil projections, constraints, custom builder, stress tests, all rejected candidate reasons and saved version.
Analysis / What-If only
Simple: select farm → field → baseline run → a few understandable factors/presets (hotter, less rain, less irrigation). Show before/after status, whether the best eligible rotation changes and a plain explanation.
Advanced: exact baseline and override values/units; per-crop fit; all rotation score/rank changes; driver attribution; constraint threshold crossings; supported water balance chart; save/duplicate/branch/compare scenarios. Always display “This is a what-if exploration, not a forecast” and “This does not change your real plan.” Use same engine as New Analysis; hypothetical never overwrites real state.
Calendar
Simple: month/list, event labels and crop identities, selected-day actions only if supported, planned vs actual distinction.
Advanced: detailed planting/harvest windows, multiple field lanes, linked reasoning, actual-vs-planned history and source/assumption details. Never treat an expected harvest as an actual harvest.
Deep Analysis
Simple: overall supported conclusion, strengths, concerns, risks, recommended actions, changes since last comparable run, important limits and clear run status.
Advanced: all indicators; provenance/freshness; formulas and input snapshot; component breakdown; history overlays; sensitivity if supported; exact execution trace, durations/status/errors, data gaps, AI investigation with citations and hypotheses clearly distinguished from facts.
Deep Analysis Logs / History
Simple: date/time, farm/fields, readable conclusion, run status, biggest supported change and link to open.
Advanced: run ID, manual/scheduled/partial/failed/cancelled status, precise source times, cache and model versions, trace events, errors/retries, compare-two-run view, full stored record. Historical logs are immutable snapshots; don't regenerate them using today's code.
Water
Simple: supply/demand status, plain-language reason, whether evidence is observed/estimated/modelled and one important limitation.
Advanced: ET0/ETc, Kc/stage, effective rainfall, irrigation, supported root-zone storage, deficit/stress calculations, units, time series, assumptions and formula steps.
Soil
Simple: texture/soil-test summary and relevance to current crop.
Advanced: properties with units, depths, dates, methods, measured vs mapped labels, crop-relative pH, EC/salinity, carbon/organic matter, CEC, available water, structure/compaction where measured, uncertainty. Never invent nutrient/pH values.
Vegetation / Field Signals
Simple: latest valid observation date, change label, cautious interpretation and inspection suggestion if supported.
Advanced: provider/product/band/pixel scale, cloud/no-data mask, valid polygon coverage, actual NDVI/EVI calculation, baseline/observations/dates/values. If imagery wasn't obtained and processed, show Unavailable instead of mocked figures.
Data & Trust
Simple: sources used in this run, observation and retrieval time, missing sources and plain description.
Advanced: exact product/endpoint/variables/area/time window/resolution/units/transformations/cache/status/errors with secrets redacted, source and parameter versions, formula references, provenance export.
Settings
Simple: global mode, units, in-app alerts, local backup/export/import, clear data and a non-interactive “Dark forest” theme label.
Advanced: provider status and env var names (never key values), real test-connection result, cache/refresh, engine/schema/crop catalogue version, scheduler/farm selection/last-next run, local scheduling limitations, retention/export options.
AI Agronomist
Simple: concise plain-language answer grounded in selected farm and actual accessible records, evidence links and a link to the exact indicator.
Advanced: math/formula, exact source/product/time, structured inputs, limitations and alternate hypotheses. Never show hidden chain-of-thought. Show actual trace/events, not private internal reasoning.
Network
Its intended full UI is specified later. In local-only mode, no fake people, messages, unread counts or regional averages. Show an honest shared-backend-unavailable state.
42.5 Advanced mode organization
Advanced is not “all text visible at once.” Use progressive layers: conclusion → metric group → expandable metric detail → formula/trace → source metadata. Provide local expand/collapse, global Expand all/Collapse all for execution trace, sticky table headers only where useful, sort only meaningful columns, units in headers, empty/error states, export only where supported. Keep the plain-language conclusion visible.
ADDENDUM: Detailed Maps and farm geometry contract
V2 SCREEN DETAIL — MAPS, GEOMETRY, FARM CREATION, AND CANONICAL FARM VIEW
This section is the implementation contract for Maps and farm geometry. It supersedes any previous map-panel placement described differently. The supplied screenshot is a composition and density reference, not a branding/UI template to copy literally.
1. The desired map composition
Desktop baseline: 1440 × 900 viewport
Use a full application shell with a 62–68 px horizontal top navigation header. Below it, place the Maps workspace in a rounded enclosure that fills the available viewport height without forcing a page-long scroll. Reserve 12–18 px outer gutters. Suggested starting layout at 1440 px:
- left Inspector: 272 px; min 248 px; max 320 px;
- central interactive map: flexible, about 770–820 px at this viewport;
- right My Farms panel: 300 px; min 280 px; max 340 px;
- 1 px low-contrast separators, with 8–12 px column gutters where appropriate;
- workspace corner radius around 18 px, panel radius 12–16 px;
- header, panels and map align to the same top/bottom edges.
These are design targets, not brittle hard-coded screen coordinates. Use CSS grid with grid-template-columns: minmax(250px, 292px) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(280px, 320px) at wide breakpoints. The main map must remain the dominant visual surface. At 1280 px, shrink panel widths and keep useful map area. At narrower tablet widths, collapse the left Inspector into a slide-over or expandable lower panel and keep My Farms accessible as a right drawer. On phones, use full-screen map with small floating controls and bottom sheets; do not squeeze three columns into tiny widths.
Panel roles — do not swap them
Left: selected-farm inspector. Shows the selected farm's identity, overall status, city/region, field count, latest supported weather/context, concise farm-level conditions, and field summaries. Do not fill it with numbers unless the source and time range exist. Each field row may show a status badge and latest relevant crop; selecting a field opens its field view. Small charts/sparklines are allowed only with enough real timestamped samples; otherwise use an empty-state hint (“Trend will appear after enough observations”).

Centre: satellite map canvas. Use a genuine satellite/aerial basemap from a licensed provider or configured service. Show attribution. Avoid global filters that invert hues or hue-rotate natural imagery. The supplied screenshot’s natural earth-tone aerial image, crisp white polygon boundaries, small legible labels, restrained controls, and map-first layout are useful reference cues. Overlay private farm polygons without obscuring imagery. Selected boundary gets a brighter outline, a restrained translucent fill, and a clear label; unselected boundaries use lower emphasis. Never make polygon colour the only way to represent status.

Right: My Farms. This replaces the sample screenshot’s “Map Layers & Data” panel for the main Maps experience. Header: “My Farms”; right-aligned + icon/button with accessible label “Add farm”; optional collapse icon at smaller widths. Under the header, a search field (“Search your farms”), small count (“4 farms”), optional sort/filter menu, and a vertically scrollable list. Each farm row: unique crop/farm thumbnail or restrained icon, farm display name, town/region (as owner-private detail; avoid publishing exact address), field count, current overall status, and optional last-updated hint. Row click selects and eases map viewport to the farm while visibly highlighting it. A distinct … or context action offers Rename, Edit boundary, Remove drawing from map, and Delete farm only under separate guarded actions. Do not put destructive Delete beside the main click target.
Left Inspector sizing and hierarchy
At the top: small “Selected farm” eyebrow; farm name 19–22 px semibold; location subtitle 12–13 px; 32–36 px status pill with label + icon; View farm / Open farm action. Next: farm-level context section with compact weather/conditions if live/source data is available, including source/update timestamp. Then “Fields” section with rows that show field label, crop-cycle state, crop when confirmed, and a neutral state where unknown. Avoid a wall of seven identical KPI cards. Use one prominent status summary, 2–4 compact metrics, and expandable details. A selected field should clearly switch the Inspector from farm-level to field-level context while retaining a breadcrumb back to the farm.
Visual reference treatment
Use the supplied screenshot’s visual grammar: satellite imagery has the strongest color and texture; dark graphite/forest panels create legible contrast; borders are fine, not glowing; map boundary lines are crisp; labels float on semi-opaque dark chips; numerical values align; information panels scroll internally instead of pushing the map out of view. Adapt all colors, labels, icons, controls and hierarchy to Field Shift and its forest-intelligence brand. Do not reproduce EarthEye branding, the screenshot’s exact layout copy, or its inaccurate example metrics.
2. Add Farm: complete interaction workflow
The top-right + Add Farm action must start a progressive, explicit process. It must not silently create an empty farm or save an invalid boundary.
Step 1 — start the draft
Clicking + inserts a visible inline draft card at the top of My Farms and focuses the Farm name input. Keep the map usable behind the draft. Provide Cancel and Continue buttons; Continue remains disabled until the name passes validation. Draft state is temporary until the farmer confirms saving.

Suggested draft card:
- heading “Add a farm”;
- farm name input, placeholder “e.g., North Valley Farm”;
- optional location search input (geocoding helps move the camera; it does not prove the farm boundary);
- helper text: “You’ll draw the overall farm outline next. Fields and crop plans are managed inside the farm.”;
- Continue to map primary action and Cancel secondary action.
Name validation
Trim leading/trailing whitespace; collapse repeated spaces for display/normalization; reject blank values, control characters, unsupported extreme length, and names composed only of punctuation. Allow common Unicode characters and ordinary punctuation. Compare names case-insensitively within the user's local farm list. If a name already exists, show inline error with the existing farm’s location and choices Open existing farm, Edit its boundary (requires a confirmation that a previous geometry will be replaced by a new version), or Choose another name. Do not silently overwrite, create duplicate names, or link the new geometry to an existing farm solely because the text matches. The stable farm ID is the linking identity; names are labels only.

When the matching name belongs to a farm created through New Analysis, explain: “This name already belongs to a farm record with saved fields/analyses.” The user may explicitly choose to link the drawing to that farm or choose a different name. If linked, maintain the existing farm ID, analysis history and fields. If new, create a new stable ID only after valid geometry is saved.
Step 2 — enter drawing mode
After Continue, enter a visible “Draw farm outline” mode. Show a slim map instruction bar near the top centre (not over the nav): “Click the map to add boundary points. Connect at least 3 points, then close the outline.” Also show controls: Undo point, Redo point, Finish outline, Cancel drawing, and a small point count. Buttons are disabled when inapplicable. Provide a clear active pen icon in the map toolbar and right panel. Map click events during drawing must not trigger normal farm selection or pan-to-farm actions.

Each map click adds a numbered draggable vertex. A preview line joins the last vertex to the new one. A close-to-first-point affordance may snap the final point to the starting vertex, but an explicit Close outline control is always available. Never auto-close an unintended loop without a visible indication.
Step 3 — edit and validate the polygon
Before saving, validate:
1. at least three distinct vertices;
2. finite longitude/latitude values within valid ranges;
3. not all vertices collinear;
4. polygon ring is closed according to GeoJSON polygon conventions;
5. no self-intersecting edges (unless the chosen geometry library intentionally supports valid multi-polygons, which is not needed for V1);
6. positive non-zero calculated area;
7. geometry lies within an acceptable numeric range and survives serialization/deserialization;
8. geometry is not implausibly large for a farm without explicit acknowledgement (warn; don't impose an arbitrary small maximum that prevents large farms).
If invalid, identify the issue on map and in a short human-readable message, highlight relevant edge/vertex where possible, and keep edit controls active. No silent geometry simplification. If geometry library supports correcting minor precision issues, preserve the source vertices and record what changed.

Map drawing editor must support drag-to-adjust vertices, click a segment to insert a vertex, selecting a vertex and pressing Delete/Backspace where safe, undo/redo, restart, Cancel, and Finish. On touch devices, use adequate hit areas and a non-drag alternative to edit vertex coordinates (e.g. select point → adjustment controls) so drawing is not drag-only.
Step 4 — review before save
Show a compact review summary: name, map thumbnail, boundary outline, calculated area in m²/hectares/acres according to locale/setting, vertex count, and an edit link. If fields already exist through New Analysis, don't replace their typed areas with the polygon area. The drawn polygon represents the overall farm footprint; individual field areas remain field-level information. Show a warning if the sum of typed field areas exceeds the overall polygon area by an agreed tolerance (e.g. over 5% after unit conversion), but explain that roads, non-arable land, buffers, farm buildings and boundary estimates can explain a difference. Never silently rewrite field areas based on the polygon.
Step 5 — save transaction
Only now create or update stable farm geometry. Save a new geometry version associated to farm_id. If replacing an existing boundary, ask confirmation and preserve the old geometry version if storage/history design allows; do not delete the entire farm. If persistence fails, report failure, keep draft geometry in memory, allow retry/export, and don't tell the user the farm is saved. On success, show a restrained toast and animate the new farm row into My Farms; fit map to the polygon bounds with a short easing transition. No giant celebratory animation.
3. Farm and field entities are different
A farm is the top-level property, with one private drawn footprint in the first version. It can contain multiple fields, each with its own typed area, soil, water configuration, crop history, rotation plan, lifecycle and analysis. The map does not require separate field outlines at launch. Clicking anywhere inside the farm polygon selects the farm; then Farm View lists its fields. A field row opens full field analysis. Do not pretend field-level geometry exists if the farmer has not drawn/imported it.

Farm-level values: location context, weather station/grid point, broad climate/terrain, farm metadata, farm boundary geometry, list of fields and overall status computed from fields. Field-level values: soil inputs/tests, rotation, crop history, field area, irrigation practices, crop cycle, field analysis, field alerts and activity history. A value may be inherited as a default but must indicate “farm-level estimate/inherited” until overridden at field level. Farm-wide weather can be shown in the overview, but don't duplicate it as independent field measurements.

Overall farm status uses the worst field among fields with enough evidence, following one shared status evaluator. If a field has an alert, farm outline cannot be Good. If none of the fields has sufficient data, farm outline uses neutral grey/striped “Insufficient data” instead of Good. If some fields are unknown and others have known Good status, the farm status must indicate partial coverage and must not hide unknown fields. The exact rules and thresholds must be implemented once and reused by Home, Maps, Farm View and alerts.
4. Selecting, navigating, and preserving state
- Clicking a saved farm row selects it, smoothly pans/zooms to its polygon, updates the Inspector, and highlights it. Do not navigate away until the user explicitly clicks polygon/Open Farm.
- Clicking inside the selected polygon opens the canonical Farm View and passes farm_id, last map center/zoom and return route.
- In Farm View opened from Maps, show a compact floating mini-map at bottom-left. It is a live map preview, not a static image. Expand map returns to Maps and restores saved center/zoom and selection. X closes the floating mini-map only; it must not mean Delete or navigate away. A separate Back to Maps control returns to the full map.
- Farm View's field list uses stable field IDs. Clicking a field opens its Field View, with breadcrumb Farm name / Field name. Back returns to the same farm context, not Home by default.
- Home's map card navigates to the Maps route, focused on the currently selected farm, not an embedded-only map route.
- Browser history/back should work. Store non-sensitive camera position and selection state in route state/session where appropriate, but exact farm coordinates must not appear in public URLs or analytics.
- If a farm has no geometry, its list row still opens its Farm View. Show No boundary drawn and a clear Draw boundary action. Never refuse to display a valid farm simply because it has no polygon.
5. Map layer controls
Keep the map-view controls separate from My Farms list actions. A compact floating toolbar over the central map may offer:
- basemap selector (satellite first, other basemaps only when genuinely supported);
- optional terrain overlay if licensed/source available;
- farm boundary visibility;
- drawing/editing tool;
- zoom in/out and locate map centre;
- fit selected farm;
- measurement/area tool only if it has a defined use;
- layer legend/info drawer for actual data overlays.
Do not use a panel full of fictional vegetation/nutrient/thermal layers. In the local-only version, optional scientific layers appear only when the actual provider returns valid observations and the pipeline processes them. Unsupported layers are unavailable with reason, not sample values. A city-level public condition layer belongs to Global Farming Network and must not be overlaid as precise plot data.

Basemap source must be configured with attribution and checked against license, usage limits, CORS and token requirements. Use MapLibre GL JS with a maintained polygon drawing/editing integration such as Terra Draw when feasible, and Turf for geodesic polygon area. Keep the map provider behind an interface so changing basemap provider doesn't rewrite farm records. Do not depend on Google's deprecated Drawing Library. Public OpenStreetMap Nominatim's public endpoint is not a free unrestricted autocomplete service; use a compliant geocoder configuration and debounce/limit requests. Handle map-tile failures and geocoder failures without blanking the entire app.
6. Private versus public geometry
Private Maps may show the owner's true polygon, because it is an owner-controlled record. Public Global Farming Network must never serialise, draw, link to, or infer exact geometry/coordinates. Public mapping uses a city marker or official city area only, based on published farms. Use a separate public projection/DTO built from an allowlist; do not pass a private farm object to a public component and hide geometry with CSS. Avoid URL, log, event, screenshot or map tile leakage of private boundary data.
7. Required empty/loading/error states
- Map loading: preserve app shell and show a map-shaped skeleton, not a full-page spinner.
- Tiles fail: show error banner with retry, keep My Farms list visible.
- No farms yet: show a short instruction and prominent + Add Farm, optionally Start New Analysis; never seed fake farms by default.
- No boundary: show a clear map placeholder centred on geocoded/entered location if available, with Draw boundary action.
- Geocoding fails: keep user-entered coordinates/place context and let them pan/zoom manually; report unavailable geocoder.
- Polygon invalid: explain issue and preserve draft.
- Area mismatch: warning with explicit fields and units; permit saving after acknowledgement if otherwise valid.
- Farm deleted: require distinct confirmation, explain linked field/rotation/history consequences, and support export/undo window if implementation supports it.
- No history: hide sparklines or show explanatory placeholder, never use fabricated lines.
8. Map acceptance tests
1. Create a new farm with a unique name; add 3+ points; close; edit a point; undo/redo; save; reload; verify exact geometry persists.
2. Try duplicate name with different case/spaces; ensure no silent duplicate or overwrite and all choices are explained.
3. Try a polygon with two vertices, repeated points, collinear points, crossing edges, zero area, and out-of-range coordinate; each must be rejected with a recoverable error.
4. Update an existing boundary after explicit confirmation; ensure farm ID and field/rotation/history records remain the same and old geometry isn't silently treated as current.
5. Click farm row and polygon; confirm camera focus, selection, Inspector and view navigation behave consistently.
6. Open Farm View then field, return to farm, expand mini-map; verify center/zoom restored.
7. Check that multiple fields appear under one farm without requiring field polygons, and field-specific soil/rotation doesn't leak across fields.
8. Verify farm status is shared with Home and never Good if a field Alert exists or all data are unknown.
9. Verify public map rendering and serialized public data contain no private polygon or exact coordinate.
10. Test desktop, tablet, phone, keyboard, touch, map provider failure, geocoder failure, offline state and persistence failure.
ADDENDUM: Visual system, imagery and interaction design
V2 SCREEN DETAIL — VISUAL SYSTEM, COMPOSITION, IMAGERY, MOTION, AND PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE
1. Design goals
The website must feel like a serious agricultural intelligence product that combines the visual clarity of a premium productivity application, the spatial tools of GIS software, the explanatory graphics of a scientific platform, and the tactile feedback of a carefully built modern web app. It must not look like an AI-generated generic dashboard consisting of a hero gradient, twelve identical cards, random sparkles, and long blocks of text.

Use the supplied satellite-map screenshot as a layout reference for a dark instrument panel around a high-resolution aerial map: clear field outlines, overlay labels, restrained status color and a useful side panel. Use NASA Worldview as inspiration for layer/time controls; OneSoil for the way field data, NDVI dates and seasonal charts can relate to field records; John Deere Operations Center for the operational idea that maps, fields, activity and agronomic records belong together; Linear's current UI direction for consistent navigation, calm focus, and icons that aid scanning. These are inspiration references, not sources from which to copy proprietary visuals.

Research references (checked 25 October 2026; availability and UI can change):
- Linear UI refresh: https://linear.app/changelog/2026-03-12-ui-refresh
- NASA Worldview: https://worldview.earthdata.nasa.gov/
- OneSoil web/mobile capabilities: https://help.onesoil.ai/en/articles/5237584-how-the-onesoil-web-and-mobile-apps-differ
- John Deere Operations Center: https://www.deere.com/en-us/products-solutions/technology-solutions/precision-ag-technology/operations-center/
- MapLibre drawing example: https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/examples/draw-geometries-with-terra-draw/
- WCAG 2.2: https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG/
2. Exact visual foundation
Use a single dark-only palette as the only product theme. Do not ship the older cyan, amber, multiple-dark-theme switcher. Status accents can vary only where they encode meaning. Suggested tokens:
- page background: #0B1511 (deep forest-black, still distinct from true black);
- shell/header: #101B16;
- primary panel: #14221B;
- nested/elevated surface: #1A2A21;
- hover surface: #20342A;
- border: #2A3B31;
- border strong: #3A5143;
- primary accent: #8FBE72 (muted crop green);
- accent deep: #416D48;
- teal data accent: #63B7A1;
- main text: #F0F4EF;
- secondary text: #B2C0B5;
- muted text: #829187;
- warning: #D9AD59;
- alert: #D97869;
- neutral/unknown: #8B9690;
- blue information accent: #7AAAC7.
Validate actual rendered contrast rather than blindly trusting token choices. Normal body text must meet WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1); large text and essential visual boundaries need suitable contrast. Do not use green/red alone to communicate a state; pair each state with text and an icon/pattern. The UI remains readable with reduced transparency and when blur is disabled.
Typography
Use a polished legible sans serif such as Inter, Plus Jakarta Sans, or an equivalent licensed web font. Choose one body family and a controlled numeric/monospace family for formulas, IDs, data timestamps and code-like trace details. Don't mix five font families. Use tabular numerals for comparable metrics. Type hierarchy:
- page title: 28–32 px / 1.15–1.25 line height, medium or semibold;
- large hero metric: 28–36 px, semibold, tabular numerals;
- section headings: 17–20 px;
- card heading: 14–16 px semibold;
- body: 14–15 px / 1.45–1.6;
- labels/helper/meta: 12–13 px, never too low contrast;
- compact data table: 13 px minimum on desktop.
Layout and spacing
Use a consistent 4 px baseline scale. Standard spacing values: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64 px. Standard card radius 12–16 px; main map shell 18–22 px. Use 1 px borders, low-contrast fills, and subtle shadows only where they communicate depth. Avoid high-blur glass panels everywhere; use near-opaque dark surfaces over aerial imagery for reliable text legibility. The website should not depend on a heavy gradient or expensive backdrop-filter to appear premium.

Build a genuine responsive grid with content max width around 1440–1600 px on non-map pages, comfortable wide margins, and page-specific compositions. At 1440×900, the primary content should fit intelligently without hiding important content below the fold; not every section must fit above the fold, but key status/actions must. At 1024 px, two-column layouts should collapse intentionally. At 390 px, convert multi-column cards into sensible vertical sections, tables into horizontally scrollable/row-detail alternatives, navigation into a mobile shell, and side panels into drawers/sheets.
3. Component grammar — avoid “card spam”
Use more than one visual pattern, but give every pattern a defined job:
- hero image/overview for a farm or crop, with image-only gradient/black overlay and one primary action;
- status strip for one prominent farm condition and a short explanation;
- metric row for 2–4 comparable values with unit/source/time labels;
- analytical plot for trends or seasonality;
- timeline for crop sequence and lifecycle;
- comparison matrix for rotations or runs;
- map workspace for geography and overlays;
- split evidence panel for a conclusion alongside supporting sources;
- accordion/disclosure for advanced calculations or verbose explanation;
- activity list for chronological events;
- empty state with a meaningful next step;
- bottom sheet/drawer for contextual actions on smaller screens.
Do not turn every sentence into a card. Use whitespace, section dividers, structured text, compact lists, and charts where the data genuinely has a temporal or comparative relationship. A page should have one visual focal point, one secondary focal point and several lower-emphasis utilities—not 15 competing accent blocks.
4. Photo and illustration strategy
Images are a purposeful part of the UI, not background clutter. The user asked for lots of visual content, but each image must have a unique role and not displace useful data.
Crop imagery
Create a crop-image registry with a distinct image per crop identifier. Do not reuse the same photo for wheat, lettuce, beans and maize. A crop record should have imageUrl, alt, sourceName, sourceUrl, license, attribution, and lastVerified. Use crop-specific genuine photos where available, or a clearly designed illustration when photo attribution cannot be verified. The user permits sources broadly, but that does not remove the need to check license and attribution before embedding. Wikimedia Commons is acceptable only when the individual file license and credit requirements are retained: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Commons:Reusing_content_outside_Wikimedia/licenses/en. Never embed random hotlinked image URLs without checking persistence, permission and accessibility.

Example image directions:
- wheat: close, tactile golden wheat heads in a field, soft late-afternoon side light;
- lettuce: fresh lettuce rosettes with visible layered leaves, cool natural daylight;
- legumes/beans: green bean foliage or mature pods that clearly match the database crop;
- maize: maize stalks with visible cobs and broad green leaves;
- alfalfa: dense forage stand with purple blooms if the crop registry means flowering alfalfa;
- fallow/cover crop: appropriate cover vegetation or exposed soil only if that matches the selected cover type.
These are image-search/composition descriptions, not a request to generate fake agricultural evidence. Crop images are decorative/educational, never a source of calculated agronomic values.
Image treatment — exact CSS principles
For an image-backed banner/card, place the dark overlay inside the image wrapper and above the image, not over the whole card/content. Example pattern:
.crop-hero { position: relative; isolation: isolate; overflow: hidden; }
.crop-hero__image { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; z-index: -2; }
.crop-hero::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(8,18,12,.88) 0%, rgba(8,18,12,.58) 52%, rgba(8,18,12,.16) 100%);
  pointer-events: none;
}
.crop-hero__content { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
Use this only when it improves legibility. For a card with text centered on an image, use a dark vertical gradient from transparent near the top to 75–85% black near the text at bottom. For a photo detail panel, retain natural image colour; don't apply a global CSS filter. Use a mild image vignette only within the image mask. If adding a soft center glow, make it a low-opacity radial gradient inside the image region only (e.g., radial-gradient(ellipse at 55% 45%, rgba(143,190,114,.14), transparent 58%)), with no neon edges or pulsating blobs. Do not stack multiple gradients until the photo becomes muddy. Use object-position to protect crop subject; provide fallback color and error placeholder.
Other visual assets
Use small custom SVG illustrations for soil horizons, root-zone water balance, rotation sequencing, crop-stage timelines, data pipeline and explainability. Use 3D models only if a concrete interaction benefits (e.g., optional root-zone/soil-profile educational viewer); a non-interactive decorative 3D tractor or spinning Earth is not worth the performance cost. Prefer an accessible lightweight SVG/WebGL illustration or diagram. Provide a static fallback, lazy-load 3D, and respect reduced-motion settings. Do not load huge models on Home just to advertise “3D”.
5. Iconography and state semantics
Use one coherent icon set (Lucide or compatible outline family), consistent 18–20 px in nav and 16 px in dense lists, consistent stroke. Never use emojis as primary production icons or arbitrary crop emojis. Use dedicated status icon+label pairs. Icons are paired with visible labels or tooltips where meaning is not universal. Use a distinct badge design for Recommendation Confidence versus Data Coverage:
- Recommendation Confidence: compact circular/compass/shield-like indicator in blue-green accent; based on stability/strength of ranking evidence, defined in its model. It answers: “How robust is this ranking given the evaluation method and candidate differences?”
- Data Coverage: separate stacked-layers/database badge with a differently shaped neutral/teal style; based on actual input completeness, freshness, resolution fit and provenance. It answers: “How much suitable evidence was available?”
  These two values may diverge. High data coverage does not prove the recommendation is correct; high ranking stability does not mean complete evidence. Never blend them into one badge. In all views, show text and an accessible label, not just a color/icon.
6. Motion language
Use Motion/Framer Motion (or the existing motion package), CSS transitions and native browser primitives only where useful. Motion must communicate state, spatial continuity, or progress, not distract from farm work.

Suggested durations/easing:
- hover/focus: 100–160 ms, ease-out;
- small state transition: 160–220 ms;
- drawer/modal: 200–280 ms with ease-out, slight 8–12 px translation;
- map fly-to: ~350–650 ms depending on distance; stop if user manually interacts;
- route/page transition: 120–200 ms opacity/translate, no giant zoom;
- progress animation: match actual asynchronous work and update state; never fake percent complete.
Use shared layout transitions for farm rows and detail panels when IDs remain stable. For saved boundary, use a brief line/opacity transition; no green fireworks. For a field opening, show map panel minimize and Farm View arriving with coordinated motion. Keep dropdown and date picker motion small. Avoid infinite animation on every card, excessive blur, infinite bobbing, or motion that keeps drawing attention.

Support prefers-reduced-motion: reduce: remove travel/fade movement, stop decorative animations, shorten transitions, and keep state changes instantly understandable. Never communicate status only through animation. Loading placeholders should match the shape of content, stop when complete, and never keep a shimmer after a failed/finished request.
7. Microinteractions and feedback
- Hover and focus show a low-contrast border/background change, not big scaling.
- Buttons have loading, disabled, pressed, success and error states, as appropriate.
- Drawn map vertex: hover/focus ring, selected point number and coordinates in edit mode.
- Farm row selection: quiet outline/background plus map pan.
- Slider: current value and unit visible; keyboard arrows work; after changes, recompute only after Apply/short controlled debounce, with clear dirty/baseline state.
- Charts: hover/focus tooltip includes date, value, unit, data source/status and supports keyboard row/table alternative.
- Copy/export buttons confirm action and handle failure.
- Unsaved edits use “Save changes” / “Discard” with clear consequences.
- Destructive actions require typed confirmation only for genuinely consequential deletion; don't ask repeated confirm dialogs for routine safe changes.
- No toast-only feedback for serious errors; also show durable error text near the component/action.
8. Charts, tables and data density
Use charts only for real quantitative relationships. Time series have a date axis and labeled units; rainfall and temperature should not share the same scale without an intentionally labelled dual axis. A missing observation is a gap, not zero. Never draw connecting lines across long or material data gaps without a gap indicator. For NDVI/date comparisons, show date/quality and imagery availability. For multi-rotation results, use clear horizontal bars or aligned criterion values; a radar chart is optional but must not replace exact scores/table. Use line chart for trends, stacked bars only for meaningful parts-of-whole, scatter for two measured/model variables, and range bands when uncertainty is available. Avoid 3D charts and decorative donut charts with no decision value.

Every metric label follows: name → value/unit → source type/status → observation/model period (where relevant) → short interpretation. For an estimated soil property, mark Estimated · regional map · depth and show the correct resolution. For farmer-entered input, mark Farmer reported. For calculated model output, mark Calculated. A data source link opens a shared evidence drawer with metadata and limitations.

Tables must be scannable: sticky header for long tables, aligned numeric columns, units in headers or values, consistent decimals, sort only where useful, row expansion for detailed formulas, and a stacked responsive row view on phones. Do not shrink font to make a giant table fit a desktop width.
9. Progressive disclosure / Simple vs Advanced visuals
Simple mode is not missing functionality; it is the default decision explanation. Use 1–2 sentence plain-language summaries, a few meaningful visuals and the relevant action. Advanced mode adds detailed data, formula, unit, period, source, intermediate calculations, confidence factors, assumptions and raw table access. Keep navigation and calculation results consistent when mode changes; only presentation/detail changes. Advanced panels may open inline or as a detail drawer. Do not duplicate whole reports in both modes.

The after-analysis prompt to offer Advanced mode should appear once after a successful New Analysis or Deep Analysis completion while the user is in Simple mode: “Want to see the calculations and evidence behind this result?” actions Open Advanced Mode, Not now, and X. It must not appear after failed/cancelled/partial runs unless the user explicitly opens the result; don’t repeatedly pester. Persist global setting only when the user selects the mode in Settings.
10. Accessibility and interaction quality
Target WCAG 2.2 AA. Visible focus rings, semantic heading order, landmarks, button labels, names for icon-only controls, contrast, reduced motion, alt text, keyboard operation, focus management in modal, Escape-to-close where safe, focus restoration, and touch targets. 24 × 24 CSS px is a WCAG 2.2 minimum-size criterion with exceptions, but use roughly 36–44 px for main controls where layout allows. Dragging functionality (like map vertices) must have a non-drag alternative. Do not make a color-only health layer the only information channel; supply a textual legend and list.
11. Frontend asset performance and integrity
Use local asset references or a controlled asset registry rather than hot-linking images with no fallback. Lazy-load below-fold images, 3D models and heavy charts. Use responsive image sizes/modern format where supported; avoid loading 4K source images into 70 px thumbnails. Reserve media dimensions to avoid layout shifts. Cache only assets allowed by their license and technical headers. Provide descriptive alt for meaningful photos; use empty alt for purely decorative images. If a crop photo fails, show a crop-specific fallback icon/color, not the same generic wheat photo for all crops.
12. Visual QA acceptance criteria
Every required page must have an explicit reference layout at 1440×900 plus tablet and phone layout. Capture screenshots after implementation; inspect the full page and key modals/drawers. Confirm no horizontal page overflow, no clipped labels, no overlapping map/panels, no inconsistent padding, no tiny low-contrast text, no image reused for different crops without explicit reason, no fabricated graph data, no permanent skeletons, no decorative motion in reduced-motion mode, and no state communicated solely by hue. The UI should read as a coherent design system even though map, analytics, timeline, and chat each use different purposeful compositions.
ADDENDUM: Network and messaging, with local-only limitations
V2 SCREEN DETAIL — GLOBAL FARMING NETWORK, FARMER CHAT, AND LOCAL-ONLY REALITY
1. Product intention
The Global Farming Network is the intended long-term shared layer of Field Shift. Its purpose is not to turn the app into an unrelated social media product. It connects farmers through public farm profiles, privacy-safe city discovery, farmer-to-farmer communication, communities, regional comparisons, and evidence about real farming outcomes. It should use the same field/farm visual language and canonical farm records as the rest of Field Shift.

This section supplements the full Global Farming Network annex included later in this master prompt. Where that annex describes cloud-backed users, public publishing, friends, messages and community data, treat it as the full future product specification. It does not override the current confirmed local-only deployment decision.
2. Current release limitation — not negotiable
The current release has no user account, no shared database, no server-side identity system and no cloud sync. Therefore it cannot genuinely discover other users' farms, host cross-user messages, synchronize friendships, moderate community content, or calculate regional public-farm aggregates. Do not create fake users, fabricated conversations, invented “verified farms”, fake online states, fake message counts, or seed public farms to make this page look populated.

Keep a future-ready route/component model and create a polished Network landing state, but present it as unavailable until the necessary shared backend is intentionally configured. The screen must clearly say why the feature is unavailable and what it will provide; the user can still use Maps and private farms. Avoid showing dead-looking controls for features that cannot work. A clearly labelled preview of the information architecture is acceptable, but all mock content must be explicitly and continuously labelled “Illustrative demo data” and must not be mixed into user records or presented as real people. Default initial install should show no demo data unless the user explicitly enables an isolated demo mode.

Any future activation requires an explicit architecture change (e.g. authentication and database service), privacy/security design, moderation procedures and permission tests. Because the current plan forbids cloud storage, do not quietly add Firebase, authentication or a backend database to make chat appear functional. External AI/data APIs may still be called, but their keys must stay server-side where secrets are required.
3. Navigation placement
Network belongs in the main horizontal navigation only if the application handles unavailable state elegantly: an accessible “Network” nav item opens a concise introduction with a labelled Requires shared service state, reason, and link to Settings/Architecture notes. Alternatively, if the builder concludes even that creates confusion, place it under a Connect item or More menu. Pick one route and implement it consistently; do not add multiple chat entries. The top-right message icon is not shown as an active inbox when no backend is configured. If present, it opens the Network availability page, not a fabricated conversation list.

When a backend is later enabled, the Network route can contain subtabs Explore, Messages, Communities and My Profile; keep the main farming navigation unchanged. Messages are a product utility, not a duplicate full-size top-level farming analysis feature.
4. Future chat interface contract
The eventual chat experience should use a familiar three-pane layout on desktop, adapting to one-pane/two-pane mobile navigation:
Desktop (minimum 1200 px)
- left conversation column: approximately 280–320 px, with heading Messages, compose/new-message button, search, filter (All, Unread, Farmers, Communities), conversation rows and unread badges;
- central conversation canvas: flexible; header with profile avatar, display name, relationship/contact status, shared context if any, and menu; scrollable message history; sticky composer at bottom;
- optional right context panel (280 px or collapsible): shared farm cards, selected public farm info, relevant shared post/document links and pinned items. It must not expose private farm data that another user did not share.
At 1440 px, reserve about 64 px top header and use the remaining available height as the chat workspace. Conversation list and message viewport should scroll independently. Sticky composer stays visible. Messages have restrained bubbles with clear author/time, accessible reactions and attachments; don't turn every single message into a card.
Conversation list row
Avatar or initials, display name, last-message preview, timestamp, unread badge, and optional shared farm/community marker. Do not show exact farm locations in list previews. Sort by latest activity, with pinned conversations at top if this is user-controlled. Search should include people/communities and message text only within the user's permission scope. Unread status updates only after the conversation is actually viewed according to a documented read-state rule.
Conversation behaviours
- one-to-one and group conversations only when supported by the configured service;
- send plain-text messages, and safely handle supported images/documents if uploads are implemented;
- message status (Sending, Sent, Failed, Delivered/Read only if provider truly supports it);
- retry failed message without duplicating successful sends;
- edit/delete own message only when server/service supports it; show an edit marker if edited;
- reply/quote, reactions, copy, report, block, mute, search within conversation, pinned/shared items where useful;
- attachment type/size validation, previews, alt text, malware scanning/service support where appropriate, and clear unsupported-file state;
- network disconnect banner, local draft autosave, reconnect state and duplicate-send protection;
- clear empty state “No conversations yet” with a working route to discover a real published farmer only when that directory is genuinely enabled;
- block and mute must be enforced across all entry points, not just hidden from the UI;
- report/moderation pathways and community rules before enabling public messaging.
Do not invent delivery/read receipts where the provider can't guarantee them. Don't display online now based on a stale timestamp. Do not automatically send private farm data into a chat prompt. Any assistant-suggested summary of a conversation must be based on messages visible and authorized to the current user.
Composer and accessibility
Sticky composer with multiline auto-growing input, attach button when supported, send button disabled for empty/whitespace message, Enter to send and Shift+Enter for newline on desktop; expose alternative settings for keyboard/screen-reader users. Preserve draft while switching conversations. Keep control targets large enough, announce send failure and new messages accessibly, and move focus predictably after modal or conversation navigation.
5. Global Farming Network map (future shared-service mode)
Use a separate global map and its own semantics; don't conflate it with the user's private Maps page. The public map can display city-level summaries derived only from explicitly published farms. Clicking a city opens a list of eligible farms published in that city. Clicking a profile opens a permission-filtered public view. Public farms must never reveal actual coordinates, boundaries, routes, field-specific map locations or private farm notes.
City aggregation logic
- only explicitly published farms contribute;
- each metric has its own validity and comparable cohort rule;
- city aggregate must expose sample count, window, freshness and coverage (subject to privacy thresholds);
- suppress or use “insufficient data” when the sample is too small or the values are incomparable;
- do not assume low moisture is always poor or high vegetation index is always healthy; interpret by crop, stage, local climate and metric definition;
- show distribution/variation, not only a single average where possible;
- only rebuild aggregates after source snapshots/publication permissions pass validation;
- preserve the previous valid aggregate and timestamp if refresh fails;
- no farms published in a city means “No farms published to Field Shift here”, not “No farming exists.”
A private map outline uses private farm/field status; city public-layer shading uses published city aggregates. The two can differ and should be visually explainable. Public city layers may use a subdued green/amber/coral wash over official city shapes or safe city markers; never approximate the private farm polygon as a public map heat source at exact location.
6. Compare My Farm with Regional Averages (future shared-service mode)
This feature sits inside Farm View/Farm Analysis and can be reached from the Network city summary. It compares relevant, comparable farm-level/field-level metrics using only permissioned published data, official environmental sources and eligible time-matched records. Possible comparisons include rainfall anomalies, crop stage-adjusted vegetation trends, soil texture class (not lab nutrient precision), crop water use/recorded irrigation if reported with equivalent units/period, and selected rotation outcomes where crop/context cohorts are comparable.

Display the user's value, regional median/range where meaningful, sample count, date window, source, normalization rule and comparability warning. Don't compare absolute yield for different crops/areas/stages without normalization and contextual controls. Use percentiles only when sample size supports them. Explicitly state when the data cannot support a fair comparison.
7. Public profile permissions and privacy model
Farm owner explicitly chooses publish/unpublish and each group of fields to share. A public profile is a distinct public projection built from an allowlist; never send private farm object JSON and hide sensitive widgets in CSS. Public fields may include farm display name, city/country, chosen description, selected crop/rotation summaries, approved indicators and owner-approved reports. Private fields include exact coordinates, drawn polygon, detailed field layout, private soil tests, farmer notes, unpublished yield, full personal watering routine and complete analysis trace unless each disclosure is explicitly supported and appropriate.

The unpublish operation must immediately remove the profile from search and future aggregate participation according to the backend's documented consistency rules. Cached pages and public map aggregates need invalidation/rebuild. Friend/block rules apply across search, maps, conversation creation and community content. Public posts are unverified unless explicit verification records exist; community anecdotes are not silently injected into deterministic agronomy logic.
8. Future backend model (design only until enabled)
Document distinct logical entities for accounts/profile, private farm, field, geometry, publish-settings, public projection, relationship/friend request, block/mute, conversation, participants, messages, message attachments, community, memberships, posts/comments/reactions, reports/moderation actions, regional metric snapshots, city aggregate versions, notifications, refresh jobs, and audit events. Apply least privilege, owner checks, permission tests, rate limits, report/abuse paths and secret management.

Keep chat payloads and private farm data separated. Never include API keys in browser code, public profiles, trace logs, exports or AI prompts. Cross-user collaboration, global publishing and city aggregation are backend-dependent and must remain inactive in the local-only build. The master should be ready for later work, not pretend current local browser records are synchronized across users.
9. Network acceptance criteria
1. In local-only mode, no fake profiles/messages appear and the unavailable reason is clear.
2. Private Maps and Farm View remain fully usable when Network is unavailable.
3. No header icon claims unread messages when no message store is connected.
4. In future shared-service mode, public profile response schema is tested to exclude private geometry and exact coordinates at API level.
5. Publishing/unpublishing, block, mute, read state, failed send and permission errors each have explicit tests.
6. Map clicks expose only city-level public locations; search never reveals private/unpublished farms.
7. City aggregates expose freshness, sample size and insufficient-data states; an API failure cannot silently refresh or zero-out the score.
8. All controls, conversation composer and responsive layouts are keyboard accessible and usable on phones.
10. Further detailed reference
The full 11,500-word Global Farming Network specification is reproduced as an annex later in this master document. It contains expanded publication, city scoring, searching, regional diagnosis, environmental history, early warnings, friend management, communities, case studies, collaborative experiments, notifications, privacy, failure scenarios, data model, examples and acceptance metrics. Read it in full. The current local-only restriction in this section takes precedence over any old statement that assumes the social layer is already cloud-backed.
ADDENDUM: Scientific methods, APIs and provenance
V2 SCIENTIFIC AND DATA SOURCE CONTRACT — API REGISTER, EQUATIONS, QUALITY RULES, AND EVIDENCE
Verification date for service-status notes: 25 October 2026. APIs, model availability, prices, license terms, provider endpoints and authentication rules can change. The builder must re-check official documentation during implementation and record the verification date, rather than assuming the URL or service is permanently available.
1. General principle: use source evidence, not a brand name
The app may be branded as using NASA/remote sensing, but each displayed value must come from a documented source and valid transformation. A working HTTPS endpoint is not automatically a scientifically suitable input. Each source adapter must expose a typed record containing:

provider, dataset/product, variable, value, unit, observation_start, observation_end, retrieved_at, spatial_resolution_or_support, temporal_resolution, method_or_processing_level, quality_flags, missing_reason, source_url/citation, cache_age, and an evidence_class such as measured, remote-sensing observation, reanalysis, forecast, mapped estimate, farmer-reported, model-calculated, or unverified demo.

Keep raw/provider response (where license and privacy allow), normalized values, and derived calculations separate. Never change the semantic meaning of a source during conversion. Units must be normalized in one shared conversion module with tests and retained source units for provenance. If a source can't be retrieved, the displayed result is missing/stale—not replaced with invented plausible data. The app must distinguish “retrieved just now” from “measured just now.”
2. API and dataset register
2.1 NASA POWER — weather/climate context
Official docs: https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/ and https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/tutorials/api-getting-started/.

Use as the first broadly available global source for meteorological/solar series relevant to crop planning. Select variables by documented availability and coordinate/time support, using daily or monthly products appropriate to the model. Preserve source's time scale and grid/support. Do not imply a point result is a sensor measurement from the exact field. POWER is not, by itself, a weather forecast. Use it for historical/currently available meteorological context, climate normals and long-term summaries where the returned period permits. Check API variable names and supported temporal aggregates from current documentation.

Do not show a “30-year mean” unless you actually fetched and validated the complete stated period and documented coverage. Derive actual_start_date, actual_end_date, valid_sample_count and missing_sample_count; display actual available years.
2.2 Open-Meteo — forecast and historical weather
Official docs: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs and https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api; terms: https://open-meteo.com/en/terms.

Use Open-Meteo forecast API for an actual forecast, and historical weather/reanalysis endpoints for their documented retrospective purposes. Keep forecast, prior forecast archive and reanalysis as distinct evidence classes; never relabel a historical product as a live forecast. Record model/provider, issue/retrieval time, requested range and valid range. Distinguish forecasts that have not yet verified from observations/reanalysis. Handle forecasts that update during the day and the age of cached results.

The free API terms state that free use is for non-commercial applications with usage limits and CC-BY 4.0 attribution conditions. This project is described as non-commercial/hackathon/personal, but the builder must re-check current licensing and usage caps. Use caching and debounce requests. If future commercial use is proposed, re-evaluate eligibility and paid plans before continuing with free access.
2.3 Copernicus Sentinel-2 — imagery-derived vegetation indicators
Official collection: https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/data-collections/copernicus-sentinel-missions/sentinel-2. Sentinel Hub Statistical API docs: https://documentation.dataspace.copernicus.eu/APIs/SentinelHub/Statistical.html.

Sentinel-2 multispectral bands have different spatial resolutions; red and near-infrared inputs needed for conventional NDVI are in 10 m bands for Sentinel-2. Do not imply all bands/products have the same spatial resolution. A polygon statistic needs suitable imagery, an authenticated provider/project as applicable, polygon geometry, time interval, cloud/no-data mask, processing options, and valid pixel count. The first live analytical integration should use a proven provider API or an appropriate hosted processing service; downloading full scene stacks and processing them in the browser is not a sensible default for this webapp.

NDVI definition:

NDVI = (NIR - Red) / (NIR + Red) where the denominator is non-zero and both bands are valid and comparable reflectance values. Apply appropriate scale/offset handling and masks according to the product documentation. Store number of valid pixels, cloud/no-data flags, observation date and processing recipe. Don't calculate NDVI from rainfall, weather, soil-moisture percentiles or a placeholder. If real red/NIR imagery and processing are unavailable, NDVI is unavailable. Do not draw fake “satellite vegetation” heatmap.

NDVI change:

ΔNDVI = NDVI_latest - NDVI_previous

Only compare dates/periods where spatial extent, crop/field geometry and processing are comparable and quality flags pass. When sufficient seasonal history exists, compare to a crop- and season-appropriate baseline. Vegetation change is an investigation trigger, not proof of disease, nutrient deficiency, drought or yield. Explain alternative causes and combine with weather/crop-stage evidence before stronger interpretation.
2.4 NASA GIBS / NASA Worldview — visualization and layers
NASA GIBS documentation: https://nasa-gibs.github.io/gibs-api-docs/access-basics/; Worldview: https://worldview.earthdata.nasa.gov/.

Use GIBS/Worldview-capable products for visible imagery layers and temporal map exploration when the selected dataset and required layer are supported. It is a map visualization/data product service; a pretty overlay alone does not create precise plot-level analysis. Record product ID, date, projection, resolution and attribution. Separate raster display from analytical polygon statistics. A coarse/global layer can supply context but must not be presented as a field laboratory measurement.
2.5 NASA/USDA Global Soil Moisture — coarse context only
Dataset info: https://earth.gsfc.nasa.gov/hydro/data/nasa-usda-global-soil-moisture-data. The described product has 0.25° × 0.25° spatial grid and integrates SMAP/SMOS-based information with model inputs. Use as regional-scale moisture context where API/usage permits; do not assign it a field-root-zone exact value. It is not a substitute for a soil probe, lab test, or validated field-scale balance. Label depth/product/support and exact source date.
2.6 SoilGrids — paused REST API, do not make mandatory
Current official status: https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids and https://docs.isric.org/globaldata/soilgrids/index.html.

As checked on 25 October 2026, ISRIC says the SoilGrids REST API is temporarily paused owing to a service issue and gives no restoration timeline. Its REST API is beta and carries a fair-use note of 5 API calls per minute. Do not build a core flow that requires this endpoint. Options: use supported SoilGrids WMS/WebDAV/download products after checking access and applicable license; alternatively support farmer-entered/manual lab values, another suitable maintained source, or an unavailable state. A WMS display may support visual context but does not necessarily give a reliable numeric soil profile for a crop model. For U.S. farms, consider official SSURGO/Soil Data Access, noting geographic coverage. Do not claim global coverage if source has limited geography.
2.7 Map basemap, geometry and geocoding
Use MapLibre GL JS with a supported satellite/vector raster style and a maintained drawing tool such as Terra Draw: https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/examples/draw-geometries-with-terra-draw/ and https://makewithmaplibre.com/plugins/terra-draw/. Use Turf area documentation https://turfjs.org/docs/api/area for polygon area. Do not use Google's deprecated Maps JavaScript Drawing Library; Google documents its unavailability beginning in May 2026: https://developers.google.com/maps/deprecations.

Configure map providers and tokens via settings/server config as appropriate. Respect provider attribution, terms, tile limits, CORS, token exposure rules and caching restrictions. Don't use one provider's tiles outside permitted use. No global CSS hue/invert filter; satellite images remain natural. Public Nominatim policy: https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/ — the public endpoint is not an unrestricted high-volume autocomplete service; use a compliant, rate-limited geocoder provider or configurable service.
2.8 Crop reference and agronomy parameter sources
Use authoritative/reference material where possible, including FAO crop-water guidance, regional agronomic research, university extension publications, national agriculture departments, and peer-reviewed sources. A crop parameter must include source URL/title, publication/version date, geography/climate applicability, crop species/variety where known, growth-stage applicability, units, evidence basis and uncertainty. A generic source for “beans” must not be blindly applied to every legume species or local variety.

FAO-56 reference: https://www.fao.org/4/X0490E/X0490E00.htm. Crop water estimate where required inputs are valid:

ETc = Kc × ET0

where ET0 is reference evapotranspiration and Kc is the crop coefficient appropriate to crop and growth stage. If ET0, Kc or stage are unsupported, do not output a precise crop water requirement. If using an alternative simplified method, clearly name it, state assumptions and lower confidence. Do not mix methods across rotations without disclosure.
3. Scientific calculation contract
3.1 Data normalization
Every source adapter validates HTTP/network status, response schema, units, finite values, dates, range and quality flag before normalizing. Time series need timezone rules and clear daily boundary conventions. Reject values that fail domain constraints; preserve source response or a redacted error record where appropriate. Never translate missing value into zero unless provider definition explicitly means zero.
3.2 Daily water-balance model
Use an explicit root-zone water-balance model only when suitable inputs are available. Baseline concepts:
- ET0: reference evapotranspiration from a documented source/formula;
- ETc = Kc × ET0 where applicable;
- P_eff: effective precipitation after a documented runoff/drainage handling assumption;
- I_eff: effective irrigation = applied irrigation × application efficiency if recorded/estimated from a documented parameter;
- S_t: root-zone water storage at time t;
- S_(t+1) = clamp(S_t + P_eff + I_eff - ETc - drainage_or_runoff_losses, S_min, S_max) only when each term's interpretation is consistently defined;
- S_max depends on root-zone depth and the applicable soil available-water capacity, not a made-up default;
- water deficit/stress is estimated relative to supported crop-specific readily available water/thresholds and crop stage.
This is a model, not a direct sensor reading. Record initial water storage assumption, soil available water, effective rooting depth, crop stage/Kc source, rainfall/runoff assumptions, irrigation amount/frequency/efficiency, and missing values. If important inputs are missing, display a limited qualitative result or ask the farmer for input; don't invent a perfect soil bucket. Do not claim actual irrigation requirement if the app only knows historical rainfall and generic soil type.
3.3 Soil dynamics over rotation
Any multi-season effect on nitrogen, organic matter, water holding, erosion exposure, disease/pest pressure or soil condition is a simulation/index, not a measured current soil property. Keep the component states separate. Each parameter needs an initial condition (measured or explicitly estimated), state-transition method, crop coefficients/rotation effect, and uncertainty. Don't fabricate direct nutrient values or precise tonnes of organic carbon change without an accepted model and adequate inputs. Explain the model horizon and sensitivity.
3.4 Crop suitability
Evaluate each crop against documented crop-specific parameters and actual/estimated field conditions. Use hard constraints for infeasibility, such as impossible season length, crop-temperature thresholds, incompatible soil conditions or unavailable irrigation when essential. Use scored preferences only after feasibility. A high score must not override a critical agronomic veto. Missing key factor lowers recommendation confidence/data coverage; it is never awarded an ideal score by default.

Where a weighted geometric mean is used, implement a documented transformation to avoid ln(0) and choose a defensible lower-bound/constraint rule; explicitly distinguish zero meaning “impossible/infeasible” from zero meaning “lowest preference.” If a hard constraint fails, mark infeasible rather than burying it in the arithmetic mean.
3.5 Rotation sequence scoring
Use one shared deterministic engine for candidate cards, ranking, comparison, risk questions, custom rotation builder, calendar and What-If. Candidate evaluation has two stages:
1. feasibility/constraint checker (valid crop duration/season windows, sequence and crop-family rules where supported, resource conflicts, multi-season constraints, missing critical parameters);
2. objective score for feasible candidates according to normalized farmer priorities and configured weights.
Priority weights must use a documented normalization. If raw weights are w_i ≥ 0, use W_i = w_i / Σw_i when sum is positive; if all weights are zero, ask the user or apply a clearly labelled balanced default. Show the contribution of each criterion and score interpretation. The default rank ordering is highest score first, since “higher score means better fit” in prior project decisions. Do not use “ascending order” where top result would appear last. If differences are smaller than score precision or model uncertainty, call them “similar” and do not imply a decisive winner.

Do not mix incompatible dimensions without transparent normalization: water demand, soil projection, suitability, climate risk and resilience need defined transforms and supported parameter ranges. Version the scoring weights and crop data. Preserve snapshot of original inputs, candidate scores, exclusions and reason codes for replay.
3.6 Risk tolerance / adaptive trade-off questions
After initial calculations but before presenting final recommendations, compare feasible candidates to identify material trade-offs that are actually present. Generate only questions grounded in candidate data; e.g. “Rotation A may better satisfy the productivity preference but has higher modeled insect-pressure risk than Rotation B. Would you accept this trade-off?” The specific risk, estimate/source, uncertainty and mitigation must be shown before asking. Do not manipulate by framing acceptance as morally or financially superior.

User choices affect risk filters/preferences; they cannot override hard constraints or turn unknown parameters into known data. If rejected, rerank/filter within accepted risk preferences and return the best feasible balanced alternative. Save the user's risk choice with that analysis run. If no valid alternative meets constraints, say so and explain which constraint blocks it.
3.7 Rotation duration and actual dates
Use actual date windows when known/selected: Oct 2026–Mar 2027 · Wheat. “Year 1” can group multiple season windows only for a multi-year plan; never show “Year 2026” beside “Winter” as if mutually equivalent. Ask crop history as crop + approximate planting month/year and harvest month/year rather than fixed Winter/Spring/Summer/Autumn slots. Crop duration depends on crop type, variety, region, temperature/growing-degree-day context, planting method and documented growth stages; perennial/multi-year crops cannot be treated as one annual slot. If data support only approximate dates, display an estimate with source and ranges.
3.8 Growing degree days (only where crop/stage thresholds exist)
Common form: GDD_day = max(0, ((Tmax + Tmin)/2) - T_base) under the selected reference method, with documented treatment for temperature caps and invalid data. Accumulate only across valid dates and use crop-specific base temperature where supported. Do not calculate GDD from a single annual mean or apply one base temperature to all crops.
3.9 Status logic
One shared status evaluator drives Home, farm polygon outline, field list, field page and alerts. At least statuses Good, Needs Attention, Alert, and Insufficient data. The threshold table must state metric, crop/stage/context, source and timeframe; no status based on unsupported universal thresholds. Priority rules should be explicit: an active validated high-severity alert makes the field at least Alert; lower-level evidence may produce Needs Attention; if all key indicators are unavailable, Insufficient data, never Good. For multi-field farms, overall farm status uses the worst known field status while also showing coverage; unknown fields cannot be silently concealed by another field's Good reading. The status card must show the primary reason and evidence link, not only a color.
3.10 Confidence and data coverage — separate concepts
Recommendation Confidence describes robustness of rotation ranking/model inference under known uncertainty and comparison of feasible candidates. It is not a claim of absolute truth or harvest success. Define a method (e.g. rank stability under supported perturbations/ensemble/scenario ranges, model-domain fit, separation between top candidates) and expose its components.

Data Coverage describes input completeness, freshness, spatial relevance/resolution, and provenance. Use real available-input inventories and quality checks; do not hardcode 0.92 or another nice-looking completeness number. Both badges use distinct icons/shapes/accent palettes and labels. They may disagree. If no defensible confidence method exists yet, show “Not assessed” rather than a fabricated high/medium/low status.
3.11 Temporal comparison and historical alerts
Only calculate change on comparable time ranges and sources. Report the baseline period, valid sample count and data gaps. One measurement should not trigger a trend alert unless it crosses a genuine documented hard threshold or a carefully justified signal detector. For change detection, require suitable baseline sample size and use robust summaries when outliers/missing data exist. Preserve older snapshots and record model/source version; if calculation definitions change, mark comparisons non-identical and offer recalculation as new runs rather than silently rewriting history.
4. Gemini/AI integration contract
The project source currently uses @google/genai server routes /api/ai-description and /api/ai-chat. The rebuild may preserve Gemini as one provider but must verify the currently supported model name against the official model list: https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models. Use current SDK and documented Structured Outputs/Function Calling where appropriate:
- https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/structured-output
- https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/function-calling
If an API key is needed, store it only in server-side environment/secret configuration; never ship it to browser bundles or write it to localStorage, screenshots, logs, export files or prompts. Validate payload shape before sending. Remove fake defaults such as Salina/Kansas, silt loam, 820 mm rainfall, a 55th-percentile moisture value or “Rotation 1” if those values were never provided/retrieved. Missing context must be represented as missing, then asked from the user or described as a limitation.

The AI is an explanation/interface layer, not the canonical numeric engine. It may explain known calculations, interpret farmer-entered natural language into a typed candidate structure, suggest follow-up questions and investigate evidence. Structured parsing workflow: show parsed interpretation and units → ask user to confirm/edit → then save approved values. Never silently convert “I water a bit each morning” into a precise 3 L/m² schedule without asking missing details.

If Gemini is unavailable, use deterministic template explanations based on actual structured score contributions. Report that AI prose is unavailable when relevant; never fabricate that a search/source was checked. The AI must cite data references already collected by the app for factual claims; it must not make up live weather, source retrieval or calculation results.
5. Source confidence, fallback and status semantics
Create a provider registry and feature/variable support matrix. For each feature, define primary source, acceptable fallback, geographic coverage, spatial/temporal resolution, auth requirement, cache TTL, unit normalization, license/attribution, failure states, and what the UI displays when unavailable. Use fallback only if it measures the same intended concept sufficiently well. A mapped soil class is not an equivalent fallback for lab N-P-K; historical reanalysis is not equivalent to a forecast; global moisture grid is not equivalent to field root-zone moisture; a weather threshold is not equivalent to NDVI.

Use statuses such as Live response, Latest available observation, Cached (age shown), Estimated, Farmer reported, Calculated, Unavailable, Failed. “Verified” must refer to a named verification method and source, not be used as a decorative badge. If one data source fails, the pipeline may continue if independent remaining calculations are valid; mark the run Partial if the missing source materially affects conclusions, Failed if required core calculation cannot run, and Cancelled when user cancels. Retry should be bounded with exponential backoff/jitter and should not produce duplicate saved records.
6. Data and scientific security
