1. Executive summary and product goals
1.1 Product vision
Home, Maps, and Farm Analysis should operate as three connected views of the same farm record. Home gives a concise daily-status view. Maps provides geographic orientation, private farm outlines, public city-level agricultural context, and a navigation route into a farm. Farm Analysis is the detailed, evolving record of a farm's planned rotation, current/recorded crop progress, measurable conditions, trends, issues, actions, and history. Deep Analysis remains a separate action within Farm Analysis; it is not replaced by the normal overview. Its log list has its own secondary-navigation page, and opening an individual log displays a detailed popup/modal with a top-right close (X) control, as already specified by the project owner.

The experience should be minimal at the surface and deep on demand. The top-level cards answer “How is the farm doing?” and “What needs attention?” Detailed scientific values, provenance, calculation logic, field activity and historic trends are available through sections and expandable detail panels.
1.2 Main questions answered by each page
Home answers: Which farm is selected? What is its latest supported status? What is the weather outlook? Is something important worth my attention? Where can I open the map or farm detail?

Maps answers: Where are my saved farms? What is their drawn overall footprint? How do my farms compare visually with published-farm conditions in their cities? How do I locate a place, inspect a private farm, or navigate to its analysis?

Farm Analysis answers: What rotation was recommended and what is actually planned or active? What crop/rotation stage is recorded? What do current/latest available weather, water, soil, vegetation and crop indicators say? What has changed? What is missing or uncertain? What risks and actions require attention? What records and prior assessments support the current picture?
1.3 Guiding principles
- One farm identity across the site; use a stable internal farm ID, not the farm name, as the relationship key.
- One consistent source of truth for farm settings, selected rotation, data snapshots and history.
- Recommendations, plans, active crops and observed outcomes are different concepts and must be labelled differently.
- A retrieved API value is not necessarily a measurement from the time it was fetched.
- Modelled and gridded data must not be presented as precise field sensor readings.
- Missing or stale information is not evidence that conditions are good.
- Individual indicators remain visible; do not hide a critical risk behind an average or a single opaque health score.
- The public Global Farming Network map displays city-level farm context, not exact published farm markers or public farm boundaries. The owner’s own drawn farm outlines can be displayed privately to that owner.
- Home remains concise; full logs and the calendar remain in their existing separate places.
- Existing working features and calculations must be audited and preserved before redesign or replacement.
2. Confirmed requirements from the project owner
2.1 Home
- Home is a minimalist, brief tracking page.
- It shows whether the selected farm appears to be doing well or poorly, with a background colour/gradient that reflects the assessed status.
- It includes a professional weather card.
- A card containing an Earth map preview opens the full Maps page when clicked.
- The selected farm controls the status, weather and short indicator content shown.
2.2 Maps
- Maps opens as a full-screen map page.
- There is a search bar near the top-left for a place such as a city or country.
- There is a left sidebar listing the user's farms that have been added to Maps.
- Clicking an own-farm sidebar item moves/zooms the map to its saved location.
- The map supports satellite imagery.
- A drawing tool lets the user place node points connected by straight line segments and close them into a farm boundary.
- The farm boundary is for the overall farm footprint. The farmer does not need to draw internal field boundaries, even if the farm has multiple fields.
- The drawing is attached to an existing farm name originating from a farm created through New Analysis. The user selects from the already-created farm names; the system must not create a disconnected duplicate farm just to name the drawing.
- The user's own farm outline colour represents that farm's own status.
- City background colours represent the aggregated status of eligible publicly published farms in that city only. This follows the Global Farming Network specification. The city and an individual farm can legitimately have different colours.
- Clicking the user's own farm boundary opens its Farm Analysis page. A minimised map appears at the bottom-left of the Analysis page; clicking the map returns to Maps.
- Farm name, drawing, re-analysis and deletion actions must behave consistently across pages.
2.3 Farm Analysis
- Analysis displays available information about the selected farm, including the complete crop rotation, crop durations, the currently growing crop where that can be established, and days remaining only where dates and status support that calculation.
- It shows weather, temperature, humidity, precipitation, soil moisture, soil health, pH, risks, alerts, solutions and “keep an eye on” items as relevant.
- Risk/alert/solution/watch items should appear when appropriate; when no priority issue is detected in the available sufficiently reliable data, display an explicit state such as “No priority issues detected” rather than empty or misleading content.
- Deep Analysis is a button on the Analysis page. It has a detailed flow and separate log-history navigation defined by the existing Deep Analysis specification. Each selected log opens in a detailed popup with an X at the top right to close it.
- A secondary navigation item called Rotation navigates to the existing Rotations page; it should not be rebuilt as a duplicate page inside Farm Analysis.
- A settings option supports rename, re-analysis and deletion. Rename updates the farm name everywhere. Re-analysis allows the farmer to re-enter/change inputs, priorities or location and generates a new best-fit rotation as information/time change. There is also an option to edit or remove the farm drawing for convenience.
- The project already has a separate Calendar page in the main navigation. Farm Analysis can show crop milestones and link to Calendar, but must not build a duplicate full calendar system.
3. One connected farm identity and system architecture
3.1 Stable farm ID
Every farm created through New Analysis should have a stable farm_id independent of its editable display name. All linked objects—map geometry, analysis runs, saved rotations, farmer activity records, tasks, settings, scenario references and deep-analysis logs—refer to that ID. A rename changes the display name, not the identity.

Example: farm_id = F-1042 is displayed as “North Field”. The user renames it “North Farm”. Home, Maps, Analysis, rotation records and history resolve the new label automatically; historical records still show the name recorded at the time if useful, while the current farm link still points to F-1042.
3.2 Distinct but connected records
Keep these concepts separate:
- Farm profile: stable ID, name, owner, analysis-location inputs, privacy/publication status and general configuration.
- Map geometry: optional polygon drawn by the owner for the overall farm footprint, geometry version, save time and visibility. Geometry is not a surveyed legal boundary unless the user separately establishes that.
- Analysis run: the inputs, retrieved data snapshot/references, calculations, rotation candidates, priority settings, data versions and results for one New Analysis run.
- Rotation plan: a chosen candidate rotation and its crop sequence/durations, status (recommended, planned, active, completed, archived), dates and farmer confirmation.
- Observation/indicator record: a value, unit, field/farm scope, method/source, measured/valid time, retrieval time, quality flag and uncertainty where available.
- Farm activity record: farmer-reported event such as planting, irrigation, harvest, fertiliser application, pest observation or field inspection.
- Deep Analysis run: the detailed evaluation and audit trace; keep aligned with the existing Deep Analysis spec.
- Task/action: recommendation or planned action, linked finding, status, due date and outcome note.
- Scenario record: hypothetical values from What-If Simulator, separate from real farm facts.
- Public city aggregate: aggregated data derived only from eligible published farms; never a store of public exact-farm markers.
3.3 Source-of-truth rules
The farm profile owns the current name and shared settings. A New Analysis run owns the snapshot of its inputs and result at that time. A rotation plan owns the farmer's chosen/active status and actual dates. A sensor/API observation owns the source value and timestamps. A scenario owns hypothetical inputs. Home displays a derived summary of these records; it must not independently invent a different health assessment.
3.4 Update propagation
- Rename: updates the current farm display name everywhere without breaking references.
- New Analysis re-run: creates a new run and updates the “latest recommendation” pointer; it does not erase old runs.
- Rotation adoption: creates or updates the chosen plan status with user confirmation; a recommendation alone does not prove planting occurred.
- Geometry edit: creates a new geometry version; it does not change soil/crop data or historical analysis values by itself.
- Drawing removal: removes the geometry from Maps while preserving the farm profile/history.
- Farm deletion: a separate destructive workflow that handles linked records, scheduled jobs, privacy/publication and retention rules; never behave like map-only removal.
- What-If result: remains hypothetical until the user explicitly adopts or records a real-world change.
4. Main navigation and page-to-page behaviour
4.1 Main navigation
Home, Maps, Analysis/farm selection and the existing Calendar are part of the main product navigation according to the current design. The exact existing navigation architecture should be inspected rather than replaced blindly. Calendar remains a separate main-nav page.
4.2 Page flow
1. Home opens with the last-selected farm or a sensible farm-selection state.
2. Clicking the map preview opens full-screen Maps while preserving the user's selected farm and the map camera if the user returns later.
3. In Maps, clicking an own-farm sidebar row centres/fits the map to that saved farm geometry/location. A separate explicit action or click on its drawn boundary opens Farm Analysis.
4. Analysis opens for that stable farm ID. The bottom-left minimised map preview remains visible where layout allows. Clicking it returns to Maps and restores the previous camera/selection state.
5. Rotation navigation opens the existing Rotations page and preserves the farm context where supported.
6. Deep Analysis is a button inside Farm Analysis. Its logs are accessed through the separate secondary-nav page as described in the existing Deep Analysis specification. Selecting an individual log opens a detailed popup/modal with a visible top-right X close control. Closing the popup returns to the log list without losing the selected page or scroll context.
7. Calendar is opened from the main navigation; Farm Analysis can link to a relevant crop/harvest/calendar entry but must not duplicate the calendar's complete event management.
4.3 Back-navigation and preserved state
Use browser/back navigation and in-app controls consistently. Preserve selected farm ID, map centre/zoom, map layer toggles, search term when useful, selected analysis tab, and log list position where technically appropriate. Do not preserve sensitive values in shareable public URLs unless intentionally allowed. If a farm has been deleted or the user no longer has access, redirect safely with an explanation.
5. Home page: minimalist farm tracking
5.1 Purpose and layout
Home is a quick daily-status page, not a duplicate of Analysis or Deep Analysis. Recommended content order:
1. Selected-farm switcher/name.
2. Farm-status/health summary with a subtle, meaningful background gradient.
3. Professional weather card.
4. A compact row/grid of the most useful supported indicators or changes.
5. Map preview card for quick access to full-screen Maps.
6. Small “What needs attention?” area for actionable alerts or explicit all-clear/limited-data state.
7. Optional recent-analysis/update line linking into Analysis/Deep Analysis without displaying full logs on Home.
Avoid a wall of metrics. The first screen should make the current context understandable at a glance. Additional information is reached by clicking into Analysis.
5.2 Selected-farm switcher
The user selects a farm by its current name. Changing the selected farm refreshes all Home cards together: health, weather/location context, key indicators, alerts and map preview. Avoid a mixed state where health belongs to Farm A while weather belongs to Farm B. Show a clear “No farm selected” or “Create your first farm via New Analysis” state if no eligible farm exists.
5.3 Farm-status card and background gradient
The status card communicates a concise assessment, not a scientific fact independent of the underlying data. It can use a green/amber/red or neutral visual scheme, but colours must be driven by defined assessment rules. Example states:
- Stable / generally favourable: no high-priority concern found by the supported checks, data quality is adequate for the displayed claims, and indicators are within context-appropriate limits.
- Attention needed: at least one meaningful risk or adverse trend merits review.
- Urgent concern: a defined high-priority rule is crossed, with sufficient evidence to justify urgency.
- Limited data: critical indicators are missing, stale, contradictory or not applicable; do not colour this state green.
- No assessment yet: there is no completed analysis or no useful data.
The gradient should be subtle and readable, not an unexplained traffic-light decoration. Display a text label, the main reason, a data-freshness indication and a “View farm analysis” action. The exact threshold and weighting rules must be documented; health status cannot simply reuse the New Analysis rotation suitability score because those scores answer different questions.
5.4 Weather card
The weather card belongs to the selected farm's analysis location (not necessarily the phone/browser location). Display the available current/latest conditions and forecast clearly separated, with:
- Temperature and daily minimum/maximum where available.
- “Feels like” only if the provider supplies or the system calculates it using a valid method.
- Relative humidity.
- Precipitation amount/forecast and probability only when supplied by the selected forecast product; do not confuse rainfall probability with expected rainfall amount.
- Wind speed and gusts where available; direction if supported and useful.
- Solar/radiation/UV only if the product supports it and it is relevant.
- Short forecast summary or next-24-hour view if available.
- Valid/measurement time, forecast period, source, last retrieved time and stale-data label.
Use exact units, local timezone, and an accessible explanation. If the provider's observation is from an earlier time, label it “latest available observation” instead of “live now.” Do not imply weather data directly measures soil moisture. If forecast lookup fails, retain prior data with a visible timestamp or show a safe unavailable state.
5.5 Key indicator cards
Show only a small number of high-value facts relevant to the farm and data available—for example latest soil-moisture estimate/measurement, vegetation trend, a soil-related indicator, current crop/rotation stage if confirmed, or a meaningful change since the last run. Each mini-card should include value/status, unit when applicable, last valid time, source category, a small trend (only if a comparable series exists), and a link to the corresponding detail in Analysis. Do not show a number without explaining whether it is measured, estimated, modelled or farmer-reported.
5.6 Map preview card
Show a useful satellite or map preview and the selected farm outline if one exists. City-level regional health colouring can be included where the layer is available, but avoid overcrowding the small preview. Clicking the card opens full-screen Maps. It should not make the map preview itself a public sharing surface or reveal exact private location to other users. If no drawing exists, show an inviting “Open Maps to add this farm's outline” state.
5.7 “What needs attention?”
Surface only relevant actionable items: a new priority risk, a notable adverse indicator change, an overdue task the user created, a failed/partial recent analysis that materially limits interpretation, or a needed review based on a real record. Each alert links to the supporting Analysis section or Deep Analysis. If nothing meets the rules, say “No priority issues detected in the available data” and provide the freshness context. Do not claim “everything is perfectly healthy” merely because no alert fired.
5.8 Home loading/error/empty states
- Loading: skeletons/layout placeholders; do not flash a green default status.
- Partial data: show available cards plus a limited-data notice.
- Failed weather provider: show last valid observation and age, or an unavailable state.
- No farm: explain the entry path through New Analysis.
- Farm without map geometry: show map preview without fabricating an outline.
- No recent trend points: show current supported indicator without a trend line.
6. Maps page: satellite map, drawing, search and navigation
6.1 Purpose and default map
Maps is a full-screen interactive geographic workspace. The intended baseline is satellite imagery, with controls that permit navigation and a separate overlay for user farms and eligible city-level public health. A labels/roads overlay can be optional if it helps orient the user. Imagery provider, attribution, caching and terms must be reviewed before implementation. A map library such as MapLibre GL JS can render interactive base maps, raster imagery, GeoJSON polygon layers and map interactions; it still needs a suitable imagery/tile source. See the official MapLibre documentation in the references.
6.2 Main layout
- Top-left search: place/country/city geocoding/search, plus farm-name search as a separate result type.
- Left farm sidebar: user's farms with saved map geometry/location and health state. Provide search/filter if the list grows.
- Map canvas: satellite basemap, user-owned farm polygon(s), optionally visible city-health layer and relevant boundaries/labels.
- Map controls: zoom, reset/fit, layer visibility, drawing tool, cancel/undo/finish controls during a drawing, and help/legend.
- Selected-farm context: a compact info panel or popup showing the selected farm name, its owner-only health status, and the action to open Farm Analysis.
6.3 Search behaviour
Search supports a city/country/place and farm names. Distinguish result types visibly. A place result moves the camera to the place and should not automatically open a farm. A private farm-name result selects/fits that user's geometry. Results must not expose private coordinates or exact boundaries to other users. Handle no results, ambiguous place names and geocoder errors with useful choices rather than arbitrary selection.
6.4 Drawing an overall farm footprint
The drawing feature creates a closed polygon from nodes and straight line segments. Required flow:
1. User opens Draw.
2. User clicks to place successive boundary vertices; connecting line segments follow the clicked points.
3. The interface previews the current polygon as the user continues.
4. User finishes/closes the polygon explicitly (or by clicking the starting node if that interaction is supported).
5. Validate that enough points exist and that the geometry is valid (not self-intersecting or otherwise invalid for storage/area calculations).
6. Prompt the user to link the drawing to an existing farm created through New Analysis and select its current name.
7. Show the farm name and a “Save boundary” confirmation.
8. Persist polygon geometry, coordinate reference and geometry version against farm_id.
This is an overall farm footprint. If the farm contains multiple internal fields, do not require the user to draw each field. Do not imply every field has been individually mapped or every point inside has the same crop or soil properties. The polygon is a user-drawn visual outline, not a land-title/legal survey unless separately verified.
6.5 Drawing editor controls
Include cancel, undo last vertex, finish, delete unfinished shape, edit existing polygon, move/reshape vertices and save/cancel edits. Display a clear confirmation before deleting a saved drawing. Warn on invalid polygon geometry and show how to fix it. Save changes as a new geometry version or retain a simple edit history if suitable for the product. If a user starts a drawing but leaves the page, offer to discard or resume only if draft persistence is implemented safely.
6.6 Linking to farm names and preventing duplicates
The dropdown should list farms already created through New Analysis—those with stable farm IDs and current names. Selecting a name links geometry to that existing farm. It should not create another farm record or a duplicate analysis profile. If that farm already has a geometry, offer to view/edit/replace it rather than making a second unlabelled boundary. Renaming a farm updates the sidebar and boundary labels automatically.
6.7 Own-farm sidebar behaviour
Clicking a row centres/zooms the map on that farm and highlights its outline. It should not necessarily navigate away from Maps; opening its Farm Analysis should be a separate, clear action or occur when the boundary itself is clicked as described by the owner. If there is no drawing, centre on the saved analysis location only if that location is allowed for the owner and available, then offer “Draw boundary.”
6.8 Boundary click and minimised map return
Clicking the owner's saved boundary opens Farm Analysis for that farm ID. Farm Analysis includes a minimised map in the bottom-left corner. Clicking the mini-map returns to Maps, restores the previous camera position/zoom and selected farm where appropriate, and does not reset filters or lose work. If the map is unavailable, display a non-blocking placeholder and keep Analysis usable.
6.9 Layer legend and colour semantics
Clearly distinguish:
- Own farm outline/status: owner's current individual farm-health assessment.
- City fill/background: regional status aggregated from eligible publicly published farms in that city only.
- No data / too few farms / stale aggregate: neutral or hatched state with an explanation.
- Selected/highlighted farm: additional outline or selection treatment, not a different health colour.
Use text/shape cues as well as colours; do not rely on red/green alone. Provide a legend and a panel explaining how each colour is derived and the time window it covers.
6.10 Map layer performance and accessibility
Load layers progressively; avoid fetching all map data at every pan/zoom. Cache permitted map metadata with appropriate expiry. Respect imagery attribution and provider terms. Provide keyboard-accessible search/controls where possible, readable labels, high contrast, and a non-map list path to reach a farm. If the map fails to render, preserve farm selection and show a recoverable state rather than blanking the entire product.
7. Public city health layer and farm-level colour semantics
7.1 Required public-location privacy rule
The Global Farming Network public map does not show exact individual farm locations. A city with published farms can be coloured using an aggregate, and clicking a city can show the list of public farm profiles; however, the public map must not place a marker at each public farm's precise coordinates or render the owner's exact boundary to other users. The private owner map is separate.
7.2 City score population
Only eligible farms that have deliberately published the relevant data can contribute to that city's public aggregate. Private/unpublished farms must not influence the public colour. The aggregation must use a documented eligible data set, fixed time window and defined indicator normalisation. Exclude invalid/stale values according to rules; disclose sample size and coverage. Suppress or avoid showing aggregates for groups too small to protect individual privacy and prevent simple differencing attacks.
7.3 Defining the aggregate
Do not blindly average unlike raw values such as pH, moisture, vegetation and crop yield. Convert relevant indicators to defensible comparable scores/flags, account for crop/season/soil context where possible, and define weights or a categorical aggregation rule. A city health colour should be an informative summary, not an absolute diagnosis of every farm in the city. Display sample count, time window, indicator coverage and limitations in the city panel.
7.4 The user's farm outline score
The owner's farm outline colour is based on that farm's own current supported assessment. It may differ from the city's public aggregate. Examples:
- City is green, own farm amber/red: the farm may have a farm-specific concern, or its own data may show a problem.
- City is amber/red, own farm green: the farm may be performing better on the assessed metrics than the public regional aggregate.
- City is green, own farm neutral: insufficient data for the individual's farm does not become green just because the city is green.
The comparison is descriptive, not causal. Never infer why the farm differs without evidence.
7.5 City click behaviour
Clicking a coloured city should open its public regional summary and list of published farm profiles/records permitted by the network specification. Public profiles must obey each owner's sharing settings and must not reveal exact coordinates or private boundary geometry. The analysis page may offer comparisons to regional aggregates without disclosing individual hidden farms.
8. Farm Analysis: layout and information hierarchy
8.1 Purpose
Farm Analysis is the detailed dashboard for the stable farm identity. It combines the current farm/rotation context, supported measurable indicators, risk/alert/action state, history and farm-entered records. It must not duplicate Deep Analysis reports/logs or the separate Calendar page. It should make the farmer's actual knowledge and recorded operations visible alongside satellite/API estimates.
8.2 Recommended top-level layout
1. Farm header: current name, selected farm, status, location context (privacy-aware), most recent data/analysis time.
2. Secondary navigation or tabs for Overview, Rotation (routes to existing Rotations page), History/Records if implemented, and the Deep Analysis entry control according to existing architecture.
3. Overview/status card with defined farm-health status and data quality.
4. Current/planned rotation panel with crop sequence and status.
5. Weather and environmental summary.
6. Water/irrigation indicators.
7. Soil condition indicators.
8. Crop/vegetation condition indicators.
9. Current risk/alerts/solutions/watch items.
10. Recent activity and tasks/actions.
11. Trends and links to detailed history.
12. Bottom-left minimised Maps panel.
Actual UI may use tabs, cards or sections; the information and navigation requirements remain.
8.3 Progressive disclosure
Show the useful result first; keep each detail expandable. Each indicator card should open a detail view containing definition, value, units, data type, last measurement/valid time, retrieval time, source/provider, spatial/temporal resolution, method or calculation, comparison/trend, quality/uncertainty, and relevant caveats. Missing metadata should be labelled unavailable rather than filled in with guesses.
8.4 Overview status card
The overall status summary should name its strongest current evidence and most important limitation. It should show status, trend where comparable data exists, latest run/date, high-priority risk count or concise summary, and one next action. The status formula is separate from New Analysis's rotation ranking score and from a single indicator. Do not average away critical conditions.
8.5 Current condition card hierarchy
Separate domains rather than presenting a long undifferentiated stat list:
- Weather and climate.
- Water and irrigation.
- Soil chemistry/fertility.
- Soil physical condition.
- Soil biological condition (when measured).
- Crop/vegetation and crop-stage status.
- Pests/diseases/weeds (where reported/modelled responsibly).
- Terrain/land cover/erosion context.
- Management activities and outcomes.
- Regional comparison (eligible public aggregate only).
A typical farmer sees the relevant summaries; a researcher can open the full indicator catalogue and historical chart.
9. Farm identity, location, drawing and lifecycle rules
9.1 Farm name and location
Each farm record has a name and analysis location/input configuration from New Analysis. The map geometry is a separately stored outline that can be created later. The displayed name updates across Home, Maps, Farm Analysis, saved rotation records and related UI. Historical run snapshots may retain the old name at run time for audit, while links use stable farm ID and current name where sensible.
9.2 Editing location during re-analysis
Re-analysis may change the analysis location, soil/field inputs, priorities or other New Analysis answers. It should create a new run and update the latest recommendation. Location change must trigger re-fetch/recalculation of location-dependent datasets and re-evaluate crop/rotation candidates. Do not automatically shift the map outline to the new location. Ask the farmer whether the saved drawing still represents the intended farm and needs moving/redrawing, or preserve it until an explicit edit.
9.3 Multiple fields
The farm may include multiple fields in the analysis model. The overall map drawing covers the farm footprint and is not required to show internal field boundaries. Farm Analysis should distinguish field-specific data from farm-level/region-level values when fields exist. If no field polygons are recorded, do not fake precise field boundaries. Allow a field selector where the underlying data has field-level scope; otherwise label a value as farm-wide or regional.
9.4 Rename
Rename updates the profile display name globally. It must not create a new farm, clear history, reset public publication settings unexpectedly, or sever links. Audit references may keep the name-at-time snapshot but should resolve to the same stable record.
9.5 Edit/remove map drawing
Map drawing edit modifies the geometry for the selected farm and confirms the change. “Remove from Maps” removes just the drawing/map association while preserving analysis and history. If the drawing is required for an enabled public/published feature, explain which public-layer information remains or must be unpublished; public exact geometry is never exposed regardless.
9.6 Delete farm
This is a separate destructive action. Show a clear explanation of what will be deleted, archived, retained for operational/legal requirements, and how linked analysis history, private logs, tasks, publication status and schedules are handled. Ask for confirmation; do not reuse the UI control for “remove drawing.” All access paths to the deleted farm should fail safely and not show stale private data.
10. Rotation plan, current crop and crop-progress tracking
10.1 Recommendation versus reality
A New Analysis rotation is initially a recommendation. It does not prove that the farmer planted the first crop. Give rotation state labels such as Recommended, Saved candidate, Planned, Active, Completed, Replaced or Archived. The farmer must confirm adoption/planting and relevant dates before the page describes an actual crop as current.
10.2 Rotation panel contents
Show:
- Rotation name/ID and originating New Analysis run.
- Crop sequence in order.
- Duration for each crop and unit (days/months/seasons; exact semantics documented).
- Planned or confirmed start/end/transition dates, where available.
- Current stage/crop only if active and dates/status support it.
- Estimated days remaining only if the relevant start/end rules are valid; mark calculated estimates.
- Rotation overall score and breakdown only as a recommendation metric, not farm health.
- Farmer priorities and risk-tolerance choices that shaped the recommendation.
- Known rotation risks and mitigations linked to their source/assessment.
- Edits/customisations and whether the sequence has been recalculated.
10.3 Date and progress logic
If a rotation has not been marked planned/active, show crop order and duration without a “currently growing” claim. If it is planned, show scheduled dates as planned. If active, use confirmed start date and crop-stage logic where available. Days remaining can be calculated from the relevant expected end date minus current local date; explicitly state that this is a calendar estimate, not proof of biological maturity. If planting was delayed or harvest occurred, let the farmer record that and update forward estimates without erasing the original plan.
10.4 Calendar integration
The main Calendar page remains the authority for full calendar management. Farm Analysis may show “next milestone” or “planting window” summary and link to the corresponding calendar item. Avoid a second calendar editor, duplicate reminder system or competing event records.
10.5 Example
A farmer selects a rotation with crops A, B and C. The result is saved but not yet planted. Analysis says “Recommended rotation — not yet marked active.” The farmer later marks crop A planted on a confirmed date. Analysis then shows crop A as active and estimates the scheduled transition date from the rotation's duration. The timeline labels the date as an estimate and the farmer may update it if field conditions shift the schedule.
11. Complete farm indicator catalogue: weather and climate
Important: the following catalogue defines the full set of useful indicators to consider, not a promise that every API returns them for every location. Each metric should be enabled only when a source or validated calculation supplies it. The interface must display unit, scope, valid/measurement time, retrieval time, data type, source and quality notes when available.
11.1 Current/latest weather and forecast
- Air temperature: current/latest, min/max, hourly/periodic values and relevant extremes where available.
- Relative humidity.
- Precipitation/rainfall: observed totals over documented windows, forecast amount, and probability if provided (probability and amount are separate fields).
- Wind speed, gusts and direction if supported and relevant.
- Solar radiation / incoming shortwave radiation.
- Cloud cover, dew point, pressure and other variables only if the provider supports them and product use justifies them.
- Forecast period, provider, issue time, valid time and time zone.
- Heat/cold/frost alerts only under crop/location-appropriate documented rules.
Display logic: clearly distinguish observed/historical values from forecast/modelled values. A forecast is not a measurement. Do not label a delayed observation “live now.” If forecast data is unavailable, show the last valid value with age or an unavailable state.
11.2 Historical climate and seasonal context
- Daily/weekly/monthly precipitation totals where source resolution supports them.
- Temperature maxima/minima/means and unusual hot/cold periods.
- Long-term or seasonal baselines when an appropriate historical climate product is available.
- Rainfall anomalies relative to a relevant baseline.
- Drought/climate anomaly indices only when the selected product and period are appropriate.
- Growing-degree days (GDD) if required temperature data and crop-specific base/upper thresholds are defensible and recorded.
Calculation note: a basic GDD method may use an agreed daily mean temperature and crop-specific base threshold, but the implementation must explicitly document how daily maxima/minima are clipped and whether an upper cap applies. Do not use one base temperature for every crop. Show the formula and applicable crop model.
11.3 Evapotranspiration and atmospheric demand
Where the weather provider supports the necessary inputs or supplies the estimate:
- Reference evapotranspiration (ETo).
- Crop evapotranspiration (ETc) when crop and growth-stage coefficients are available.
- Cumulative ET over crop/rotation periods.
- Vapour pressure deficit or other atmospheric stress indicators only if valid inputs and a method exist.
The FAO-56 crop coefficient approach can estimate standard crop evapotranspiration using ETc = Kc × ETo (or a suitable dual coefficient method). ETo calculation requires weather data such as radiation, temperature, humidity and wind under the FAO Penman–Monteith approach; field-shift results should state the actual method and the input completeness. [R3, R4]
11.4 Weather example
The card shows latest available temperature and humidity with an observation timestamp; a separate forecast strip shows expected conditions and its validity window. If rainfall may help a crop, Analysis must still compare forecast/observed rainfall with estimated crop demand and not assume all rainfall is effective or reaches roots.
12. Complete farm indicator catalogue: water and irrigation
12.1 Soil moisture
- Satellite/gridded surface soil-moisture estimates where available.
- Root-zone soil moisture only if the selected source or validated model supplies it; do not infer it automatically from surface moisture.
- Direct sensor moisture readings if the farmer later connects sensors.
- Moisture trend/anomaly relative to comparable dates and data source.
- Moisture measurement/model depth, spatial resolution, quality flag and timestamp.
SMAP product versions differ. For example, the cited daily SMAP L3 radiometer product is gridded at 36 km; other SMAP-related products use other resolutions. The display must use metadata for the actual selected product, and cannot label a coarse regional pixel as a direct farm sensor reading. [R6]
12.2 Water supply and balance
Where inputs support it, calculate:
- Rainfall input over a documented period.
- Effective rainfall estimate, with its method and assumptions.
- Irrigation volume recorded by farmer/sensor.
- Estimated crop water requirement/demand.
- Available water supply (with source: allocation, storage/volume, well/pump report, irrigation schedule or farmer estimate).
- Estimated water deficit/surplus or balance over a period if all required terms are available.
- Water-use trend and period comparisons.
- Irrigation events and changes in delivery method/schedule.
Do not calculate a numerical deficit from rainfall alone without accounting for relevant demand, storage/soil-water context and the selected method. Distinguish crop demand from applied irrigation because rainfall, soil storage and irrigation efficiency differ.
12.3 Water-related derived indicators
- ETc/cumulative crop demand where method and crop data support it.
- Irrigation requirement estimate.
- Water productivity/output per unit of water, only when valid output and water input records exist.
- Water-use efficiency comparisons across comparable seasons/fields.
- Water risk category based on documented threshold/model, crop stage and uncertainty.
- Seasonal, field and rotation water budget when related data is available.
12.4 Water quality
If a farmer enters or uploads a suitable test, track irrigation-water EC/salinity, pH and relevant sodium/chloride/boron indicators as available. Do not infer these from rainfall, location or satellite imagery alone. Preserve test date, sampling location, lab, method and units.
12.5 Example
A rainfall product reports a recent accumulation and the crop model estimates ETc. Analysis shows both values and the time windows, then estimates whether rainfall covers a portion of demand under the implemented effective-rainfall method. If irrigation records are absent, label the balance incomplete instead of assuming zero irrigation.
13. Complete farm indicator catalogue: soil chemical properties
13.1 Existing and proposed chemical indicators
- Soil pH.
- Electrical conductivity (EC) as a salinity-related indicator.
- Soil organic matter or soil organic carbon (label the actual property; they are not interchangeable without a conversion assumption).
- Total nitrogen and/or available nitrate only if the actual dataset/test provides the relevant form.
- Available/extractable phosphorus.
- Exchangeable/available potassium.
- Secondary nutrients such as calcium, magnesium and sulfur where relevant.
- Micronutrients such as iron, zinc, boron, copper, manganese and molybdenum where measured/reliably supplied and relevant to the crop.
- Cation exchange capacity (CEC).
- Sodicity-related metrics such as exchangeable sodium percentage (ESP) or sodium adsorption ratio (SAR) only from appropriate soil/water lab data or a validated calculation with required inputs.
- Soil carbonate/gypsum or other properties only where suitable data exists.
13.2 Why these matter
pH influences nutrient availability but does not say whether sufficient nutrients are present. EC indicates soluble-salt effects but is interpreted with method, crop and conditions. Organic matter/organic carbon provides context for soil functioning and longer-term trends. CEC relates to a soil's capacity to retain exchangeable cations. Nutrient tests depend on extraction method, depth, units and local crop calibration.

USDA NRCS recommends evaluating soil function using multiple physical, chemical and biological indicators, with examples including pH, EC, soil nitrate, extractable phosphorus/potassium, organic matter, bulk density, infiltration, water-holding capacity, earthworms and microbial indicators. These are not all obtainable from general APIs. [R1, R2]
13.3 API soil predictions versus tests
A global gridded soil product such as SoilGrids can provide modelled predictions of selected soil properties at mapped depths/resolution, but these are spatial estimates, not a lab test from the farmer's exact field. SoilGrids documentation describes global property predictions at 250 m and six standard depths; current service availability and terms should be checked before production use. [R7]

Display modelled soil properties with prediction depth, map resolution, uncertainty if provided, and source date/version. For nutrient recommendations, do not treat coarse estimated total nitrogen as equivalent to immediately plant-available nitrogen or an agronomic lab recommendation.
13.4 Soil chemical display example
“Predicted pH: 7.8, modelled at the mapped depth” is different from “Lab pH: 7.8, sample collected 12 March.” If measured and modelled values disagree, show both with dates/methods and explain the discrepancy; do not silently replace the lab result with the map prediction.
14. Complete farm indicator catalogue: soil physical and biological properties
14.1 Physical properties
Track when measured or suitably modelled:
- Texture fractions: sand, silt, clay; classified texture if defensibly derived.
- Bulk density.
- Coarse fragments/stoniness.
- Soil depth and restrictive layers where known.
- Compaction/penetration resistance from field measurement.
- Infiltration rate.
- Drainage/waterlogging observations.
- Available water-holding capacity (with method and depth).
- Aggregate stability/soil structure.
- Surface crusting.
- Erosion/runoff/ponding observations and ground cover.
- Topography/elevation/slope where a suitable terrain model is used.
14.2 Biological properties
For researchers or farmers with relevant sampling:
- Earthworm counts/observations under a repeatable method.
- Microbial biomass carbon/nitrogen.
- Soil respiration.
- Enzyme or potentially mineralisable nitrogen tests.
- Particulate organic matter / active-carbon indicators if actually measured.
- Soil biodiversity observations where a defined method exists.
Do not invent these values from general weather or location APIs. Explain the sampling method, sample date, depth and laboratory/field protocol. A single biological indicator is not a universal soil-health verdict.
14.3 Why physical and biological measures matter
Soil texture, compaction, infiltration and water-holding capacity influence the way water and roots interact with soil. Biological indicators provide information about soil organisms and nutrient cycling, but are method-dependent. A soil dashboard should treat soil health as a set of functions and indicators, not a single absolute number. [R1, R2]
14.4 Example
A field may have pH in a locally suitable range but a recorded infiltration test indicates slow water entry. Analysis should list these as different findings: acceptable pH does not cancel out an infiltration concern. If no infiltration test or suitable source exists, the indicator remains “not measured,” not “good.”
15. Complete farm indicator catalogue: crop, vegetation, terrain and land surface
15.1 Crop/rotation status
- Crop sequence and crop durations from a New Analysis result.
- Rotation score, individual crop suitability scores, priority fit, pros/cons, risks and mitigations from that specific run.
- Farmer-confirmed plan status and actual/estimated dates.
- Current crop/growth stage only where recorded or supported by a valid method.
- Expected planting/transition/harvest windows and days remaining as plan estimates.
- Crop variety/cultivar if the farmer records it and the decision engine uses it.
- Crop-specific temperature/water requirements and growth-stage context only where a suitable crop dataset/model exists.
15.2 Satellite vegetation indicators
Where data access and quality support it:
- NDVI and/or EVI from a product that explicitly supplies those values or from compatible bands and a validated method.
- Vegetation-index time series and anomaly from an appropriate baseline.
- Leaf area index (LAI), fraction of absorbed photosynthetically active radiation (fAPAR), gross/net productivity or biomass proxy only where a suitable product/method provides it.
- Land-surface temperature or thermal anomaly where the product resolution, cloud quality and interpretation are appropriate.
- Data quality/cloud/observation flags and product compositing period.
For example, NASA MODIS Aqua/Terra vegetation-index products can provide NDVI/EVI at 250 m in 16-day composites; this can be useful for landscape/regional trends but may not resolve a small farm or internal field. Do not assume it is a daily field observation. [R5]
15.3 Interpreting vegetation changes
Compare like with like: crop type, growth stage, season, dates, sensor/product, resolution and quality flags. A vegetation drop can reflect harvest, seasonal dormancy, planting gaps, water stress, disease, nutrient limitations, cloud/processing effects or other causes. It is a change signal, not a diagnosis.
15.4 Terrain and land-surface context
Where available, show elevation, slope, aspect or relative terrain position as contextual layers, and possible drainage/runoff implications only when the method supports them. A DEM does not prove the current drainage direction or soil condition without suitable hydrological context. A terrain layer may help interpret risk but should not be shown as current farm activity.
15.5 Crop suitability versus real crop condition
Keep New Analysis's predicted suitability separate from the real/observed status of an active crop. “Crop suited to location” is a recommendation/model result; “vegetation trend declined” is an observation signal; “disease caused decline” is a diagnosis requiring further evidence. These labels must not be merged.
16. Complete farm indicator catalogue: pests, disease, weeds and biodiversity
16.1 Pest/disease/weed observations
Support dated records of:
- Crop and affected area/field.
- Observed pest/disease/weed name if known, or “unknown / needs identification.”
- Evidence type (farmer report, field scout, photo-assisted identification, laboratory/extension confirmation).
- Severity and extent using a defined scale, plus observation time.
- Action taken and follow-up.
- Environmental risk flags from a suitable crop-specific model or official alert where actually available.
16.2 Risk versus diagnosis
Weather conditions may make a risk more/less plausible, but do not prove that a pest or disease exists. Do not show a specific infestation probability unless a validated model and the relevant inputs exist. A suggested identification from an image or AI must be labelled as provisional and the user should be guided to appropriate agricultural extension guidance when the issue is consequential.
16.3 Biodiversity and beneficial organisms
Potential records include pollinator/beneficial-insect observations, earthworms, habitat/ground cover, non-crop vegetation strips or other defined biodiversity observations. They require appropriate sampling or farmer reports; a general global map does not measure farm biodiversity directly.
16.4 Example
A farmer logs “leaf damage observed” on a crop with date and photo. Analysis creates an observation record, asks for/records an identification state, links current weather/context and suggests monitoring/official local guidance if warranted. It does not convert the observation into a confirmed disease without evidence.
17. Complete farm indicator catalogue: operations, yield, resource use and outcomes
17.1 Farm activity log
Let farmers record time-stamped activities, linked to farm/field/crop where known:
- Planting, transplanting, thinning, pruning, harvest and crop removal.
- Irrigation dates, amount/volume, duration and method where known.
- Fertiliser, compost, manure, lime, gypsum and other amendment type/amount/date.
- Tillage, cultivation, machinery traffic or field preparation.
- Pest/disease/weed scouting and interventions.
- Soil/water sample collection and lab result attachment.
- Irrigation-system repair, drainage work or other relevant infrastructure change.
- Crop loss, weather damage or exceptional event.
- Farmer notes and optional photo/attachment where supported.
Each event must be labeled farmer-reported unless verified. Where values use units, show them explicitly and preserve raw input and structured fields. Prompt for confirmation when natural-language parsing could materially change a value/date.
17.2 Harvest/yield and quality
When available, track harvested quantity, crop area, yield per unit area, harvest date, rejected/lost share, quality/grade and the farmer’s notes. Financial and market-price figures are excluded. Separate actual measured/recorded yield from estimated yield, and compare compatible crops, areas, varieties, seasons, crop duration and conditions.
17.3 Cost/resource data (optional and user-supplied)
For this release, do not build cost accounting, profit, gross-margin, commodity-price or financial simulation. Water volume, irrigation frequency, fertilizer amount or other agronomic management quantities may be recorded where they are relevant to scientific analysis, but do not attach monetary values.
17.4 Efficiency indicators
Potential calculations include output per unit of recorded irrigation, nutrient applied per unit of yield, or input-use trend. Only calculate these when numerator/denominator definitions, time boundaries and units are consistent. A higher efficiency ratio does not guarantee lower environmental impact or better total performance; show trade-offs and comparison basis.
17.5 Action tracking
Recommendations can become tasks with status (planned/in progress/completed/dismissed/not applicable), due date, note, and linked finding. Follow-up can compare subsequent indicators to a baseline but should not claim causation solely from temporal order.
17.6 Example
A farmer logs irrigation on a date and harvest quantity later. A subsequent report may compare vegetation and water indicators before/after irrigation and compute output per recorded water volume, if units and period match. It should state that other influences may explain the differences and retain the farmer-supplied status of the records.
18. Calculations, interpretation and health-status logic
18.1 Separate scores that answer different questions
- Crop suitability score: how well an individual crop fits the specific location/inputs/priorities according to New Analysis.
- Rotation score/rank: how well a complete crop sequence meets priorities and feasibility constraints.
- Indicator result: a defined measured/estimated/calculated value such as pH, soil moisture or vegetation index.
- Farm health status: a summary of the current supported state and risk rules.
- Data quality/confidence: how adequate/reliable the available inputs and method are for the conclusion.
- Regional city aggregate: the status derived from eligible published-farm data for that city.
- Scenario result: a hypothetical output under What-If assumptions.
Never use these as synonyms. A rotation score of 88 does not mean the current farm health is 88/100 and does not mean an 88% chance of success.
18.2 Farm-health calculation principle
Prefer a component-indicator view plus an explainable summary. The summary may use defined indicator status rules and weights only if the team has a defensible scoring method. Define crop/location/season context, missing data handling, critical-risk overrides, source quality and how incomparable indicators are combined. If there is no validated method for a single combined score, use a categorical assessment with reasons rather than a spurious decimal.
18.3 Critical concern versus average
A weighted average can hide a critical condition. If a validated high-priority risk applies, the overview can show “Attention needed” even if other indicators are stable. Document the rule that causes this override and its evidence requirement. Conversely, a single noisy/low-confidence indicator should not automatically turn the entire farm red without a justified rule.
18.4 Health status versus data quality
Keep two dimensions visible: condition and confidence/coverage. For example “Attention needed — limited confidence due to stale soil-moisture data” is better than assigning neutral/good status because the system lacks evidence. An indicator can be unknown even when the overall page looks otherwise stable.
18.5 Trends
Only calculate increase/decrease when two comparable points exist and show comparison period. Check source/product versions, units, time windows, field/region scope and method changes. Do not interpolate silently across missing observations. Use seasonal/growth-stage context where available.
18.6 Risk prioritisation
Each risk item needs scope (farm/field/crop), signal, timestamp, evidence source, method/rule, confidence, severity/urgency basis, mitigation, follow-up and state (active/resolved/unconfirmed). Risk priority should be explicit, not based on AI wording alone.
18.7 Example
Vegetation data is stale, pH is from an old soil map, and no recent farm sensor data exists. The system should not label the farm green just because no threshold is crossed. It should show available historical values with dates, set data coverage/assessment confidence to limited, and explain what is needed to strengthen the assessment.
19. Sources, APIs, freshness and scientific limitations
19.1 Potential source catalogue
The following sources are known from the project's context or are potential candidates. Confirm the exact product, endpoint, license, access method, geographic coverage and availability before implementation. An API being listed here does not mean it is already connected or that it provides all values for the selected farm.

NASA POWER: solar and meteorological time series. Its Daily API serves parameter-by-day data and advises using original source resolution; the global grid depends on parameter/data product. Preserve parameter IDs, dates, units, source grid/resolution and returned quality information. [R8]

NASA GPM / IMERG: satellite-derived precipitation estimates; IMERG combines satellite and other estimates across fine temporal/spatial scales. Confirm product/version, accumulation window, spatial resolution and latency before using it as observed precipitation. [R9]

NASA SMAP: satellite-derived soil-moisture products. Product temporal/spatial resolutions vary; one global daily L3 radiometer product is 36 km, which is far too coarse to call a small farm sensor reading. Record actual product metadata. [R6]

NASA MODIS vegetation indices: examples include NDVI/EVI products with 250 m and 16-day compositing. A data point represents a product composite, not a daily measurement. Use the correct cloud/quality flags and spatial-resolution caveats. [R5]

SoilGrids / ISRIC: modelled global soil-property predictions at 250 m and standard depth intervals. Do not treat as direct lab sample. The official documentation notes the REST API is beta and may have service interruptions; check status and current access alternatives before relying on it in production. [R7]

NASADEM / terrain model: may support elevation and derived topographic context if product/coverage/access are verified. Terrain-derived slope/aspect requires a defined calculation and suitable resolution; it does not directly measure drainage or farm waterlogging.

GRACE / GRACE-FO: can support broad regional mass/water-storage indicators depending on the selected product, but its spatial scale is not suitable for claiming a precise single-farm groundwater level. Only display a farm-relevant interpretation when the selected product and method justify it.

Weather forecast provider: a distinct forecast source is required for forward-looking weather; NASA historical/environmental data does not automatically constitute a local forecast. Keep provider issue time and valid forecast window.

Other sources: local agricultural agencies, soil/water laboratories, crop-specific extension databases, validated crop models, sensors and farmer-entered records can supplement satellite products. Record attribution, terms, units, method, timestamps, resolution and limitations.
19.2 Data lineage fields
For every datum keep, as available: source/provider, endpoint/product/version, parameter, unit, spatial resolution, geographic coverage, temporal resolution, measurement/valid time, retrieval time, publication/processing time, QA flag, transformations, data type (observed/forecast/modelled/lab/sensor/farmer-reported), license/attribution, uncertainty and missing-data reason.
19.3 Refresh and caching
Do not refetch every source for every card on every page load. Define source-specific refresh windows and cache keys based on location/grid cell, variables, period and product version. Refresh when an analysis or source-appropriate update requires it. Record when cache was last validated. A dashboard refresh must not falsely imply every dataset was newly measured.
19.4 API failure behaviour
If one source fails, continue only with calculations that remain valid. Mark the affected section partial and list what is missing. Keep the last valid result with its timestamp if useful, but clearly label it old. Never silently replace missing values with zero, average defaults, or invented data. Backoff/retry and rate limits must be respected.
19.5 Spatial resolution, uncertainty and scale
Each API-derived value must retain its effective spatial scale. If a pixel/grid cell spans a region larger than the farm, label it as regional/gridded estimate. A drawn polygon can support a location query or overlay, but it cannot improve the source resolution or turn regional estimates into measured field values.
20. Activity log, tasks, alerts and record history
20.1 Activity log
Provide a dated record list/search for real farm events entered by the farmer or connected system. Filters may include field, crop, event type and date. Each item has source provenance, units/attachments where relevant, and edit/correction history. Keep a distinction between a farmer's observation, an API signal and a model conclusion.
20.2 Tasks and recommendations
A recommendation from Deep Analysis can be saved as an action and displayed in Farm Analysis as an actionable item. Calendar remains the full scheduling interface. Farm Analysis can show a small summary such as “2 actions awaiting review” and link to the task/calendar details. Avoid creating a second source of truth for due dates or reminders.
20.3 Alert lifecycle
Alerts can be active, acknowledged, in progress, resolved or dismissed when applicable. Store the condition and data snapshot that triggered the alert, current status, owner action and time. Avoid repeated identical notifications; update an existing event or use a defined reminder policy. A “resolved” state should be based on defined follow-up logic or farmer confirmation, not simply because a new run failed to fetch the indicator.
20.4 Historical dashboard
Trend views should include supported values, units, timestamps, data gaps, event overlays (e.g., planting/irrigation/harvest), and prior analysis markers. Keep the calendar separate but allow links to relevant events. Comparing seasons requires comparable crop/growth stage and data/method context; do not draw fake daily continuity from sparse data.
21. Deep Analysis integration and log popup requirements
21.1 Keep Deep Analysis as the detailed engine
Farm Analysis's Deep Analysis button launches the comprehensive workflow already described in Field_Shift_Deep_Analysis_Master_Specification. Do not duplicate its entire scientific investigation, calculation trace and report in the normal overview. The regular Analysis page surfaces the latest assessment, important risks and key indicator summaries; Deep Analysis investigates in greater depth.
21.2 Separate log secondary-navigation page
Deep Analysis logs have their own secondary-nav page. The page lists dated runs/logs; selecting one opens a popup/modal showing the full saved log content in more detail. The modal has a visible top-right X to close. Closing it returns to the same log list and preserves appropriate scroll/selection state. Clicking outside or Escape may also close it only if the UI design supports safe dismissal; the X must remain clear and accessible.
21.3 Log contents and link back
The popup includes its saved farm/run context, report summary, source freshness and failures, indicators/findings, risk explanations, recommendations, and expandable execution trace as defined in the Deep Analysis specification. Link to the corresponding indicator/field where possible. It must show the historical snapshot as generated, not silently replace old values with today's current values. If a linked current farm or source is unavailable, keep the log readable and explain that the current link cannot be opened.
21.4 No duplicate log system
Farm Analysis history may summarise a previous Deep Analysis run, but the full trace/log lives in the dedicated Deep Analysis log page and popup. Avoid separate conflicting copies. Use shared run IDs.
22. Settings, re-analysis, rename and deletion semantics
22.1 Settings entry
A clear settings/gear action in the Analysis secondary navigation or header opens farm-specific options. Options include rename, re-analysis, edit/remove map drawing, relevant farm settings, publication controls if supported, and delete farm. Destructive items should be visually separated from routine actions.
22.2 Rename
Update the visible name across Home selector, Maps sidebar/outline, Analysis header, Rotation contexts, Deep Analysis summaries where current name is used, calendar references and activity/task lists. Keep stable IDs and immutable historical snapshots. A rename must not rerun calculations or change data by itself.
22.3 Re-analysis
Re-analysis opens the New Analysis questionnaire with existing values prefilled where practical. The farmer can update location, soil/conditions, priorities, hard constraints and risk preferences. After submission, fetch suitable current data, run the same rotation engine, feasibility and risk-tolerance workflow, save a new immutable run, and set it as the latest recommendation. Provide a comparison against the prior recommendation and identify whether differences arose from changed farmer inputs, new data, or a new calculation/model version. Re-analysis does not automatically mean the farmer adopted the new rotation or planted the first crop.
22.4 Map drawing options
Edit drawing opens the polygon editor. Remove drawing removes only the map geometry; farm/analysis/rotation/history remains. If a public network feature uses published city aggregates, handle publication settings separately; never expose precise boundary publicly.
22.5 Delete farm
Show what will happen to linked records, map drawing, tasks, scheduled analyses, public publication, scenario references and historical logs. Apply the product's retention/deletion policy, revoke background jobs/access, and provide confirmation. Deletion is not the same as archive, rename or remove-from-Maps. Where deletion has irreversible consequences, make them explicit and avoid vague confirmation text.
23. Data model, consistency, privacy and security
23.1 Suggested core entities
- Farm: farm_id, current name, owner_id, analysis location/configuration, privacy/public status, created/updated timestamps.
- FarmGeometry: geometry_id, farm_id, polygon coordinates, coordinate reference, geometry version, created/updated timestamps, owner visibility.
- AnalysisRun: run_id, farm_id, input snapshot, priorities/constraints, timestamps, source manifest, calculation versions, candidate/results, status.
- RotationPlan: plan_id, farm_id, originating run_id, ordered crop stages/durations, status, actual/planned dates, farmer confirmations.
- Observation: observation_id, farm/field scope, indicator/property, value/unit, source type, valid/measurement time, retrieval time, QA flags, uncertainty and method.
- ActivityEvent: event_id, farm/field/crop scope, type, event time, structured values, notes/attachments, provenance and correction history.
- RiskAlert: alert_id, linked indicator/run, rule/model/version, priority, state, evidence, mitigation, timestamps.
- TaskAction: action_id, linked finding/recommendation, status, due date/calendar reference, notes and outcome.
- DeepAnalysisRun: deep_run_id, farm_id, status, saved output, source manifest and trace/log references.
- Scenario: scenario_id, base run/rotation ID, hypothetical input changes, version, name, results and creation time.
- CityAggregate: city identifier, aggregate period, eligible published farm count, indicator/score definition, quality flags and aggregate values; no public exact farm coordinates.
This is a logical model; map to the existing backend/data store after code audit.
23.2 Immutability and versioning
Historical run inputs/results should be immutable snapshots for audit. New data, changed priorities or new logic produces a new run. Current labels may update through stable IDs, but old report values must not silently change. Geometry edits are versioned separately from scientific runs unless the geometry materially changes the location query, in which case a new data retrieval/re-analysis may be appropriate.
23.3 Privacy
- Owner-only exact map geometry remains private.
- Public network maps use city-level aggregate representation only; do not show public exact farm markers, private boundary geometry or coordinate details.
- City scores only use eligible public farms.
- Protect private farm data, activities, analysis runs, weather/soil details where considered private, notes and logs with owner-level access checks.
- AI/data retrieval must respect the same farm permissions as the UI.
- Small-sample city aggregates must be suppressed or generalised to reduce re-identification risk.
23.4 Security and secrets
API keys should not appear in browser code, logs or trace popups. Use the appropriate server-side secret handling, redact sensitive payloads, validate user inputs and map geometry, and apply authorisation to every linked record. Avoid putting private farm identifiers/coordinates into public URLs or external referrers without deliberate design.
24. UI states, accessibility and responsive behaviour
24.1 Status and data labels
Use labels such as Measured, Satellite estimate, Modelled, Forecast, Laboratory test, Farmer reported, Regional aggregate, Stale data, Limited data, Partially available and Not measured. The meaning should be available through a short tooltip/detail. Do not rely only on colours.
24.2 Expandable details
Indicator cards should expand into details without forcing all technical content onto the default view. The expanded view should show definition, unit, scope, timestamp, source, method/calculation, comparison, uncertainty, data limits and related actions. If no defensible value exists, show “Not available from current data” rather than a placeholder zero.
24.3 Accessible contrast and colour
Status gradients/backgrounds must preserve text contrast. Pair each status colour with text/icon/shape and provide a neutral limited-data state. Red/green should not be the only distinction. On maps, a legend must state whether colour represents city aggregate, own-farm status or selected state.
24.4 Mobile/responsive behaviour
On smaller screens, sidebar may become a drawer, but search and draw controls remain discoverable. The analysis mini-map should not obscure important actions or dialogs. Modals/popups, including Deep Analysis logs, must have usable close controls and fit the viewport; log popup X remains at top-right.
24.5 Loading and error states
Never present a default green status while data is loading. Distinguish loading, partial, failed and stale states. Maps should allow a user to continue in Analysis if imagery fails. API errors should say which information could not be refreshed, not expose raw stack traces or secrets.
25. Examples of complete user journeys
25.1 First farm and map drawing
1. User creates a farm via New Analysis, which gives it a name and stable ID.
2. Home selects this farm and displays available status/weather with clear data freshness.
3. User clicks the map preview and enters Maps.
4. User searches the city/country, zooms to the area, uses Draw to create an overall boundary with nodes and lines, closes/validates the polygon, picks the existing farm name and saves it.
5. Sidebar now lists the farm. Clicking the row fits the map; clicking the boundary opens Farm Analysis.
6. Analysis shows rotation recommendation status and available tracking indicators. Since no planting date was confirmed, it does not pretend a crop is active.
25.2 Farm health differs from city status
The user's farm outline is red/amber because its own supported assessment includes a high-priority water-related issue. The city's background is green because eligible published farms in the city are generally favourable for the selected aggregate period. The interface explains these are different data populations. It does not expose other farms' exact positions. The owner opens their Analysis to inspect the evidence; the city aggregate alone is not treated as causal explanation.
25.3 Crop status and calendar
A farmer saves a New Analysis rotation and marks it planned with a target start date. Analysis shows a planned crop timeline, not actual growth. The separate Calendar page contains the full event/reminder record. When planting actually occurs, the farmer records the date; Analysis updates the active stage and estimated remaining days. If the crop is harvested early, the farmer records it and the history remains intact.
25.4 Soil properties and data uncertainty
A soil-map source returns a modelled pH and organic-carbon estimate at its product's specified resolution/depth. Analysis labels both as modelled estimates and links the source. The farmer later uploads a lab test with a sample date/depth. The two values are displayed with distinct provenance; the lab data is not silently overwritten by the map estimate, and the comparison accounts for depth/method differences.
25.5 Crop vegetation change
A MODIS time-series point changes between two composites. Analysis shows the product’s composite time window and resolution, compares only compatible points, and checks crop stage/harvest/activity records. It flags a vegetation change for investigation, not a definitive pest or water diagnosis. Deep Analysis can examine the record in more detail.
25.6 Re-analysis and rename
The farmer renames “Old Farm” to “North Farm”. The stable farm ID keeps map geometry, activities, calendar links and history connected. The farmer then re-runs New Analysis with changed priorities and new data. The new run becomes the latest recommendation and is compared to the old run; old inputs/results remain accessible. The farm is not automatically marked as planted with the new rotation.
25.7 Deep Analysis log popup
The farmer clicks Deep Analysis from the Analysis page, completes the deep assessment, and opens the separate logs secondary-nav page. They click a dated run; a popup shows that run's exact summary, data/source status, calculations/trace and recommendations. The top-right X closes the popup and returns to the same log list. It does not replace old results with today's values.
26. Edge cases and expected behaviour
- No farm exists: Home offers a clear New Analysis path; Maps has no own-farm list state; Analysis is not rendered with fabricated sample farm data.
- Farm exists but no map drawing: sidebar indicates “No boundary drawn”; map can centre on an owner-visible saved location if available and offers Draw.
- Invalid polygon: prevent save, explain crossing/insufficient geometry, offer vertex editing.
- Duplicate drawing for same farm: offer edit/replace; do not silently create a second disconnected geometry.
- Ambiguous location search: present multiple results; do not jump to a random one.
- Geocoder/map tiles fail: display recoverable error and keep non-map farm details accessible.
- Private farm not published: it never contributes to city public aggregate and never appears as a public farm marker.
- Too few published farms: suppress or neutralise city colour/aggregate and show insufficient sample.
- Old city data: show aggregate period/freshness; do not colour it as current without qualification.
- New Analysis run has different location: re-fetch location-dependent data; ask about the map footprint; do not move geometry automatically.
- No active crop dates: show sequence/durations but no false current-crop or countdown statement.
- Missing weather API: show old value with date or unavailable; never default to a plausible temperature.
- Stale vegetation or soil data: show source age and scope; no current-condition claim without caveat.
- No soil test: nutrient or chemical values that require lab input are “Not measured” or show modelled estimate if a suitable model source exists.
- Conflicting measurements: show source/method/time differences; do not silently merge.
- Analysis run partial: retain data it successfully obtained and flag affected conclusions.
- Deleted/removed farm: linked UI paths must not display stale private data; remove drawing and farm are separate operations.
- Rename during open session: refresh names without losing stable farm selection.
- Deep Analysis modal closed: return to logs list and preserve context; no data loss.
- Scheduled analysis failed: show failure and source effects; don't mark “all good.”
- Risk disappears because its data failed: do not mark resolved merely because the signal is missing.
- Scenario values appear in farm status: must never happen unless the user explicitly adopts and records the real change; What-If outputs remain separate.
27. Acceptance criteria
Home
- Current farm selection consistently drives all Home cards.
- A status does not default to green while loading or when critical data is missing.
- Health and New Analysis rotation scores are kept distinct.
- Weather observations and forecasts show separate timestamps/periods.
- Each important indicator has units/data-type/freshness context where available.
- Map preview opens full Maps; no public exact coordinate is exposed.
- Priority alerts link to the supporting detail and the all-clear language is appropriately qualified.
Maps
- Full-screen satellite map has working place/country/city and farm-name search.
- Left sidebar lists owner farms; clicking a row navigates/fits map, and clicking/open action reaches the right farm Analysis.
- Drawing tool adds connected nodes/straight segments, closes and validates a polygon, links it to an existing New Analysis farm, and supports edit/cancel/remove.
- Internal field boundaries are not required.
- Private outline is only shown to the owner/authorised party; public map is city-level aggregate only.
- City aggregate uses only eligible published-farm data, exposes sample/time context, handles insufficient data, and never leaks precise public locations.
- Farm outline status and city aggregate colour are separately explained.
- Returning from Analysis through mini-map restores prior map context where possible.
Farm Analysis
- Page shows rotation sequence/duration and distinguishes recommended/planned/active status.
- Current crop/days remaining is only shown when records/dates support it and is qualified as estimated where appropriate.
- All indicator values show source/type/time/unit/scope details where available; missing data is never treated as a healthy zero.
- Weather, water, soil, crop/vegetation, risk, activity and history content is grouped with expandable detail.
- User activity records and recommendations remain linked to dates/crops/farm and are labelled by provenance.
- Calendar remains a separate main-nav page; Analysis only surfaces relevant milestones/links.
- Deep Analysis remains a button; log list remains a distinct secondary-nav page; individual log popup has top-right X and shows saved historical content.
- Rotation navigation routes to existing Rotations page.
- Rename updates labels globally with stable identity; re-analysis creates a new run; drawing removal does not delete farm; farm deletion is explicit and handles linked data.
Scientific integrity and platform
- Each displayed indicator is classified as observed/API, forecast, modelled, lab-tested, sensor-measured, farmer-reported or calculated where applicable.
- Source resolution, valid time, retrieval time and quality limits are stored/displayed where available.
- Crop/soil/health claims are supported by a documented method and valid inputs.
- Farm health, rotation ranking, individual crop fit, data quality, city aggregate and scenario results are distinct concepts.
- Missing/conflicting/stale API data produces clear limitations and does not invent values.
- Privacy and permission checks cover map data, logs, activities, exports and background jobs.
28. Recommended implementation roadmap
Phase 1 — Audit and foundations
Inspect the existing project, data model, New Analysis farm records, Deep Analysis logs, Rotations page, Calendar page, current API integrations and UI. Map each requirement to existing/partial/missing. Establish stable farm IDs and verify current rename/delete/re-analysis flows before building UI on top.
Phase 2 — Home and Analysis baseline
Implement a selected-farm Home summary with honest data freshness, weather and map preview. Organise Farm Analysis into clear groups, ensure existing crop rotation and Deep Analysis entry points work, and add provenance labels to current indicators without introducing new unsupported scores.
Phase 3 — Maps and geometry
Implement map search, satellite basemap/provider attribution, owner farm sidebar, polygon drawing/editing, farm-name linking, private geometry rules and preserved navigation state. Keep public city aggregate in a distinct layer with explicit privacy/sample handling.
Phase 4 — Indicator catalogue and soil/water detail
Audit currently available API variables. Add high-value indicator details (water demand/balance, soil chemical/physical data, vegetation trends) where methods and data support them. Expose source/valid time/quality/units and implement missing-data states before adding complex composite scores.
Phase 5 — Farm activity and history
Add farmer-reported planting/irrigation/fertiliser/scouting/harvest records, crop plan-vs-actual status, key indicator trends and links to the separate Calendar. Keep tasks and date management on the agreed existing calendar/task architecture.
Phase 6 — Regional context and advanced tracking
Integrate safe city aggregates, regional comparison, additional lab/sensor records, efficiency indicators and research-oriented biological metrics only where justified. Avoid exposing exact locations or claiming unavailable data.
Phase 7 — Test and harden
Test farm rename, re-analysis, different locations, geometry edit/delete, private/public states, missing APIs, stale data, invalid polygons, history immutability, Deep Analysis popup behaviour, access controls, responsive layouts, accessibility and source provenance. Confirm no implementation breaks the current feature logic.
29. Indicator availability matrix
The matrix below is a scoping aid, not a promise that a given API currently provides every value.
Indicator / information	Typical source type	Directly observed?	Core display rule
Temperature/humidity/rain/wind	Weather API or station	Provider-dependent	Show valid time, forecast/observation label, units and source
Historical rainfall	Gridded/product or weather archive	Usually estimate/gridded product	Preserve accumulation period and grid resolution
Forecast precipitation	Forecast API	No; forecast	Show forecast window and provider issue time
Reference ET (ETo)	Provider or meteorological calculation	Calculated/modelled	Document method and meteorological inputs
Crop ET (ETc)	Crop model using ETo and crop coefficient	Calculated/modelled	Needs crop/stage coefficient and valid assumptions
Satellite surface soil moisture	SMAP/product	Model/retrieval estimate	Show product resolution, measurement time and quality flags
Root-zone moisture	Sensor/model/product	Depends on source	Never claim from surface moisture alone
Soil pH	Lab, soil map prediction	Depends on source	Display depth, sample date or model resolution
N/P/K	Soil lab or suitable dataset	Depends on exact property	Distinguish total vs plant-available/extractable forms
Soil EC/salinity	Soil/water test or suitable sensor	Depends on source	Need method, unit and sample context
Soil organic matter/carbon	Lab or soil model	Depends on source	Do not interchange properties without declared conversion
Soil texture	Lab/soil map	Depends on source	State texture class/source depth/resolution
Bulk density/CEC	Lab/model source where available	Depends on source	Display depth, method and uncertainty
Compaction/infiltration	Field test/sensor	Usually field measurement	Never infer exact value from generic API alone
Soil biological metrics	Lab/field sampling	Usually measured	Require method, sample date/depth and protocol
NDVI/EVI	Satellite product/bands	Derived remote-sensing estimate	Show pixel size, composite window and QA flags
Crop stage/current crop	Farmer record or crop model	Depends on source	Mark model estimate vs farmer-confirmed stage
Crop suitability / rotation score	New Analysis model	Calculated	Preserve run inputs, method version and priority weights
Pest/disease presence	Scout/lab/farmer record	Depends on verification	Separate risk from confirmed diagnosis
Pest/climate risk	Crop-specific validated model	Modelled	Do not infer exact infestation from weather alone
Irrigation amount	Sensor, meter, farmer record	Depends on source	Preserve unit, timing and whether measured/reported
Water balance/deficit	Calculation from multiple inputs	Calculated	Explain missing terms, period and assumptions
Actual yield	Farmer/harvest record	Recorded/measured	Include area, units, crop and harvest period
Water/nutrient efficiency	Calculation with valid inputs	Calculated	Use consistent boundaries and units; show uncertainty
Elevation/slope	DEM plus calculation	Modelled/geospatial	State dataset/resolution; not proof of current drainage
Regional city status	Published-farm aggregate	Aggregated	Public eligible population only; show sample/time/coverage
Farm activity	Farmer/device record	Reported or sensor-derived	Provenance, timestamp and corrections visible
Farm health status	Defined component rules	Derived summary	Distinct from rotation score and confidence/data coverage


30. Glossary and terminology
- Farm ID: stable internal identifier that survives a rename.
- Farm footprint / drawing: user-drawn overall map polygon, not necessarily a surveyed boundary and not internal field geometry.
- Analysis location: location used for New Analysis data retrieval and crop/rotation recommendations; may not automatically move the saved map drawing.
- Observation/valid time: when a measurement is said to represent conditions or a forecast is valid.
- Retrieval time: when Field Shift fetched the record.
- Modelled estimate: a value inferred by a model/product, not directly measured at the farm.
- Farm-reported: information entered by the farmer and not independently verified.
- Rotation score: assessment of a candidate crop sequence relative to the inputs/priorities in a specific New Analysis run.
- Farm health status: separate summary of current supported conditions and risks.
- Data quality/coverage: adequacy, freshness, spatial fit and completeness of the available evidence.
- City aggregate: a public regional summary based only on eligible published farms, not an average of every private farm in the city.
- Scenario: hypothetical What-If inputs/results; not real farm state until separately adopted/recorded.
- Planned crop: farmer intends to grow it; not proof it is already growing.
- Active crop: farmer-confirmed current crop/period or a clearly labelled model inference supported by the system.
