1. Overview
The Global Farming Network is a connected feature inside Field Shift. It links a public directory of participating farms, an interactive global map, farmer-to-farmer communication, communities, regional comparisons, regional environmental history, and evidence that can help Field Shift investigate farm problems and evaluate crop-rotation recommendations.

The feature has three connected purposes:
1. Discover: Let users explore cities and countries, see where farms have chosen to publish, and inspect the agricultural information those farms have shared.
2. Connect: Let farmers find one another, exchange practical knowledge, message each other, and form communities around any useful shared interest.
3. Understand and improve: Use appropriately permissioned farm data, public-farm aggregates, environmental datasets, historical observations, and weather services to compare farms, investigate unusual conditions, identify regional patterns, and evaluate Field Shift's recommendations.
The feature is not simply a map or a generic social network. The map helps users discover farms and regional patterns; communication helps people exchange context and experiences; comparisons help farmers understand how their farm differs from comparable farms; and the evidence system helps Field Shift learn what the collected information can reliably support.
1.1 Core principle
Connect farms without exposing their exact locations, and turn shared information into useful agricultural context. A farmer's precise coordinates, boundary, access route, or other details that could reveal the exact location must never appear in the public network. Public farm locations are represented at the city level only. This is a mandatory product rule, not an optional privacy setting.
1.2 What success looks like
A user can search for a city, open its published-farm list, inspect farms that have chosen to share information, compare their own farm with regional averages, connect with relevant farmers, and use regional context to investigate a problem. A farmer can participate without publishing their farm, and a user can explore public regional information without owning a farm. The network must still provide value when relatively few farmers have joined.
2. Confirmed decisions and design boundaries
The following items are part of the agreed concept.
- Public farm discovery: Farmers can choose to publish a farm to the network. Published farms can be discovered publicly.
- Public location privacy: Publicly visible farm location is city-level only. Exact coordinates and exact farm placement are never exposed publicly. There is no public exact-location option.
- City map colour: Cities with published farms receive a green-to-red condition colour based only on the eligible data from farms published in that city. Private/unpublished farms do not contribute. External datasets may appear as separate map layers, but must not silently alter this public-farm score.
- Selecting a city: Clicking a coloured city opens a list of the public farm profiles associated with that city.
- Selecting a farm: Clicking a listed or otherwise selectable public farm opens a deeper profile using a detail layout similar to the owner's farm-detail view, while excluding private location information and any fields the owner has not shared.
- Owner's farm list: The map screen includes a side panel listing the signed-in user's own farms. Selecting one navigates the map to that farm for the owner. The owner's own view may use the saved location according to existing access permissions; this must never make exact coordinates visible to other users.
- Search: Users can search for cities, countries, and named farms. Every farm has a name. Search results navigate to the relevant map area, city list, or farm profile according to the result selected.
- Communication: Users can message other farmers from the network, subject to permissions and safety controls.
- Relationships: Common chat/social controls include add friend, remove friend, block, unblock, mute notifications, and unmute notifications.
- Communities: Users can create or join communities for any useful reason, including city, soil type, water availability, crops, agricultural priorities, shared interests, or friends.
- Farm diagnosis: Field Shift can compare a farm's observations with other published farms in the same region to help assess whether a problem appears farm-specific or region-wide. This is supporting evidence, not a guaranteed diagnosis.
- Regional information: Field Shift can store and organize environmental and agricultural history by region.
- Weather support: Regional history can establish normal patterns and anomalies. Weather APIs or forecast services provide forecast information. Historical data alone must not be presented as a reliable live weather forecast.
- Farm-to-region comparison: A dedicated feature lets a user compare their farm's available metrics with regional averages and other regional statistics. This is a priority use case.
2.1 What is not assumed
This document does not assume that the feature is already implemented, that every farm has sensors, that every farmer shares the same metrics, or that all user-submitted claims are verified. It also does not assume all data should influence the core recommendation score. The platform must identify data sources, timestamps, coverage, uncertainty, and permission status before making use of a value.
3. People and access levels
3.1 Visitor
A visitor can browse whatever public pages are intentionally available without seeing private farm data. Whether visitors can perform actions such as messaging or joining groups depends on account requirements; recommended behaviour is to require an account for interactive and contribution features.
3.2 Registered user
A registered user can search, explore public farms, read public information, manage their profile and notification preferences, and participate in communities where allowed. A user does not need to own a farm to be a member of the network.
3.3 Farm owner
A farm owner controls whether each farm is published and which permitted farm details are shared. The owner can update the public data, pause publication, unpublish the farm, and manage friend and message settings. The owner's private farm-detail view can show information that must never be exposed in the public view.
3.4 Community moderator or administrator
Community administrators can manage community descriptions, membership rules, invitations, moderation actions, and reported content within their community. They must not gain access to private farm coordinates simply because they manage a group.
3.5 Platform administrator
Authorized platform staff may need limited tools for abuse response, data integrity, support, and system health. Access should be permission-based, logged, and limited to the minimum necessary. Administrative access is not a justification to expose private data to ordinary users.
4. Main user experience and navigation
4.1 Global Map screen
The map is the primary entry point to the network. The recommended desktop layout has:
- Main map canvas: A world map that can be zoomed and panned.
- Search bar: Searches for a city, country, or farm name.
- Map layer controls: Let users switch between the public-farm condition view and separate environmental or information layers, where available.
- My Farms side panel: Lists the signed-in user's farms and their current private/public status.
- Selected-city panel: Appears after a coloured city is selected and lists its published farms, city-level summary, data coverage, and last-updated time.
- Selected-farm detail panel/page: Opens after a particular public farm is selected.
- Map legend: Explains the colour scale and what is included in the score.
- Last-updated and data coverage information: Makes the age and limitations of the displayed information visible.
On smaller screens, the same information can use stacked panels, drawers, or tabs rather than forcing a desktop layout onto a phone.
4.2 Map interaction rules
1. Opening the map shows the global view and available public-farm city indicators.
2. Clicking a city opens that city's public-farm list. It does not expose the farms' exact positions.
3. Clicking a farm in that list opens the farm's deeper public profile.
4. Clicking one of the signed-in user's own farms in the side panel moves or zooms the map to that farm's saved location for the owner and selects it. The private owner view must remain separate from the public location representation.
5. Searching for a city or country moves the map to that place. Searching for a farm name opens or highlights that farm's public profile and city-level representation.
6. If a city has published farms but does not have enough current, comparable data to calculate a trustworthy score, show the city as having published farms but use an explicit “insufficient data” state rather than inventing a colour score.
7. Clicking blank map space should not accidentally select a nearby farm. Selection targets must be clear and accessible.
4.3 Public map location rule
For every public farm, the public map uses the city-level location only. It may highlight an official city area, or use a city marker if reliable city boundary data is not available. It must not use exact farm coordinates, plot boundaries, satellite zoom levels that reveal a private plot, exact distance/routing details, or directions to the farm. The owner can still see their own private farm location where permitted by the existing private farm view.
4.4 My Farms side panel
The side panel helps the owner move quickly between their own farms. Each row can show the farm name, crop or farm type if available, latest analysis status, last-updated time, and whether it is private or publicly published. These are owner-facing details and should not imply that publishing the farm exposes all fields.

Selecting a row should pan the map to the farm's saved position and open an owner-specific preview. An explicit action can then open the full private farm detail. Public visitors cannot use this owner-side-panel behaviour to discover exact coordinates.
5. Publishing a farm and controlling what is shared
5.1 Publishing workflow
1. The owner opens a farm they control and chooses Publish to Global Farming Network.
2. Field Shift explains that the farm will be discoverable by its name and city-level location, and that exact location will never be shown publicly.
3. The owner chooses which fields can appear in the public profile, such as crops, growing stage, selected soil metrics, environmental trends, selected analysis results, farmer-written description, or reported outcomes.
4. The system previews the public profile exactly as other users will see it, including the city-only map representation.
5. The owner confirms publication.
6. The farm becomes searchable and is included in that city's public-farm profile list and public-farm aggregation once its eligible data is available.
7. The owner can edit fields, pause publication, or unpublish the farm later.
5.2 Shareable field groups
The owner should be able to control visibility by field group rather than facing a long list of individual database fields. Recommended groups include:
- Public identity: farm name, optional public description, public profile image.
- Crop information: crops grown, growth stage, season, and rotation history selected for publication.
- Soil and water: selected soil properties, irrigation practices, water-use metrics, and other measurements the owner approves.
- Environmental context: selected conditions and trends relevant to the farm.
- Analysis: selected Field Shift findings, recommendation summaries, and the owner-approved parts of the analysis.
- Outcomes: voluntary harvest results, soil changes, water-use results, and lessons learned.
- Community activity: posts and comments, controlled separately from farm-profile data.
The platform must not assume that publishing a farm means publishing every detail from its private record. Exact coordinates, plot geometry, private notes, access routes, credentials, and other sensitive fields are excluded from the public projection by design, not merely hidden with a front-end toggle.
5.3 Unpublishing and changing permissions
When a farm is unpublished, it should disappear from public search, public profile routes, and future public-farm aggregates after the system's defined refresh process. Previously computed aggregate data requires a documented policy: for example, recompute recent summaries and mark historical statistics as historical where immediate removal is not technically or legally appropriate. The interface must explain what happens to old public posts and previously aggregated results. Unpublishing must not leave a public profile accessible through an old direct URL.
5.4 Example
A farm owner called “Desert Spring Farm” chooses to publish crop types, soil texture, a water-use trend, and a selected crop-rotation result. The public map shows the city, not the farm's precise position. A user can find “Desert Spring Farm” by name, open its profile, and read the approved fields. The owner's coordinates, exact boundary, private notes, and unpublished analysis remain unavailable to other users.
6. City condition colours and regional scores
6.1 Purpose
The coloured cities let users quickly identify where published farm information is available and get a broad sense of the conditions reported by those farms. A colour is a summary signal, not a definitive judgement of every farm in the city, the quality of the farmers, or the economic success of the whole agricultural sector.
6.2 Required data boundary
A city's public-farm condition score must be calculated from eligible data belonging only to farms that have chosen to publish and are associated with that city. Private/unpublished farms must not be included. External environmental datasets can be shown in separate map layers and used elsewhere in Field Shift's analysis, but they must not silently change this public-farm city score.
6.3 Recommended score design
Use a clearly named Published Farm Conditions Index, on a 0-100 scale, where higher means more favourable measured conditions under the selected index definition. The index should avoid implying that one number captures every part of farm health. It should show component indicators and data coverage whenever a city is selected.

Possible components, subject to reliable data availability, include:
- Soil condition indicators relevant to the farm and crop.
- Water availability or water-stress indicators.
- Crop-condition indicators or vegetation trends where they are appropriate.
- Crop suitability for observed environmental conditions.
- Changes over time, including deteriorating or improving trends.
- Other measurable farm-condition indicators selected and documented by the product team.
A universal score should not treat all crop types and climates identically. Each input must be interpreted in context: dry conditions may be normal for a drought-adapted crop, and a high yield cannot be fairly compared without considering crop, growing stage, area, season, and relevant management constraints.
6.4 Suggested calculation flow
1. Select eligible farms: Include only farms published by their owners and associated with the city; exclude unpublished farms and fields the owner has not shared.
2. Select a shared time window: Prefer comparable recent snapshots. Do not average unrelated dates as though they are simultaneous.
3. Normalize metrics: Convert compatible values to common units and scale them to a documented score where required. For example, water use may need a per-hectare or per-unit-output measure instead of raw total volume.
4. Interpret metrics in context: Use crop type, season, local climate, soil type, and measurement source where the interpretation depends on them.
5. Calculate a farm-level index: Combine eligible component scores using documented weights. The weights should be versioned and reviewed; missing values must not silently count as good or bad.
6. Aggregate public farms: Calculate a robust city summary from the eligible farm-level indices. A median or a trimmed mean can reduce the influence of extreme values. The chosen method must be consistent and explained.
7. Calculate data coverage and confidence: Record how many farms contributed, how many component values were available, how recently they were refreshed, and how variable the farm scores are.
8. Assign the city colour: Map the score to a documented gradient from green through yellow/orange to red. The colour must have a visible numeric or descriptive explanation.
9. Publish the summary: Display the score, sample size, relevant indicator breakdown, source/measurement status, and latest update when a city is selected.
6.5 Illustrative colour interpretation
A tentative display could use green for relatively favourable measured conditions, yellow for mixed or moderate concerns, orange for substantial concerns, and red for severe concerns in the selected index. Exact thresholds must be tested and approved rather than treated as already decided. Include a neutral insufficient data state whenever the score cannot be calculated responsibly.
6.6 Variation matters
The city score should not hide large differences between its farms. A city might have a moderate overall index but contain both healthy farms and farms facing serious problems. The city panel should therefore show a distribution or range, not just the composite number.
6.7 Example
Assume five published farms in Green Valley have illustrative farm-condition scores of 75, 64, 58, 42, and 71. A robust city summary could be around the middle of that distribution, resulting in a mixed-condition colour. Clicking Green Valley would show the five farms, the city's summary, the range of results, the components that contributed, and the fact that only five published farms supplied the data. These numbers are fictional and do not describe a real city.
7. Search: cities, countries, and farm names
7.1 Search behaviour
The global search supports three primary result types:
- City: Move the map to the city and show its public-farm list and regional summary.
- Country or broader region: Move the map to the area and show available regional layers and the cities with published farms.
- Farm name: Open the public profile, provided it is published and the current user has permission to see it. The map only highlights the city-level representation.
Search should support partial names, reasonable spelling tolerance, and disambiguation when multiple places share a name. For example, “Springfield” may return multiple cities with country/state labels. A farm name can include a city in the result subtitle to distinguish farms with similar names.
7.2 Search privacy
A private or unpublished farm must not be returned by public farm search. Guessing a private farm's name or changing a URL must not reveal its existence or data. Search indexing must use the same public projection and permissions as the profile page.
7.3 Example
A user searches “Green Valley.” They can choose Green Valley City, Country Z, which pans the map to that city and opens its public farms. If they search “Sunrise Orchard,” the public farm profile opens and the map highlights only its city, never a precise orchard location.
8. Farm public profiles and detailed farm views
8.1 Goal
Clicking a farm from the city list opens a detailed public profile with the same general depth and familiar structure as a private Field Shift farm detail page. The public version is a filtered view, not a separate unrelated profile design.
8.2 Recommended profile sections
- Header: Farm name, public profile image if supplied, city and country, short description, publication status, and last-updated time.
- Overview: Selected condition summary, crop list, growing stage, and the most useful shared indicators.
- Soil: Shared soil properties and their source/date where available.
- Water: Shared irrigation practices, water-availability information, and normalized water metrics where available.
- Crops and rotations: Current crops, selected historical rotations, and owner-approved rotation information.
- Environmental context: Relevant climate, moisture, vegetation, or terrain information, clearly labelled as a farm observation, external dataset, or estimate.
- Charts and trends: Historical trends based on eligible shared snapshots, with gaps and timestamps visible.
- Analysis summary: Only analysis results the owner has chosen to share.
- Reported outcomes: Yield, soil, or water results only when voluntarily published, with source and verification status.
- Community activity: Public posts or discussions linked by the owner, subject to moderation and sharing settings.
- Contact actions: Add friend, send message, or view shared communities, subject to the target user's settings and blocks.
8.3 Public/private rendering rule
The public detail page should use an allowlist of fields that are safe and approved for public display. It must not load the private farm object and simply hide sensitive widgets in the browser. Public API responses should not contain exact coordinates, private boundaries, private notes, hidden analysis details, or fields the owner has not approved.
8.4 Example
A user clicks “Desert Spring Farm” in a city list. The profile shows the farm's shared crop rotation, a soil-texture description, a water-use trend, an owner-approved analysis summary, and the date of the latest update. The layout feels as detailed as a farm's own detail page, but no exact pin, boundaries, directions, or private notes appear.
9. The priority feature: Compare My Farm with Regional Averages
9.1 Purpose
This feature answers: “How does my farm compare with other farms in my area, and is there anything unusual about my conditions or results?” It should translate regional information into practical context for the user's own farm.
9.2 Entry points
A user can open comparison from:
- Their farm detail page.
- The Global Farming Network map after selecting one of their farms.
- A regional summary or city detail panel.
- A farm problem investigation flow.
9.3 What can be compared
Where matching data exists, possible comparisons include:
- Soil metrics and trends.
- Soil moisture or water availability.
- Normalized water consumption or irrigation metrics.
- Vegetation/crop-condition trends.
- Crop suitability or growth-stage indicators.
- Selected crop-rotation practices and outcomes.
- Yield per area, only when crop, season, unit, and measurement basis are reasonably comparable.
- Current environmental conditions against a regional baseline.
The comparison screen must not imply that every metric exists for every farm. Missing data should be clearly shown as unavailable rather than estimated silently.
9.4 Comparison population
By default, the regional farm comparison uses data that owners have allowed to be included from published farms associated with the selected region. The screen must state that the comparison represents participating, published farms, not necessarily every farm in that city. Where useful, a separate external-data comparison can be provided, but it must be labelled as a different source and not merged invisibly into the public-farm average.

The user should be able to select a supported comparison area such as the same city, broader administrative region, or country, when enough appropriate data exists. The city-level public location rule remains unchanged.
9.5 Logic and calculation steps
1. Identify the user's selected farm and load the owner's authorized private measurements.
2. Identify the selected comparison region using configured administrative boundaries or city associations. Do not expose another farm's exact coordinates to perform this operation.
3. Retrieve only eligible, permissioned data from published farms in that area.
4. Match each metric by unit, measurement method, time window, crop, growing stage, and other context required for a fair comparison.
5. Normalize metrics where needed. For example, total water use is often not comparable between small and large farms, so a per-hectare or crop-output measure may be needed. The correct metric depends on what is being evaluated.
6. Calculate the regional average and preferably also show a median and range. The average is useful but can be skewed by unusually large or extreme values.
7. Show the user's value next to the regional comparison, the difference in original units and, where meaningful, percentage difference.
8. State the number of farms included, time period, data freshness, missingness, and the degree of comparability.
9. Explain the result in plain language without automatically declaring that being above or below average is good or bad.
10. Offer useful next steps, such as viewing the metric history, reading relevant farm experiences, or running a deeper analysis.
9.6 Example: water use
Illustrative numbers only: the user's farm reports normalized water use of 5,000 m³ per hectare over a stated period. Comparable published farms in the selected region have a median of 6,200 m³ per hectare and an average of 6,500 m³ per hectare. Field Shift can say the user's reported value is about 19% below the regional median. It must then explain that lower water use is not automatically better: crop type, climate, output, plant health, growing stage, and measurement reliability must be considered. If yields and crop conditions are comparable, the result may be evidence of relatively lower water use, not proof of greater efficiency.
9.7 Example: vegetation trend
A farm's vegetation indicator has declined by 15% relative to its own seasonal baseline, while the participating farms' median change is a decline of 3%. Field Shift flags the farm's decline as more unusual than the regional pattern and opens a possible-investigation panel. It does not claim to know the cause. It suggests checking irrigation, crop stage, soil conditions, pest/disease observations, and sensor/data quality.
9.8 Recommended display
Each metric card should include:
- The user's farm value and unit.
- Regional average, median, and range where data supports them.
- Direction and magnitude of the difference.
- A plain-language interpretation.
- Number of contributing farms.
- Period and latest update.
- A data-quality or comparability note.
- A link to the underlying farms' approved shared information or relevant discussions, without exposing private data.
9.9 Fairness and quality safeguards
Do not compare raw totals when farm size differs meaningfully. Avoid comparing different crops or growing stages as though they were identical. Exclude stale, invalid, or incompatible values with documented rules. Where outliers may distort the average, show the median and distribution. If the sample is small, label the result low-confidence or unavailable. Never imply a user's farm is inferior solely because its value differs from the average.
10. Diagnosing farm problems using regional context
10.1 Purpose
Use the network to help determine whether something happening on a user's farm looks unusual for that farm and its region, or whether a similar pattern is occurring across the area. This is a decision-support tool, not an automatic diagnosis system.
10.2 Data used
- The user's own time series and farm observations.
- Eligible measurements from published farms in the region.
- Regional environmental and weather history.
- Current weather forecasts when relevant.
- Relevant vegetation or remote-sensing indicators where coverage and resolution are suitable.
- Farmer-reported observations, clearly labelled by verification status.
10.3 Investigation flow
1. Detect a meaningful change on the selected farm, or receive a user-reported problem.
2. Check the farm's own historical baseline to determine whether the change is unusual for that farm and season.
3. Compare with current and recent patterns among eligible published farms in the region.
4. Check whether independent environmental datasets support a broader regional change.
5. Check whether reports are sufficiently recent and whether the farms/crops being compared are reasonably similar.
6. Classify the pattern as possibly regional, possibly farm-specific, mixed/unclear, or insufficient data.
7. Present evidence, uncertainty, alternative explanations, and practical checks. Avoid unsupported certainty.
8. Allow the user to run Deeper Analysis or record what they discover.
10.4 Example: a farm's vegetation drops
A farmer reports that a crop looks weaker than usual. The farm's vegetation indicator has declined noticeably. Other published farms in the same region have broadly stable indicators, and regional water data is not showing a matching decline. Field Shift flags the issue as potentially farm-specific and suggests checking irrigation delivery, local soil moisture, crop stage, pest or disease observations, recent farm activities, and measurement errors.

If many nearby published farms show a similar decline and external environmental data also shows worsening water stress, Field Shift instead says the evidence is consistent with a possible regional issue. It may suggest reviewing irrigation plans and current forecasts. In both cases, it explains that the evidence does not prove a single cause.
10.5 Detection thresholds
Thresholds for an anomaly should be configurable and tested. A proposed approach is to use the farm's seasonal baseline and robust regional distributions, with more confidence when a deviation persists across multiple observations or is supported by independent data. One isolated measurement should generally produce a cautious warning rather than a definitive conclusion. Exact statistical thresholds must be chosen based on the metric, sensor quality, crop, and available history.
11. Regional data store and historical intelligence
11.1 Purpose
Field Shift can organize environmental, weather, and aggregated agricultural information by region so it can compare current conditions with historical patterns, support seasonal planning, and explain whether a current reading is unusual.
11.2 Potential regional record contents
- Historical weather values and relevant climatological baselines.
- Current and historical forecast records where the provider makes them available and storage/use is permitted.
- Soil-moisture or water-stress datasets.
- Vegetation indicators and their time periods.
- Soil or terrain datasets at their documented spatial resolution.
- Aggregated published-farm indicators and regional sample counts.
- Timestamped farmer-reported problems and their verification status.
- Selected crop calendars or seasonal agricultural context from appropriate sources.
- Data source, version, retrieval time, geographic coverage, units, quality flags, and limitations.
11.3 Region and time handling
Regions should use stable administrative identifiers where possible, instead of relying only on text names. A city name may appear in more than one country, and administrative boundaries can change. Each data record should identify the region, dataset, measurement period, unit, source, and retrieval timestamp.

Historical values must not be overwritten by newer values without preserving the period they describe. Corrections to data should be traceable. The system should distinguish the date a condition occurred from the date the data became available.
11.4 Example: understanding a hot day
A farm is experiencing a 35°C day. The regional record shows that this temperature is common in that region at this time of year, but the same record also shows unusually low soil moisture compared with prior seasons. Field Shift can explain that temperature alone may be typical while the combination with low soil moisture deserves attention. Crop type, stage, irrigation, and forecast conditions still matter.
11.5 Important distinction
Historical records describe what happened and help define normal ranges or seasonal tendencies. They do not by themselves provide a dependable short-term weather forecast. Forecasts should come from appropriate weather APIs or meteorological model services, and Field Shift should record the provider, issue time, valid period, and update time when available.
12. Weather and environmental forecast support
12.1 Purpose
Combine weather-service forecasts with regional history and farm context to support planning. This is an extension of the regional intelligence system, not a claim that farm crowd data can replace weather models.
12.2 Logic
1. Retrieve forecasts from a configured weather API or trusted source.
2. Store/associate the forecast with its issue time, valid time, location coverage, provider, units, and retrieval time.
3. Compare forecast conditions with relevant historical seasonal patterns and farm requirements.
4. Use the user's crop, soil, water situation, and selected priorities to explain potential implications.
5. Update the view when a newer forecast arrives and retain past forecast snapshots if permitted, so forecast performance can later be evaluated.
6. Label forecasts as predictions, not observations. Label historical baselines and farmer reports separately.
12.3 Example
A farm plans to plant a crop in two weeks. A weather service indicates a forecast period of higher-than-usual temperatures and limited rain. Field Shift compares that outlook with the region's historical seasonal pattern, the farm's crop plan, and its water constraints. It can advise the user to review planting timing and water planning, while clearly stating the forecast uncertainty and source.
13. Regional alerts and early warnings
13.1 Purpose
Notify users about relevant regional developments instead of requiring them to check every map layer manually.
13.2 Alert categories
- Official weather warnings or forecast changes from supported services.
- Important environmental changes in available datasets.
- Increased farmer reports of a potential issue in a region.
- Significant crop-condition or vegetation trends where coverage is appropriate.
- Relevant community posts or new discussions, if the user follows that topic/region.
- A change in the user's own farm relative to its baseline or regional peers.
13.3 Alert decision logic
Each alert should identify the trigger, source, period, geography, confidence, and what the user can do next. Use configurable thresholds and deduplication so minor repeated changes do not produce floods of notifications. Farmer-reported patterns must be described as reports, not confirmed outbreaks or official warnings. Official alerts must retain their source and severity language.
13.4 Example
Several farmers in one region submit recent reports of water shortages. The platform detects an unusual increase in reports and sends an alert to users who opted into regional water updates. The alert says “Multiple farmers are reporting water shortages in this region,” shows the time window and number of reports if privacy rules allow, and distinguishes this signal from an official water authority warning.
14. Farmer connections and messaging
14.1 Purpose
Let farmers contact one another directly from the map, a public farm profile, search results, or a community. Communication should be connected to useful agricultural context but should still behave like a familiar chat application.
14.2 Core functions
- Open a public farmer profile and send a message where the recipient permits it.
- Send, receive, and read direct messages.
- View conversation history and unread-message indicators.
- Add a user as a friend, accept/decline a request where friend requests are used, and remove a friend.
- Block and unblock users.
- Mute and unmute notifications for a person or conversation.
- Manage message-request permissions and who can start conversations.
- Report spam, harassment, impersonation, or unsafe content.
- Optionally archive conversations and manage notification preferences.
- Provide common usability features, such as timestamps, delivery state where supported, and attachments if the product's storage and safety design supports them.
14.3 Friendship is not the same as visibility
Adding someone as a friend must not automatically reveal their exact farm location or any farm fields they have not published. Public farm visibility is governed by farm-publication settings; private relationships do not override those rules.
14.4 Blocking, muting, and unmuting
- Mute: Stop or reduce notifications from a person or conversation while leaving the relationship otherwise intact.
- Unmute: Restore the selected notifications.
- Block: Prevent the blocked user from initiating direct contact and apply the product's documented interaction restrictions. Do not reveal private data through other screens.
- Unblock: Remove the block restrictions according to the product rules; it should not automatically recreate a friendship or restore any previously removed permissions.
A block should take precedence over friend status, direct-message access, community interactions where appropriate, and contact recommendations. Exact behaviour for shared public posts must be documented consistently.
14.5 Example
A farmer sees a public farm profile in a city and wants to ask about a crop rotation. They select Message, send a question, and the recipient can reply or manage the request. If the conversation becomes unwanted, the recipient can mute it or block the sender. None of these actions reveals a private farm coordinate.
15. Communities and farming groups
15.1 Purpose
Allow users to create communities around any relevant reason, not only predefined categories. Groups let users ask questions and share experience with people who have a specific connection or interest.
15.2 Possible community types
- Same city or region.
- Similar soil type.
- Water availability or irrigation constraints.
- Crop type or rotation interest.
- Similar environmental conditions or climate.
- Shared priorities, such as water saving, soil health, resilience, or yield stability.
- Farming techniques or agricultural research topics.
- Friend groups or invite-only networks.
- Custom topics created by users.
15.3 Community functions
- Create a group with a name, description, topic tags, profile image, and rules.
- Choose public, private, or request-to-join membership.
- Search, discover, join, leave, invite, and manage membership.
- Publish posts and replies, share permitted images/files, and pin useful information.
- Set admins/moderators and review reports.
- Mute/unmute group notifications and manage message/post preferences.
- Link a post to a public farm, region, crop, or shared metric without exposing private farm information.
- Keep community-specific data separate from public farm profile data and from the core recommendation engine.
15.4 Example
A user creates “Water-Saving Rotations for Sandy Soils.” Members share crops and irrigation practices they have tried, and users can add voluntary details about their environment. Field Shift may help members find relevant regional comparisons, but it should not treat every group discussion as validated agricultural evidence.
15.5 Moderation and trust
Every community needs a way to report content, handle spam, remove harmful or misleading posts under published rules, and appeal moderation where appropriate. A community must not be able to require its members to publish exact farm locations or other sensitive fields.
16. Agricultural knowledge library and searchable case studies
16.1 Purpose
Useful information should not disappear into old chats. The platform can make selected public discussions and farm experiences searchable by conditions, crop, technique, region, and reported outcome.
16.2 Logic
1. A user or moderator marks a useful discussion or submits a structured case study.
2. Field Shift records the relevant context: crop, soil, climate, water constraint, practice, season, outcome, limitations, and source status where known.
3. Search indexes only public or permissioned content.
4. Users filter results by condition and topic.
5. The result explains the setting and limitations, so a farmer can judge whether the experience is relevant.
AI may help summarize or tag content, but it should preserve source links and distinguish a summary from a verified fact. Content should not be used to train or alter a decision engine without the appropriate permission and evidence process.
16.3 Example
A user searches for “legumes in sandy soil with limited irrigation.” The library returns a set of public case studies and discussions. Each result shows the climate and soil context, technique tried, farmer-reported outcome, and any known limitations. The user can then ask follow-up questions in the related community.
17. Tracking rotations and real farm outcomes
17.1 Purpose
Let farmers record what they planted, how the season progressed, and what happened at harvest. This creates a history that is useful to the farmer and can contribute to network intelligence when the farmer opts in.
17.2 Possible tracked information
- Planned and actual crop sequence.
- Planting/harvest dates and growing stage.
- Water usage and irrigation practice, if measured or reported.
- Soil indicators before and after a rotation, if available.
- Yield per area and crop quality where suitable.
- Crop failures, pest/disease issues, unusual weather, or management changes.
- The original Field Shift recommendation and its version/date.
- The farmer's notes about what worked and what did not.
17.3 Logic
Store a time-stamped record of the planned rotation, actual events, measurements, and source status. Compare planned versus actual outcomes. When data is used for network statistics, apply the owner's sharing choices and compare only compatible observations. Changes to calculation rules or recommendation versions should remain traceable so outcomes are not judged against an undocumented moving target.
17.4 Example
Field Shift recommends a rotation aimed at improving soil health while reducing water demand. The farmer records that they followed the sequence, reports irrigation use, and later adds harvest and soil measurements. Field Shift can show the farmer's own before-and-after history. If the owner shares the outcome, eligible fields may contribute to broader comparisons with similar farms.
18. Collaborative farming experiments
18.1 Purpose
Farmers with similar conditions can coordinate informal trials and share the outcomes of different practices. The network can help structure the observations so results are easier to compare.
18.2 Logic
1. A user creates a trial topic and states the question, practice options, conditions of interest, and the measurements to record.
2. Participants volunteer and choose what data to share.
3. Field Shift records the context and the participants' observation schedule.
4. The system summarizes results, missing data, and differences among farms.
5. The conclusion is labelled exploratory unless the design and evidence justify stronger claims.
The platform should not present a casual comparison as a controlled experiment. Farms may differ in soil, management, weather, crop variety, and other factors that affect outcomes.
18.3 Example
Several farmers want to compare two irrigation schedules for a particular crop. Field Shift helps them record water use, crop stage, soil type, and harvest outcome. The final report shows the observed pattern and differences among participants, but warns that the results do not prove that the schedule alone caused the outcome.
19. Seasonal planning and regional agricultural context
19.1 Purpose
Use regional history, current forecasts, crop calendars, and eligible community evidence to help users prepare for a coming season.
19.2 Logic
- Identify the user's intended crop and planting window.
- Show relevant historical seasonal conditions and current forecast information separately.
- Compare the farm's conditions with the crop's known requirements and the user's priorities.
- Present relevant experiences from comparable published farms where evidence exists.
- Explain risks, uncertainties, and options to investigate.
19.3 Example
Before planting, a farmer reviews recent forecasts, historical dry-season conditions, the water constraints of their farm, and experiences shared by farmers growing a similar crop. Field Shift can help them compare possible planting windows or rotations. It must not promise that a particular forecast will occur or that a community result will repeat on their farm.
20. Evaluating and potentially improving Field Shift recommendations
20.1 Purpose
Use accumulated, appropriately permissioned outcomes to evaluate whether the decision engine's recommendations perform as expected under different conditions. This is a long-term benefit and requires quality control.
20.2 Evidence pipeline
1. Capture the original recommendation, its inputs, and the version of the logic/model used.
2. Collect voluntary follow-up results from farmers.
3. Check data completeness, source, time alignment, crop and context compatibility, and potential measurement errors.
4. Compare predicted outcomes with reported or measured outcomes without implying causation where the data cannot support it.
5. Analyze consistent patterns across enough independent farms and seasons.
6. Have the product/scientific validation process review potential changes.
7. Test proposed changes offline and against validation cases before deployment.
8. Version the change and monitor whether it improves performance.
Community data should not immediately change the core score or recommendation automatically. Initially, it should be used for contextual insights and evaluation. Only validated evidence should influence production calculations, and all changes should be traceable.
20.3 Example
Field Shift repeatedly recommends a rotation for a certain soil and climate profile. Several seasons of adequately documented outcomes show that the expected water reduction is not consistently achieved under a particular irrigation setup. The team investigates the conditions and tests a change to the decision logic. The network supports the evaluation, but a few posts or one unusual season are not enough to justify a global change.
21. Regional insights and pattern detection
21.1 Purpose
Find useful patterns across multiple public farms and environmental data, rather than treating every farm as an isolated case.
21.2 Example patterns
- Many participating farms in a region report water shortages.
- Several farms with similar soil types report an unexpected crop-condition decline.
- One farm's metric is substantially different from the regional pattern.
- A regional environmental indicator is unusual compared with the historical baseline.
- Farmers are reporting a new problem that deserves investigation.
21.3 Logic
Use comparable time windows, a minimum evidence-quality check, counts of contributing farms, and the relevant data-source status. Separate correlation from causation. A pattern should link to the metrics and observations supporting it, and show the uncertainty or data gaps.
21.4 Example
The regional dashboard notices a rise in farmer-reported irrigation problems during a period when a relevant environmental dataset shows falling soil moisture. It presents the combined evidence as a possible regional water-stress pattern, labels the report counts and dates, and recommends reviewing the relevant official information. It does not announce a confirmed emergency unless an authoritative source has done so.
22. Notifications and personal relevance
Notifications should be useful and controlled rather than overwhelming. A user can follow selected cities/regions, communities, friends, farm metrics, or alert categories. They can mute a person or conversation, mute a community, or adjust regional alert settings separately.
Example
A farmer follows regional water conditions and a community about their crop. They receive a regional update when a supported alert threshold is reached and a separate notification when a community member replies to their question. Muting the community does not have to disable critical account/security messages, and system messages should be categorised clearly.
23. Data sources, provenance, and confidence
Every metric used in public profiles, regional scores, comparisons, or diagnostics needs provenance. The platform should track, where available:
- Source/provider or submitting user.
- Whether the value is sensor-measured, externally retrieved, manually reported, estimated, or independently verified.
- Units, measurement method, and timestamp/period.
- Retrieval time and update interval.
- Geographic coverage and spatial resolution.
- Data quality flags, missingness, and any transformations.
- The calculation version used to generate a score or aggregate.
Example
A regional soil-moisture value is labelled as an external dataset estimate with its spatial resolution and date. A farmer's irrigation figure is labelled as farmer-reported unless a suitable record supports it. The user can see both, but the interface does not present them as equally direct measurements.
24. Privacy, safety, and trust requirements
24.1 Location privacy is mandatory
- Public farm location is city-level only; there is no exact-location publishing option.
- Exact coordinates and private boundary geometry must not be included in public API responses, search records, profile HTML, map payloads, analytics events visible to other users, or public export files.
- Do not show directions, exact distances, or map zoom that could reconstruct a farm's precise placement.
- When an owner views their own farm, the private map view can retain the detail needed for their farm work, subject to existing access rules.
- Public aggregate calculations must use appropriate internal region associations without exposing underlying coordinates.
24.2 Public data must be deliberately selected
A public profile is generated from an explicit allowlist of owner-approved information. Private and public farm data should be separated by access rules and server-side filtering. A hidden front-end control is not sufficient protection.
24.3 Aggregation and re-identification
Regional statistics can still expose information when only one farm contributes or when a region is very small. Recommended safeguards include minimum sample thresholds for sensitive metrics, broader geographic roll-ups when needed, suppressing or coarsening sensitive statistics, and showing sample counts. These safeguards should complement the city-only location rule rather than replacing it.
24.4 Social safety
Provide account controls, blocking, reporting, rate limits, anti-spam measures, moderation tools, and clear rules. A user who blocks someone should not continue to be exposed to unwanted direct contact through another network entry point. Public posts must not be used to bypass farm sharing permissions.
24.5 Data ownership and deletion
Owners must be able to unpublish or remove shared farm information, and users must be able to remove eligible posts and community contributions. Define how deletion affects caches, search indexes, recent aggregates, backups, and legally required records. Communicate any limitations clearly.
25. Recommended technical architecture and data flow
The Global Farming Network requires shared cloud-backed data. A purely local-only website storage model cannot by itself support public global profiles, synchronized friend relationships, cross-user messaging, shared communities, or regional aggregation. The exact provider and architecture should be chosen during implementation, but these responsibilities need to exist somewhere in the system.
25.1 Logical data areas
1. Private farm records: Owner-only farm data, precise coordinates, private notes, and full analyses.
2. Public farm projections: Only approved, safe fields for farms that are published.
3. Metric snapshots: Time-stamped values with units, provenance, data quality and permission labels.
4. Regional aggregates: City/region summaries derived from eligible public data, with a calculation version and contributing sample count.
5. Environmental datasets: Regional historical values and external data with provider, period, and spatial-resolution metadata.
6. Social graph: Friend requests, friend relationships, blocks, mutes and account-level communication settings.
7. Conversations and messages: Authorized private messages, delivery/read state where supported, and moderation/report metadata.
8. Communities: Community metadata, membership, roles, posts, replies, attachments and moderation actions.
9. Farm outcomes and case studies: Planned rotations, voluntary results, public experiences and their verification status.
10. Alerts and subscriptions: User preferences and notifications generated from eligible triggers.
11. Audit and job records: Publication changes, aggregate rebuilds, data refresh jobs, moderation events, and operational errors.
25.2 Main data flow
Farm owner publishes -> server checks owner permission and fields -> public projection is created -> farm becomes searchable at city level -> eligible snapshots enter the city aggregation pipeline -> city score and regional statistics are rebuilt -> map and comparison panels read the approved results.

External dataset refreshes -> provider/source metadata is retained -> regional historical series is updated -> separate environmental layers and comparison/diagnostic services use the new values -> UI shows source and update time.

User reports a farm problem -> the system retrieves the user's private history plus eligible regional aggregates -> anomaly/diagnostic logic compares farm and regional patterns -> the UI presents evidence, possible explanations, and limitations.

Farmers communicate -> access and block rules are checked -> messages/posts are stored in their relevant social context -> permitted content can be discovered or summarized, but is not automatically treated as validated scientific evidence.
25.3 Suggested core entities
The implementation can use different database names, but it needs equivalent records for:
- User account and public profile.
- Farm private record.
- Farm publication settings and public projection.
- Farm metric snapshot and measurement provenance.
- Region/city identity and supported boundaries.
- Regional aggregate and score version.
- External dataset record and refresh metadata.
- Friend request and relationship state.
- Block and mute settings.
- Conversation and message.
- Community, membership, post, reply, attachment, and report.
- Crop rotation plan, outcome record, and case study.
- Alert rule, subscription, and generated notification.
- Audit log and background-job status.
25.4 Synchronization, refresh, and cache rules
Use clear refresh schedules for external datasets and regional aggregates. Public pages should not display a new “current” score until the source data and aggregation jobs have completed successfully. If a refresh fails, retain the last valid result with its old timestamp and a stale-data indicator rather than silently displaying it as current. Publication/unpublication changes need to invalidate affected public caches and search records.
25.5 Security and resilience
Use server-side authorization for every read and write. Do not rely only on client-side hiding. Validate user-supplied values, rate-limit messages and posts, protect API credentials, use secure authentication, enforce limits on uploads, and log sensitive administrative access. Provide retry/backoff for external API failures and monitoring for jobs that stop updating.
26. Example: one problem using the whole network
A farmer's crop condition is declining. The farmer opens their farm in Field Shift and chooses Compare with Region. The system shows that the farm's vegetation trend is worse than the median among comparable published farms in the same city, while the regional distribution is mostly stable. It also shows that the user's soil-moisture reading is lower than its own historical baseline.

Field Shift explains that the combination may be farm-specific, but it does not assert a cause. The farmer checks irrigation and soil moisture, then opens a community for similar crops and asks whether others have noticed a comparable problem. A few farmers share relevant experiences. Those reports are clearly marked as community reports, not verified diagnoses.

If other evidence begins to show the same regional pattern, the regional dashboard can update its analysis and, when thresholds and quality rules are met, notify users who follow the region. The farmer records the action they took and the eventual outcome. If they choose to share that outcome, it can contribute to future comparisons. Over time, appropriately validated outcomes may help Field Shift evaluate its own recommendations.

This example demonstrates the intended integration: private farm analysis, city-level public discovery, regional comparison, messaging/community knowledge, alerts, history, and evidence evaluation all work together without exposing exact farm locations.
27. Complete use-case catalogue
This catalogue consolidates the uses discussed for the feature. Each use case states its purpose, operating logic, and an example.
Use case 1: Discover public farms globally
Purpose: Help users find participating farms around the world.
Logic: Search or select a city; show farms that chose to publish there; expose only city-level locations. Private farms are excluded.
Example: A user exploring a country sees that several cities have published farms and opens one city's farm list.
Use case 2: View a detailed public farm profile
Purpose: Learn about another farm's shared conditions and practices.
Logic: Clicking a public farm opens the detailed filtered profile, which includes only owner-approved fields.
Example: A user reviews another farm's shared soil texture, crop sequence, and water-use trend without seeing its coordinates.
Use case 3: Search for cities, countries, and farm names
Purpose: Navigate directly to a place or farm.
Logic: Return permission-filtered results; selecting a city opens its summary/list, selecting a country moves the map to that region, and selecting a farm opens its public profile.
Example: Searching for “Sunrise Orchard” opens the matching public profile and highlights its city.
Use case 4: Show city conditions with a green-to-red map colour
Purpose: Give an at-a-glance summary of conditions across published farms in a city.
Logic: Aggregate eligible data only from farms published in that city, using consistent time periods and a documented index; show sample size and uncertainty.
Example: A city is yellow because its participating farms have mixed condition indicators, and the city panel explains which indicators contribute to that score.
Use case 5: Navigate between the owner's own farms
Purpose: Make it easy for a user to find their farms on the map.
Logic: The side panel lists the user's own farms; selecting a row navigates the owner's private map view to the selected farm. This does not expose that location publicly.
Example: A farmer with three farms selects “North Field” from the side panel and the map centres on its saved location in the owner view.
Use case 6: Compare a farm with regional averages
Purpose: Show how a user's farm differs from participating farms in the same area.
Logic: Match comparable metrics and periods, normalize where needed, calculate average/median/range, and show sample size and limitations.
Example: A farm's normalized water use is below the regional median; Field Shift explains that this may be useful context but does not automatically mean the farm is more efficient.
Use case 7: Investigate whether a problem is regional or farm-specific
Purpose: Help farmers narrow down possible causes of an unusual condition.
Logic: Compare the farm with its own history, the regional distribution, and independent environmental data; report possible explanations with uncertainty.
Example: One farm has a vegetation decline while other local farms remain stable, prompting checks of its irrigation and soil conditions.
Use case 8: Identify wider regional patterns
Purpose: Detect meaningful changes across multiple farms.
Logic: Aggregate comparable, recent observations and cross-check external datasets where appropriate.
Example: Several participating farms show water stress and an environmental dataset indicates falling moisture, prompting a possible regional-trend insight.
Use case 9: Store regional agricultural and environmental history
Purpose: Give current measurements context from previous seasons and years.
Logic: Store timestamped, source-labelled regional time series with units, coverage, and version details.
Example: A current hot day is compared with historical seasonal temperature and soil-moisture patterns to determine whether the combination is unusual.
Use case 10: Support forecasts using weather APIs
Purpose: Help users plan while distinguishing predictions from observations.
Logic: Retrieve forecast data from supported services, retain forecast time/valid period, and interpret it alongside historical baselines and farm needs.
Example: A forecast suggests a hot, dry period; Field Shift helps the farmer review planting timing and water planning without guaranteeing the forecast.
Use case 11: Notify users of regional conditions
Purpose: Proactively inform users who follow relevant locations or topics.
Logic: Trigger alerts using configured thresholds, official warnings, or clearly labelled increases in community reports; deduplicate repeated alerts.
Example: Followers receive a notice that multiple farmers are reporting water shortages, explicitly distinguished from an official warning.
Use case 12: Connect with other farmers directly
Purpose: Enable practical one-to-one knowledge exchange.
Logic: A user selects Message from a public profile; the platform checks message permissions and blocks.
Example: A farmer asks another farmer about their experience with a published crop rotation.
Use case 13: Manage friends, blocks, and notifications
Purpose: Give users control over relationships and unwanted contact.
Logic: Add/remove friends, block/unblock, and mute/unmute notification sources with consistent permission rules.
Example: A user mutes a busy conversation without blocking the other person, then later unmutes it.
Use case 14: Join or create communities
Purpose: Bring together people around shared agricultural interests.
Logic: Discover or create groups, set access rules, manage members, post and reply, and moderate content.
Example: Farmers with sandy soils and limited irrigation join a community to discuss water-saving rotations.
Use case 15: Preserve knowledge in a searchable library
Purpose: Make useful agricultural experiences reusable beyond a chat timeline.
Logic: Organize public, permissioned discussions and case studies by crop, soil, climate, practice, and outcome, with clear source status.
Example: A search for “legumes in sandy soil” finds related case studies with the conditions and limitations visible.
Use case 16: Track crop rotations and outcomes
Purpose: Help farmers build a season-by-season record and voluntarily share results.
Logic: Store planned/actual crop sequence, measurement dates, outcomes, and original recommendation version; compare like with like.
Example: A farmer records the result of a rotation and later compares water use and yield with the original plan.
Use case 17: Run collaborative farming trials
Purpose: Let farmers share structured observations about alternative practices.
Logic: Define a question and measurements, gather opt-in records, compare outcomes, and clearly label exploratory results.
Example: Several farms compare irrigation schedules and Field Shift summarizes results and limitations without claiming proof of causation.
Use case 18: Support seasonal planning
Purpose: Help users plan crops with regional and historical context.
Logic: Combine crop requirements, current forecasts, historical season patterns, and relevant farm experiences.
Example: A farmer considers a planting window using the regional dry-season history and the current forecast.
Use case 19: Evaluate and potentially improve recommendations
Purpose: Determine whether Field Shift's recommendation logic performs as expected.
Logic: Compare versioned predictions against reliable follow-up outcomes, validate patterns, test changes, and deploy only reviewed improvements.
Example: Repeated documented outcomes suggest a particular water-saving estimate is unreliable for one context, so the team investigates and tests a targeted revision.
Use case 20: Give regional context to environmental readings
Purpose: Help users interpret a measurement instead of viewing it in isolation.
Logic: Compare current values with the region's historical baseline and relevant seasonal context, while accounting for dataset resolution.
Example: A moisture reading appears low compared with the region's usual conditions, prompting the user to review irrigation and crop needs.
Use case 21: Help users discover relevant people and discussions
Purpose: Reduce the effort required to find useful expertise.
Logic: Use opt-in profile tags, farm conditions the user has chosen to share, community topics, and user-selected interests to suggest relevant people and groups. Do not use private farm data to reveal or target users without permission.
Example: A farmer interested in soil regeneration discovers a public community and farmers who have chosen to discuss related practices.
Use case 22: Build regional agricultural summaries
Purpose: Present a concise view of available information for a region.
Logic: Combine eligible public-farm aggregates with separately labelled external environmental data and report coverage and source differences.
Example: A region page displays the published-farm condition distribution, current environmental indicators, data freshness, and active public discussions.
Use case 23: Keep users informed about their own farm relative to a changing region
Purpose: Detect when a farm begins to diverge from regional patterns or returns toward typical conditions.
Logic: Recalculate comparisons as new eligible data arrives, show trend direction, and avoid interpreting one fresh datapoint as a meaningful shift.
Example: A farm that previously differed sharply from regional vegetation trends moves closer to the regional range after the farmer changes irrigation; Field Shift displays the trend without claiming the change was solely responsible.
28. Failure states and edge cases
- No public farms in a city: The city has no public-farm list. Do not imply that the city has no agriculture; only say there are no farms published to Field Shift there.
- Published farm but insufficient metrics: Show the farm profile and indicate unavailable metrics. Do not fabricate a condition score.
- Stale farm snapshot: Show the last update and mark the data as stale according to the relevant metric's refresh expectations.
- City name collision: Use country/state labels and stable region IDs to disambiguate.
- Farm changes city association: Recompute its public association and affected regional aggregates; preserve an audit trail where required.
- Farm is unpublished: Remove it from public search/profile routes and future aggregate input according to the documented removal process.
- Metric units differ: Convert safely if definitions are equivalent; otherwise do not combine them.
- Different crops or growing stages: Avoid direct yield/performance comparisons unless the system can account for the difference.
- Conflicting reports: Display variation and uncertainty rather than picking one claim silently.
- Weather API unavailable: Show the last valid forecast with its timestamp and mark it stale, or show that forecast data is temporarily unavailable.
- Regional data source changes resolution: Preserve metadata and avoid presenting a broad regional estimate as a precise farm measurement.
- A user blocks another user: Prevent the blocked relationship from bypassing controls through map/profile/community entry points as defined in policy.
- Small sample size: Show a caution/insufficient-data state or broaden the area only with clear user-visible notice.
- Aggregate refresh fails: Keep the previous valid result and its timestamp; do not imply that the refresh succeeded.
29. Recommended delivery order
Phase 1: Public map and publication foundation
Build the shared-data foundation, publication controls, public farm projection, strict city-only location display, city search, farm-name search, city-to-farm list, public profiles, and the owner's My Farms side panel. Add source/last-updated display and the first documented city condition-score calculation only when the required data is available.
Phase 2: Regional comparison and problem investigation
Implement Compare My Farm with Regional Averages, comparable-metric rules, regional sample size and freshness, farm-vs-region trend checks, and cautious investigation summaries. This phase delivers a direct analytical benefit even before the social network is large.
Phase 3: Social connections and communities
Implement friend requests/relationships, direct messages, block/unblock, mute/unmute, message permissions, communities, membership controls, content reporting, and moderation tools.
Phase 4: Regional history and alerts
Build the regional environmental history store, supported forecast integration, regional subscriptions, alert triggers, stale-data handling, and source labels.
Phase 5: Outcomes, knowledge library, and evidence evaluation
Add season-by-season rotation tracking, case studies, collaborative trials, searchable farming knowledge, and a validation workflow for evaluating recommendations against eligible real-world outcomes.

These phases are a recommended sequence, not a requirement to remove the long-term features from the master specification. The architecture should leave room for them, even if they are not shipped initially.
30. Acceptance criteria
The feature should not be considered complete until the relevant checks below pass.
Map and location
- A published farm appears only at city level in all public views.
- No public API payload, profile page, search index, or map event exposes its exact coordinates or plot boundaries.
- Clicking a city opens the correct published-farm list.
- Clicking a public farm opens its detailed public profile.
- The owner can navigate between their own farms using the side panel without changing public visibility.
- City colour uses only eligible published farms in that city and displays its meaning, data count, and update time.
- Insufficient data is not disguised as a green or red score.
Profiles and search
- Public profiles include only owner-approved, safe fields.
- Unpublished farms do not appear in public search or open through old public routes.
- City/country/farm-name search results are correctly disambiguated and route to the appropriate view.
Regional comparison and diagnosis
- Comparison metrics are unit-compatible and use comparable periods and contexts.
- The UI shows sample size, summary statistic, range where available, timestamp, and important limitations.
- The system distinguishes a possible regional pattern from a possible farm-specific anomaly without claiming a cause it cannot support.
- External data, farmer reports, and verified observations are visibly distinguished.
Social and communities
- Users can add/remove friends, block/unblock, and mute/unmute notifications.
- Blocking and privacy controls apply consistently across messages, search, profiles, and communities.
- Users can create/join/leave communities with the configured access rules.
- Reporting and moderation workflows are available.
Data quality and reliability
- Regional history preserves period, source, unit, retrieval time, and coverage metadata.
- Forecasts are labelled as forecasts, not historical observations.
- Failed refreshes retain a timestamped last-valid value or display an explicit unavailable state.
- Recommendation changes are not driven automatically by unvalidated community posts.
31. Metrics for evaluating the feature
Potential product-health measures include:
- Number of farms intentionally published and percentage with recent eligible data.
- Number of cities/regions with enough data to show reliable summaries.
- Percentage of public profiles with complete source and freshness labels.
- Usage of Compare My Farm with Regional Averages and repeat use over time.
- Number of meaningful comparisons that lead to a user opening a detailed analysis or relevant resource.
- Community participation, useful replies, and successful moderation response times.
- Frequency of stale-data or failed-refresh states.
- User reports about inaccurate profiles, unsafe contact, or misleading regional summaries.
- Coverage and quality of outcomes used to evaluate crop-rotation recommendations.
Do not optimize only for the number of messages or public profiles. The goal is useful, trusted agricultural information and a better-informed farming decision, not engagement for its own sake.
32. Final product definition
The Global Farming Network is a privacy-first, city-level public directory and intelligence layer connecting participating farms worldwide. Its map shows cities coloured according to conditions calculated from published farms in those cities; city selection opens public farm profiles, and farm selection opens a detailed but privacy-filtered view. Users can search for places and farms, connect through friends and direct messages, manage blocks and notifications, and create or join agricultural communities around any shared interest.

The network also enables farmers to compare their own farms with regional averages, investigate whether a problem is local or regional, explore regional environmental history, receive relevant alerts, learn from searchable case studies, track crop rotations and outcomes, and participate in collaborative trials. Weather forecasts come from appropriate forecast services, while historical regional data provides context rather than pretending to predict weather on its own. Over time, well-governed real-world outcomes can help evaluate Field Shift's recommendations and may support validated improvements to the decision engine.

The central idea: published farms create a privacy-safe picture of agriculture at city and regional level; the community turns that picture into shared knowledge; and Field Shift uses reliable, appropriately permissioned evidence to help farmers understand their own farms and make better decisions.
ANNEX G — COMPLETE FARM STATE, CROP LIFECYCLE, AND SHARED DATA SYSTEM SPECIFICATION
