Field Shift — Farm State & Crop Lifecycle + Shared Farm Data System
Specification type: Approved product and architecture extension
Status: Approved for documentation; implementation follows an audit of the current repository
Scope: Farm State and Crop Lifecycle System + Shared Farm Data System
The Shared Farm Data System is the foundation. Crop Cycle is the visible capability farmers interact with.

1. Purpose and scope
This specification documents two approved, connected foundations for Field Shift: (1) the Farm State and Crop Lifecycle System, the visible capability farmers use to track what is planned and actually happening; and (2) the Shared Farm Data System, the common data architecture that keeps relevant pages consistent.

It extends the existing Home, Maps, Farm Analysis, New Analysis, Rotations, Calendar, Deep Analysis, What-If Simulator, and Global Farming Network plans. It does not replace their existing requirements.

Out of scope for this approval: a separate Action and Follow-up System, Data Reliability and Confidence dashboard, Recommendation and Outcome Tracking feature, and Decision Centre. These ideas were rejected for now and must not be added through this document.
2. Product principles
One farm identity: a farm has a stable internal ID independent of its displayed name. Each tracked field/zone also has a stable ID.

Recommended does not mean planned; planned does not mean planted. Actual crop state must be explicitly confirmed or supplied by a clearly identified trusted integration.

Preserve history: new plans, changed dates, crop changes, and new analysis runs must not silently overwrite historical records.

Separate real and hypothetical information. What-If scenarios must never mutate actual farm state.

Keep the existing professional dark agricultural-intelligence visual direction: deep navy/forest tones, restrained green/teal accents, clear hierarchy, realistic data visualisation, no neon or glowing-AI styling.

Do not invent missing data. If planting date, crop stage, area, or current crop is unknown, display that it is unknown/unconfirmed instead of calculating a false countdown.

Public sharing must remain privacy-safe. Global Farming Network must never expose exact farm location; public farm location remains city-level only.
3. Definitions and lifecycle states
Farm: top-level agricultural property or operation, identified by immutable farm_id.

Field/zone: separately trackable growing area within a farm. A farm can initially have no zones and later have multiple. The farm boundary is not automatically the same as an individual crop zone.

Recommendation: output of a particular New Analysis run. It is a proposal, not proof of planting or farmer commitment.

Crop plan: a farmer-selected rotation or crop sequence saved for future use. Saving a plan does not mean planting has occurred.

Crop cycle: the lifecycle record connecting a field/zone to a planned or actual sequence of crops and dates.

Activity: dated event such as planting, transplanting, irrigation, fertiliser application, inspection, pest observation, harvest, or crop change.

Recommended: generated by New Analysis; not active.

Planned: farmer explicitly saves/selects a recommendation as a plan and confirms intention to use it. Dates are intentions, not actual events.

Active: farmer confirms planting or another applicable crop-cycle start event. Record actual date, crop and field/zone. Do not automatically activate because a planned date has passed.

Paused/changed: farmer indicates the cycle is paused, replaced, abandoned, or differs from plan. Preserve the original plan and record the change.

Completed: farmer confirms crop-cycle/crop completion; record actual completion date if supplied.

An individual crop item has its own state and dates. A rotation may be Active while its first crop is Active and later crops are Upcoming. Support 'Not confirmed' when data is incomplete.
4. Where the feature appears
Home: keep the existing compact dashboard. Add a small Crop Cycle summary for the selected farm/field: confirmed active crop/stage when known, lifecycle state, next planned milestone, and a Manage Crop Cycle link. Do not expand Home into a large management dashboard. If no crop is confirmed active, show planned/not-started.

Farm Analysis: primary management surface. Add a Crop Cycle section near rotation details. Show selected field, selected rotation, crop sequence, planned and actual dates separately, lifecycle status, confirmed current crop if any, and next relevant milestone. A Manage Crop Cycle action opens a panel/drawer/modal consistent with existing UI.

Maps: retain existing farm-boundary drawing/editing. Support optional field/zone polygons for different crops. Selecting a zone opens an information panel with crop-cycle status and a link to Farm Analysis. Farm boundary and crop-zone geometry are separate concepts. Removing a drawing must not delete farm/crop history.

Calendar: keep it as a separate main-navigation page. Display events linked to crop-cycle records: planned planting, expected harvest, inspection and other supported events. Changes to future planned dates update linked future planned events by defined rules; completed events/history remain intact.

New Analysis: each run retains inputs, priorities, data context, candidate rotations, scores, explanations and model/calculation version where available. Provide Save as Plan. This requires explicit confirmation and creates a planned record; it does not mark planting as active.

Rotations: show saved plans and their source analysis run. Distinguish reusable rotation templates from a rotation assigned to a particular farm/field if both exist.

Deep Analysis: read the same farm/field IDs, confirmed crop state, dates, activity history and relevant analysis history. It can refresh data and create a new investigation/log but must not silently change crop state or rewrite history.

What-If Simulator: initialise a scenario from a selected recommendation/plan and its assumptions. Store hypothetical inputs/results separately. They must not update actual crop cycles, dates, Calendar events or farm status.

Global Farming Network: public data is a separate allowlisted view. Public location remains city-level only; never expose exact coordinates or boundaries. Publishing a profile does not automatically publish all crop, activity, yield or soil-test records.
5. Crop Cycle interface details
Header: 'Crop Cycle', selected farm/field, and a lifecycle badge such as Planned, Active, Changed, Completed or Not confirmed.

Rotation summary: crop sequence and link to its source recommendation. Label it accurately as plan or active sequence.

Timeline/list: each crop has crop name, planned dates, actual dates if recorded, status, and optional stage. Planned and actual dates must be visually distinct.

Primary action: Manage Crop Cycle. Show only actions relevant to the current state rather than every possible control at once.

Planned actions: edit intended dates, confirm the plan, or record planting. Before activation, request actual planting date and field/zone if missing.

Active actions: record activity, update stage if known, record harvest/completion, or record a crop/rotation change. Changes create history events.

Changed/paused actions: record replacement crop/sequence or resume where appropriate. Preserve old plan and history.

Completed actions: view history, add missing harvest details, or create a new plan. Do not silently reopen a completed cycle.

Empty state: explain no crop cycle is assigned and link to New Analysis or an existing recommendation.

Responsive behaviour: use a right-side panel on wide screens and a bottom sheet/modal on small screens, consistent with the existing design system. Include keyboard access, clear labels, destructive-action confirmation and unsaved-change warnings.
6. Shared data architecture
The Shared Farm Data System is a data contract and ownership model, not necessarily a new page or immediate cloud migration. Reconcile it with the existing repository and persistence choices first.

Farm record: farm_id (stable immutable ID), display_name, owner reference, location fields with privacy classification, created/updated timestamps, settings, and lifecycle/archive state.

Field record: field_id, farm_id, display_name, optional geometry reference, area/unit when known, and metadata. Renaming or editing its boundary must not silently change field_id.

Crop cycle record: cycle_id, farm_id, field_id (nullable only where farm-level tracking is explicitly supported), source_analysis_run_id, selected rotation or sequence snapshot, lifecycle status, planned/actual start and end dates, timestamps and record version.

Crop-sequence item: item_id, cycle_id, sequence index, crop identifier/name, planned/actual dates, state, stage if available, and source/provenance for reported or estimated values.

Activity record: activity_id, farm_id, field_id, optional cycle_id, activity type, event date/time, values/units, notes, source (farmer-entered/imported/sensor-derived), created_at and correction linkage.

Analysis run: analysis_run_id, farm_id, relevant field_id, input snapshot, priorities, source data references/timestamps, calculation/model version where available, candidate results, selected result and created_at. Preserve historical snapshots.

Map geometry: geometry_id, farm_id and/or field_id, geometry type/coordinates, version, visibility/privacy scope and timestamps. Removing a drawing is distinct from deleting the farm/field.

Calendar event: event_id linked to farm/field/cycle, event type, planned/actual date, status and source. Avoid orphaned duplicate events after date changes.

Scenario record: scenario_id, base analysis run/cycle, hypothetical input overrides, result snapshot and created_at. Explicitly marked hypothetical and isolated from actual state.

Public profile/share record: public profile ID and explicitly approved shareable fields. Public views must be allowlisted and must never serialise private geometry or exact coordinates.
7. Data ownership and synchronisation rules
Each domain record has a clear owner/source of truth. Pages use validated shared data-access operations rather than independent copies.

Renaming a farm changes its displayed label everywhere while retaining farm_id and history. Renaming a field retains field_id.

Changing planned dates updates future planned milestones linked to the cycle; it does not rewrite completed events or historical analysis snapshots.

Recording actual planting creates an explicit activity and transitions the crop item/cycle to Active according to lifecycle rules.

Changing a rotation after planting creates a change event and revised future plan. Never erase crops already planted or rewrite the old recommendation.

Rerunning New Analysis creates a new analysis_run_id and does not automatically replace the current plan or alter an active crop cycle.

Deep Analysis may read current records and create a new log, but cannot silently promote a recommendation to Active.

Removing a farm from Maps hides/removes geometry from that map view only; it does not delete farm, crop history, analyses or Calendar history.

Farm deletion is a separate destructive workflow with explicit confirmation and a defined treatment of linked fields, geometry, cycles, logs, analyses and events.

All page updates use saved shared records. If saving fails, show an error and preserve user edits where possible; never present an unsaved change as saved.
8. Scientific and data integrity rules
Farmer-confirmed events are distinct from model estimates and intended dates. Store source and timestamp for important facts.

Satellite observations may indicate vegetation presence/change, but do not alone prove crop identity, variety, planting date, or a nutrient/pest diagnosis.

Estimated crop stage must be labelled estimated and used only when crop identity, dates, data coverage and a suitable method support it.

Never infer active crop solely because a planned start date passed.

Do not calculate days remaining unless date and duration assumptions are available; label planned vs actual clearly.

Recommendations are model outputs, not guarantees. Preserve input context, assumptions, uncertainty and score context.

Missing data reduces precision; it must not produce invented values or false 'all good' states.
9. Privacy and permissions
Private farm records and exact geometry are available only to authorised users under the existing access model.

Public profile publishing is explicit. Public visibility is separate from internal availability.

Global Farming Network's city-level public location requirement is absolute: never expose exact coordinates or precise boundaries in public map tiles, APIs, page state, logs, URLs, analytics events or downloads.

Making a profile public does not make all crop-cycle, activity, yield or soil-test records public. Share only fields explicitly allowed by the public-profile schema.

Enforce permissions at the data layer/server side wherever a backend exists; hiding a UI control is not sufficient.

Scenario data, private notes and unpublished records must not leak into public aggregates.
10. Migration and compatibility
Audit the current repository before implementation: existing farm IDs, local persistence, field representation, analysis result shapes, rotation storage, map geometry and Calendar event logic.

Map legacy records into the shared schema without discarding user data. If old records lack IDs or lifecycle states, assign stable IDs and use Not confirmed rather than inventing planting status.

Preserve existing features, routes, components and working calculations. Refactor incrementally behind compatible data-access functions instead of replacing the entire application at once.

Use versioned migrations with validation and recovery. Test empty storage, partial records, malformed legacy data, duplicate names, renamed farms, missing geometry and failed writes.

If the current app is local-first, keep that for the first implementation unless the code audit identifies a concrete blocker. Stable contracts should allow a future backend without changing record meanings.
11. End-to-end example
A farmer runs New Analysis and receives a wheat → beans → maize recommendation. The run is saved with its inputs and result snapshot.

The farmer selects Save as Plan, chooses North Field, reviews intended dates and confirms. A Planned crop cycle is created and planned milestones appear in Calendar.

The farmer later confirms wheat was planted on a particular date. Field Shift records the event and marks wheat Active. Home and Farm Analysis show wheat as active; beans and maize remain upcoming.

Deep Analysis reads the confirmed crop and dates while assessing available environmental data. Missing inputs are acknowledged; Deep Analysis does not change lifecycle state.

The farmer changes the future rotation. Field Shift records the change, preserves the original analysis and planting event, and updates only future planned items/events.

The farmer runs What-If Simulator with a different rainfall assumption. Scenario results are separate and do not alter the real crop cycle.

If the farmer publishes a profile, the public view uses city-level location only and exposes only explicitly shared information.
12. Required edge cases
Farm exists without drawn field zones; multiple fields have different crops/states; a plan is saved but planting is never confirmed; a planned date passes with no confirmation.

Farmer plants a different crop, corrects a date, abandons a crop, or plants only part of a field. Support a sub-zone if needed rather than assuming the whole field has one state.

Farm/field is renamed; map geometry is removed while farm records must remain; New Analysis is rerun during an active crop; Calendar dates conflict with completed events.

Missing/contradictory dates, units or crop identifiers; concurrent edits from multiple tabs/devices; storage unavailable/full or a write fails.

Public sharing is enabled while private geometry and crop history exist; farm deletion is requested while linked records exist.
13. Acceptance criteria
Every farm and field uses a stable identity independent of display name.

Recommendations are visibly distinct from saved plans and confirmed active crops.

A rotation can be saved as a plan without falsely marking it planted; planting and harvest/change events can be explicitly recorded.

Home and Farm Analysis show the same saved lifecycle state for the selected farm/field.

Calendar events link to crop cycles and remain consistent after planned-date edits.

Maps displays farm and optional zone geometry without coupling geometry removal to farm deletion.

Deep Analysis reads shared records but does not silently mutate crop state; What-If scenarios never overwrite real state or historical snapshots.

Rerunning New Analysis preserves older runs and does not replace an active plan without explicit action.

Renaming updates labels without breaking links; public output never exposes exact location or unapproved records.

Missing data is shown as unknown/unconfirmed; migration preserves existing data and working features; key flows work on desktop and mobile.
14. Implementation sequence and non-goals
Phase 0 — Audit the codebase and current data structures/persistence before implementation.

Phase 1 — Establish shared data contracts, stable IDs, validated data-access functions, persistence/migration and immutable analysis snapshots.

Phase 2 — Implement crop lifecycle states, crop-sequence items, explicit planting/harvest/change events and plan confirmation.

Phase 3 — Add Crop Cycle to Farm Analysis, compact status to Home, and Save as Plan to New Analysis.

Phase 4 — Integrate Calendar events and optional Maps field zones without conflating geometry deletion and farm deletion.

Phase 5 — Integrate Deep Analysis and What-If Simulator; keep hypothetical data isolated.

Phase 6 — Integrate privacy-safe Global Farming Network public views.

Phase 7 — Run cross-page, migration, privacy, lifecycle, failure and responsive-layout regression tests.

Do not build a duplicate Calendar, separate Action Centre, standalone confidence dashboard or outcome-tracking product as part of this approval. Do not make satellite crop detection a prerequisite. Do not let AI become the source of truth for crop status. Do not expose exact farm locations publicly or replace all current persistence without an audit.
