43.3 Visual rules that apply to every screen
1. Every pixel group must serve a purpose. Each visual grouping must be a navigation aid, status, action, comparison, data visualisation, context cue or decorative image that improves understanding. No purely decorative data-looking widgets.
2. Avoid “card spam.” Do not put every line inside a box. Use editorial image surfaces, unboxed section headings, thin separators, metric strips, quiet list rows, full-width charts and occasional floating map panels. Nest one card inside another only when the inner item is a genuine subordinate entity.
3. No text dumps. Put the takeaway first, then 3–5 concise supporting facts. Hide detailed formulas, parameter lists, raw source metadata and trace events behind expandable sections or the Advanced Mode presentation layer.
4. Visuals should explain data. Crop photos identify crops; the temperature curve communicates temporal patterns; rings/segments show defined status dimensions; mini-maps show location; timelines show sequence; maps show geometry. Never add an animated globe, 3D plant or ornamental graph if it doesn't carry meaning.
5. Images need deliberate treatment. A photograph must be cropped with a focal point, contrast treatment and correct aspect ratio. Do not use a random thumbnail behind text. Use an image only when the content is identified and the source/license is recorded.
6. All data claims have visual provenance. Use compact source/freshness labels, a detail popover or evidence drawer. Do not bury a crucial “estimated” or “stale” warning in tiny grey text.
7. Status meaning is consistent. Good, Needs Attention, Alert and Insufficient Data have distinct labels, icons and colors everywhere. A field's health outline, Home summary and Farm View must use the same status logic.
8. Use exact UI copy from this document. Do not invent vague filler such as “Your farm is thriving!” unless the evidence supports that literal conclusion. No fake slogans or filler charts.
9. No mandatory animations. Motion enhances selection and state change; it must not delay a task, block information, or make the application feel like a promotional site.
10. Do not hide important text behind clouds or imagery. The reference's atmospheric text layering can be imitated using foreground/background separation. Where text overlaps a bright cloud, darken that local image area or use a subtle scrim so required text remains legible. The illusion may be cinematic, but usability wins.
