46. Image, photography, illustration and CSS-effect system
46.1 Asset categories required
Create an intentional, documented asset library; do not randomly repeat generic images across the product. Every local asset has an ID, file path/URL, license/source, attribution when required, crop/focal-point metadata, alt text and fallback. The asset manifest must be editable by developers.

Required categories:
- Weather hero backgrounds: at least five distinct wide images or generated originals—clear warm sunlight, overcast/cloud, rain, storm/heat haze, and night/early-morning. Store no weather result in the image itself; the data layer selects an image matching current condition only when a real condition exists.
- Crop photography: one visually distinct, correctly identified image for every crop supported in the crop catalogue: e.g. wheat heads/grain field; lettuce head/row; beans/legume pods; maize/corn plants/cob; alfalfa/forage; any additional crop. Never use the same crop photograph for two different crop identities. If multiple cards show the same crop, reuse that crop's dedicated image consistently, but crop each instance differently only where useful.
- Farm/location thumbnails: satellite-derived map snapshots or user-provided landscape photographs, with a generated fallback pattern if missing. Never pass a generated image off as the actual farm.
- Soil and root-zone diagrams: original SVG or CSS vector diagram for texture layers, root depth, water movement and drainage; diagram labels must reflect the values/assumptions.
- Rotation sequence illustrations: connected thumbnails for the actual crops in the sequence.
- Empty states: restrained original line illustrations for no farms, no logs, no history and unavailable data; do not use a photo that implies a successful measurement.
- Network/people imagery: do not generate fake profile photographs that suggest real registered users in local-only mode. Placeholder avatar initials or a clearly labelled default botanical mark are allowed until real accounts exist.
46.2 Exact CSS image treatment for cinematic surfaces
Use layered backgrounds rather than a flat uniform black overlay. A weather hero's underlying image should fill the card with background-size: cover; choose the photo's focal point to avoid placing the sun/cloud feature under the main temperature text. Add in this order: (1) a full-surface dark green/black tint at roughly 15–28% to integrate image colour; (2) a left-to-right gradient linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(5,14,10,.76) 0%, rgba(5,14,10,.42) 44%, rgba(5,14,10,.22) 100%); (3) a bottom gradient linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(4,12,8,.92) 0%, rgba(4,12,8,.52) 38%, transparent 78%); (4) optional small radial highlight around a sunrise/sun area with low opacity, blended into image using screen only when it improves the photo. Adjust using actual contrast checks. Do not place a strong gradient behind map tile imagery globally.

Use a CSS pseudo-element for overlays (::before with pointer-events none and z-index 0); content must be in a higher stacking context. Never put the overlay on the clickable container if its pseudo-element intercepts pointer events. The image itself is decorative with aria-hidden when weather data text already describes it; if it conveys a real event or location, provide alt text. Test with image disabled and overlay fallback.

For selected crop/farm cards, use a 16–20% dark gradient only at the text edge. Crop images should show real texture/detail; don't tint all crop photographs green so heavily that crops become indistinguishable. Use a 1 px subtle inner edge highlight. For a selected farm/field image, display actual satellite/map snapshot if available; otherwise a crop-specific illustration and the label Map preview unavailable, not fictional imagery described as true farm location.
46.3 Weather image and data are separate
Never infer temperature, precipitation, wind, or farm condition from visual image colour. The image is aesthetic/context only. A weather code/condition selects a matching image, while actual values come from the weather provider. If the API fails, show a neutral fallback image, keep previous readings with a Last updated time if valid, or state that conditions are unavailable. Do not present image-generated text/values as data.
