47.5 Recommendation Confidence versus Data Coverage
These must be visibly different even if rendered in monochrome. Recommendation Confidence uses a compass/target glyph and a segmented/ring-like mark; tooltip: How stable is this recommendation under the available evidence and stated assumptions? This is not a guarantee of yield. Data Coverage uses stacked layers/database glyph and a horizontal coverage bar or layered badge; tooltip: How complete, recent and spatially suitable are the inputs used for this result? Never style them as interchangeable percentage pills. If no valid calculation supports a numeric value, display a named qualitative state. The formula and displayed category must match the actual computed value.
47.6 Data freshness/provenance chip
A tiny source icon followed by source/type and age, e.g. Open-Meteo · updated 12 min ago, Sentinel-2 · observation 18 Oct 2026, Mapped soil estimate, Farmer reported, Calculated. In Simple Mode, collapse to source type + date; Advanced reveals endpoint/product/variables/resolution and transformation. Keep chip horizontally wrap-safe; it must not obscure title. Tooltip on hover/focus.
47.7 Chart panel
Every chart includes: clear title; period selector where relevant; one-sentence summary if interpretation is useful; plot with axis labels/units; legend with visible series names; source/coverage note; point-hover tooltip; and accessible tabular alternative. No unlabeled arbitrary values. Do not rely on tooltips as the only access to exact values. On mobile, charts become horizontally scrollable within the panel only if necessary and expose a View data table alternative.
47.8 Expandable evidence row
Default: concise label, summary value/status, chevron. On open: source and time, relevant inputs, formula, intermediate values, limitations and links. Animate height/fade 160–220 ms. Preserve expanded state for same page session. If many rows are present, add Collapse all / Expand all only at the section level. Don't put raw logs in an ordinary end-user summary.
47.9 Modal, drawer and popover
Modal width 520–680 px default; wide detailed workflow up to 880 px; max height 85vh; header and action footer remain visible while body scrolls. Title left, close X right, Escape closes when it is safe to dismiss, and dirty form warns before discard. Focus is trapped within modal; focus returns to opener. Use right drawer 360–420 px for contextual detail, 280–340 px for side panels where map area is critical. Popovers stay anchored and avoid viewport edges.
47.10 Empty, loading, error and stale states
Never leave an empty rectangle or empty graph. Use short title, explanatory sentence and one useful action. Examples: No farms added yet / Add a farm to connect your map, crop records and analyses. / button Add farm; No Deep Analysis runs yet / Run Deep Analysis to create your first dated report. / button Run Deep Analysis; Data unavailable / This source could not be reached. No new value has been added. / button Retry. Loading uses skeletons that match final shapes and one status sentence; no random fake number increments. Stale data retains timestamp and a visible Stale badge. Error must not silently fallback to a current-looking number.
47.11 Crop thumbnail card
Use crop image ratio 4:3 or 1:1 depending on context; image cover; crop name directly below; small crop family/stage label where known; status and duration visible as chips or aligned metadata, not baked into image. Distinct crops must use different species-correct assets. For sequence cards, use 100–140 px image width desktop and 88–104 px mobile. Use crop-specific image alt text. If the image URL fails, show illustrated crop fallback with an accessible label.
48. Home — exact visual composition and behaviour
48.1 Purpose and default layout
Home must answer, in roughly five seconds: which farm is selected, whether it needs attention, current/recent weather, what crop/rotation is actually active or planned, and how to open the map. Keep the daily tracker compact and visually rich; it is not the place to dump all indicator charts.

At 1440 × 900 below the horizontal header, use the full routed content width as a 12-column grid. Top row: page title/farm selector at 32 px/36; date/time and small Updated text at right. Next row: left 7 columns a Weather Intelligence hero (height around 300–340 px) and right 5 columns a Farm Status card (height matched within 24 px). Below: left 7 columns a Farm Map Preview (height 260–320 px) and right 5 columns stack Crop Cycle and Active Alerts/Recent Signals compact panels. If the selected farm has no data, the empty state takes the relevant panel without showing false demo metrics.

Do not place five equal-sized metric cards in the first row. The weather surface is editorial/visual; farm status is a compact analytic summary; map preview is spatial; crop-cycle is a timeline; alerts are rows. Use composition variety.
48.2 Weather Intelligence hero — pixel-by-pixel hierarchy
Container: 100% width of its grid column; 320 px target height desktop, 280 px tablet, 300 px mobile only when forecast row can still fit; radius 20 px; clip overflow; use a real weather-specific photograph or generated background with no baked text.

Layer 0 — image: full-bleed photo, cover, focal point chosen from asset metadata; add a muted green colour grade. The photo should evoke the real weather condition without claiming measurement.

Layer 1 — scrim: a left-weighted black/forest gradient so text sits over darker cloud/sky; a separate bottom dark gradient for the forecast chart. Do not uniformly blacken the entire image so much that it loses its tonal character.

Top row: left 20 px inset, top 18 px: weather/condition icon in a 38 × 38 px glass-like square; to its right small uppercase CURRENT CONDITIONS (11 px, medium, white at 80% opacity), then location/farm label on the next line (15 px semibold) and small location pin. Right side: small last-updated label at 12 px and a 30 × 30 refresh icon button, but only if manual refresh is supported. If weather is forecast only, eyebrow must say FORECAST, not CURRENT CONDITIONS.

Main reading: around 28 px below header, use 56 px temperature on desktop, 48 px mobile, weight 500, white #F3F6F1. Put degree/unit at matching baseline, never separate in a random chip. Below it: one-line condition (Mostly sunny, Cloudy, Showers likely) in 15 px, then Feels like 36° in 12–13 px secondary if available. To right of temperature, two compact stacked capsules H 37° and L 26°, each 84 × 28 px, frosted dark backing with 1 px translucent border. Only show highs/lows if provider actually supplies them for the stated period.

Secondary metrics: bottom-left/centre row of three tiles for humidity, wind and precipitation. Each tile about 105–125 × 54 px (depending on width), with 15 px icon left; value 16 px semibold; label 11–12 px muted; units on value. The cards are only 8–10% lighter than the image layer, with 1 px border; no neon glow. If one metric is unavailable, remove or label it unavailable rather than borrowing a value from another period.

Forecast line graph: bottom 92–112 px, width minus 32 px. Seven values at equal intervals only if seven valid daily points exist. Temperature line is a smooth monotone curve (no spline overshoot), 2 px light mineral/cream; show daily highs or a clearly named single temperature series. Each point has a marker. Selected/current day: larger 8–10 px dot with thin amber ring; sun/weather symbol 16 px above point where enough vertical space exists; one vertical dotted guide down to x-axis. Labels above/below curve are sparse so line and temperatures don't collide. At the very bottom, weekday labels 11–12 px and values 14–16 px tabular. Example labels: TUE then 23°, WED then 26°. Current day highlighted in white; others muted at 70%. The curve is a chart, not a decorative wave: x position equals time and y position maps to a documented temperature scale. Use a correct axis/accessible data summary even if axis labels are omitted from the compact hero.

Atmospheric visual trick: a subtle radial gradient may create a warm sunlight opening around the image's focal point, but it must not manufacture a weather phenomenon that contradicts the condition. Use mix-blend-mode: screen only on a light overlay texture at very low opacity, and provide a static fallback. Never make the temperature text partially invisible.

Click behaviour: clicking non-control portions opens detailed Weather/Conditions inside selected Farm View; it must not switch the selected farm without consent. Refresh icon refreshes the correct selected location, shows pending state, then updates timestamp; on failure keep the last valid data and show Couldn't refresh weather.

Exact labels: Current conditions; Feels like; High; Low; Humidity; Wind; Precipitation; 7-day forecast; Updated. Labels with values omitted must also be omitted when the metric is unsupported. Do not show “live” unless the provider/source freshness supports that label.
48.3 Farm Status card
Container aligns with weather hero height; use raised surface rather than photo background. Header row: leaf/field icon inside 36 px circle; title Farm condition; status badge top right. If there is a defensible composite status score, render a 92 px circular gauge left or centred within the first content row, with score and denominator; otherwise replace with simple status label and no fabricated number. To right, three rows: Water, Soil, Crop cycle, each with icon, short name, status text and small colored dot. Row height 32 px. Below, a full-width alert row only when alert exists; icon + 1 active alert, short observation, arrow to evidence. If no alert, show a concise neutral line such as No active alerts based on available data only if coverage is adequate; otherwise No active alert detected; some data is unavailable.

Overall status follows the documented deterministic rule and is derived from the worst qualifying condition among fields (farm status uses worst field status, not an unweighted average) unless a specific validated aggregate model says otherwise. Status order: Alert > Needs Attention > Good; Insufficient Data remains explicit if a meaningful status can't be assigned. The status chip never claims a measured “health score” if the number is a model index.
48.4 Farm Map Preview
Map preview is a real interactive map using actual map tiles, saved farm geometry and real selected location. Ratio 16:9 or 7:4; height 260–320 px. Show the farm outline in status-linked color: good leaf-green, attention amber, alert coral/red, unknown neutral dotted grey. Use a dark subtle overlay only along edges to hold labels; do not apply global filters that invert or destroy satellite colours. Overlay left-bottom label with farm name and field count, bottom-right Open Maps icon/action. Click anywhere except controls navigates to Maps and selects the farm, preserving location/zoom context. If geometry missing, show a location map/placeholder with Draw farm boundary CTA. Never use fake example boundaries.
48.5 Crop Cycle summary
Title Crop cycle; right side View cycle. Show confirmed crop with distinct crop image thumbnail 52 × 52 px; state/stage line (Active · Vegetative stage, or Planned · not yet planted); a thin sequence/progress track with upcoming crop thumbnails; one next milestone label with date only where known. If no plan exists, copy: No rotation plan saved for this field. and action Explore rotations. If a plan exists but not confirmed active, never label the first crop “Current”.
48.6 Signals and alerts
Only display actual current alerts or valid Field Signals; no placeholder. Each row has severity icon, brief title, farm/field, time, one sentence, and View details. Place in right lower stack below Crop Cycle. Cap visible rows at three; then View all alerts. When zero alerts and data is adequate, show one compact “No active alerts” row; otherwise display unavailable coverage.
49. Weather detailed view — full visual and logic specification
This is a detail view accessible by clicking the Home weather card or a farm's weather context. It should not become a separate global location-weather site; all values bind to the selected farm unless the user explicitly chooses a location explorer.

Desktop composition: page heading and farm/location selector at top; hero weather scene spans 8 columns and about 300 px; right 4-column location context panel contains a small globe/minimap with a pin and location name label beneath it. Under hero: hourly forecast horizontal timeline; below that a 7-day forecast row; then expandable environmental context relevant to agriculture.

The mini globe/location panel: use a genuine simple globe/orthographic projection SVG or small globe asset with a plotted location point based on the selected farm's coordinates. A marker should approximate the lat/lon on the globe; the label below is the selected place name/city and country. Do not use a falsely precise location marker if only a city-level place is known. This is a visual location context cue, not a second real interactive globe unless implemented. Clicking it opens the location info or View on Maps.

Time series: temperature curve for hourly or daily forecast; precipitation probability bars only when forecast provides probability; rain amount in mm is a different series and cannot be substituted for probability. Selected timestamp uses amber sun/weather marker and vertical guide. Hover/focus shows exact time, value and unit. If weather points are missing, break the line (do not interpolate across missing days without marking it). Use a fallback table/list for screen readers and mobile.

Agricultural context panel: only supported fields: likely rain/heat exposure, ET0 or reference evapotranspiration when source supplies/derives it correctly, solar radiation, humidity, wind, and historical anomaly when the comparison baseline exists. Keep consumer weather facts separate from model-derived farm implications. Do not convert weather temperature into leaf temperature or crop heat stress unless a valid model/sensor supports that.

Weather image treatment: use a condition-appropriate original background and layered gradient. The weather headline may overlay a low-detail area of the image with text opacity 92–100%; not literally masked behind foreground objects. Decorative clouds may approach behind small secondary text, but all essential values must maintain contrast. If no suitable image exists, use a minimal gradient + weather glyph rather than a broken image.
50. Maps — exact workspace layout, drawing tool and states
50.1 Workspace geometry
At 1440 × 900: top horizontal app header 64 px; the Maps workspace below fills nearly the full viewport width (about 1440 px less 24 px outer gutters) and height. Use three columns: left farm inspector 248–272 px; central satellite map flex (about 800–840 px at this viewport); right My Farms panel 300–324 px. With 12 px outer gutters and 8 px internal gaps, preserve at least 760 px for the central map. Map remains the visual focus. Use 1 px dividers. Side panels scroll independently; the map remains full-height and independent.

On 1280 px, inspector may collapse to 220 px and right panel to 280 px before the centre is squeezed. Under 1100 px use map + one panel with a panel switch (Farm details / My Farms), not three narrow columns. On mobile the map owns the first screen, the left inspector becomes a bottom sheet, My Farms a right drawer or bottom sheet, and drawing controls remain reachable.
50.2 Centre map content
Use a real interactive satellite basemap when provider/terms allow; retain provider attribution and fallback map style. Use MapLibre GL JS with a maintained drawing adapter such as Terra Draw, and geospatial area/validation helpers such as Turf.js, after checking current versions. Do not depend on deprecated Google Maps Drawing Library. The base map must pan, zoom, zoom-to-farm, preserve camera, display attribution and give a recoverable tile-load error. Use natural imagery; no global invert/hue-rotate filter on imagery.

Map control stack at top-right within the map column: Zoom in (+), zoom out (−), Recenter/locate, Layers, and optional Fullscreen. Buttons 40 × 40 px with 8 px gap and solid dark translucent backing. Tooltips show names. Bottom-left: current basemap/layer dropdown, e.g. Satellite, Map layers. Bottom-right: scale bar, small minimap/overview where useful. Attribution at lower edge remains visible and never gets hidden by a card.
50.3 Left inspector
Default title Selected farm; if none selected, title Map overview and instruction Choose a farm from My Farms or add a new farm to see its details here. When selected: compact thumbnail/mini location context (64 × 48 px), farm name 16 px, city/region 12 px; status badge; outlined area value if computed; fields count; last relevant update. Below, compact data rows for weather context, soil context, water status and crop cycle (farm-wide vs field-specific must be labelled). Each row uses 16 px icon, 12 px label, 14 px primary value, metadata in 11–12 px. This is a summary, not the entire Farm View. Action row: Open farm view and Get latest analysis only if an analysis can actually run.

When drawing a new boundary, inspector transforms into the Draw farm boundary step panel with help text, live area estimate, point count, validation status, Undo, Redo, Close boundary, Cancel drawing and Save boundary actions. Keep these controls visible and not hidden beneath a scroller.
50.4 Right panel — My Farms
Panel header 56 px: map/farm icon at left; title MY FARMS (or readable title case My Farms, choose one consistent style); + Add Farm green button at right, height 36–40 px, width 112–124 px. The button uses a plus icon and label, not icon-only on desktop. Below: 8–12 px search input Search your farms…, then filter control if useful (All, Good, Needs attention, Alert, Not analysed). The list is vertically scrollable. Cards 88–118 px depending data; 10–12 px gap; 12 px padding; radius 12 px; 1 px border. Each card includes 44 × 44 px farm image/map snapshot, farm name, location, overall status, field count/area where known, and right chevron. A selected card has a 2 px green/amber/alert boundary matching status, subdued selected fill and a clear Selected cue for assistive technology.

Cards may include a compact crop icon/name and 1–2 state values if space allows; do not try to show all soil, water and crop metrics on every card. For multiple fields, show 3 fields and the most concerning field count only if derived. Not analysed is not the same as Good. Empty list: No farms yet / Add a farm to draw its boundary and connect its fields, rotations and analyses. / button Add farm. The add button remains at panel top when the list scrolls.

Click a farm card: animate map camera to saved polygon using padding that accounts for both side panels; select that farm; show its selected state in the card and update left inspector. Animation 450–800 ms; on reduced motion use shorter ease or immediate fit. If polygon is absent, zoom to location coordinates if known; otherwise show no map position and prompt user to locate it.
50.5 Add farm — complete flow and duplicate rules
Click + Add Farm opens an in-panel staged form rather than a blocking whole-screen dialog. Step header: Add a farm and step counter 1 of 3. First visible field: Farm name with placeholder e.g. North Field Farm. Helper: Choose a name you’ll recognise. You can rename it later. Trim whitespace and normalise for duplicate check (case-insensitive; collapse repeated whitespace; compare Unicode-normalised forms). Don't allow blank name; maximum 80 characters. Show field-level validation immediately after blur/type.

If the name is new: show Available as subtle confirmation (not a celebratory toast). CTA Continue to map; secondary Cancel.

If a matching farm exists: don't silently create a duplicate or replace a boundary. Show A farm named “{name}” already exists. Then two explicit actions: Link to existing farm and Choose a different name. Linking keeps the stable farm_id and its analysis history; the next step asks what this boundary represents: farm outline update or an additional farm boundary only if the data model supports multi-boundary farms. The default data model is one saved overall polygon per farm, so existing boundary changes require explicit confirmation. Replace existing boundary must display a preview of old/new boundary and confirm Replace boundary; Keep current boundary remains available. If the record represents a farm with multiple fields, don't confuse a second field with a second farm polygon.

Step 2 Locate farm: search box Search city, address, or place…; results show place name and region. Selecting a result moves the map and records coordinates as location context; this search alone does not create a boundary. If no geocoding result, offer map pan/pin placement where supported.

Step 3 Draw boundary: map enters polygon mode. User can click successive points; the line follows pointer; click first point or Close boundary to close it. Live preview fills polygon with status-neutral translucent leaf-green (fill ~14–20% opacity) and 2 px border. Show area estimate after at least 3 non-collinear vertices; area recalculates on each valid vertex edit. Label is temporary (New boundary), not an invented farm name.

Completion review: farm name, place/city, computed polygon area with selected units, approximate vertex count, and note This outline is the overall farm boundary. Fields and rotations are managed inside the farm view. CTA Save farm boundary; secondary Continue editing; tertiary Cancel. Save writes a local durable draft/record only after validation. If save fails, retain the drawing and show We couldn't save this boundary. Your draft is still here. Try again.
50.6 Drawing toolbar — positions and exact controls
When not drawing, a narrow vertical toolbar floats 16 px from map left and 50–64 px below workspace top, never under the left inspector. Buttons: Select/Inspect (cursor), Draw farm boundary (polygon/pen), Edit boundary (vertices), Layers, optional measure tool only if real. Tool button is 40 × 40 px, with tooltip and visible selected state.

In draw mode, a compact toolbar appears near the bottom or top-left of map, not inside the sidebar; it has: Undo point, Redo point, Close boundary, Cancel. Show point count and live area at its far edge. On desktop, shortcuts Ctrl/Cmd+Z and Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z may undo/redo only when drawing focus is active; do not override text-input undo. Escape ends drawing only after a confirmation if any vertices exist. On touch screens, every operation must have a button alternative; vertex dragging alone is not the only way to edit.

Polygon editing: vertices show visible handles when Edit mode is active (minimum 12 px desktop, 16 px touch). Midpoint handles let the user add a vertex. Click/select a vertex then Delete vertex; prevent deletion below three valid vertices. A boundary must be a closed polygon, no self-intersection, valid longitude/latitude, non-zero area and sensible maximum/minimum area according to configured rules. Show human-readable validation reason, not only a red outline. Keep the prior valid version until the user saves; Cancel restores it.

Undo/redo: use an edit operation stack for vertex operations; undo of a new point removes only that point; undo of boundary edit restores the previous coordinate; undo does not undo already-persisted unrelated farm data. Save and deletion are not undone by drawing's Ctrl+Z; they require explicit actions.

Delete convenience: saved boundary can be deleted from Edit menu and Farm Settings. Menu item Remove map boundary must be accessible without starting edit mode. Confirmation text: Remove the map outline for “{farmName}”? The farm record, fields, rotations, analyses and history will remain. Actions Remove boundary (destructive) and Keep boundary. If deleted, keep farm location record if independently stored; map card displays Boundary not drawn and CTA Draw boundary again. Removing a farm's Maps listing/geometry is never the same as deleting the whole farm.

Draft recovery: persist in-progress vertices locally after each point/edit. If user reloads or leaves, reopen with Resume boundary draft / Discard draft. Do not auto-save invalid geometry over the last valid boundary.
50.7 Farm map colour rules
Private farm outline colour follows farm status from its fields: Alert if any field is Alert; otherwise Needs Attention if any field Needs Attention; otherwise Good only if sufficient data supports Good; otherwise neutral/unknown dashed outline. The fill is subtle so satellite imagery remains visible. Add a small status glyph/legend; do not recolor the entire real satellite raster as a health heatmap. A public Global Farming Network city layer is a separate public aggregation and must never show individual farm boundaries/coordinates.
50.8 Layer panel interaction
My Farms keeps the right column; map layers use a floating small layer drawer anchored to bottom-left or a 320 px drawer overlaying the map canvas without replacing My Farms. Categories: Base map; Farm boundaries; Environmental layers; Field Signals; Measurement overlays. Only show layers that have an implemented source or calculation. Unavailable layers are disabled with a reason and optional Learn more, not selectable placeholders. Each layer shows name, source/time window, visibility toggle and legend. Use distinct icon for layer visibility and source details. Close button is accessible; reopening restores selection.
50.9 Farm selected from map
Click inside saved private polygon: select polygon and open the canonical Farm View. Transition: collapse/hide map workspace into a floating mini-map anchored lower-left on the resulting Farm View, keeping map camera and polygon visible; main content transitions in with subtle 180–240 ms fade/translate. Top-right of the detail overlay/view has × with tooltip Back to Maps; clicking it restores Maps and selected polygon/camera. Clicking mini-map expands Maps at the same extent. On Home, clicking the map preview simply navigates to Maps with current farm selected; it does not jump into analysis. On public Network map, city marker selection only opens city list; do not expose private polygon semantics.
51. My Farms portfolio — exact layout
Title row: My Farms; subtitle Manage farms, fields and their analysis history. Primary button Add farm opens the same Maps drawing flow. Summary strip of three compact values only if known: number of farms, total tracked fields, farms needing attention. Each summary value is derived from local data and clicking a count filters the list.

Main content has search/filter bar across width; below, grid of farm cards on wide desktop (two columns only if imagery is large enough) or compact rows. Prefer rows for many farms. Each card: map/farm thumbnail 72 × 56 px, farm name 16 px semibold; city/country 12 px; status badge; 4 fields / area where known; updated time; right chevron. Lower small strip may show 2 indicators (e.g. water and crop cycle) only if meaningful. Overflow menu has Open farm, Rename, Edit boundary, Export farm data, Delete farm. Don't bury Add Farm in an overflow menu.

On selecting a card, open canonical Farm View; never create a separate farm detail layout specific to this route. If selected farm lacks a polygon, show its location thumbnail or fallback and Boundary not drawn. Sorting: Name, Status, Recently updated; status sort uses explicit ordering. Search excludes unpublished network content and searches local records only.
52. Canonical Farm View and Field View — page composition
52.1 Farm View overview
Farm View is one shared view reached by Maps, My Farms, Home crop/map cards and global search. It is the canonical detail object. Top left: breadcrumb Maps / {Farm Name} or My Farms / {Farm Name}; farm name 28 px; city/region 13 px; farm status; Edit farm menu. Top right: primary Get latest analysis if supported, secondary Manage fields, overflow.

Below header: one broad status area, then a field list. Farm-wide weather/terrain/climate context appears as a clearly labelled Farm conditions section. Do not duplicate weather inside each field panel as though each field had a separately measured value. Field cards are distinct objects and display field-level soil, crop cycle, irrigation and analysis status.

Farm overview card: status badge/reason first; if valid score exists, show a circular/half-ring gauge at 80–96 px, score at centre, denominator/unit immediately below; next to it show 2–3 concise facts; avoid giant numbers without basis. If fields vary, show worst state and count summary like 1 field needs attention; don't average incomparable field scores.

Fields section: title Fields and count; action Add field. Each row includes field name, field area and units (typed by farmer), crop cycle state, current confirmed crop or plan label, soil profile status, one key alert or No active field alert, and chevron. If no fields: No fields added / Add a field to give this farm its own crop history, soil and rotation plan. CTA Add field.
52.2 Field View / Farm Analysis visual layout
This page owns the detailed field-specific dashboard. Header: breadcrumb Farm / Field; field name 26–30 px; status and area; field settings button; field selector dropdown. A compact farm-wide context banner below: Farm conditions with weather summary/location/source/time. It explicitly says Farm-level weather context if it isn't field resolution.

Content hierarchy:
1. At-a-glance strip: Crop-cycle status; crop currently planted only if confirmed; soil summary; water status only if supported; active alert count. 4–5 compact values, not 9 cards.
2. Crop Cycle: rotation sequence with crop photographs, dated windows and actual/planned state; Manage crop cycle.
3. Deep Analysis card: short most recent report summary, time and run status; action Run Deep Analysis and link View report; log trace/history link in secondary navigation.
4. Conditions and indicators: grouped sections Water, Soil, Weather/Climate, Crop/Vegetation, Risks/Observations. Simple Mode shows compact takeaway; Advanced Mode reveals charts/values/provenance/formulas.
5. Activity/history: recent farmer-recorded events; missing-data notes and Add activity where supported.
Do not put 12 tabs at the top. Use a horizontal anchor row or tabs for at most five groups (Overview, Crop Cycle, Conditions, History, Settings) and within Conditions group, accordion subgroups. The vertical page remains navigable with sticky section labels on desktop.
52.3 Field-specific data hierarchy
Field-specific: area, soil test/soil estimate, irrigation setup, crop history, current/planned crop, crop stages, rotation, activity records, field-specific alerts and field analyses. Farm-level: location/name, overall polygon, general weather/climate/terrain and farm metadata. Any shared values used in field calculations must be labelled with their spatial scale.

On field view, if a data value isn't measured at the field it must state Regional estimate, Farm-level weather, Satellite-derived, Farmer reported or the correct provenance. Don't label a coarse regional measure as “field moisture” without qualification.
53. Crop Cycle module — exact layout and state handling
Container spans full available width, not a tiny corner card. Header left: 32 px crop/leaf icon tile; title Crop Cycle; subtitle field name; status badge right. Under header, show a rotation line such as Wheat → Beans → Maize, with 3 crop tiles. Each tile includes species-correct image, crop name, planned date window, actual start/finish if recorded, and status. Use arrows between tiles, not a dense table unless Advanced Mode needs exact dates.

Dates always include a month and year, e.g. Oct 2026 – Mar 2027; no vague “Year 1” beside a seasonal label. If multi-year, group by year as a separate timeline header above date windows. Recent-crop history form asks crop + approximate planting month/year + harvest month/year; fixed Winter/Spring/Summer/Autumn slots are not the source of truth.

State display: Recommended badge belongs on analysis candidates; after explicit Save as Plan, Planned; after confirmed planting, Active; when farmer changes/pauses, Changed or Paused; upon confirmed harvest/completion, Completed. Upcoming crops in an active rotation display Upcoming, not Active. Planned start passing does not imply planting occurred.

Manage Crop Cycle opens an edit panel with only valid actions for current state: Save as plan, Confirm planting, Record activity, Record harvest, Change crop/rotation, Pause cycle, Mark completed, Correct a record. Every action collects the date, field and necessary values; confirmation says which pages will update (e.g. Calendar) and which historical records remain unchanged. Date edits change future planned events, not actual historical events.
54. New Analysis wizard — exact screen patterns
54.1 Wizard frame
Use a focused stepper layout instead of an extremely long form. At desktop width, main form is 760–920 px centred, with a contextual side panel 260–300 px on the right only if it genuinely helps. Header: New Analysis; subheading Find a rotation that fits this farm, its conditions and your priorities. Stepper shows named steps, completed step checkmarks and current step. Progress bar is a thin line; user can return to previous completed steps without losing valid answers. Mobile collapses side summary and uses a compact Step 2 of 6 indicator.
54.2 Step 1 — farm and location
Use a location search with a pin icon; typed search results show place, region/country and coordinates only in Advanced. If entering from a saved farm/field, prefill existing record and label Using saved farm data; allow explicit Change location. Never silently use a default US location. If location is not found, support choosing point on map where available. Current coordinates are optional in Simple, visible in Advanced.

Below location, provide farm/field selector if an existing farm has multiple fields, and Create new farm if no existing record. The farm and field identities are linked by stable IDs, not names alone.
54.3 Step 2 — soil and growing conditions
Layout is a two-column form on desktop: soil basics left, water/irrigation on right. Every input has visible label, units in control suffix or helper, optional info tooltip, and source indicator if prefilled. Soil texture uses a compact illustrated texture selector or dropdown with small swatch/diagram; pH and nutrients are optional and must never have a secretly ideal default. Select source for known value: I have a soil test, Use available mapped estimate, I don't know. If unknown, continue and reduce coverage rather than blocking unless a critical feasibility calculation cannot proceed.

Water setup: Rain-fed, Irrigated, Both, Not sure; if irrigated, reveal source/method and limits if known. Add I don't know and helper. No unsupported numeric “water quota” by default.
54.4 Step 3 — crop history
Repeatable rows containing crop selector with crop thumbnails, approximate planting month/year and approximate harvest month/year, plus Add previous crop. Show Fallow/uncultivated as explicit choice. Each row has remove icon and confirmation only if it contains user data. Use date-window model, not season name hardcoding. The form should preserve order, validate harvest doesn't occur before planting, and allow approximate dates flagged as approximate.
54.5 Step 4 — planning horizon and priorities
Horizon control uses a clear timeline selector or season/year range. Each crop sequence tile shows date windows only when enough data supports them. Priority sliders/cards for Water Saving, Soil Health, Climate Resilience and any currently supported priority. Use a short explanation under each, not raw engineering jargon. Slider input has numeric accessible value and a Reset to balanced action. Weights must normalise in engine; the UI communicates relative emphasis and not literal percent unless displayed as normalized percent.
54.6 Step 5 — calculate and progress state
Show real progress stages: Checking your inputs, Retrieving available environmental data, Validating crop parameters, Evaluating feasible crop sequences, Comparing trade-offs, Preparing explanations. Each progress item changes to complete only when that stage genuinely finishes. On a partial source failure, show the failed source and continue only if the affected calculations remain valid. No fake 99% progress or predetermined spinner duration. Cancel stops pending operations when possible and writes a cancelled record only if run began.
54.7 Step 6 — adaptive risk trade-offs
Only show contextual risk-choice questions after initial candidate calculations identify material trade-offs. Each question card has a short heading, context sentence, option descriptions and an evidence/uncertainty expandable row. Example: Would you accept a higher pest-pressure risk for a rotation that better matches your production priority? options: Yes, show the higher-priority option, No, prefer a more balanced rotation, Show me the trade-off first. Include a distinct risk badge; explain consequences and mitigations; do not state risk as certainty. Store the answer against this analysis run. If the user rejects the risk, re-filter/re-rank viable candidates using the same deterministic engine and return a balanced feasible option where one exists.
54.8 Results screen
Top row summary includes title Recommended rotations, subtitle with farm/field and analysis timestamp; distinct Recommendation Confidence and Data Coverage badges; source/data status. Best feasible rotation shown first. Each rotation card contains a sequence with separate crop photos, crop/date windows, total cycle length, overall score with definition, 3 concise reasons, key limitation/risk, and actions View details, Compare, Save as plan. Avoid one long wall of AI text.

Under top recommendation, show 1–2 alternatives (if available) with trade-off labels. The whole results layout includes a score breakdown accordion and Why this rotation? panel; AI description may explain but cannot alter score/order. Explain why individually high-scoring crops might not form the top sequence if sequence constraints, crop family, timing or resource competition changes the result. Use a clear no-feasible-candidates state with reasons and controls to change constraints.
54.9 Save as Plan behaviour
Button Save as plan opens a lightweight confirmation panel: select existing farm/field or create farm/field; name plan if needed; confirm intended dates. Copy: Saving creates a plan. It does not mean the crop has been planted. On save, persist link to analysis run, snapshot of chosen sequence and calendar milestones. It does not make actual crop status active. The result remains accessible in Rotations and the farm's history.
55. Rotations — candidate browser, detail, comparison and custom builder
55.1 Rotations page layout
Header Rotations / Compare crop sequences for your farm. Selected farm/field picker and last-run timestamp at top; main content has recommended/saved rotation list. Use clean rows/cards with 4:3 crop thumbnails, small score/risk badges and sequence preview. Sort by best feasible fit, water priority, soil priority, climate resilience, newest, and saved. Sorting options only appear for fields with defined component scores.
55.2 Rotation card exact hierarchy
Top: name (16 px semibold) and score at right (24 px with /100 or definition). Under: crop sequence, thumbnails 72–96 px per crop and arrows. Dated windows under crop name (e.g. Oct 2026–Mar 2027); period uses month + year. Status text Recommended, Saved plan, Active plan, or Historical run; never confuse them. Lower row has top 2 strengths, one main limitation, badges for Recommendation Confidence / Data Coverage, and actions Open details, Compare, Save as plan. Overflow has rename plan, duplicate as new plan, export, archive/delete subject to record policies.
55.3 Rotation detail
Hero image: a crop sequence composition using the actual crop images in this rotation, not a random farm photo. Overlay on left with rotation title and a gradient behind text; preserve all crop visuals. Top metrics strip shows total cycle length, number of crops, water demand category/estimate (if supported), soil trajectory (if modelled and clearly labelled), confidence and data coverage.

Timeline: horizontal sequence track on desktop, vertical sequence on mobile. Each stage has crop image, name, duration, date window, role in sequence, and Why here? disclosure. At the bottom show sequence-level risk and projected changes (estimated, not actual) with one evidence link per material claim.
55.4 Rotation comparison
At desktop, compare 2–3 rotations side-by-side only if width allows; each column is the same width and uses same data points. A summary row at top states the key difference in plain language. Below aligned dimensions: priority fit, water, soil, climate suitability/resilience, constraints, cycle length, crop sequence and risk. Show direction and absolute values/units when valid. On mobile, use a switcher between plans with a dimension-by-dimension table rather than cards so wide enough text remains readable. Compare opens a persistent compare tray with selected plans; maximum 3. Add Clear comparison. Do not compare unsupported metrics or hide “not comparable”.
55.5 Custom Rotation Builder (Advanced, within Rotations)
Access through a secondary action Build a custom rotation within Rotations, not main navigation. Workspace: left crop catalogue searchable/filterable; centre sequence timeline with drag/drop plus keyboard move up/down controls; right impact inspector that updates after valid candidate calculation. User can add/remove crop, change duration, and see feasibility constraints. Dragging has button alternatives. Invalid sequence shows the constraint and suggested resolution. It never bypasses hard agronomic constraints just because user manually arranged it. Save as a new plan; preserve input rotation/version.
55.6 Rotation Decision Replay
Add a small action inside the detailed rotation page: Why did this rank first? opens a decision replay panel. Ordered steps show normalized inputs, candidates generated, constraints that removed options, score components, priority weights and final trade-offs. This is a deterministic trace of actual engine steps, not private AI thought. Include version and source context. If historic engine version isn't recoverable, display Detailed replay unavailable for this older run and retain the saved explanation.
56. Analysis page — What-If simulator only
This is the only primary Analysis page and contains only What-If; do not put a general analysis menu, rotations dashboard or competing pages in its main surface.
56.1 Main composition
Header Analysis and subtitle Explore how changed conditions could affect a saved rotation. Step path across top: Farm → Field → Baseline → Scenario. Left 7-column scenario controls; right 5-column live impact summary and comparison chart on desktop. On mobile, controls first, results below.
56.2 Baseline selection
Step 1 selects farm; step 2 selects field; step 3 selects saved analysis run/plan. Show its creation date and exact base assumptions. Do not assume the active farm's first rotation is the intended baseline. Button Use this baseline.
56.3 Scenario controls
Group controls into Weather/Climate, Water, Soil and Planning Assumptions. User changes only variables supported by engine. A slider shows current baseline value, scenario value, units, min/max justified by data/domain, and Reset on each variable. Include sensible named presets (Hotter season, Lower rainfall, Reduced irrigation) only if the preset values are defined, documented and apply to supported variables. Don't use arbitrary ±50% merely for visual effect.
56.4 Results and output graph
At right display Baseline vs Scenario with a two-column comparison, top outcome What changed?, rotation rank if candidate engine recomputed, crop-level effects and the drivers of change. Use grouped paired bars or before/after dot plot; label values and units; positive/negative direction explained. Show a small difference label such as −12% estimated irrigation demand only if calculated and denominators/time window are clear.

Persistent disclaimer above results: This is a scenario, not a forecast. It does not change your real farm plan. Save scenario, duplicate scenario, compare scenarios, reset to baseline and open baseline analysis. If baseline data is insufficient for a recalculation, show unsupported factor and do not present fabricated precision. Store hypothetical override in separate scenario records.
57. Calendar — professional crop schedule
57.1 Layout
Header Calendar; subtitle Planned and recorded crop activity across your farms. Top control row: farm filter (All farms or one farm), field filter, view selector Month, Week, Agenda, date-range navigation, Today, and Add event only if manual events are supported. Keep control order stable across views.

Desktop: main calendar 8–9 columns; right side selected-day agenda 3–4 columns. Month cells show date, up to three event chips, and a +N more overflow if necessary. Crop event chips have a crop glyph/thumb (small), short crop/event name and status pattern. Different kinds of event use icon as well as a muted tint; never show only a color.

Week view uses time/date columns only if time-of-day is relevant; farming events are commonly date-based, so all-day event timeline is default. Agenda view gives grouped rows with date, field, event, planned/actual status and source.
57.2 Event card
Selected-day panel title e.g. Friday, 16 October. Event row: 16 px icon, event title, crop/field, planned/actual badge, date/time, short note. Clicking opens event detail with source record link; Open field navigates to Field View. Actions Edit planned date, Mark completed, Delete personal event only when action is valid.
57.3 Date rules
Planned date updates propagate from Crop Cycle to future planned Calendar events. Actual planting/harvest history never gets shifted by changing planned dates. If an event references a removed field, preserve history with Archived field record reference instead of breaking event. If no planting date known, don't create false current crop events. Keep Calendar page separate from Crop Cycle summary; the field page only shows next milestone and link to Calendar.
58. Deep Analysis report — editorial hierarchy and detail panels
58.1 Report entry state
On Field View, Run Deep Analysis action opens a pre-run context panel only if the farmer has unresolved practice inputs; otherwise starts with a confirmation summary. Selected field/farm, data freshness, the saved practice profile and planned data refresh are shown. Do not ask the farmer to repeatedly enter the same stable watering routine; store it as a profile with date/source and ask whether it changed.
58.2 In-progress report screen
Use a calm report workspace rather than an empty spinner. Top has Deep Analysis in progress, field name and run ID short identifier. Left side lists actual stages such as Loading farm and field records, Refreshing weather data, Checking soil and crop inputs, Running water/soil/crop calculations, Investigating changes, Preparing report. Right side shows a quiet status summary and cancellation button. Every step changes based on real execution events only. Source failures show which step failed and whether run can continue. No fake percentages.
58.3 Completed report layout
Top summary band includes: overall status (Good/Needs Attention/Alert/Insufficient Data), overall health metric only if a real defined composite calculation exists, report time, run status, Recommendation Confidence and Data Coverage badges. Under it, a two-column report: left 8 columns Executive summary, Strengths, Concerns, Priority risks and Suggested improvements; right 4 columns Key indicators, Data limitations, Change since last run. For mobile stack summary, then concerns, then indicators.

Each finding must show: concise heading; observed/calculated fact; why it matters to this crop/field; supporting evidence link; confidence/uncertainty; action/mitigation if supported; and source/provenance. Example pattern: Vegetation activity declined → Latest valid observation is 0.09 below previous comparable observation on {date} → This is a reason to inspect, not a diagnosis; crop stage/cloud quality may explain part of the change. Never hardcode illustrative values into live output.
58.4 Findings cards
Findings use a compact vertical list, not ten giant identically styled boxes. Severity icon left, title and one-line reason, status on right, Evidence disclosure, Possible causes disclosure and Suggested check. Red/amber only for actual risk level. Strong but uncertain hypothesis must display uncertainty and alternative explanations. Avoid a large invented overall health score if only a few indicators are available.
58.5 Historical change summary
If previous comparable run exists, show Since the previous comparable run and 2–3 real changes, direction and units; include time gap and comparability badge. If source, time window, model version or field config changed significantly, mark comparison Limited comparability and explain why. If no previous run: This is the first saved Deep Analysis for this field.
58.6 Report text and actions
Simple mode uses short sections and plain language. Advanced mode expands formula/evidence details; don't turn the report into an unstructured model essay. Every actionable recommendation must have a basis and avoid a fabricated deadline. If no supported action exists, say No specific action is supported by the available evidence right now.
59. Deep Analysis Logs, trace viewer and automation Settings
59.1 Log/history page
Place Deep Analysis Logs as a secondary/main-nav item only if the agreed navigation uses it; do not create competing History, Logs, Automation and Audit pages all with duplicated records. The canonical Logs page has tabs/filters within one page: Runs, Trends, Automation. If older navigation specifies a secondary navigation bar inside Farm View, link to this same canonical route.

Top row: title Deep Analysis Logs; selected farm/field filter; date filter; status filter; search run notes; export only if export is implemented. Summary chips: Completed / Partial / Failed and last/next scheduled run only when scheduler supports it.

Log table/list: date/time, farm/field, run type Manual/Scheduled, status, overall conclusion, largest supported change, data coverage. Row click opens a detailed popup/modal with an X in upper-right; the modal shows saved summary, source snapshots, trace rows, calculated outputs and errors. The historical record must render from its saved snapshot, not today's current API response.
59.2 Execution trace rows
Each trace row: time at left (12 px mono), event icon, stage name, status badge (Completed / Partial / Failed / Skipped), duration if measured, disclosure chevron. Expanded panel includes actual action, input/source identifier, exact non-sensitive parameters, output summary, formula reference or transformation, warnings and error details. Trace event list is generated by instrumented application services, not generated by the LLM after the fact. Do not expose internal hidden reasoning/private chain of thought; show deterministic execution events and evidence.

Top controls: Expand all, Collapse all, filter by Data, Calculations, AI research, Persistence, Warnings/Errors. Search can filter only records loaded locally. Sensitive API keys, personal email, hidden location and raw private payloads are redacted.
59.3 Trend chart
Trend page shows selectable indicator with units and definitions; line chart with data-gap markers, source/quality note, date range and per-point tooltip. Only plot a history that actually exists. Distinguish score/model version changes with a vertical marker. Do not draw lines across gaps as though continuous.
59.4 Automation settings, truthful local behaviour
Automation control belongs in Settings and is available from Deep Analysis entry as a shortcut. Options can be Off, Daily, Every 3 days, Weekly, Custom interval if supported by local implementation. Before enabling, show saved farm/field scope and ask whether watering/farming practices remain consistent; user can choose Yes, use saved practices, Ask me before each run, or edit practice profile. If rain, irrigation or crop state changes, use stored activity/weather records that genuinely exist.

Browser-local scheduling limitation is explicit: the site cannot guarantee execution while the browser is fully closed. In local mode, save schedule configuration and due time in IndexedDB; when app opens and user permits, detect that a run is due and perform it once. UI label: Runs when Field Shift is open and helper A local browser session cannot guarantee background execution while the app is closed. Do not imply a background job/server is running. After a run, display actual last run time and next eligible check. Retry safely; no duplicates on reload.
60. Settings — dedicated professional page
Settings is a full page in main navigation/utility footer, not a crowded header popover. Header Settings / Configure how Field Shift works on this device. Left vertical subnav 220 px on desktop, content panel right; on mobile a select/list then section. Groups: General; Appearance & Accessibility; Simple/Advanced Mode; Units & Regional Format; Data Providers; AI Agronomist; Notifications; Local Data & Backups; Automation; Privacy & Reset; About & Diagnostics. Only include controls with real functionality.
60.1 General
Controls: default farm (select from saved farms), default landing page (Home or last opened where supported), time format, timezone source choice only if genuinely needed. Persist local preferences. State exact helper text and show a non-blocking Saved status after persistence confirms.
60.2 Appearance & accessibility
Theme displayed as fixed Dark Forest Intelligence with a visual sample; no theme switch because dark-only was selected. Additional real controls: reduced motion (Respect system default / Reduce motion), chart animation (on/off or follows reduced motion), compact-density preference only if implemented, larger text if supported. Do not introduce alternate palette themes.
60.3 Simple/Advanced Mode
Dedicated section Information detail with radio cards: Simple Mode — Plain-language summaries, the most important metrics and practical interpretation. Advanced Mode — Expanded measurements, sources, calculation steps, assumptions and detailed history. Selected state is clear, persisted locally, and updates the presentation across all pages without changing calculations or data. After a successful New Analysis or Deep Analysis in Simple Mode, show a one-time per-run prompt only after results are ready: title Want to see the full reasoning?, body Advanced Mode reveals detailed calculations, data sources, confidence limits and the reasoning behind the result., buttons Switch to Advanced and Not now, with a close X. It must not appear on error, during running, or repeatedly on render. It must not be in the nav/header.
60.4 Units and region
Temperature: Celsius/Fahrenheit; area: hectares/acres; water depth: mm/inches; speed: km/h/m/s; date locale/format. Conversion must happen only in presentation unless an engine explicitly needs normalised SI values. Store canonical units and maintain correct unit conversions; no hidden mixed units in calculations.
60.5 Data providers
Grouped cards for Weather; Satellite imagery; Soil; Crop parameters/knowledge; AI. Each shows provider name, role, configuration status, last successful check if actual, and a button Test connection only if a real test is implemented. For any secret provider, show variable names and instruction to configure local server environment, never show key itself. Do not store secret keys in localStorage or IndexedDB. State: Not configured, Connected, Failed, Rate limited, Unavailable based on actual test outcome.
60.6 Local Data & Backups
Show local record count/families and local storage status. Actions: Export all Field Shift data (versioned JSON package with schema/version metadata and user-readable warning); Import backup with validation/preview; Download analysis report only if implemented; Clear cache separate from Delete all farm data; Delete all local data requires destructive confirmation with list of affected records and optional export first. Do not promise cloud backups.
60.7 Notifications and privacy
In-app notification toggle and types; no email/push in current scope. Privacy explains that farm records are stored on this device in local mode, while data sent to external APIs/AI provider is transmitted to those services according to their terms. Do not claim fully offline/private if the app calls external services. Provide data reset and clear source payload guidance.
60.8 About and diagnostics
Show app version/build, local schema version, crop catalogue version, engine version, API status and export support, with Copy diagnostics that redacts secrets and private exact coordinates. Provide links to data-source documentation/attribution. No fake All systems operational badge.
61. AI Agronomist chat — precise UI and local behaviour
The AI Agronomist is different from farmer-to-farmer messaging. It uses currently accessible records and may call a configured Gemini endpoint/server route. Never use AI output as a replacement for the deterministic scoring/calculation engine.
61.1 Layout
Full page route AI Agronomist, accessible from a header assistant control, a Field View button Ask AI Agronomist, and relevant result cards. Desktop has a left conversation list 280–300 px, central chat 620–760 px, and optional right context drawer 260 px that can be collapsed. Header shows selected farm/field context chip and New conversation. At mobile only one pane at a time, with back to conversations.

Conversation list rows: title, last message preview, time, optional pinned marker; search placeholder Search conversations…; actions Rename, Pin/Unpin, Archive, Delete with confirmation. New convo names may be generated from first user message using deterministic truncation, not made-up AI metadata. Store history locally.
61.2 Message composition
Composer anchored at bottom of central pane, width full, min height 52 px, max 160 px before internal scroll. Placeholder Ask about this farm, rotation, soil, water, or analysis…. Send button arrow with accessible label Send message; Enter sends, Shift+Enter newline. Disable send only when empty or genuinely in-flight; show stop/cancel only if streaming can be cancelled. Show selected context chip (North Field · Wheat rotation) with remove/switch. File attachments only if fully implemented and storage behaviour defined; don't display paperclip if it does nothing.
61.3 Chat bubble styling and content
User message aligned right, muted leaf surface, rounded 14 px, max width 80%. Assistant message aligned left, transparent/raised surface with 18 px assistant icon; body 14–15 px line height 1.55, bullets and tables with sensible spacing. No giant branded bot card around each message. Each agronomic claim can have evidence/source chips below. Message footer: timestamp and Copy; thumbs feedback only if captured/used; retry if failed. Show AI can be wrong; verify high-impact decisions with reliable data or an agronomy professional in a discreet first-session notice, not every message.
61.4 Context and reliability
A collapsible right panel Farm context used lists selected farm, selected field, saved crop cycle/status, latest analysis ID/time and source records actually supplied to the model. If context is absent, tell user No farm selected and ask which local record to use rather than inserting a default US farm. The model may receive only relevant minimum data, and all private context stays in local/private views.

Answer presentation is concise by default, with Show evidence to inspect source records, inputs and calculation references. Do not reveal hidden reasoning. If no key/config is present, use deterministic explanation/FAQ where possible and show AI provider isn't configured on this device; don't pretend a live AI response was generated.
62. Global Farming Network, messages, and communities — polished end-state / honest local mode
62.1 Separate the concepts
Three surfaces are distinct: (1) AI Agronomist chat (can function locally if configured); (2) Global Farming Network discovery/compare; (3) farmer-to-farmer Messages/Communities. Do not conflate the assistant bot with a farmer profile.
62.2 Global Network layout when a backend exists
Workspace resembles a polished professional map/social hybrid. Top header Global Farming Network; search Search farms, cities, countries…; subnav Discover, Messages, Communities, My Network, Compare My Farm. Map uses public city-level markers/coloured city areas only. Left panel includes geographic context and map legend; right panel selected city and published farm list; bottom/side context includes regional comparison. Public profiles have a detail design consistent with Farm View but a strict allowlist.

City click opens selected-city drawer with city/country, published-farm count, sample size, update time and quality state. It never reveals exact farms' coordinates, outlines, directions or proximity. City color is derived only from eligible published farms and uses a documented aggregate algorithm; if sample is small, stale or incomparable, show Insufficient data instead of a falsely precise colour.

Public farm profile: farm name, city/country only, owner-approved image/about, shared crops/rotation, approved indicators and trends, timestamps/source, Add connection, Message, and relevant community links. Do not fetch private record then hide it in UI; backend returns only approved public projection.
62.3 Messages UI when a shared backend exists
Desktop three-column layout: conversation list 300 px, message thread flexible, contextual contact panel 260 px (collapsible). Conversation list top: Messages, search, filter All/Unread/Archived, compose icon. Each item: avatar/initials, display name, last message preview, time, unread badge based on actual stored state, pinned/muted icon if enabled. Clicking thread sets read state only after a legitimate open and persisted update. No fake unread number.

Thread header: name, shared farm/city at city-level only, connection status; actions Search conversation, Mute, Block/Report and overflow. Messages bubble with sender distinction, timestamps, day separators, delivery/read markers only if the backend supports them. Composer supports text, emoji/attachments only if supported, keyboard send behavior and input length. Show sending/pending/failed states; failed send can Retry. When offline, queue only if secure local queue semantics are specified; never claim sent until backend ack.

Conversation detail panel: public profile summary, shared interests/communities, View public farm profile; never show exact private address. Block prevents further contact from all entry points; report opens category/reason modal. Mute hides notifications but preserves messages. Delete/archive confirmation explains whether it removes only local view or server-side conversation according to actual policy.
62.4 Communities UI
Communities page includes search/filter by topic/region/crop/practice and list cards with name, description, members count only if real, privacy/type, last activity only if actual. Community detail page has header/cover image with dark gradient, description, rules, posts feed, pinned post, members/moderators and join/leave controls. Post cards have author, time, content, optional media, reactions/comments only if implemented, report action and context tag. Do not promote community anecdotes into scientific facts. Future moderation requires report queue, block controls, content deletion and access checks.
62.5 Local-only mode
Because current product decision is no login, no cloud database and local-only farm records, true cross-user profiles, synced friend graph, shared messages, cross-device chat and real regional aggregates cannot operate. Do not populate fake registered people/farms/communities, messages, online indicators, unread counts or fake comparative averages. Show a refined information page with header Global Farming Network, one-line explanation Connect with other farmers and compare shared regional evidence when a shared network service is enabled., status Not connected in local mode, and sections Preview / What becomes available. State explicitly that local farm data remains private on this device. All local Farm View, AI Agronomist and local analysis remain usable. Do not call this limitation a successful social interaction.
63. Page Guide / interactive help — exact behaviour
Open via Help in lower navigation or a contextual ? affordance. Modal width up to 1100 px desktop, height 80vh, split 32% chapter list left / 68% interactive preview right. Header Field Shift Guide, search Search help topics…, close X. Left list grouped: Getting started; Farms & maps; New Analysis; Rotations; What-If; Deep Analysis; Data & confidence; Settings & privacy.

The preview uses the actual local app component tree/route or a purpose-built walkthrough state that shares real components. It must not be a static fake screenshot if it promises interactivity. A guide cursor animation may highlight a control, click it in a safe sandbox state, and cause the preview to update; main user's data is not modified unless the user deliberately selects Try it in my farm and confirms. Controls: Play/Pause, step progress, Next/Previous, reset example. Explain each click with one concise callout. When route or feature not implemented, hide chapter or mark Not available yet; never simulate functionality that is broken in the actual app.
64. Page-by-page responsive variants
64.1 Widths to test
Test 1920, 1600, 1440, 1280, 1024, 768, 430, 390 and 360 px. At large widths main layout uses declared column ratios. At 1280, compress primary nav spacing and move lower-priority links into More; do not introduce a global sidebar. At 1024 and below, hide one Maps side panel behind a contextual drawer. At 768 and below, page content stacks. At 430 and below, use one main column with compact card padding 14–16 px. No page-level horizontal scroll except chart/table/map containers with deliberate horizontal interaction.
64.2 Map responsive
At <1100 px, use one overlay panel at a time: buttons Farm details and My farms; panels slide in as drawer while the map retains pan/zoom. At <680 px, My Farms appears as bottom sheet over map with max-height 55vh and list scroll; drawing tool rail moves above bottom safe area, never behind sheet. A visible button Hide panel returns full map. Add safe-area padding for mobile browsers.
64.3 Forms responsive
Form rows become one column below 720 px. Numeric inputs keep unit suffix attached; controls have 44 px height (or larger); avoid two 44px controls within a narrow row. Validation text wraps and moves content below the control; it must not overlay other fields.
64.4 Tables and chart fallback
Advanced data tables turn into row cards on small screens with label/value layout, or use a horizontally scrollable container with frozen label column only if tested. Always make complete information available without precision dragging. For chart, include View table as accessible route.
64.5 Modal, drawer, keyboard and reduced motion
Modal becomes near-full screen on mobile with pinned title/close and sticky bottom action bar. All critical drag operations have a click/tap alternative. Touch controls meet 44 × 44 px target where practical; follow WCAG 2.2. Use system reduced-motion setting to disable decorative parallax, long fly-to, chart draw-ins and pulsing. Never flash colors.
65. Motion and micro-interaction catalogue
Interaction	Motion	Required state/logic
Route change	160–220 ms opacity + 4 px translate	route changes only after valid nav/confirm
Select farm on map	450–800 ms eased camera transition	fit bounds with panel padding; selected card/inspector update together
Select field	180 ms card/detail transition	use stable field ID; no stale previous values
Open right drawer	200–240 ms slide/fade	focus moves into panel; map remains navigable if overlay is narrow
Open modal	160–200 ms fade/scale from 0.985	focus trap and unsaved-change guard
Polygon vertex	small point-scale reveal	update preview/area only when geometry valid enough
Close polygon	line joins start point smoothly	ring validation, self-intersection warning
Save	button pending state then confirmation check	check durable save completion before success label
Weather chart	short one-time path draw	line based on actual values, exact values available immediately
Status update	no score count-up; brief tint on changed label	only when persisted status change is real
Skeleton	low-contrast shimmer 1.2–1.6 s or static block	reduced-motion uses static skeleton
Toast	220 ms enter, 180 ms exit	only after real operation response; dismissable


No repeated pulse on farm outline, endless gradient movement, artificial blinking alerts, fireworks/confetti for ordinary farm events or global particle effects behind scientific content.
66. Visual states and exact microcopy
Use consistent language across pages.

Network/connection: Connected, Not configured, Unavailable, Rate limited, Last successful update: {time}, Couldn't reach this source. No new reading was saved.

Data quality: Measured, Farmer reported, Modelled estimate, Regional estimate, Satellite-derived, Calculated, Demo data, Stale, Insufficient data. Each term must match real provenance.

Crop status: Recommended, Planned, Active, Paused, Changed, Completed, Not confirmed, Upcoming.

Analysis run: Queued, Running, Completed, Completed with limitations, Partial, Failed, Cancelled. Never use Completed to imply all providers succeeded.

No farms: No farms yet / Add a farm to connect a map boundary with fields, crop cycles and analysis history. / Add farm.

No fields: No fields added / Add a field to track soil, crop history, irrigation and rotation independently. / Add field.

No plan: No rotation plan saved / Run New Analysis to compare crop sequences for this field. / Start New Analysis.

No actual crop: No active crop confirmed / A saved recommendation is not proof that planting has started.

No analysis history: No Deep Analysis runs yet / Run Deep Analysis to create your first dated report. / Run Deep Analysis.

Source data missing: This value is unavailable from the current data sources. Optional supporting text names the missing dependency; never display 0 for missing data.

Unsaved drawing: You have an unfinished farm boundary. buttons Resume drawing, Save draft and leave, Discard draft.

Local scheduling: Runs when Field Shift is open / A local browser session cannot guarantee background execution while the app is closed.
67. Image and chart quality control
Before a screen is accepted: inspect every crop image for correct species; inspect hero image crop and text contrast at desktop and mobile; ensure no repeated generic crop image across different species; ensure alt text/source/credits are recorded; check image load failure; check chart axes/units/point values; ensure overlay pseudo-elements don't block controls; confirm map satellite tiles remain natural and provider attribution readable.

For high-quality imagery, prefer original generated non-text photographs, verified open-license assets with attribution, or a stable licensed provider. Don't scrape images from a design portfolio and include them as final UI media. A screenshot reference is not a deployable asset. Use separate original assets for each distinct crop; weather photos can be drawn from an approved asset set, with source and license saved in manifest.
