69. End-to-end UX walkthroughs (must all be tested)
Walkthrough A — First use with no stored data
1. App opens to Home with no fake verified farms and no fabricated metrics.
2. Home displays a calm illustrated empty state plus primary Add a farm and secondary Start New Analysis.
3. Selecting Add farm opens Maps creation flow and sets a boundary draft; the user can cancel, resume or delete it.
4. Saving farm creates local farm ID; My Farms list and Home update from the same store.
5. User adds first field, enters soil known/unknown state and crop history; fields connect to farm ID.
6. New Analysis uses that field's info; unknown data stays unknown and coverage badge reflects it.
7. Saving a rotation as plan creates planned cycle and Calendar milestones; no active crop appears until planting is confirmed.
Walkthrough B — Farm map drawing mistakes are recoverable
1. User clicks + Add Farm, types duplicate name; inline conflict presents Link Existing or Rename.
2. User locates place and starts polygon; 4 points create outline and area estimate.
3. User places point incorrectly, clicks Undo point, then adds correct point.
4. User accidentally clicks a previously set point or self-crosses; validation names problem and allows vertex adjustment.
5. User leaves by mistake; draft autosaves locally and can resume.
6. User saves, then later opens Edit boundary, adds/removes vertex, reviews recalculated area and saves.
7. User deletes map boundary; confirmation says farm data remains; map shows boundary-missing state rather than deleting farm.
Walkthrough C — New Analysis and crop lifecycle
1. Start New Analysis from Home or Field View; selected farm/field is prefilled.
2. Fill inputs; user can select unknown soil properties; no fake pH/water quota is inserted.
3. Initial candidates calculate; contextual risk question appears only when a materially different risk/benefit trade-off exists.
4. Farmer rejects risk; engine recalculates/filter ranks feasible balanced alternatives and explains.
5. Farmer opens rotation card and sees crop images, duration/date windows, pros/cons and comparison.
6. Save as plan; it shows Planned. Calendar gets planned events.
7. Farmer confirms planting with actual date; only then it becomes Active; Home/Farm View update.
8. Farmer records harvest/crop change; planned future items update, actual past events persist, prior analysis snapshot unchanged.
Walkthrough D — Deep Analysis, logs, automation and data failure
1. Farmer opens Deep Analysis; routine practice values are loaded and can be reviewed/updated, not asked anew every time.
2. The report trace displays real data fetch/calculation events.
3. One API fails: report status becomes partial if affected content is limited; no placeholder success or new current number.
4. Completed report is stored with run ID, source timestamps, formulas/version and data-quality state.
5. User opens historical log popup; it shows saved report and detailed actual trace; X closes and returns to same log filters.
6. User enables every-three-days local schedule; it explains app must be open; on next open, due-run logic fires once and avoids duplicates.
7. Two runs are compared only where inputs/time windows/model versions are sufficiently comparable.
Walkthrough E — Settings and Advanced Mode
1. User opens Settings via dedicated nav/footer; there is no Simple/Advanced toggle in main header.
2. User changes mode to Advanced; setting persists.
3. Field View now exposes detail panels and formula/source disclosures without changing score values.
4. Re-running the same analysis with identical versioned inputs produces same deterministic ranking; display mode never changes engine.
5. User exports data, imports backup preview, and understands what will change before import.
Walkthrough F — local-only limits and chat
1. AI Agronomist page works only when configured provider endpoint exists; missing provider shows explicit config status and deterministic help where possible.
2. Global Farming Network displays a polished but honest unavailable state for cross-user functions in local-only mode; no fake members/messages/online badges.
3. Local farm data remains usable. When backend mode is added later, public location projection remains city-level only and publication uses explicit allowlists.
