73. Why this annex exists and how the builder must apply it
Do not interpret “professional UI” as a colour palette plus a row of generic cards. Implement the exact information hierarchy, component family, spatial relationship, copy, data binding, interaction contract, loading and failure state described for each page. The user expects every feature—not merely Home, Maps, and Weather—to receive comparable design care. This means New Analysis, rotation browsing and comparison, crop-cycle management, Deep Analysis reports, trace logs, settings, local data, AI chat, calendar, help, and future network surfaces all receive defined layouts and behaviour.

Before coding a route, create a page implementation checklist containing: (1) its user question; (2) selected farm/field context; (3) visible components in top-to-bottom order; (4) exact labels and value formats; (5) imagery asset requirements; (6) chart or visualization type; (7) actions and their consequences; (8) Simple Mode content; (9) Advanced Mode content; (10) loading, empty, stale, partial, unavailable, and error states; (11) keyboard/touch behaviour; (12) persistence effect; and (13) acceptance test. Do not mark the route complete until all thirteen are checked against the live implementation.

The final product must remain visually rich without becoming a wall of cards. Use composition, proportion, photography, visual hierarchy, whitespace, small data stories, maps, timeline controls, sparklines, focused comparison layouts, disclosure panels, and context-preserving side drawers. Do not put every paragraph inside a rounded rectangle. Use containers only to signal a genuine group, hierarchy, or task boundary.
74. Art direction: specific visual language and usable cinematic effects
74.1 Core mood
The website should feel like a premium earth-intelligence workstation at the intersection of environmental editorial design, GIS software, and high-quality agricultural decision support. It should not feel like a generic admin template, a sci-fi neon console, a cryptocurrency dashboard, or an overly glassy mobile app stretched onto desktop.

The visual composition should alternate between three deliberate surface types:
1. Cinematic visual surfaces: weather hero, selected-farm map context, crop-sequence hero, empty-state discovery image, and occasional page introduction. These can use full-bleed photography, controlled overlay gradients, atmospheric highlights, and restrained text overlap.
2. Operational data surfaces: metric strips, status lists, compact cards, tables, map panels, timeline items, filters, and controls. These use opaque-enough dark surfaces for quick scanning, subtle borders and minimal decoration.
3. Technical evidence surfaces: formulas, source records, execution traces, parameter tables, model assumptions and confidence explanations. These prioritise legibility, code/mono numerals, grouping, copyable values and expandable density rather than cinematic imagery.
Do not use the cinematic treatment on every surface. The contrast between a few impressive visual regions and calm functional regions is what makes the product premium instead of noisy.
74.2 Colour design tokens
Use a dark-only design system called Forest Intelligence. These tokens are the initial target; the implementation may tune them slightly after contrast testing, but must maintain the same intent and semantics.
Token	Target	Use
--fs-bg-0	#07110E	Deepest application background
--fs-bg-1	#0B1712	Main page background
--fs-surface-1	#101F18	Standard panel/card
--fs-surface-2	#15271E	Raised or selected panel
--fs-surface-3	#1C3025	Hover/expanded content surface
--fs-forest	#244B35	Primary forest identity
--fs-leaf	#78AE78	Primary action/positive botanical accent
--fs-leaf-bright	#A7D49B	Selected graph point/primary hover, used sparingly
--fs-teal	#62C7B2	Water/secondary analytical series
--fs-sky	#83B9D7	Weather/environment information
