Status semantic colours must not be repurposed for decoration. Green means healthy/positive only when the defined rules support it. Amber means needs attention or material caution. Red means active high-severity concern or destructive failure, not “high score”. Blue/teal is information, not an implied severity. Grey means neutral/unknown/unavailable. Pair every color state with a text label and icon or shape; never communicate meaning through colour alone.
74.3 Typography and numerical hierarchy
Use a consistent humanist sans-serif for interface text (recommended: Plus Jakarta Sans or a similarly readable family) and a tabular/monospace style only for exact technical values, coordinates, IDs, timestamps, or formula input/output. Do not make every text line monospace.

Target scale at desktop:
- Product wordmark: 15–17 px, 650–750 weight, expanded tracking around 0.10em only on the wordmark.
- Page title: 28–34 px / 1.16 line height / 600–700 weight.
- Main visual metric, such as temperature or a headline index: 42–56 px / 1.0 line height, tabular numerals.
- Section title: 16–19 px / 1.3 / 600 weight.
- Card title: 14–16 px / 1.3 / 600 weight.
- Body text: 13–15 px / 1.45–1.6.
- Compact labels: 10–12 px, normal case, restrained tracking; never use tiny caps for critical information.
- Units and provenance: 11–12 px with contrast adequate to read.
- Formula/trace values: 12–13 px tabular/monospaced.
Use font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums for aligned values. Always render units near the value: 34 °C, 14 km/h, 12.4 ha, 36 mm, 0.62 NDVI. Do not use a superscript unit style that makes the value hard to scan. If units are converted for display, retain canonical storage units underneath and test conversions.
74.4 Spacing, grid, card geometry and responsive shell
At 1440 × 900: top bar 64 px; page content max-width 1600 px; standard page horizontal padding 28 px; vertical top padding 24 px; primary grid 12 columns with 20 px gutters; major section spacing 28–32 px; card-internal spacing 16–20 px; compact row gap 8–12 px. Standard card radius 14 px; large visual hero/map frame 18–22 px; input/button radius 9–11 px. Avoid excessive pill shapes—use pills for compact statuses/filter chips, not every button and panel.

At 1280 px: decrease content padding to 22 px and preserve the essential labels. At 1024 px: hide non-essential right-side secondary rail behind a drawer; avoid squeezing three columns of metrics smaller than a readable width. At 768 px: collapse content into a single main column and use horizontal chips only when the scrolling affordance is obvious. At 390 px: 16 px side padding, one main column, map takes near-full width/height, panels become bottom sheets, main action remains reachable with one thumb, and long crop names/metrics wrap without overlapping. No general horizontal page scroll is allowed.
74.5 Gradients, backdrop filtering, grain and lighting
Use CSS effects as layers with distinct purposes. Example cinematic image treatment:
.cinematic-surface {
  position: relative;
  isolation: isolate;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--fs-surface-1);
}
.cinematic-surface::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  background:
    linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(5,15,11,.88) 0%, rgba(5,15,11,.52) 42%, rgba(5,15,11,.12) 100%),
    linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(5,15,11,.82) 0%, rgba(5,15,11,.05) 56%, rgba(5,15,11,.25) 100%);
}
.cinematic-surface::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -20%;
  z-index: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse at 70% 10%, rgba(244,205,130,.16), transparent 34%);
  mix-blend-mode: screen;
}
.cinematic-surface > * { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
Use a component modifier to place the light bloom according to the image focal point; it should not always be top-right. Keep the main contrast scrim above the photo but behind text and controls. Use backdrop-filter: blur(14–22px) saturate(115%) on select floating panels only; fallback to an opaque dark fill when unsupported. Do not stack many translucent cards on top of each other, as that muddies text.

Optional grain: use a very low-opacity, local SVG noise overlay or small packaged texture with pointer-events:none; opacity should be barely visible (around 0.025–0.045). Never apply grain to small text or charts in a way that affects legibility. Add edge-vignette for cinematic photographs with radial-gradient(ellipse at center, transparent 45%, rgba(0,0,0,.34) 100%); clip it to the image wrapper. Do not use CSS text glow. The perceived sunlight glow in the weather reference should be a light bloom attached to the photo, not a neon effect attached to text or card edges.

When using blend modes, verify all text and focus states on the actual final browser. Layering must not swallow pointer actions: all pseudo-elements are pointer-events none; controls retain a higher stacking context. Avoid animations that continuously shimmer large areas or drain battery.
74.6 Asset selection and photography contract
Every meaningful photo asset requires a named subject, purpose, crop treatment, attribution/licence record where required, expected focal point, fallback image and alt text. Do not reuse the same hero image for unrelated crops. Do not use one generic leaf photo for all crops. Crop cards must have distinct crop-appropriate photos/illustrations. If exact real-world crop imagery cannot be sourced safely, use a coherent, custom illustrated crop portrait rather than mislabel an unrelated photograph.

Required asset families:
- Weather hero: real atmospheric sky matching the current weather category when a legitimate condition-to-image asset mapping exists. Never imply the background image is the measured weather observation. Store image category metadata independently from weather values. Provide clear-night, cloudy, rain, storm, hazy, hot/sunny fallbacks.
- Crops: wheat, maize/corn, beans/legumes, lettuce, alfalfa/forage, rice, tomato, potatoes and every additional crop in the supported crop knowledge base; each crop has its own image ID and alt text. Do not show unverified crops as supported only because an image exists.
- Farm context: satellite map is a functional map tile source, not a screenshot background. For cards, use a cropped aerial photograph only as non-authoritative visual context and label it as such.
- Soil and water: restrained scientific illustrations for soil layers, root zone, rainfall, irrigation, drainage, nutrient cycles where they aid comprehension; these must not imply field sampling has occurred.
- Empty states: small editorial images such as a field notebook/map/seedling, consistent with forest palette; use only for truly empty states, not when data retrieval has failed.
- Help illustrations: lightweight vector animations/screen captures generated from actual UI interactions, not fictional values.
Set image CSS per role: crop portraits use object-fit: cover, 4:3 aspect ratio, focal position adjusted per crop; weather hero uses cover and a minimum 480 px desktop height, with focal point metadata; tiny avatar/thumbnail images never become the only identifier. All image loads need a fallback, decoding="async", appropriate loading priority (eager only for top hero), and a stable aspect-ratio box to prevent layout shift.

