75. Global design-system components — each must have a real contract
75.1 Page heading
Structure: optional breadcrumb above; page title and one-sentence purpose on the left; farm/field context and last successful update near the title if relevant; one dominant primary action on the right; overflow actions grouped under a clearly labelled menu. Title row height target 44–52 px, purpose 13–14 px below title, then 20–24 px gap before page content. Do not show four primary actions equally strongly. Exact copy is page-specific and listed below.
75.2 Farm/field context selector
Selected entity is a compact thumbnail or icon + farm name + field name if applicable + down chevron. It must not occupy an oversized pill. Clicking opens a searchable dropdown or anchored popover with recent farms, farm status, location label, fields nested under farms, and a “View all farms” link. Each item shows name, location at appropriate privacy level, compact state icon, and selected checkmark. Selecting a farm/field updates the entire route context atomically; show a small skeleton during refresh and never leave old farm data visible beside the new farm name. On pages where a specific field is mandatory, changing to a farm with multiple fields requires field choice before displaying field metrics.
75.3 Status badge versus confidence badges
Use three distinct concepts with visually different iconography and shapes:
- Farm/Crop health status: filled/semi-filled compact label with leaf/shield symbol; green “Good”, amber “Needs attention”, red “Alert”, grey “Insufficient data”.
- Recommendation confidence: outlined hexagon/target or gauge icon, label Recommendation confidence: Moderate when expanded; confidence refers to reliability of the model result given coverage/validation/uncertainty.
- Data coverage: database/layers icon in a rectangular neutral badge, label such as Data coverage: 4 of 6 inputs. It refers to inputs collected/usable, not probability that the recommendation is correct.
Do not display both confidence concepts as nearly identical green pill badges. Confidence should use an outlined meter/target icon and neutral/blue-grey treatment; coverage should use a stacked-layers/database glyph and a grey-blue segmented strip. Their detailed popovers define the method and display missing factors. Never use false numerical confidence as a decoration.
75.4 Metric tile
Default structure: small icon aligned top-left, short label immediately beside or below it, large value underneath, unit adjacent to value, one compact interpretation line, optional sparklines in lower 25–30% of the tile, and a quiet source/freshness label at the bottom. At desktop use 150–210 px width and 96–128 px height for ordinary metrics. Do not place a large icon and a small metric at opposite corners with no relationship. Use consistent icon line weight (1.6–1.8 px stroke, 16–20 px size). Sparkline range and colour must have meaning, not be decorative. On hover/focus, reveal a tooltip with definition, source, timestamp, temporal scope, resolution and the implication for the selected farm. Simple mode shows value + unit + one-line interpretation; Advanced adds range, chart, source, method, uncertainty and calculation details.
75.5 Data provenance, freshness and availability
Every API-derived, satellite-derived, lab-measured, farmer-entered, estimated or calculated value receives a compact label or accessible tooltip. Use consistent labels Observed, Forecast, Satellite estimate, Mapped estimate, Soil test, Farmer-reported, Calculated, Modelled scenario, Unavailable, Stale. Provide a clock/source icon, but don't force a large badge on every metric—use a quiet metadata line and shared accessible details disclosure. Data freshness should show exact timestamp or period, plus “updated 2 hours ago” only when computed reliably. A fresh fetch of an old historical measurement remains historical; retrieval time must not be misrepresented as observation time.
75.6 Button hierarchy
One filled forest/leaf primary action per visible action group; secondary actions use low-contrast surface/border; tertiary actions use text or icon buttons with tooltips. Destructive actions are separated at the bottom of menus/dialogs and use red only when confirmed. Every icon-only control requires an accessible label and tooltip. Touch target minimum 24×24 CSS px as baseline; target around 36–40 px for frequently used controls. Hover is not the only source of information. Disable button only with a short reason or inline validation message; don't leave a grey button that gives no explanation.
75.7 Expandable row / accordion
Use for evidence, formulas, raw API details, longer explanations, crop-specific caveats and trace items. Header shows a concise title, icon, status/summary and expand chevron. Entire header is keyboard-operable; expanded content preserves reading order and does not animate height in a way that clips focus. Default state: closed for advanced technical payload in Simple Mode; selected or most important section can be open. Advanced Mode may open the first explanatory panel, but never auto-expand every row into a 30-screen wall of text. Remember expansion state locally for the current route if safe; changing farm clears entity-specific expansion to avoid leaking old context into the new page.
75.8 Dialog, drawer, popover and bottom sheet
Use a modal only for a task requiring focused confirmation or a multi-step small interaction. Use a side drawer for contextual evidence, notification details and chat. Use an anchored popover for quick selection of one/few options. Use a mobile bottom sheet for map/farm details that would otherwise obscure the map. Dialog max width 560 px for standard forms, 720–880 px for complex review/compare views; 20–24 px internal padding; title top-left, close × top-right, body below, actions bottom-right. Escape closes only if safe; unsaved edits require confirmation. On close, restore focus to the opening control. Backdrop click should not accidentally discard a form; use confirmation for dirty state.
76. Global shell and navigation — exact placement and behaviour
Use a horizontal top bar, not a permanent global left sidebar. At 1440 px, header height 64 px, with 28 px outer padding. Left: Field Shift mark (botanical/contour leaf icon) + wordmark, divider, subtle descriptor Agricultural intelligence. Centre: compact nav items in this order: Home, Maps, New Analysis, Rotations, Analysis, Calendar, Global Network. Analysis is the What-If workspace only; do not add a separate top-level Simulator item. Use a selected tab background with quiet rounded rectangle, not bright underline plus glow. Right: global search, compact weather/location indicator when a farm is selected (only if useful; it must open weather details), alert bell with true unread count, AI Agronomist icon, and Settings icon/link. Keep Settings accessible but not as an oversized primary nav tab. If width is insufficient, move secondary actions into a labelled overflow; never allow overlapping nav labels.

The header remains sticky only if it does not obscure focus targets; use z-index below modal/drawer layers and above page content. Background is semi-opaque deep forest with subtle 1 px lower divider and optional 8–12 px backdrop blur, not a large halo. At mobile: wordmark shortens to the brand mark + Field Shift; primary nav becomes a compact horizontal scroll/tab drawer or dedicated menu; all main destinations remain reachable within one additional tap; global search and alert actions remain discoverable.

Simple/Advanced selector is not a top-bar button or main navigation item. It lives in Settings → Appearance & Information Detail. The selected mode is a global presentation setting applied consistently across pages; it never changes calculation methods, scores, source retrieval, farm state or saved data.

Global search supports farms, fields, crop species, past analyses and saved rotations. Show result category icon, title, parent farm, context and status. Search must not expose private data to a future public context. In local mode, search indexes only records on this device. Keyboard / or Ctrl/Cmd+K may open search if not typing in an input; Escape closes it and focus is restored.
77. Home — full component order and exact visual composition
77.1 Desktop layout
Home answers: “Which farm needs attention, what are conditions like, and where should I go next?” The page uses a 12-column grid, but not 12 identical tiles. First row is a selected-farm title/context row and a small current-state indicator. Primary content uses a 7-column weather feature and 5-column farm status/alert region on desktop. A second row uses a wide map preview (7 columns) and crop-cycle/rotation overview (5 columns). A compact recent analysis/activity strip sits below only when real records exist. Maintain a visible New Analysis primary button near the title/context row, not buried at the bottom.
77.2 Weather cinematic hero
Desktop target: 7-column region (approx. 760×340 px at 1440-wide content), image container minimum height 320 px. Use a high-quality photo of sky/weather that matches the category. Apply layered gradients: strong left/bottom dark scrim for text, transparent mid-right region preserving atmospheric light, bottom vignette for line chart labels, and a very subtle golden radial light bloom near the image's natural light source. Image object-fit:cover, focal position preconfigured per asset. Put location pin + Current conditions + farm locality at top-left, observation time at top-right in a small badge.

Left half: a weather condition icon 34–42 px in its own soft surface; temperature 44–52 px with °C or selected display units; weather phrase 14 px; Feels like below in secondary text. Put High/Low in two small stacked or paired outlined chips to the temperature's right—not at a random far edge. Under main metric, create three secondary weather tiles across the card: Humidity, Wind, and Rain chance or Precipitation, depending on available data. Each tile shows icon 16 px, label 11 px, value 17–19 px, unit, and optional directional sublabel. Don't show precipitation probability if the source doesn't provide it.

Bottom ~34%: forecast chart integrated over photo with a translucent black-to-transparent band behind x-axis labels. Use a smooth SVG/line chart, 6–7 forecast points; actual dates/weekdays under points; y-range auto-bounded to sensible interval; a thin curve in a muted mint gradient; no huge full chart grid. Highlight current/selected day with a vertical dotted line and small sun/cloud glyph positioned at its point. Selected value white; other values muted but readable. Hover/tap shows tooltip with date, max/min, precipitation probability only if valid. The selected current day uses Today, not an arbitrary weekday. If the source only returns daily max/min and not hourly shape, the line represents daily forecast values and must be labelled Daily high; do not imply minute-by-minute weather. If only historical weather is available, do not render a future curve and call it forecast.

Simple mode: location, current temperature/condition, feels-like when available, three supporting metrics and a compact 5–7 day forecast curve. Advanced mode adds full hourly/daily toggle, apparent temperature/humidity/wind/precipitation chart series, source/model and update time, data gap indicators and historical-normal comparison when supported. Keep forecast model assumptions in a disclosure, not on the hero face.
77.3 Farm status block
At the top of its block: small leaf glyph + Farm condition, badge with defined status, and a timestamp. The main visual is a 72–92 px ring or segmented arc representing the defined composite score only when its formula and inputs exist; if it does not, use a status label without numeric ring. Put numeric score in centre, with /100 below. To the right, use three compact horizontal status rows: Water, Soil, Crop cycle, each with a distinct icon, state word and small dot/indicator. Don't give a precise score for unknown soil. Below rows, show the highest-priority current alert as a short horizontal strip with warning icon, concise finding, location within farm if known and chevron. Clicking goes to the exact evidence panel, not a generic alert list. If no alerts, use a quiet one-line No current alerts from available data. only when the data coverage supports that claim; otherwise state No active alerts; some indicators are unavailable.

Simple mode: status, score only if valid, 2–3 key domains, one priority observation. Advanced mode: domain scores and their defined formulas, contributing metrics, time window, raw values, data confidence and last run IDs. Clicking status card opens the shared Farm View.
77.4 Map preview
A wide card with genuine interactive map thumbnail centered on selected farm polygon; don't use a static screenshot. At 7 columns × lower-row height around 250–290 px, show satellite tiles, subtle outline of selected farm, farm name pin/label and a 1 km/appropriate scale cue. Add a top-left Farm map label, layer icon and right-aligned Open Maps action. Clicking background or outline navigates to Maps preserving selected farm and map viewport. Do not make an exact map background into a fake miniature static screenshot if map engine supports interactive rendering. If external map tiles fail, show a designed static fallback with saved polygon, location name and retry state.
77.5 Crop Cycle summary
Show a horizontal crop sequence of three crop portrait tiles if three stages fit; if longer, show first/current/next and +N crops or use a thin timeline rather than squeezing eight small tiles. Each crop image is distinct and appropriately licensed. Crop name 13 px semibold; month-year date window 11 px; status word (Active, Planned, Upcoming, Completed) with small icon. Highlight actual active crop via a 2 px leaf-green outline and Current label. If the cycle is only recommended and not saved/active, use Recommended rotation and do not invent a currently growing crop. CTA Open crop cycle leads to Farm View crop section.
77.6 Signal/alert strip and recent activity
Signals are compact rows sorted by priority and recency. Each row shows severity symbol, 1-line title, one-line why-it-matters, relevant farm/field and age. Avoid oversized red banners unless a high-severity condition truly exists. Swipe-to-dismiss may be available on touch but must also have a visible overflow/menu option. Dismiss doesn't delete underlying evidence. Recent activity only renders when there are saved activities/analysis runs. Empty state is a small illustration with copy Your farm activity will appear here as you record planting, irrigation, inspections and harvests. + Record an activity action.
78. Full Weather view — cinematic visual system plus usable data
Weather view should feel like a designed environmental scene, not a spreadsheet. At desktop, hero spans content width at ~360–430 px high, using the same weather image family and gradient strategy as Home, scaled with consistent focal point and careful contrast. Top line: location pin, full farm/field locality (private, not public map), source-time label, location/field selector. Top-right: unit selector is in Settings; a small overflow menu can provide Open source details, Refresh data and Copy forecast summary where supported.

Main temperature sits left of centre, 52–64 px. Weather description below, real-feel, high/low chips immediately adjacent. If provider supplies next-hour precipitation, use a compact precipitation strip only with real probability/time. Right side may hold a small animated condition illustration, but no independent “3D weather model” is required unless a real 3D asset exists and it improves meaning.

Below hero: horizontal hourly forecast rail (scrollable, cards 88–104 px wide) with time, weather glyph, temperature, precipitation if available. Then a 7-day strip with day labels, condition glyph, high/low, rainfall totals. Then a chart section with tabs/selector for temperature, precipitation, wind; only series that exists is selectable. Chart axes have units; tooltips contain timestamp + provider value; legend toggles series; selected time point synchronizes with forecast card. Add a What this means for your farm insight section only when rules/data support it, such as heat alert during a known crop stage. It must cite relevant inputs and state that a forecast is not a guarantee.

Advanced mode adds model/provider, update cadence, forecast horizon, hourly table, historical comparison and calculation/evidence notes. Simple mode retains temperature, conditions, high/low, 3 key metrics, hourly or daily preview and a short supported farm implication. Don't display the whole weather API payload by default.
79. Maps — pixel-level interaction contract and drawing convenience
79.1 Workspace layout
The Maps page is a dedicated top-level nav destination. At 1440×900 below the 64 px global bar, use a fixed-height workspace ~calc(100vh - 64px) with no body scrolling while map is the active canvas. Three columns: left selected-farm inspector 300–330 px, central map flexible (minmax(420px, 1fr)), right My Farms panel 300–330 px. Gap: 6–10 px; workspace frame radius 16 px; map reaches edges within the rounded frame. The left panel supports selected farm context, status, key environment facts, currently visible layer/date and Open Farm View. The centre is the large satellite map. The right panel title My Farms, a concise count, and a visible + Add Farm button aligned on the same header row. These are contextual map panels, not a global nav sidebar.

The central map must remain the dominant visual: full-colour natural satellite tiles with correct attribution; no whole-tile CSS inversion/hue manipulation that distorts the imagery. Apply dark treatment to floating controls and panels only, not to the satellite pixels. Selected farm polygon has a crisp 2–3 px leaf-green/amber/red outline according to defined farm status with a translucent interior fill under 10–16% opacity, enough to see the imagery. Other farms use lower-opacity outline. Hover highlights outline; selected state adds white/leaf outline contrast and a compact tooltip label. Map background should not be obscured by a permanent oversized heatmap unless that layer is selected.
79.2 Map controls
At central map top-left: search/locate input Search a place or address… with results for city/region/country and chosen coordinate. For privacy, exact owner coordinates are private; when public network backend is active, public farm search only resolves to city. At map top-right, vertical controls in a 40 px high rounded dark-opaque stack: +, −, Locate selected farm, Layers, and optional fullscreen. Buttons have tooltips and keyboard accessibility. At bottom-left: current layer/date and provider attribution. Bottom-right: scale line and a small minimap/overview only if it adds real orientation value; map may show north indicator if map rotation is possible. If rotation isn't supported, do not display a fake compass control.
79.3 Right-side My Farms list
Panel header height 52 px. My Farms left aligned, total count as small text; + Add Farm green medium-weight button right. Panel search bar full width beneath header. List item target height 78–100 px, depending on displayed metadata. Each farm item has a 56×48 thumbnail crop/aerial image or custom generated placeholder appropriate to known farm type; farm name 14 px; status label/dot; city/country text; area if known; one compact row of up to three meaningful indicators (e.g. active crop, water status, health) only when those indicators exist. Right edge chevron. Do not cram five metric icons on every row. Selected item receives a 1 px leaf border, dark green tint and slim left selection bar. Hover changes surface slightly. Click focuses map to its saved geometry; if it lacks geometry, focus its location and show No boundary drawn yet with Draw boundary. Clicking a polygon on the map opens the same farm view, not a second alternative farm detail UI.

At small widths, right panel becomes a slide-over drawer with header and sticky + Add Farm; its list remains keyboard accessible. On mobile, user can hide left/right map panels separately, but drawing controls remain easy to reach.
79.4 Add Farm naming and polygon flow
Click + Add Farm: open a right-panel workflow (not an unrelated centred modal). Step 1 fields: Farm name required, optional location search if no location selected, and Continue to outline. Copy/helper: Use a name you'll recognise. You can add fields and crop plans after saving the farm. Name normalization should trim leading/trailing whitespace and compare case-insensitively after Unicode normalization; don't silently change the user's visible name. Disallow empty name, all-whitespace, and duplicate active farm names within the local dataset unless the product explicitly later supports same names by location. Duplicate state inline: A farm named “North Field” already exists. Actions: Open existing farm and Choose another name. If the user intentionally creates a separate farm with the same display name, require explicit distinctness policy (e.g. location suffix) rather than silently overwriting. Name uniqueness must not use mutable display name as entity ID.

After name, the map enters drawing mode and a floating toolbar appears docked to map upper-left/centre edge: Draw boundary, Undo point, Redo point when applicable, Close polygon, Cancel drawing, Clear draft, Save boundary, Help. First choose Draw boundary then click successive map points. After two points, show live line segment. Pointer movement previews the next segment. Clicking near the first point can close polygon only when at least three unique vertices exist; alternatively press Close polygon. Once closed, render fill and calculated area preview. Vertex handles (8–10 px dots, active vertex larger) appear in edit mode; midpoints appear when hovering segment; clicking midpoint inserts a vertex. Dragging is supported, but each edit has Undo and keyboard/delete alternative. Show a dashed draft boundary until valid/closed; valid saved boundary is solid.

Validation rules: at least 3 distinct vertices; no zero-area or self-intersecting polygon; closed-ring coordinates; valid longitude/latitude ranges; reject impossible coordinate values; warn if polygon crosses the antimeridian if unsupported; require map zoom high enough to draw useful boundaries; show area estimate from geodesic calculation in hectares/acres with rounding appropriate to geometry accuracy. Warn that a boundary drawn by map clicks is an estimated outline, not a land survey. User can cancel without saving, and draft autosaves locally when a drawing is in progress so accidental navigation can be recovered. On route exit, if valid dirty draft exists, show Resume drawing, Discard draft, Stay here (never silently discard).

After saving outline: ask whether to add fields now or later. Since a farm can contain multiple fields, make it easy to create field records from the farm view. Optional area from polygon is farm boundary area; do not automatically treat it as sum of separately entered field area unless those semantics are explicitly defined. Data model distinguishes farm outline and field area. If user tries to use the same boundary to create a field, ask whether this farm currently has one field or several. A farm boundary need not show individual field boundaries on the main map; the farm polygon remains the high-level outline, while field management occurs after opening the farm.
79.5 Editing, delete, and recovery
Click selected outline or Edit boundary: enters vertex edit mode with Save changes, Cancel, Undo, Redo, Reset to saved outline. Cancel restores the exact previously saved geometry. Save displays recalculated area and asks confirmation if area changes materially (suggested threshold 10% or user-adjustable/validated); explain what changed. Remove boundary from map removes only geometry visibility/record according to explicit confirmation and preserves farm, fields, crop records, analyses, calendar and logs. Delete farm is a separate destructive operation under farm settings with an explicit related-record impact list. Never trap a user in drawing mode: Escape opens a small confirm menu; Cancel drawing exits while preserving or discarding draft according to user's choice. Undo must undo the last map edit only, not a general page navigation or field record edit.
79.6 Layer panel
Open Layers to a 320–360 px side drawer overlaying map on the right or within My Farms panel only when the user closes/suspends that panel; do not stack three competing sidebars. Sections: Base map (Satellite selected, optional streets/terrain if providers exist), Farm outlines, Environment (precipitation/temperature only if implemented), Vegetation (only with real processed imagery), Public regional condition (only when backend and eligible published data exist). Each layer row has symbol, title, one-line explanation, visibility toggle, i details, and optional opacity slider. Vegetation/NDVI cannot appear as active when only simulated sample values exist. Legend is pinned to lower-right only while a data layer requiring it is active; includes units, color ramp, source/time and “what the layer can’t tell you” detail.
79.7 Farm selected from map
Click polygon: subtle 180–260 ms outline emphasis, selected farm panel content changes, map camera gently pans/zooms to fit the polygon, but doesn't over-zoom beyond useful extent. Then show a compact in-map preview with Open Farm View and View field list. Clicking the polygon again can open the same Farm View through a clear card action; don't require a finicky double-click. Map context is preserved on return. Escape/close returns to map at saved viewport and selected farm. The page clicked from Home always routes to this same Maps nav page.
80. My Farms portfolio and shared Farm View
80.1 Portfolio layout
My Farms is accessible from Maps panel and an appropriate farm-collection entry point; don't create competing farm detail routes. A list/grid portfolio view uses page heading My Farms, caption Manage your farms and the fields inside them., primary + Add Farm, and a search/filter row (All, Needs attention, Not analysed, Has active crop). Each farm row/card image or aerial texture has 4:3 visual thumbnail, farm name, location, farm-level status, number of fields, area if verified, current/latest rotation summary, and last analysis time. Use table-like dense list for large numbers and grid cards only when there are few records; remember view preference locally if needed.
80.2 Shared Farm View overview
The same Farm View must open from map polygon, My Farms row and Home farm selector. At the top: breadcrumb Maps / [Farm name] or previous route; farm name; overall status; locality; Edit farm under overflow; Run Deep Analysis as a secondary/primary action depending on alert state; New Analysis; and Farm settings. Below: an overview panel with weather/environment context explicitly labelled farm-level; overall condition; active/planned crop cycle; and fields list. Do not repeat a second separate Farm Detail design for another entry point.

Farm-level visual layout desktop: 12-col grid. Top header and status strip spans all columns. Left 7 columns: cinematic weather/conditions card and current crop cycle. Right 5 columns: farm condition/risk summary and compact farm map inset with boundary. Below full width: Fields section rendered as cards/list depending on field count; each field card shows name, area, status, selected rotation/crop only if recorded, soil-data availability, irrigation mode if known, and last analysis. Include direct Open field action. If there are one or two fields, more visual tile layout; more than four, compact list with search/filter. Always show a small “farm-level context” label beside weather and terrain, since the field's soil/crop status varies by field.
80.3 Field View detail
Clicking a field opens dedicated Field View in the same Farm View context with a visible parent farm breadcrumb, a selector to move to sibling fields, and the selected field name + area + crop-cycle status. It must not confuse the farm's overall status with this field's status. Tabs or anchored navigation only for genuinely distinct groups: Overview, Water, Soil, Crop & Vegetation, Risks, Activity, Analyses. In Simple Mode, show an overview and just the most relevant two/three detail sections; the other information remains available via clear “View details” links, not deleted. Advanced exposes all sections and data tables without adding a dozen global tabs. Use sticky local section nav at the top of long detail pages, not a second global navbar.

Field-level summary row: crop lifecycle state, most recent valid field analysis date, water condition if supported, soil profile status, valid vegetation observation if any, current alert count. Then crop cycle. Then evidence-rich panels. Soil texture diagram uses the right texture class; pH range graphic indicates crop-specific acceptable interval where supported. Water balance graph appears only where inputs support it. Crop vegetation section requires real satellite observation metadata. Activity log is a chronological timeline with category icons, local timestamps, source labels and add activity action.
80.4 Shared state and mode visibility
Simple Mode in Farm View: overview, crop cycle, current weather, field list, two/three key indicators, risk summary, top recommendation and recommended next step if supported. Advanced Mode: each metric's source/date/resolution, formulas, data quality, detailed trend chart, parameter tables, field-level comparisons, calculation chain and relevant raw source records. The overall layout remains recognisably identical; Advanced expands sections in place or reveals additional tabs/subsections, rather than rendering a different unrelated product.
81. New Analysis — detailed wizard, forms and result-state visuals
81.1 Wizard shell
Desktop main wizard width 760–900 px, centred within content; optional side context panel 280 px only while useful, containing selected farm snapshot, selected crop cycle and missing inputs. The top row has New Analysis, concise description Build and compare crop rotations for a specific field., Save draft status, and step indicator. Use six logical steps: Farm & Field; Location & Environment; Soil & Water; Crop History; Planning Goals; Review & Calculate. Do not show a long scrolling form with every question at once.

Step navigation: bottom-left Back, bottom-right Continue/Calculate. Current step active, completed steps checkmarked, skipped optional steps neutral. Validation is inline at the field and repeated only in summary if needed. Leaving the wizard uses autosaved draft with explicit timestamp. On return, ask Resume your saved analysis? with Resume / Start new; never overwrite draft silently.
81.2 Form control visual grammar
Each question group uses title 15–17 px, concise purpose line, control label 12–13 px, help icon, units, and source badge if prefilled. Text input height 42–46 px; dropdown 42–46 px; multi-select chips have selected check and not only colour; sliders show labeled endpoints and accessible numerical values; date controls show month/year when date is approximate. Optional fields clearly say Optional. Unknown is a legitimate input option. Prefilled field values display Using saved field data with Edit/Keep controls; do not silently change current records during analysis.

Crop history uses repeatable rows: crop thumbnail 40×40, crop selector, Approx. planted month/year and Approx. harvested month/year, confidence source field (exact/approximate/unknown). Row add button + Add past crop. Fallow / not previously cultivated is explicit. If harvest precedes planting, inline error. Crop row removal is undoable or requires a short confirmation if it contains saved data.

Soil form: texture selector with small illustrative sand/silt/clay texture diagram; pH/organic matter/nutrients only as known values; per-attribute source selector (Soil test, Mapped estimate, Farmer estimate, Unknown) wherever appropriate. Water setup: Rain-fed, Irrigated, Both, Not sure; conditional controls appear only for the selected answer. Do not default a missing pH, nutrient or irrigation quantity to an ideal number. Use explicit Unknown and indicate which model components become less certain.
81.3 Priority control visual
Use 3–5 priority cards in a balanced grid, not a row of bare sliders with unexplained numbers. Each card: line icon/illustration (water droplet, soil layers/leaf, climate shield, production/harvest only if supported), title, one-line explanation, 4-step segmented slider or compact labelled range, selected descriptor (e.g. High priority) and accessible value. Provide Balanced starting point reset. Priority names and available dimensions must match engine capabilities. The slider changes only weights/priority preference; it never cancels hard feasibility constraints. Do not display weight percentages unless they are truly normalized and generated by the same engine.
81.4 Review screen
Review is a clean, structured summary of location/farm/field, area, soil inputs + provenance, water setup, crop history timeline, planning window, priorities and missing high-value inputs. Each group has Edit. An Inputs that may limit this analysis neutral panel explains the most important unknowns. Primary CTA Calculate rotations; helper Your saved farm data will not be changed by running this analysis.
81.5 Real analysis loading screen
A visual progress narrative uses a quiet horizontal/vertical process timeline with statuses: Checking inputs, Retrieving available data, Validating sources and units, Checking crop feasibility, Generating valid sequences, Scoring trade-offs, Preparing explanation. Each step changes state only when code reports it has completed. Add a real task status and elapsed time if useful. Do not show fake percentage progression, artificial delays, or confetti. If source fails, show named source with retry/continue decision and impact. Cancel operation stops requests/calculations when possible and writes Cancelled only if a run was initiated.
81.6 Adaptive risk/acceptance question
This step occurs after preliminary deterministic candidate calculations. It should not appear if there is no meaningful trade-off. A question is a focused decision card with a small risk icon/amber border, specific title, contextual statement tied to candidate differences, 2–3 options, Why we ask disclosure, evidence/uncertainty row and mitigation. Example copy: One rotation fits your soil-health priority better, but carries a higher estimated pest-pressure risk. Buttons: I accept this trade-off, Prefer a more balanced option, Compare the evidence first. Do not frame estimated risk as certain infestation. Record the decision with the analysis run. If rejected, the engine filters/reweights candidates within constraints and re-ranks; show what changed. If no feasible alternative exists, explain why and let the farmer adjust priorities/constraints instead of manufacturing a candidate.
81.7 Results page — the hierarchy
Top intro: Rotation recommendations; selected farm/field and timestamp; Recommendation Confidence and Data Coverage badges with their different icons/styles; refresh status. Under it, a single hero recommendation that is visually strongest, followed by compact alternatives in a vertically scrollable ranked list. Never put every rotation and its full detail on the same long page.

Best rotation hero: left sequence composition uses the real separate crop images in 56–76 px tiles on desktop or larger 4:3 collage background only if text remains readable; over/under each image crop name and actual planning window. Right of sequence: rank, score, score definition, 2–3 strongest reasons, one major limitation/risk, confidence and data coverage, actions Open rotation, Compare, Save as plan. Crop images may be overlaid with a dark bottom gradient for labels. Do not use an image that repeats the same crop for multiple stages.

Alternatives: compact rows 80–116 px high in a single scrollable column. Rank badge on left, rotation name + mini sequence in centre, score aligned right, one trade-off summary and status badge below. Keep the visible rank, score and sequence while scrolling; sticky section header may be used. Clicking a row opens its detail in the canonical Rotations page, selecting that exact saved run. Compare adds it to compare tray. Save as plan asks which farm/field and confirms planned dates; it does not mark crop active.

Result sections below hero: Why this fits your priorities (3 short evidence-linked reasons), Trade-offs to understand (2–3 rows), Crop suitability within this sequence (crop-by-crop, compact), and disclosure How scores and confidence were calculated. AI explanation is text generated from structured outputs; it does not modify ranking. Clear empty state if no candidates; display hard constraints that eliminate options and a Review constraints action.
82. Rotations — sophisticated browse-and-inspect layout for data-heavy outputs
This page is a particularly high-priority visual design requirement. Do not dump rotation cards one after another, each with paragraphs. Build a persistent ranked rotation browser with an updating details workspace. Its skeleton stays the same while the selected rotation changes.
82.1 Layout at 1440 px
Page header (~72 px): Rotations and subtitle Compare crop sequences, understand trade-offs, and manage saved plans. On the right: farm selector, field selector if relevant, last analysis time in muted metadata, and a secondary Build a custom rotation action visible only in Advanced Mode. Beneath, a thin compact filter/sort toolbar: Recommended, Saved plans, Active, History tabs or segmented filter; sort dropdown Best match, Water saving, Soil health, Resilience, Newest; search only if number of rotations warrants it; compare tray status 0/3 selected.

Below toolbar, 3-column structure with 20 px gap: left ranked browser width 300–340 px; centre detail canvas flex about 640–760 px; right insight rail 270–310 px. At narrow desktop 1024–1279, hide right rail behind Insights; left list narrows to 280 px. On tablet/mobile the list becomes a full-screen selectable list and selecting one opens the detail view; a persistent back-to-list control returns without losing scroll position/filter/selection.
82.2 Left ranked browser
The title row Rotation options with count, followed by scroll container with independent scroll (not the whole page) and sticky search/sort/filter if needed. Each item shows rank number in a neutral small square/circle, name 14–15 px semibold, score 20–24 px with /100 and tiny label Match score, horizontal mini-sequence of crop pictograms/thumbnails and arrows, compact status pill (Recommended, Saved, Active, Past), and one-line differentiator (Lowest estimated water demand, Balanced priorities, Strongest soil trajectory). Height 96–124 px; no long paragraphs. Selected row gets 2 px leaf-green border and dark forest tint, with clear focus ring. Add a separate checkbox/compare icon to select for comparison without changing selected item. List order initially best feasible rotation first, score descending if high means better; score and rank must follow engine results, not front-end sorting guesses. Scrolling to the bottom reveals all options; keep selection visible through scrollIntoView only if the user selects an item outside the viewport.
82.3 Main selected-rotation detail workspace
Stable layout, only data changes with selected list item. At top, hero strip with crop-sequence imagery using the rotation's real crop-specific assets, title, rank, score/100, and one-sentence actual explanation. Use a left-to-right gradient across a 180–220 px high image strip, with dark left overlay behind text; do not hide crop imagery under a solid opaque slab. On image edge, add a small label Rotation preview and source line Crop imagery for identification only where needed, not every time.

Below hero: three/four summary metrics in unboxed or subtly surfaced strip: Cycle length, Crops in sequence, Estimated water demand if supported, and Recommendation confidence / Data coverage. Each has small icon top-left, metric name 11–12 px, value 18–22 px and support line. Never invent yield or financial results. Then tabs/anchored sections: Overview, Crop timeline, Benefits & risks, Resource budget, Why this rank. In Simple Mode the page defaults to Overview and Crop timeline; Advanced shows all tabs/sections (and may include model detail) but remains one detail workspace.

Overview uses 3 short strengths rows with evidence icon and one main trade-off row; no multi-paragraph generic AI prose. Crop timeline is the centrepiece: at desktop horizontal sequential track with actual crop photos (64–84 px), crop names, dated windows (Oct 2026 – Mar 2027), duration and sequence role. Year is a grouping label only for genuinely multi-year plan; never show contradictory Year 1 next to a seasonal date label if they mean different things. If long, horizontal scroll with visible arrow cue and keyboard scroll, but avoid clipping crop name. Mobile timeline becomes vertical sequence with connecting line and each crop's date/status beneath image/name.

Clicking a crop segment opens a contextual drawer with: role in sequence; crop suitability and factors; why placed after prior crop; relevant nutrient/water/pest interactions; duration assumptions; evidence/source; risk and mitigation. Simple view uses plain-language explanation; Advanced includes factor values and constraints. The same crop details should open from rotation card, crop cycle and individual crop fit list.
82.4 Right insight rail
The right rail stays stable between selection changes. It contains: Why it ranks here with 3 compact items (e.g. priority fit; sequence compatibility; feasibility constraints), Main trade-off with severity icon, Risk & mitigation (up to 3 items), a Compare button, and Save as plan. When a selected candidate is unavailable/infeasible, show a clear explanation and don't display a score badge implying it passed. Keep this rail no more than 4 modules tall; additional explanation expands in the main panel.
82.5 Comparison flow
Clicking Compare adds a candidate to a sticky compare tray at bottom of browser/page; tray shows small crop sequence chips + names and Compare now, Clear. Maximum 3 candidates. Compare view uses 2–3 aligned columns on desktop only if each retains readable labels. Comparison begins with a summary sentence: Rotation A saves an estimated 18 mm of water in this model, while Rotation B better matches the soil-health priority. Generate such sentence only from verified score component differences. Aligned rows: priority fit, crop suitability, water estimate, soil trajectory, resilience, sequence risk, cycle length, confidence, data coverage, and constraints. Every numeric dimension contains units/time horizon and indicates unavailable/non-comparable if needed. Avoid green checkmark just for larger values; directionality differs by metric. Use neutral comparison dots/bars and explicit lower/higher labels.

On mobile, a selector switches between candidates and an aligned metric list shows each candidate value with label. Do not squeeze three columns into illegible cards. Each candidate may be removed independently and comparison state remains while navigating back to list.
82.6 Advanced custom rotation builder
Only in Advanced Mode, accessed from Rotations header/secondary action. Page/workspace split: left crop catalogue/search with a distinct crop image, crop name, supported status and compatible planting windows; centre sequence timeline where crops can be reordered; right impact inspector. Drag-and-drop must have keyboard alternative Move earlier/Move later; deleting a stage is undoable. Each proposed edit runs feasibility checks and recalculates impact indicators; show pending/loading state before values update. Hard constraint violations block save and explain exactly which constraint failed. If a candidate is feasible but risky, show explicit risk details. Save produces a new versioned plan without rewriting source recommendation. If model can't calculate an impact due to missing data, display Not enough data to estimate.
82.7 Rotation Decision Replay
Within Why this rank, button Inspect ranking steps opens a side drawer or dedicated subview. Ordered events: normalized inputs, source/data coverage, candidate generation, feasibility filters, candidate rejections with reasons, component scores, normalized weights, risk preferences, tie-break rules, selected ranking. Each step has time/run ID and relevant result. This must be derived from actual deterministic engine records, not a generated fake trace or hidden chain of thought. If the run predates trace availability, say Detailed replay wasn't recorded for this run.
83. Analysis / What-If — UI for controlled experimentation
The main nav label Analysis opens this page; its content is only the What-If Simulator, not a duplicate New Analysis or Deep Analysis page. Page title What-If Analysis and subtitle Test how changed conditions could affect a saved rotation without changing your real farm records.

Layout: desktop 12-col grid. Left 4 columns controls; right 8 columns result canvas. Top row farm selector then field selector, base rotation selector and base analysis date. Provide scenario status badge Hypothetical scenario. At left use grouped control sections that expand/collapse: Weather & rainfall, Water availability, Soil properties, Crop timing, and Management assumptions. Only allow variables the model genuinely supports. Use slider/input pairs with units and an explicit Baseline vs Scenario label. A Reset all changes action returns sliders to baseline; show modified count.

Result canvas top: baseline vs scenario split summary. Four compact deltas (water demand/balance, suitability, soil trajectory, risk/resilience—only valid metrics) with absolute value, delta and direction label. Each delta has green/amber/red/neutral only under documented semantics. Below, a line chart overlays baseline and scenario over the same timeline with distinct line style as well as colour, units, legend and tooltip. A difference panel explains the largest meaningful effect in 1–3 bullets, each tied to a model output. If a different rotation now ranks better, provide View alternative rotation; do not silently replace the source plan.

Simple Mode: use a few grouped scenario controls and an outcome summary; hide formulas. Advanced Mode: numeric parameter fields, method/assumption disclosure, sensitivity graph, component score change, exact formula outputs, source data snapshots and warning if scenario leaves the model's validated range. Save scenario name and scenario record only after user action Save scenario. Scenarios are not live farm data, do not change crop cycle/calendar/alerts, and must be visibly watermarked/labeled Hypothetical across charts/exports.
84. Calendar — crop lifecycle as a designed planning surface
Page heading Calendar / Plan crop milestones and review upcoming field activity. On right: selected farm and field filters, view switch Month, Week, Agenda, a Today button and + Add event only for supported event types. Don't place a giant calendar grid and then repeat all events as cards below it; each view has its own compact event representation.

Month view: 7-column grid with subtle grid lines, 44–54 px day-cell header; each event appears as one-line coloured/icon chip with crop name or event verb, truncating safely with +N more. Colour means event type/state, not health. Week view shows 7 columns or agenda rail with event blocks based on time only if times exist. Most farming events are date-based; don't assign arbitrary times. Agenda view is a chronological grouped list with day heading, event time/date, icon, farm/field/crop, state (Planned, Completed, Overdue, Changed), and quick action.

Select event opens a contextual side drawer: title, related farm/field, linked crop cycle, planned/actual date, details, source, state, Edit, Mark completed or View field. A planned planting date change flows back to the crop cycle and updates only linked future planned events. Actual completed events remain unchanged. Overdue planned dates should not automatically imply planting was completed; show Confirmation needed. If date has passed but no actual event, display Planned date passed — confirm actual status.

Add event flow: select event type, farm/field, date/date range, optional note, crop-cycle linkage, save. For crop planting/harvest events, prefer using crop-cycle actions so lifecycle state updates coherently. Avoid duplicate event records when action originated in Crop Cycle. Delete confirmation makes clear if only Calendar item is removed or actual crop/activity record is affected; these are not interchangeable.

Simple Mode: month/agenda, crop milestones, current planned/actual states, next 2–3 relevant dates. Advanced Mode: filters by type/status/field/crop, recurring task rules if supported, activity data provenance, exact timestamps, conflict diagnostics and import/export only if implemented.
85. Deep Analysis — report is an editorial, evidence-first experience
Deep Analysis must feel like a high-quality investigation report with a clear narrative, data visualizations and expandable technical evidence—not a long generated essay. Its three outputs are distinct: (1) user-friendly report; (2) full process trace/log; (3) dated historical snapshots and comparisons. Keep them linked but not collapsed into one overlong page.
85.1 Pre-run configuration
At the top of Field View, Deep Analysis action opens a concise pre-run panel. It names the selected farm/field and last analysis timestamp, lists what will be refreshed, and displays the farm-practice context status. If watering practice has not been captured, ask a concise, specific question. If existing routine values exist, show them with Review/update rather than asking again as if new. Natural-language example prompt: Describe your usual watering routine (for example, how often, approximately how much, and whether you skip watering after rain). Parse response into structured fields, preview the interpretation, show unknowns/assumptions, and ask Confirm and use this routine before saving. Do not silently infer litres or interval precision from vague language.

Schedule setup is in Advanced Mode/Settings, but a single-run confirmation may mention whether the user wants to use the confirmed practice record for future scheduled runs. Scheduled runs must not ask interactive questions in a background context; they use previously confirmed routine/profile data and mark it out of date if user says routines vary.
85.2 Running state
Use an editorial progress layout with left process timeline and right context summary on desktop. Timeline rows: Load farm & field records, Fetch current weather, Fetch available environmental/soil sources, Validate timestamps and units, Run water/soil/crop calculations, Compare with history, Prepare findings. Each row shows actual pending/running/complete/partial/failed state, not fake full progress. Each row can expand into the actual operation metadata if already available; users cannot see hidden private reasoning or secrets. Right summary shows the selected field, crop lifecycle status, sources currently responding and elapsed time. Provide Cancel analysis.
85.3 Completed report composition
Main page width 1120–1320 px centred; page header row with Deep Analysis, farm/field + run timestamp + status (Completed, Partial, Failed, Cancelled). Top summary panel 230–280 px tall: left title Overall farm condition, status badge and a score only if a defined, documented composite model exists; right contains compact horizontal status bars for Water, Soil, Crop/Vegetation, Risks and Data coverage. A radial chart is acceptable if the number represents the defined score; do not use a fake 90/100 decorative gauge. Under summary, a 2-column grid: left 7 columns Key findings sorted by priority; right 5 columns What to watch/Suggested checks. Further down, full-width Indicator trends with date range, Changes since previous run, Data limitations, Recommended next steps, and collapsed Detailed calculations & sources.
85.4 Findings card exact structure
Each finding row/card: icon category; severity/status badge; short title with a verb or specific condition; one-sentence evidence-backed summary; small row of 1–3 values with units; observation window; Why this matters disclosure; Possible causes with uncertainty labels if appropriate; What to check/do next as a suggestion tied to evidence; Open supporting data link. Example neutral wording: Recent rainfall is below the reference period, while estimated crop demand remains high. Avoid Your crop is dying, Disease detected or definitive causal claims unless actual validated diagnostics support them. Cap initial visible findings to 3–5; View all findings expands list. Each finding uses source references/data records, not an AI-created fact with no support.
85.5 Overall health and confidence
A composite score may appear only if documented: dimensions, weights, hard caps, missing-data treatment, temporal basis, version and whether output is modelled vs measured. Otherwise show state label plus a domain summary. Always keep Recommendation Confidence and Data Coverage distinct. Deep Analysis confidence and crop-rotation Recommendation Confidence may have related visual grammar but not the same semantics; label the basis of each. Show two visually different badges/icons: confidence uses a target/gauge icon and outlined meter; coverage uses layers/database icon and segmented fill. Do not make 72% data coverage look like a 72% probability of correctness.
85.6 Historical comparison
If prior runs exist, top of report shows a Since previous analysis row with at most 3 material changes, e.g. rainfall input, field status, water estimate; click expands exact values, date ranges and comparability warning. If inputs or calculation versions differ significantly, mark metrics Not directly comparable and explain why. Missing history is an empty state that invites future runs rather than fake trend lines.

Simple Mode report: executive status, key findings, relevant risks, supported actions, source/freshness summary and uncertainty in plain language. Advanced Mode adds detailed indicators, formula inputs, intermediate calculations, parameters, crop model limits, raw result table, source responses metadata (redacted), confidence model breakdown, and historic comparability notes. No mode changes score or report conclusions.
86. Deep Analysis Logs, execution trace and history
The log/history surface is a separate accessible destination under the farm's secondary Analysis navigation, not the Deep Analysis launch button itself. Header Analysis history / Review past checks and compare how conditions changed. Filters: farm, field, date range, status, automation/manual. List rows show date+time, overall status, overall score/status, number of material findings, source coverage summary and run kind (manual/scheduled/catch-up). A row click opens a log detail view/popup preserving filters and scroll; × close returns to the same list state. Prefer right drawer on desktop and full-screen modal on mobile if content is dense.

Trace viewer: ordered timeline with per-step icon and phase name; state glyph for success/partial/warning/failure; start time/duration; concise result summary. Expand row to show exact source/request family, relevant parameters (never API keys), source response code/status, timestamp, data quality flags, formula identifier/version, intermediate values, output IDs and error category/message. Every displayed row must originate from an actual recorded event. A failed action must appear as failed/partial—not green success. If no trace exists for an old run, say so.

Advanced mode exposes the complete detailed trace and technical metadata; Simple mode shows the six/seven major process phases plus the reasons behind report conclusions. It must not expose hidden model reasoning, provider secrets, raw private fields irrelevant to the UI, or raw API payloads containing unnecessary personal data. The trace is an operational audit—not the model's private chain of thought.

History charts: show one metric per chart or aligned small multiples, each with title, units, date axis, known gaps, source notes and method version. Do not use multiple y-axes for unrelated units without explicit clarity. User can select two runs to compare; compare only matching metrics/units/time windows when valid. Show No comparable prior result when needed. The chart selection and run list remain connected. Every saved report is immutable snapshot; new analysis creates new record.
87. Simple and Advanced Mode — mandatory, global and exhaustive
87.1 Mechanism
Global setting located at Settings → Appearance & Information Detail. Two radio cards, not nav tabs:
- Simple Mode — exact copy: Clear summaries, essential metrics, and practical explanations.
- Advanced Mode — exact copy: Detailed measurements, data sources, calculations, assumptions, and history.
  Persist per browser in app settings. Apply immediately without reloading the whole app; announce Simple Mode enabled / Advanced Mode enabled as a polite status. No second mode control in global nav. Do not change logic, scoring, ranking, thresholds or saved records when switching mode.
After a successful New Analysis or Deep Analysis in Simple mode, a one-time-per-run post-result prompt may appear: title Want to explore the full analysis?; body Advanced Mode reveals detailed calculations, data sources, assumptions, and confidence limits. Actions Switch to Advanced and Not now. Do not show during loading, on error, at every render, or before results. Store dismissal for that analysis run.
