87.3 Progressive disclosure does not mean hiding essential utility
Simple Mode is not a crippled version. Essential controls, editing, drawing, crop states, alerts, save/undo/cancel, source caveats, data gaps, and risk warnings remain visible in both modes. Advanced Mode adds technical depth, secondary metrics, formulas, metadata, additional filtering, configuration and specialised builders. It must not make the same page simply longer by opening every disclosure. On complex pages, use section tabs, sub-tabs, expandable details and sticky filter controls. If an advanced feature affects an essential task (e.g., polygon editing, choosing a field, reviewing risk trade-offs), expose the action in both modes with simple labels and put technical detail behind disclosure.
88. Settings page — a genuine settings destination
Use a dedicated Settings page, not only a modal. Left in-page settings nav 220–250 px; main pane 680–920 px; right explanatory notes only when helpful. Group settings in this order: General, Appearance & Information Detail, Units & Region, Data Providers, Analysis & Automation, Notifications, Local Data & Backups, Privacy, About & Diagnostics. Each group has heading, one-sentence description, neatly spaced controls and save feedback. Do not use a giant wall of toggles.

General: default farm/field, landing page, date/time format, unit region. Appearance: fixed Dark Forest Intelligence (no alternative theme variants), reduced motion, dense/comfortable data density only if implemented, information detail radio cards (Simple/Advanced), chart animation respecting reduced motion. Units: °C/°F, ha/acres, mm/in, speed. Conversion occurs in display layer after normalized calculations; switching units updates every route consistently.

Data Providers cards: Weather, satellite/map tiles, soil, crop parameter database, AI provider, optional external evidence search. Each card includes name, purpose, config state, source doc link, required secret variable name (but no value), last tested outcome/time if actual, affected features, attribution/license. Test connection only exists if actual test code is wired; no decorative active control. States Not configured, Connected, Unavailable, Rate limited, Request failed, Not tested are distinct.

Analysis/Automation: Deep Analysis run frequency (manual/daily/every 3 days/weekly/custom only if scheduler supports custom), farm/field scope, last run, next eligible run, auto-run setting, failure policy. Local-only rule: scheduler runs while app is active and performs overdue catch-up at next launch; it cannot promise closed-browser execution. Explain that provider cadence may mean unchanged observations even when checked daily. Never use server-scheduler language in browser-only mode.

Notifications: in-app preferences, severity types, deduplication behavior. No email/push in current local release. Local data: used storage if browser exposes it; export all data; export selected farm; import backup preview; clear cache separate from delete all records; delete one farm; delete all local data. Destructive controls separated and require impact confirmation. State copy exactly: Your farm records are stored in this browser on this device. There is no account or cloud sync in this version. Export a backup before clearing browser data or moving to another device.

Privacy: explain network/API data transmission truthfully, local-only storage limits, and that data sent to external APIs/AI provider is subject to those providers' terms. About: app version, schema version, crop database version, model/calculation version, source attribution, known limitations, Copy diagnostics with secrets and exact private coordinates redacted. Settings needs Simple/Advanced visibility too: Simple shows everyday controls and data warnings; Advanced expands provider diagnostics, thresholds, cache policy, run history and configuration. Never show raw secret key.
89. AI Agronomist and Messages — two distinct experiences
89.1 AI Agronomist context drawer
Global header AI icon opens a right drawer ~400–460 px wide, or bottom sheet on mobile. Header AI Agronomist; context selector shows selected farm/field and source scope; close X. Conversation area has a quiet welcome panel only when empty, with prompts such as Explain this rotation, What changed since my last analysis?, Help me understand this indicator. Don't use fake farm facts. Each assistant response separates What the data shows, Possible interpretation, and What remains uncertain where appropriate. Evidence references render as compact clickable source chips opening the relevant Farm View/analysis source drawer. User messages right-aligned with subtle leaf tint; assistant messages left with leaf-mark icon, no giant cartoon avatar. Add copy response, cite/evidence expand, retry if failed, cancel while streaming if supported. Composer bottom sticky: multiline textarea, send button, attach current farm context toggle / clear context, loading state. Enter sends; Shift+Enter newline (document this in tooltip/help). Ensure focus stays predictable after send.

When AI provider is missing, show AI is not configured and explain setup in Settings. Keep deterministic engine explanation if available, but do not impersonate AI or return canned responses labelled live. Store local chat history only in the active browser if the user explicitly wants that and there is a clear delete control; avoid saving large raw private data payloads into chat transcript.
89.2 Messages and Global Network future mode
The current release is local-only. Cross-user chat, real friends, shared community messages, cloud sync and multi-user Global Network cannot truthfully function as genuine shared communication without a backend and authenticated identity. Do not seed fake online people or pretend an outgoing message was delivered to a remote user. Keep the feature design and components future-ready, but use a clear local-mode unavailable/explanation state for cross-user functions until the backend exists. A local “notes” or “draft message” is not a sent message; label it accordingly if included.

When shared-backend mode is implemented later, design a dedicated Messages page with left conversation list (~320 px), centre conversation pane, and right context panel optionally showing the farm/profile/context being discussed. Top of list: search Search messages, New message button, tabs All, Unread, Requests only if those states exist. Conversation row: avatar/initials only from actual user profile, name, last message snippet, timestamp, unread badge; pinned and muted icons have distinct meaning. Main conversation: user identity + online/last seen only if true data is provided and consented; messages grouped by local date, delivery/read status only when backend emits it, reply/thread affordance if implemented, attachments with file type/size and safe open/download. Composer: multiline, attachment button, send, disabled/waiting/failure states, unsent draft persistence, retry for failed messages. Unsend/delete semantics clearly distinguish local deletion from delete-for-everyone when supported. Search results respect permissions. Block/report/mute available from contextual overflow; enforce block in every contact path, not just hide the button. Communities include member roles, rules, moderation, report, leave, and privacy. The right context pane never shows private farm detail to recipients without explicit sharing.
89.3 Simple/Advanced for chat
Simple mode: normal messages, clear citations/links and relevant farm context. Advanced mode: show source/run references, technical detail, model status and message diagnostic metadata only where safe. Advanced is not permission to reveal hidden reasoning or secrets.
90. Data & Trust Center — design the provenance experience
Page heading Data & Trust Center / See where Field Shift's information comes from and how it is used. Main summary band shows actual number of configured sources, healthy/unavailable counts only if computed. Avoid a fake trust score. Provider cards grouped by purpose. Each card: provider/logo or simple source icon, name, what data it contributes, current connection status, latest successful retrieval time, supported geography/time window, spatial/temporal resolution, attribution, View source details disclosure. Test connection button must call actual source or clearly be disabled with reason.

A source detail view shows dataset name/version where available, variable names, units, spatial/time resolution, retrieval time, measurement period, missing values, quality flags, fallback source, affected feature calculations and documentation links. Tables allow copying source URI and values, no secrets. User can inspect why a metric is labelled observed, forecast, estimate, farmer-reported, or modelled. Simple mode communicates plain-language source purpose and key limit; Advanced displays technical parameter/resolution/quality data.
91. Notifications and alert inbox
Header bell with true unread count. Open right drawer (390–440 px desktop) with title Notifications, filters All, Unread, Active, and sort Priority/Newest. A notification row has severity glyph, one-line title, farm/field name, concise cause summary, relative date/time, unread dot and action menu. Selecting routes to the exact evidence context and marks read; merely marking read/dismissing does not resolve the underlying agronomic issue. Dismiss removes from active inbox but underlying run/alert remains in history. Duplicate signal rule updates existing item unless a new period/escalation justifies another. Failed API request is a data/system status, not automatically a farm health alert.

Simple mode shows summary and action. Advanced adds trigger rule, data sources, measurement window, deduplication reason and related run ID. Notifications only in-app in local mode; no email/push promise.
92. Page Guide and onboarding — interactive, connected to the actual UI
First use with no farms should show a deliberate empty state rather than fake farms: title Start with your first farm, one short explanation, botanical illustration or satellite imagery crop, primary Add a farm, secondary Explore a sample workflow only if demo data is explicitly synthetic and labelled. A guide entry appears in header/help or Settings, not a permanent intrusive banner. The interactive guide uses actual DOM elements/routes and overlays a spotlight plus small tooltip panel; it is not a parallel fake website replica. Sequence for maps: open Maps → search location → click + Add Farm → enter name → draw three or more points → close polygon → review area → save → add field → New Analysis → Save as Plan → confirm crop planting → open Deep Analysis → view trace → change detail mode in Settings. Each step shows one concise instruction and a labelled pointer; next/back/skip/exit; pause if the target route changes unexpectedly; restore focus and state on exit. Reduced motion disables pointer travel; use fading/highlighting instead. All sample/tutorial data has a Demo label and must never enter actual calculations unless user deliberately imports or promotes it.
93. Reusable visualization catalogue — define what each visual means
- Temperature forecast curve: values mapped to a labelled y-scale or stable implicit scale with visible labels; line stroke 2–2.5 px; 6–8 points; selected-day vertical dotted line; sun/cloud icon only at selected point; tooltip contains date, units, exact forecast/source. The curve communicates change over time, not decorative movement.
- Water balance chart: aligned series for crop demand, effective rainfall, irrigation (if recorded) and modeled storage/deficit. Units mm per stated time step. Use positive/negative baseline clearly, legend toggles and source warning. Never infer irrigation from rainfall.
- Soil texture triangle/stack: sand/silt/clay values sum to 100% only if actual proportions exist; otherwise show texture class selector or mapped estimate class, not made-up proportions.
- Rotation timeline: connected crop sequence with dated windows, durations and explicit transition points. A multi-season crop spans the appropriate duration rather than being duplicated as separate crops. Date range and crop-cycle duration are consistent.
- Ranked rotation score: visible ranking and score with definition. Higher is better under defined priority fit only among feasible candidates; show any hard constraint that disqualifies a rotation. No unlabeled donut gauges.
- Component score bars: label, value/scale, direction of better performance, source/model version in Advanced. Do not sort automatically on inconsistent components.
- Trend line: date axis, unit, valid data markers, missing-data gaps displayed as gaps—not interpolated unless method is disclosed. Baseline/forecast/predicted series have separate line styles as well as colours.
- NDVI/EVI map overlay: only from actual retrieved and processed imagery with cloud/mask quality and observation date, product source, resolution, polygon clip logic and legend. NDVI formula NDVI=(NIR-Red)/(NIR+Red) in Advanced. It isn't yield, disease or nutrient diagnosis. If not available, show Satellite vegetation data unavailable not a synthetic map.
- Confidence: only a defined model output. If no calibrated probability, prefer qualitative level with method explanation instead of a percentage.
- Data coverage: based on input/source availability matrix, such as 4 of 6 required input groups available; not a probability.
- Farm portfolio overview: status categories plus counts, never a pie of fake farm health. If showing multiple fields, aggregate only according to documented method and show field count/data coverage.
- Decision trace timeline: chronological events from the run log with exact timestamp/duration/result. Never fabricate trace rows to create a more impressive animation.
Charts default to direct labels, concise legends and accessible tooltips. Tooltips must work on pointer hover and keyboard focus/touch tap. Do not use colour-only series identification: use different dash patterns, point shapes or labels when series may be hard to distinguish. Use SVG/canvas libraries that support responsive resizing and accessible table fallback; avoid screenshot charts.
94. Browser, device and visual QA matrix
Test the following viewport sizes: 1920×1080, 1440×900, 1280×800, 1024×768, 768×1024, 430×932, 390×844, 360×800. For each major route, capture images in Simple and Advanced modes. For stateful features capture empty, populated, selected, editing, saving, success, error, stale/partial and mobile states as applicable.

Visual criteria: no overlap or clipped labels; primary action visually unique; typography hierarchy consistent; all metric values have units; card density matches importance; chart y-axis and source labels fit; image focal point remains correct after cropping; gradient does not hide required copy; map controls do not cover farm polygon selection or each other; panel widths allow real names; selected field remains obvious; sticky header does not cover anchors; modals fit short screens and scroll internally; dropdowns remain within viewport; toasts don't block controls; focus rings are visible; reduced motion is respected; all pointer overlays have correct pointer-events; image errors use a fallback without layout jump.

Functional visual QA: click every button, row and control in every major state. Confirm that visible values update from data and that route context remains coherent. A control without a real action is a release blocker. Test keyboard nav, Escape rules, screen reader labels, form validation, focus restoration, chart alternatives and map drawing controls. WCAG 2.2 AA is the target for contrast and interaction where applicable; target size of at least 24×24 CSS px minimum, 36×36 or greater for frequently used map/mobile actions where space allows.
95. Explicit build obligations for the AI builder
1. Implement all visual and behavioural requirements in this annex; do not treat them as inspiration-only notes.
2. If a feature is technically unavailable in local-only mode, build an honest designed unavailable/configuration state and explain the missing backend/provider. Never populate it with fake live content.
3. Use the master document's source-file inventory to audit all existing components and state paths; preserve useful functions only after validating them.
4. Reconcile the page-level Simple/Advanced table above against every route and every repeated component. A component that appears on multiple pages must inherit the same mode semantics unless this document explicitly states otherwise.
5. Produce a route/component checklist and screenshots for major UI states. Record any requirement that could not be implemented, why, and the most accurate available alternative. Do not claim “complete” without evidence.
6. Prioritise convenience: edit and undo must be close to creation, delete semantics clear, selected context preserved across navigation, and every long data list has a search/filter or pagination/virtualization plan when volume can grow.
7. Do not move settings to the main nav as a Simple/Advanced toggle. Keep a dedicated Settings destination and the global information-detail option inside it.
8. Do not make the user read long paragraphs to interpret daily information. Use visual hierarchy first, then concise copy, and advanced disclosures for full formulas/evidence.
9. Do not invent a scientific fact, live data, source success, chat delivery, automation run or farm status. Every such state must be backed by records/events and display accurate availability.
10. Keep the original three design references (user weather screenshot, user agriculture-map screenshot, generated Field Shift concept) in the provided visual package. Treat them as high-level visual direction; adapt their patterns with Field Shift identity and data integrity.
